export const PAGES: Record<string, string> = {"overview":"Start\n\nPage 1 of 40\n\n# How it works\n\nEgg builds your GitHub repository and runs it on your Nest container as a service. Egg is independent, so it is not an official Hack Club product.\n\nNest gives you a small Linux container that you reach over SSH. Egg connects with a key you control, uploads your finished build and runs it as a systemd service on a port, so Nest’s proxy can map your domain to that port. You no longer do that work by hand in a terminal.\n\nYou need a Hack Club account, a Nest container and a GitHub repository. Your container has nothing installed ahead of time.\n\nThese docs use each word in one way only.\n\n- **Container.** Your Nest Linux container, the one you log in to with SSH. Your app runs here.\n- **Server.** Egg’s menus call a Nest container you have connected to Egg a server. One server is one Nest username.\n- **Egg machine.** The computer that runs the Egg website and its worker. If you use someone else’s Egg, it is their computer. If you run Egg yourself, it is yours. It clones your repository and packs the source.\n- **Worker.** The part of Egg that takes queued deployments and runs them.\n- **Build.** The install and build of your project. It runs on your container, in a new release folder, under a cap.\n- **Cap.** A hard limit on memory and CPU that the kernel enforces on a build, so a build that goes over it stops. Your app keeps running.\n- **Archive.** The finished build packed into one compressed file, `release.tar.gz`, for upload.\n- **Release.** One uploaded build, in its own folder on your container.\n- **Production branch.** The git branch whose pushes go live. A branch is a named line of commits in your repository.\n- **Preview.** A build of any other branch. It runs as a second service on its own port, so production keeps running.\n\n## What happens on a push\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: GitHub tells Egg about the push\n\n   GitHub sends the push to Egg with a signed request. Only matching projects with **Deploy automatically on push** on get a deployment, so GitHub must reach Egg’s web address. An Egg on localhost never hears about pushes. There, press **Deploy** or use a deploy hook.\n2. Step 2: The deployment joins a queue\n\n   The worker picks up two queued deployments at a time by default, across all projects. A project never runs two at once, so the second waits as Queued. With no worker running, every deployment stays Queued.\n3. Step 3: The worker clones your repository\n\n   This step runs on the Egg machine. Egg clones the newest commit of the branch into a work folder there and packs the source into an archive, so the GitHub token stays on the Egg machine. Nothing is installed or built here, and the Egg machine spends no memory or CPU on your build.\n4. Step 4: The build runs on your container\n\n   Egg checks your container first: the Node.js version, the package manager your lockfile needs, Python 3 and venv for a Python project, systemd-run, timeout, and at least 2048 MB of free disk. A failed check ends the deployment with one line that says what to fix, so nothing is uploaded. When all checks pass, Egg uploads the source over SSH and unpacks it into a new release folder next to the running release. Your install, build and production install commands run there, under the cap.\n   The memory cap is the server’s **Build memory (MB)**, 512 MB by default, and never more than your container’s memory minus 512 MB. A project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB while it builds, if your container has room for it. The CPU cap is **Build CPU limit (cores)**. The build gets no swap and runs at a low CPU priority, so your app wins when the CPU is busy. It stops after 15 minutes. Your running app keeps serving the whole time. One build runs at a time on a container, and another waits for up to 30 minutes. If the build fails, runs out of memory, times out or is canceled, Egg deletes the new folder, so the running release stays in place.\n5. Step 5: The service switches over\n\n   Egg writes your environment variables to a file that only the service reads. For production, the `current` link points at the new release and the service restarts, so the app is offline until the new release answers. A preview starts its own service on its own port, and production is not touched.\n6. Step 6: Egg waits for an answer\n\n   Egg asks your app on its port, from your container, for up to 45 seconds. If it never answers, the deployment is Error, so Egg switches back to an earlier production release, when one exists, and checks it for 20 seconds. A first production deployment has nothing to go back to: the service stays registered and systemd keeps restarting it every 3 seconds. Fix the app and deploy again.\n\nA push to your production branch goes live. A push to any other branch builds a preview on its own port, so production keeps running. When a deployment ends, the worker deletes its folder on the Egg machine, whether it worked or not. That folder held the clone and the source archive.\n\nWhat it does not do\n\nEgg does not run databases or give a preview a public address on its own, and does not promise that any application works on Nest. It runs the runtimes listed under Projects and runtimes, and any start command that listens on the `PORT` it is given. A Dockerfile alone does nothing, so Egg stays in native process mode until a check of your server confirms Docker works there.\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Deployments per project at once | One | The others wait as Queued. |\n| Deployments running at once, all projects | 2 by default | The rest wait. A self-hosted worker can change this. |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | The deployment fails with “The build timed out.” |\n| Build time | 15 minutes, for install and build together. It cannot be changed. | The deployment fails with “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” |\n| Build cap | The server’s Build memory (MB), 512 MB by default, at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when your container has room for it, and never more than your container’s memory minus 512 MB. Build CPU limit (cores), 2 by default. No swap. | A build that needs more memory is stopped with “The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB.” A build that needs more CPU only runs slower. If the cap would be under 256 MB, the build does not start. |\n| Build output | 500 MiB (524.288 MB), measured on the archive | The deployment fails with the size it found. |\n| Wait for the app to answer | 45 seconds | The deployment is marked Error and the previous release is put back, if there is one. |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- A push did not start a deployment\n\n  Open the project’s Settings, Build and Run, and check that **Deploy automatically on push** is ticked. A project made from a pasted public repository URL is never built by a push, because it does not store the repository name GitHub sends. Press Deploy, or use a deploy hook. GitHub must also be able to reach Egg’s web address.\n- `“The new release did not become healthy on port N. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.”`\n\n  The app started but nothing answered on its port. Read the last log lines, fix the port or the address it binds to, and deploy again.\n- `“The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  A build went over its cap and was stopped. Your app was not. The second sentence is added only when a release was already serving. Raise **Build memory (MB)** on the Container page and deploy again, or make the build lighter. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- A deployment stays Building or Deploying and does not move\n\n  The worker stopped or lost its link to the database. Egg does not free the deployment by itself, because a command it started on your container may still be running. Nothing else deploys for this project until the person running Egg follows “Recovering a stale deployment” in the self-host guide. After that the deployment shows Error and you can deploy again.\n\n### Example: Push a fix to a bot\n\n#### Given\n\nSam has a Discord bot for sharing recipes in a repository called `sam-r/recipe-bot`. It runs on Node.js, and a Nest container called `sam-r` is already connected.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Push to the production branch\n\n   Sam pushes a fix to `main` from a laptop.\n2. Step 2: Open the Deployments tab\n\n   Sam opens the project’s Deployments tab and watches the new row.\n\n#### Result\n\nA new deployment appears as Queued, then Building, then Deploying, then Ready. The Build Logs card shows the clone, the install and the build, and ends with the app answering on its port. The bot restarts with the fix and no one logged in to the container.\n\n**Related:** [Quick start](/docs/quickstart), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [GitHub and previews](/docs/github).\n","getting-started":"Start\n\nPage 2 of 40\n\n# Getting started\n\nFollow the four chapters of the guided first-project tour in text: connect your container, import a repository, watch the first deployment and give it an address.\n\nThis page follows the tour in order. Each item gives you the words the tour uses, then the button to press on the real screen, so you can read along as you work.\n\nYou need three things before you start:\n\n- **A Nest container and your Nest username.** You use the name you log in with.\n- **A GitHub repository.** You can import a public one by its address. A private one, and deploys on every push, need the GitHub app.\n- **An app that listens on a port.** Egg sets `PORT` and `HOST` for it, so a static site needs no code for this.\n\nNot on Nest?\n\nIf you run your own copy of Egg, or deploy to a server you own, some words differ, so the address chapter is the one to watch. Read [Run Egg yourself](/docs/run-yourself) first.\n\n## 1. Connect your container\n\nIn this chapter you connect your container, so Egg can reach it and check what it can run.\n\n1. **Put your app on Nest.** Egg builds your GitHub repository and runs it on your Nest container. It takes about five minutes. Egg signs in to your container over SSH with a key made only for it.\n2. **Connect your container first.** Egg needs a way in. This sets it up. On Projects, with no server yet, press **Connect a server**. That opens Connect.\n3. **Your Nest username.** The name you log in with, as in ssh username@hackclub.app. Then press Create key. Type it in **Nest username**. The status reads “Waiting for the key”.\n4. **Add the key in Nest.** Copy it, open the Nest dashboard, and paste it on the SSH Keys page. Then come back. Copy the **Public key** and press **Open the Nest dashboard**.\n5. **Test the connection.** Egg connects once and checks what your container can run. Press **Test connection**.\n6. **Check the fingerprint.** Compare it with the one Nest shows for your server. Confirm only if they match. Egg refuses any other key afterwards. This appears the first time, in the “Confirm host key” box. Press **Confirm host key** yourself; nothing confirms it for you.\n7. **Connected.** Next, pick a repository. The status turns to “Connected”.\n\n## 2. Import a repository\n\nIn this chapter you point a project at your repository, so Egg can read it and you can check the settings.\n\n1. **Pick your repository.** Search, then press one. This is the version when the GitHub app is already installed: find it under **GitHub account** and press **Import**.\n2. **Connect GitHub.** This lets Egg read your repositories and deploy on every push. This is the version when the app is set up on this Egg but not installed for you. Press **Install the GitHub App**, share the repository, then come back.\n3. **Paste a repository URL.** A public one works without any setup. This is the version when this Egg has no GitHub app at all, as in a copy you run yourself. Paste an address like `https://github.com/owner/name` and press **Continue**. Pushes will not deploy on their own.\n4. **Check what Egg found.** It guessed the name, runtime and port from your repository. Change anything that is wrong. The port starts at `3000`. Your app has to listen on that port; see the note below.\n5. **Something is already on your container.** Items marked as blocking stop the deploy until you deal with them. Egg never changes anything it did not create. This appears only when the page lists things already on the container, under “What is already on this container”.\n6. **Deploy.** Egg builds on your container and starts the app. You can watch it happen. Press **Deploy**. You land on the deployment page.\n\nYour app has to listen on the right port\n\nEgg starts your app with `PORT` set to the port you chose and `HOST` set to `0.0.0.0`. If your app binds only to `localhost`, the health check fails.\n\n## 3. Watch the first deployment\n\nIn this chapter you start a release, so a build runs on your container.\n\n1. **Watch it build.** This is the live output from your container. A first build usually takes a few minutes. You can leave this page; the tour will not block you. It is the **Build Logs** card.\n2. **Your app is running.** It listens on port N inside your container. It has no web address yet. The next step gives it one. The state reads Ready. N is the port you chose.\n3. **The build failed.** Read the last lines of the log to see why. The troubleshooting page lists the usual causes. The state reads Error and your earlier release keeps running or is put back.\n\nIf the build fails, read the last lines of the log, then open the [troubleshooting page](/docs/troubleshooting#troubleshooting-build) for the usual causes.\n\n## 4. Give it an address\n\nIn this chapter you give the app a web address, so you can open it in a browser.\n\n1. **Choose an address.** Use your-nest-name.hackclub.app (or a subdomain of it), which Nest hands out with no DNS setup, or a domain you own. Pick this project; the port is the one it listens on. Open Domains, fill in **Add a domain** and press **Add domain**.\n2. **Do this in Nest.** Egg cannot change Nest’s routing, so this one step is yours. The steps below are exact: open the Domains page in the Nest dashboard and enter the same domain and port. Then come back and press Check. The steps are in the panel headed “Next: finish setup for” and your domain.\n3. **Check it.** Egg looks at DNS and tells you whether it points here. Press **Check**. The status moves from “Waiting for DNS” to “Active”.\n4. **Your app is live at the address you chose.** What next: add environment variables, set up a deploy hook, turn on analytics. The deployment page then shows the address.\n\nThe first page below walks the same path with every button, limit and error message. The others cover each part in full.\n\n**Related:** [Quick start](/docs/quickstart), [Servers](/docs/servers), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Domains](/docs/domains).\n","quickstart":"Start\n\nPage 3 of 40\n\n# Quick start\n\nFrom nothing to a running app on your Nest container, in four steps. Every button name here matches the one on screen.\n\nEgg signs in to your Nest container with a key made only for it and clones your GitHub repository on the Egg machine, the computer that runs Egg, so your container gets a copy of the source. Your container builds it there under a memory and CPU cap and runs it as a service. This page is the shortest path through that, and each part has its own page with the full detail.\n\nYou need four things before you start:\n\n- **A Hack Club account.** You sign in with Hack Club, so Hack Club only confirms who you are.\n- **A Nest container and your Nest username.** This is the name you log in with, as in `ssh username@hackclub.app`.\n- **A GitHub repository.** You import a public one by its address, so a private one, and automatic deploys on push, need the Egg GitHub app installed on it.\n- **An app that listens on a port.** See the warning below. A static site is served for you, so it needs no code for this.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Sign in\n\n   Open the log in page and press **Continue with Hack Club**. Approve the sign-in at Hack Club and you land on Projects, where a new account sees “Connect your Nest container first” with a **Connect a server** button. The sign-in lasts 30 days, so the handshake with Hack Club has to finish within 10 minutes in one browser.\n2. Step 2: Connect your server\n\n   Open Connect, titled “Connect a Nest server”. Under **Add your server**, type your Nest username in **Nest username** and press **Create key**, so the status reads “Waiting for the key”. Copy the **Public key**, press **Open the Nest dashboard** and paste the key on the SSH Keys page there. Come back and press **Test connection**. The first time, check the fingerprint in the “Confirm host key” box and press **Confirm host key**, so the status turns to “Connected” and a “Reached your container” panel appears.\n3. Step 3: Import a repository\n\n   Press **New project**, which opens “Let’s build something new”. Press **Install the GitHub App**, share the repository, then pick it under **GitHub account** and press **Import**. To use a public repository without the app, paste its address under “Or use a public repository URL” and press **Continue**, so the page says “Looking at the repository…” while Egg reads it. Then press **Use these settings** under “We found” to fill in the runtime and commands it detected. That button only appears when a runtime was recognised.\n4. Step 4: Check the form and deploy\n\n   Check **Nest server**, **Project name**, **Application preset**, **Production branch**, **Root directory** and **Port your app listens on**. The port starts at `3000`. Fill in any **Environment variables** the example env file asked for, so an empty field is skipped. Watch the “What is already on this container” panel: a line with the badge “Blocks the deploy” keeps the button off until you fix it. Press **Deploy**, so the button reads “Starting deployment…” and you land on the deployment page.\n5. Step 5: Watch the first deployment\n\n   The **Build Logs** card streams the clone on the Egg machine, then the checks, upload, install, build and start on your container. When the state reads Ready, your app is running on your container, and the log ends with “Live on port N. Map your domain to this port in the Nest dashboard.” If you imported through the GitHub app, pushes to the production branch now deploy on their own, so a pasted public address does not. If the state reads Error, read the log from the bottom and see What can go wrong below. Your earlier release keeps running or is put back.\n\nYour app has to listen on the right port\n\nEgg starts your app with `PORT` set to the port you chose and `HOST` set to `0.0.0.0`, so listen on both. An app that binds only to `localhost` cannot be reached and fails the health check after 45 seconds. A bot that opens no port still needs a tiny web server, so the check has something to answer.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg judges a release by asking your app a question on its port: it requests the health check path, which is `/` unless you change it, and a status from 200 to 499 counts as up. A program that never opens a port cannot answer, so the check fails and the previous release is put back. Egg waits for an answer before it calls a deployment Ready, so Ready means “answering”, not just “started”.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Servers per account | 5 | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Nest username | Letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen; 1 to 32 characters; starts with a letter or digit | “That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Project name | 1 to 60 characters. Its address-safe form is lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, up to 40 | “The project name must use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (up to 40).” In the code: server/projects.ts server/deploy/layout.ts |\n| Port | 1024 to 65535, and not 20000 to 29999 | 20000 to 29999 is kept for previews. The form allows a lower port; the server refuses it with “port: Too small: expected number to be >=1024”. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Public repository address | https://github.com/owner/name | “Enter a GitHub repository URL such as https://github.com/owner/name” In the code: components/new-project-flow.tsx |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | The deployment fails with “The build timed out.” In the code: server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Build time | 15 minutes by default | The deployment fails with “The build timed out.” In the code: server/config.ts BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS |\n| Wait for the app to answer | 45 seconds | The deployment ends as Error and the previous release is put back, if there is one. In the code: server/deploy/pipeline.ts |\n| Container checks while you fill the form | 20 a minute | “Too many checks. Wait N seconds and try again.” In the code: app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“Hack Club sign-in is not set up on this installation yet.”`\n\n  This copy of Egg has no Hack Club app configured, so the button is off. Only the person running it can fix that.\n- `“You did not approve the sign-in. Try again when you are ready.”`\n\n  You pressed cancel at Hack Club. Press Continue with Hack Club again.\n- `“The sign-in took too long or came from another browser. Please try again.”`\n\n  Start again from the log in page, in one browser, and finish within 10 minutes.\n- `“That sign-in link was not valid. Please try again.” or “Hack Club could not sign you in. Please try again in a moment.”`\n\n  Try again. If it repeats, Hack Club may be having a problem.\n- `“Connect a server first”`\n\n  You have no connected server yet, so there is nowhere to deploy. Press **Connect a server**.\n- The Connect page shows “Could not connect”\n\n  Read the red message under the server name. Most often the public key was not pasted on the SSH Keys page, or the username is not exactly your Nest username. The Servers page lists each message.\n- `“Could not read this repository. If it is private, install the GitHub App so it can be read. You can still set everything by hand.”`\n\n  The repository is private or the name is wrong. Install the GitHub app on it, or set the fields yourself.\n- `“Port N is already used by another project on this server.”`\n\n  Each project on a server needs its own port. Pick another one.\n- `“Deploy is turned off because N things on this container are in the way.”`\n\n  The container already holds a service, folder or program that Egg did not create. It will not stop or change it. Change the project name or port, or deal with it yourself. Projects has the full explanation.\n- `“The project was created, but NAME was not saved: reason. Press Deploy to try again.”`\n\n  The project exists. Press Deploy again; it does not create a second one.\n- `“The new release did not become healthy on port N. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.”`\n\n  The app started but nothing answered. Read the last log lines, fix the port or the address, and deploy again.\n\n## Example: Put recipe-bot online\n\n### Given\n\nSam has `sam-r/recipe-bot`, a Node.js Discord bot with a `start` script. The bot only talks out to Discord, so Sam adds a small web server that answers on the port Egg gives it. Sam’s Nest username is `sam-r`.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Add a tiny web server\n\n   Sam adds this line to `index.js` and pushes it: `http.createServer((_, res) => res.end(\"ok\")).listen(process.env.PORT, \"0.0.0.0\")`.\n2. Step 2: Sign in and connect\n\n   Sam presses Continue with Hack Club, then creates a key for `sam-r`, pastes it in Nest and presses Test connection.\n3. Step 3: Import\n\n   Sam presses New project, installs the GitHub app on `recipe-bot`, presses Import and presses Use these settings. The summary said Node.js with npm.\n4. Step 4: Keep the defaults\n\n   Sam keeps the production branch `main` and the port `3000`, and presses Deploy.\n\n### Result\n\nThe deployment goes Queued, Building, Deploying, Ready. The Build Logs card ends with “Live on port 3000. Map your domain to this port in the Nest dashboard.” The project’s Service card shows Running, and the Logs page shows the line the bot prints when it logs in to Discord.\n\n**Related:** [Servers](/docs/servers), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","run-yourself":"Start\n\nPage 4 of 40\n\n# Run Egg yourself\n\nEgg is open source, so you can run your own copy with or without a Hack Club account. This is the short version.\n\nThe full guide is in the repository: [docs/selfhost/README.md](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md). It gives the exact steps for Windows, macOS and Linux, every setting, the GitHub app setup and a troubleshooting list. This page keeps to the choices that matter first.\n\n## Pick your setup\n\n| Setup | Who it is for | Sign-in | Start it with |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| On this computer | One person, using Egg privately. | A local password. No outside account. | `pnpm egg start` |\n| On your own server | You, or a few people you trust, at a domain with HTTPS. | Hack Club, or local mode for one owner behind your own access control. | `docker compose up -d --build` |\n| A shared instance | Several people, each with their own account. | Hack Club only. Without Hack Club there is one account. | The same, with the Hack Club values set |\n| A trial with a stand-in server | You want to see a deploy work with no Nest account. | Any of the above. | `pnpm fake-nest` |\n\nYou need Node.js 22.13 or newer, pnpm and Git on the computer that runs Egg. That computer only clones the repository, packs the source and uploads it. The install and the build run on the server your app is deployed to, so Egg needs no Docker for a local copy. The server uses Docker Compose and Caddy, which gets and renews HTTPS by itself.\n\nWindows, macOS and Linux all work, but only Linux and macOS are in the automated checks. `pnpm fake-nest` needs WSL on Windows.\n\n## Signing in without Hack Club\n\nLocal mode lets you run without Hack Club. Follow these steps:\n\n1. `pnpm run setup` writes `.env.local` with `LOCAL_MODE=1` and a private `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET`. `pnpm egg start` does this for you if the file is missing.\n2. On your first visit, the login page asks for that secret as the **recovery key**, and for a new password of at least 12 characters. Press **Set password and sign in**.\n3. Later you enter the password and press **Sign in on this computer**. If you forget it, use **Reset with the recovery key**.\n\nEgg stores only a salted hash of your password. Local mode makes one account, called Owner, and refuses to run when `APP_URL` is not localhost unless you set `LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE=1`, so use that only behind a VPN or an access proxy you control.\n\n- **Hack Club sign-in.** Set `HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID` and `HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET`, so anyone with a Hack Club account who can open your site gets their own account. Egg has no allow list.\n- **Email and password.** The card on Account turns it on only within 5 minutes of a Hack Club sign-in, so it cannot be turned on without Hack Club. The local owner has no email address and cannot use it.\n- **Confirming again.** Turning on Terminal or File changes needs a sign-in from the last 5 minutes. With local mode and no Hack Club, Egg counts you as signed in, so it does not ask. If you set Hack Club up as well, the local owner is sent to Hack Club, which cannot confirm it, so the switch stays off. Do not mix the two.\n\nOne account without Hack Club\n\nA production copy with no Hack Club has exactly one account, the local owner, so a second person cannot sign in. Set up Hack Club Auth, or run one copy of Egg for each person.\n\n## Where your apps run\n\nEgg reaches the server your apps run on by SSH, with a key made only for it. Egg was made for Hack Club Nest, so a server you own is not tested.\n\n- **Nest.** Enter your Nest username, paste the public key into the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and press Test connection.\n- **Your own server.** Set `NEST_HOST` and `NEST_PORT` in `.env.local` before you add the server, use the SSH user as the username, and add the public key to that user’s `authorized_keys`. Egg writes system service files and runs `systemctl` without sudo, so it expects root and a running systemd. Not tested on other hosts.\n- **Addresses.** The Domains page follows Nest’s rules, so on your own server put a reverse proxy in front of the app and keep its port closed to the internet.\n\nGitHub is optional, so a public repository deploys from its address with no setup. Private repositories and deploys on push need a GitHub app that you create yourself. GitHub must be able to reach `/api/webhooks/github` on your Egg to send pushes, so a copy on localhost needs a tunnel for that, or you deploy by hand.\n\nRotate values used with public static sites\n\nA public static deployment could once serve its `.env` file, so a value could be downloaded from `/.env`. If you ran one, rotate those values at their source and deploy again. Variables now live in the project’s `env/` folder on the server, outside every release, and the static server refuses any path that starts with a dot.\n\n## The full guide\n\nRead [the full guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md) for each system, every setting and every error message. [What does not work yet without Hack Club or Nest](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/design/self-host-gaps.md) is ranked, and [Customising](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/customising.md) covers the name, colours, ports and build memory.\n\n**Related:** [Run Egg locally](/docs/run-locally), [Running your own](/docs/selfhost).\n","run-locally":"Start\n\nPage 5 of 40\n\n# Run Egg locally\n\nSet up a working copy of Egg on your computer, start the development server, sign in, add sample data and run the checks.\n\nRun every command on this page in the `web` folder, unless it says otherwise. To use a copy without changing it, read [Run Egg yourself](/docs/run-yourself).\n\nWork through these steps in order:\n\n1. Check your Node.js version and turn on corepack.\n2. Clone the repository and install the dependencies.\n3. Create `web/.env.local` with `pnpm run setup`.\n4. Start the development server and sign in.\n5. Seed sample data, then run the checks and the production build.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nYou need Node.js 22.13 or newer and Git. The repository pins pnpm 11.20.0 in `package.json`, so that pnpm needs Node 22.13. This block checks your Node version, turns on corepack, which comes with Node and installs the pinned pnpm, then prints the pnpm version.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\nnode -v\ncorepack enable\npnpm -v\n```\n\nIf your computer blocks corepack, install pnpm from pnpm.io at the pinned version.\n\n## Install\n\nYou download the code and install its dependencies here.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ngit clone https://github.com/jeremy341/egg.git\ncd egg/web\npnpm install\n```\n\n## The env file\n\nThis block creates `web/.env.local` with a new `APP_SECRET` and `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET`, so it never overwrites a file that exists. Type `pnpm run setup`, not `pnpm setup`, which is a different pnpm command.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm run setup\n```\n\nThe file starts with the variables below. These are the ones the code reads for a local copy. The Hack Club and GitHub app values are optional and stay empty. See [Running your own](/docs/selfhost) for every setting.\n\n.env.local\n\n```\nAPP_SECRET=<32 or more random characters>\nLOCAL_MODE_SECRET=<32 or more random characters>\nAPP_URL=http://localhost:3000\nDATABASE_URL=file:./data/egg.db\nLOCAL_MODE=1\nDEV_LOGIN=0\nRUN_WORKER=1\n```\n\n- `APP_SECRET` seals the SSH keys and variables in your database, so back it up with `web/data/egg.db`.\n- `APP_URL` is the address you open in the browser, and `pnpm egg start` uses its port for the server.\n- `RUN_WORKER=1` runs the deployment queue inside the same process.\n\n## Start the dev server\n\nThis block starts the development server. It listens on `127.0.0.1` at port 3000, the Next.js default, and reloads when you edit code. Open `http://localhost:3000/login` in your browser, so you reach the dashboard and not the public home page at `/`.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm dev\n```\n\nThis block checks the computer first, then starts the server on the port in `APP_URL` and opens the browser. Ctrl+C stops either way of starting it.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm egg start --dev\n```\n\nThis block only checks the computer: Node.js, pnpm, `.env.local`, `APP_SECRET`, the port and the data folder. It starts nothing.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm run doctor\n```\n\n## Development sign-in\n\nDevelopment sign-in lets you in as a made-up user, so you need no Hack Club account. Add this line to `.env.local` and restart the server.\n\n.env.local\n\n```\nDEV_LOGIN=1\n```\n\n- You then see a **Development sign-in** form with a name (`dev` by default, 40 characters at most) and a **Sign in** button. Egg stores the user as `dev:` plus the lowercase name.\n- It works only in development, so in a production build the form is hidden and the route answers 404, whatever `DEV_LOGIN` says.\n- With `LOCAL_MODE=1` on, as `pnpm run setup` leaves it, the form also asks for the local password, so set one first with **Sign in on this computer** and the recovery key from `.env.local`.\n- **Origin rule.** Egg refuses a browser write whose `Origin` is not the origin of `APP_URL` or of the address the request came to, so it answers 403 “Cross-origin request blocked”. Open the site at the address in `APP_URL`, and change `APP_URL` if you use another port. After sign-in Egg redirects to `APP_URL` followed by `/projects`.\n- `pnpm run doctor` counts `DEV_LOGIN` as a way to sign in only with `--dev`.\n\n## Demo data\n\nThis block fills your local database with sample projects, deployments and charts for the `dev` user, so you can see a full dashboard. Sign in with the development form and the name `dev`. Every sample row id starts with `demo-`.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm seed:demo\n```\n\nThe sample server points at `127.0.0.1` port 1, so pages that read it live, such as the Service card and runtime logs, show a connection error. This block removes exactly the rows the script made.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm seed:demo --reset\n```\n\n## Tests and lint\n\nThis block runs every test once, with Vitest as the test runner.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm test\n```\n\nThis block runs the tests in one file, so replace the path with any test file you have.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm test src/server/config.test.ts\n```\n\nThis block runs the linter, then the TypeScript check, which writes no files.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm lint\npnpm typecheck\n```\n\n## The production build\n\nThis block makes the production build and starts it on `127.0.0.1`, at port 3000 unless `PORT` is set. Development sign-in is off in this build, so sign in with local mode or Hack Club.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm build\npnpm start\n```\n\n`pnpm egg start` does the same with the computer check first. It builds the first time by itself and uses the port in `APP_URL`.\n\n**Related:** [Run Egg yourself](/docs/run-yourself), [Running your own](/docs/selfhost).\n","servers":"Use it\n\nPage 6 of 40\n\n# Servers\n\nA server is one Nest account. You can connect up to five, and each holds its own projects and usage.\n\nConnecting a server gives Egg one way into your Nest container, and nothing else. You make a key and paste its public half into the Nest dashboard, so Egg can deploy, restart and read logs. Egg connects from the Egg machine over SSH. You need a Nest account and your Nest username, the name you log in with, for example `sam-r`.\n\n## How the connection works\n\nEgg makes a dedicated ed25519 key for each server and uses it for nothing else. The key’s comment reads `egg:` followed by your username. You paste the public half into the Nest dashboard, so Nest’s bastion routes the connection to your container as `root`, just like your own login. The page warns you: “This key gives Egg the same access as your own login, because SSH into Nest lands in a root shell inside your container. Only connect a container you are happy to deploy to.”\n\nEgg seals the private key with AES-256-GCM, tied to that server, before storing it in Egg’s database on the Egg machine, and never shows it. The first time you press Test connection, Egg reaches the host, reads its host key, hangs up and shows the SHA-256 fingerprint under “Confirm host key”. Nothing reaches the server until you press **Confirm host key**, so Egg records the fingerprint and stops any other host key before it sends anything. Until then, no deploy, file change or terminal connects. The address is `hackclub.app` on port 22, and a self-hosted copy can change both with `NEST_HOST` and `NEST_PORT`.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA key made for one server lets you end Egg’s access without touching your own login, so deleting the key in the Nest dashboard stops access at once. Egg pins the host key because it sends your environment variables and your build to whatever answers, so a different answer stops the connection instead of handing your data over.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Create the key\n\n   Open Connect. Under **Add your server** (the heading reads “Add another server” once you have one), type your Nest username in **Nest username** and press **Create key**, so the status reads “Waiting for the key” and the card shows the **Public key**.\n2. Step 2: Paste it into Nest\n\n   Copy the **Public key** field with its copy button. Press **Open the Nest dashboard** to open `dashboard.hackclub.app` in a new tab, then paste the key on the SSH Keys page.\n3. Step 3: Test the connection\n\n   Back on Connect, press **Test connection**, so the button reads “Connecting…”. The first time, the page stops at a “Confirm host key” box that says “This is the first connection to NAME.hackclub.app” and shows a fingerprint. Check it against the one Nest shows for your server, then press **Confirm host key** (“Confirming…”), so Egg reads your container. On success the status reads “Connected” and a “Reached your container” panel lists System, Logged in as, Node.js, Python, systemd and Key fingerprint. That last row is the fingerprint of the key Egg made, not of Nest’s host key. A missing tool shows as “not installed”.\n4. Step 4: Read the warnings\n\n   Under the panel, red and amber boxes list anything missing on your container. The table below says what each one means.\n\n## Testing, rotating and removing\n\n- **Test connection** opens an SSH session, asks you to confirm the host key the first time, and reads your container’s operating system, user, Node.js and Python versions, tools, systemd, CPU, memory and disk. It only reads, so it changes nothing on your container and you can press it at any time. A failure saves the message and the status becomes “Could not connect”; the next success clears it.\n- **New key** replaces the key pair. A dialog titled “Generate a new key?” says “The old key stops working once you remove it in the Nest dashboard, and you must add the new one.” Confirm with **Generate key**. Egg uses the new private key from that moment, so deploys fail with “Nest rejected the key” until you paste the new public key and test again. The server goes back to “Waiting for the key” and the saved reading is cleared. The pinned host key is kept. Remove the old public key in the Nest dashboard to end its access. The change is recorded in Activity.\n- **Disconnect** opens “Disconnect NAME?”, with the text “This closes its terminals and removes our key for it. Your running apps are not touched. Remove the key from the Nest dashboard to end our access.” If the server has projects, the dialog also shows a checkbox, “Also delete the project on this server” or “Also delete the N projects on this server”, which starts unticked. Unticked, the projects are kept: “Their apps keep running, but Egg stops managing them until you connect this server again.” Ticked, they are deleted with their deployments, logs, environment variables, hook and status page, and the apps still keep running on the container. Either way Egg’s key is removed, any terminal open on that server closes, and the change is recorded in Activity.\n\nComing back after a disconnect\n\nA disconnected server drops out of the list and no longer counts toward the limit of five. Its kept projects stay in Egg but cannot be deployed or read: any attempt answers “NAME is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.” To bring them back, add the same Nest username again on Connect. You get a new key and a new host key to confirm, and the kept projects attach to it. Egg never touches the apps on your container while the server is disconnected.\n\n| Message | Shown as | What it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| systemd is not available in this container, so Egg cannot keep your apps running or restart them. | Error | Apps run as systemd services. Without it nothing can be started or restarted. |\n| SSH did not log in as root. Nest normally does; without root, installing the service files may fail. | Warning | Writing the service file needs root. |\n| tar is not installed. | Error | Releases are unpacked on the container with tar. |\n| sha256sum is missing, so uploads cannot be verified. | Warning | The upload still goes through, without the checksum comparison. |\n| curl is missing. Health checks fall back to a plain TCP connection check. | Warning | The health check only tests that the port accepts a connection, not what it answers. |\n| Neither Node.js nor Python 3 is installed. Only projects that ship their own binary can run. | Warning | Node.js projects install their production dependencies on the container, Python projects create a virtual environment there, and static sites need one of the two to be served. |\n| Only N MB of disk is free. Releases are kept for rollback, so deploys may fail. | Warning | Shown when under 1024 MB is free. |\n\nThese are warnings about your container, so Egg only shows them and does not stop a deploy because of one. Fix the ones that match what you want to run. After a test, the Projects page also shows a usage card with CPU load, memory and disk from that reading, or “No reading yet. Press Test connection on the Connect page.” when there is none. It shows the first connected server.\n\nTaking access back\n\nDelete the key in your Nest dashboard, so access ends at once. Your apps keep running because they are ordinary services in your container.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Servers per account | 5, not counting disconnected ones | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER |\n| Nest username | Letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen; 1 to 32 characters; starts with a letter or digit | “That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Servers per Nest username | One per account | “NAME is already connected.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Connect timeout | 20 seconds | “Timed out connecting to Nest. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.” In the code: server/ssh/connection.ts |\n| Connection read | 30 seconds | The test fails and the Connect page shows the error. In the code: server/ssh/probe.ts |\n| Host key | Pinned when you confirm it on the first Test connection | Until then nothing connects: a deploy answers “Confirm host key: open the Connect page, check the fingerprint of this server and confirm it first.” Any other key stops the connection. Only Disconnect, then adding the server again, clears the pin. In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Free disk before a warning | 1024 MB | An amber warning after a test. In the code: server/ssh/probe.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“Nest rejected the key. Check that you pasted the public key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and that the username is exactly your Nest username.”`\n\n  Paste the key again, copying the whole Public key field, and check the username. After New key, this is expected until the new key is pasted.\n- `“Timed out connecting to Nest. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.”`\n\n  Wait, then press Test connection again.\n- `“Could not reach the Nest SSH host (reason).”`\n\n  The connection failed before login. Try again in a minute, and check that Nest itself is up.\n- `“Confirm host key: open the Connect page, check the fingerprint of this server and confirm it first.”`\n\n  The server has no confirmed host key yet, so a deploy, file change or terminal will not connect to it. Open Connect, press **Test connection** and then **Confirm host key**.\n- `“The host key of HOST changed (was OLD, now NEW). Nothing was sent. If you expected this, reconnect the server.”`\n\n  Egg refuses on purpose. If you know the host key changed, press **Disconnect** and add the server again. Leave the project checkbox unticked and the projects reattach, because Disconnect clears the pinned host key.\n- `“SSH error: message”`\n\n  Anything else the SSH library reported. The message is the clue; try the test again once.\n- `“That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.”`\n\n  Use the name you log in to Nest with, without `@hackclub.app`.\n- `“You can connect up to 5 servers.” or “NAME is already connected.”`\n\n  Disconnect one you no longer use, or use the server you already added.\n- `“NAME is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.”`\n\n  The server was disconnected and its projects kept. Add the same Nest username again on Connect.\n\n### Example: Connect sam-r and replace the key\n\n#### Given\n\nSam’s Nest username is `sam-r`. Sam has not connected a server yet. Two months later Sam shares a laptop and wants a fresh key.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Create the key\n\n   Sam opens Connect, types `sam-r` and presses Create key.\n2. Step 2: Paste it in Nest\n\n   Sam pastes the public key on the Nest dashboard’s SSH Keys page.\n3. Step 3: Test and confirm\n\n   Sam presses Test connection, checks the fingerprint in the “Confirm host key” box and presses Confirm host key.\n4. Step 4: Replace the key later\n\n   Sam presses New key, then Generate key, pastes the new public key in Nest, removes the old one there and presses Test connection again.\n\n#### Result\n\nThe first test shows “Reached your container” with the system, Node.js and systemd rows, and the status reads “Connected”. After the replacement the status goes to “Waiting for the key” and back to “Connected”. The host key was not asked for again, because it was confirmed the first time. Sam’s projects and apps were not touched.\n\n**Related:** [Quick start](/docs/quickstart), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Security](/docs/security), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","keys":"Use it\n\nPage 7 of 40\n\n# Keys\n\nKeys are the SSH keys Egg makes for your projects and for you. Each one is a pair: the public half can be shared, and Egg keeps the private half sealed in its database.\n\nThe Keys page lists the keys you make for a project and for yourself. The key Egg makes for each server is listed there too, with a copy button for its public half and a **New key** action. That server key is described on the Servers page.\n\n## The three kinds\n\n- **GitHub deploy key.** A read-only key that Egg installs on the GitHub repository linked to one project, through the Egg GitHub App. You paste nothing. Once GitHub confirms it, the row shows an “On GitHub” badge.\n- **Git key.** A key for a git host you run yourself. Egg makes the pair, you paste the public half into your host, and the private half is shown once.\n- **Personal key.** A key that belongs to your account rather than a project, for a script or a tool you use outside Egg. Its private half is shown once, too.\n\n## GitHub deploy keys\n\nA deploy key can read the repository and nothing else: it is read-only, so it cannot push. Egg adds it when the project is linked to a repository. Until it is, Egg refuses to add one and says a repository is needed.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA read-only key cannot change your code, even if it leaks. Revoking a deploy key removes it from GitHub before Egg marks it revoked, so the key cannot keep opening the repository after you have revoked it.\n\n## Git keys\n\nUse a git key for a git host that is not on GitHub, such as a server you run yourself. Add it to a project from the Keys page, and name it if you want. Copy its public half into your host’s SSH key settings. The private half is shown once, in the same dialog, so copy it before you press **I have copied it**. Egg keeps it sealed, and the page never shows it again.\n\n## Personal keys\n\nPress **Generate key** in the Personal keys card and give the key a name. Egg shows the private and the public half once. Copy both, then press **I have copied it**. A personal key is not tied to a project, and revoking it in Egg does not change anything outside Egg.\n\n## Adding, replacing and revoking\n\n- **Add key** is on each project’s group. It opens “Add a key to NAME”. Pick **GitHub deploy key** or **Git key**, name a git key if you want, and press **Add key**. A deploy key then reads “Added to GitHub automatically.”\n- **Replace key** is on a GitHub deploy key. The dialog says Egg makes a new read-only key, installs it on GitHub and removes the old one there. If GitHub would not remove the old key, the page says so, and you must delete the old deploy key in the repository’s settings on GitHub yourself.\n- **Revoke** asks first. A deploy key is removed from GitHub before it is revoked here, and if GitHub cannot be reached nothing changes and the key stays active. A git key or a personal key is only marked as revoked, so remove its public half from your host or from anything else that trusts it.\n- Revoked keys move under **Revoked** with the date they were revoked, and they cannot be used again.\n\n## Limits and problems\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Key name | 1 to 60 characters, on one line | “Give the key a name.” or “Keep the name to one line.” In the code: server/ssh-keys.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- The private key is gone.\n\n  It was shown once. Revoke that key and add a new one; Egg cannot show the private half again.\n- Revoking a deploy key is refused, or the key stays active.\n\n  Egg could not remove the deploy key from GitHub, so it kept the key. Check that the Egg GitHub App is still installed on the repository, then try again.\n- Replacing a deploy key says the old key is still on GitHub.\n\n  The new key is in place, but GitHub did not let Egg remove the old one. Delete the old deploy key in the repository’s settings on GitHub.\n- Adding a deploy key says a repository is needed.\n\n  The project is not linked to a GitHub repository yet. Link one to the project, then add the key.\n\n**Related:** [Servers](/docs/servers), [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n","projects":"Use it\n\nPage 8 of 40\n\n# Projects and runtimes\n\nA project ties one GitHub repository to one app on one server.\n\nA project holds everything Egg needs to build and run one app: its repository and branch, Nest server, runtime, commands, port, and the variables, hook and alerts that go with it. You need a connected server. A private repository, and automatic deploys on push, need the Egg GitHub app installed on it.\n\n## Settings\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Pick a repository\n\n   Press **New project**. Pick the account under **GitHub account**, find your repository with **Search repositories…** and press **Import**. Or paste a public address under “Or use a public repository URL” and press **Continue**, so it has to look like `https://github.com/owner/name`. A pasted address starts on your Default branch from Settings, which is `main` until you change it. With no server connected, the page shows “Connect a server first”.\n2. Step 2: Read what Egg found\n\n   The “We found” summary lists the package manager, runtime, install, build and start commands and output folder Egg read from your repository, with notes under them. Nothing is applied until you press **Use these settings**, so it fills **Application preset** and the four command and folder fields but leaves the name, branch, root directory and port alone. If the repository holds several apps, “Apps in this repository” shows a button for each folder, and pressing one sets the root directory.\n3. Step 3: Fill in the fields\n\n   Choose **Nest server**, **Project name**, **Application preset** (Node.js, Python, Static site or Custom command), **Production branch**, **Root directory** and **Port your app listens on**. Leave **Install command (optional)**, **Start command (optional)**, **Build command (optional)** and, for static sites, **Output directory** empty to use the defaults. The project name is also your container’s folder and service name (`egg-NAME.service`), so the form only accepts lowercase letters, digits and hyphens and says “Use lowercase letters, numbers and hyphens.” otherwise.\n4. Step 4: Fill in variables and deploy\n\n   If the repository has an example env file, an **Environment variables** group shows one empty field per name. A name with a value becomes a variable for All environments; an empty one is skipped. Press **Deploy**. The project is created, the values are saved and the first deployment is queued.\n5. Step 5: Change settings later\n\n   Open the project, Settings, **Build and Run**, change a field and press **Save**, so the page says “Saved. Changes apply to the next deployment.” You cannot change the server or the repository, so to move an app you make a new project.\n\n| Setting | What it does | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Production branch | A push here deploys to production. Any other branch builds a preview. | The repository’s default branch; a pasted address starts at your Default branch from Settings (main unless you changed it) |\n| Root directory | The folder inside the repository to build, for monorepos. For a subfolder, a help line shows what GitHub has there: package.json, Lockfile, requirements.txt and Start script. | `.` |\n| Runtime | How to install, build and run it. See Runtimes. | Node.js |\n| Install command, Build command, Start command | Override a command. Each is one line of at most 500 characters. | Detected from the runtime |\n| Output directory | Static sites only: the folder to serve. | The root |\n| Port | The port the app listens on. Unique on a server. | `3000` |\n| Health check path | The path requested to decide the app is up. Starts with a slash. It is not on the New project form, so set it in Settings. | `/` |\n| Deploy automatically on push | Build on every push to a branch of the repository. | On |\n\nThe “We found” step reads your repository from GitHub before Egg creates anything. Here is what it looks at.\n\n- **Language.** A `package.json` means Node.js, so otherwise `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml` means Python. Egg names a `go.mod`, `Gemfile` or `Cargo.toml` as not supported yet, with a hint to try the Custom command preset. It names a `Dockerfile` so you can find it, but your project still starts in native process mode, so a Dockerfile counts only when you choose Dockerfile mode and your server passed its Docker check.\n- **Package manager.** The `packageManager` field in `package.json`, else a lockfile (`pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `bun.lock` or `bun.lockb`), else npm. More than one lockfile gives a warning to delete the ones you do not use. Bun is detected but not built yet. The build itself picks the package manager from the lockfile only, so keep the lockfile and the field in agreement.\n- **Static site.** A Vite or Astro project with no `start` script is suggested as a Static site with output folder `dist`. A Node.js project with no `start` script gets the note “package.json has no \"start\" script. Add one, or set a start command yourself.”\n- **Monorepo.** Workspaces, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, or `package.json` files under `packages/*` and `apps/*` give the note “This looks like a monorepo. Set the root directory to the app you want to deploy.” Up to 20 folders are offered as buttons.\n- **Variable names.** Only `package.json` and the example env files `.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template` and `.env.local.example` are read, and only for their names. Values are discarded. At most 100 names are listed, and invalid or reserved names are dropped. A committed `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production` or `.env.production.local` is never read, and gives the warning “A .env file is committed to this repository. Remove it from git and rotate the values.”\n- **Size.** A file over 200,000 bytes is skipped, paths deeper than 3 folders under the root are ignored, and a huge repository adds “This repository is very large, so some files may not have been looked at.” GitHub gets 10 seconds for the file list and 8 seconds for each file.\n\nThese are the other controls on the form and in Settings.\n\n- **Copy build settings from another project** fills the form from a project on the same server: runtime, install, build and start commands, output directory, root directory and health path. Choose a project and press **Copy**. The page says “Copied from NAME. Review the fields below, then press Save. Nothing is saved yet.” Variables, hooks, notifications, the port and the repository are not copied.\n- **Start with automatic deploys off** is for a repository you already run on this container by hand. The project is created with automatic deploys off, and the form says “The first deploy is manual, and automatic deploys start only after you confirm that first deploy works.” Nothing turns them on for you: tick **Deploy automatically on push** in Settings when the first manual deploy works. This option does not import, take over or stop the copy that is already running, and Egg does not import a running service yet. The container check still runs, and if your old process holds the port you stop it yourself first.\n- **The container check.** Once the name, server and port are valid, the form waits 600 milliseconds and then asks your container, read only, whether the project would collide with something there. The result is the “What is already on this container” panel. A line with the badge “Blocks the deploy” turns the Deploy button off. “Made by Egg” and “Good to know” lines are information only. The same check runs again at the start of every deploy, before anything is written.\n- **Show on Egg Homepage** is off for every project. It is separate from the public status page. Turning it on lists the project’s latest deployments on the public Egg homepage: the first line of the commit message, the short commit, the branch, the state, Production or Preview, the time and the repository name, each linking to the commit on GitHub. Build logs, your server, ports and environment variables are never shown. Only the owner can turn it on, and only for a repository imported through the GitHub App that GitHub says is public: Egg asks GitHub when you press **Show on homepage** and again each time the list is built, at most once a minute. A private or unconfirmed repository is refused with a message, and one that turns private leaves the homepage within a minute. Press **Stop showing** to take the project off.\n- **Delete Project** asks you to type the project name. With “Also stop the service and delete releases on NAME.hackclub.app” ticked, which is the default, the app goes offline. The service, the production and preview releases and the `current` link are removed from the container, but only if Egg created them. The `shared/` folder, `~/.egg/NAME/shared`, is kept, so data your app stored there is still on the container. Unticked, only the records go and the app keeps running. A project with a deployment that is Queued, Building or Deploying cannot be deleted. With the box ticked, Egg checks this first and answers 409 before it touches anything on the container, so a refused delete changes nothing. While the removal runs, new deployments for the project are refused too, whether they come from a push, a hook, the API or the Deploy button. If the removal fails, the block is lifted and the records are kept so you can try again.\n\n| It finds | Blocks the deploy? | What to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A service egg-NAME.service that Egg did not create, or one that systemd already has | Yes | Choose another project name. Egg will not replace it. |\n| The folder ~/.egg/NAME is a link, a file, a git checkout that is not Egg’s, or holds other files | Yes | Choose another project name, or clear the folder yourself. |\n| The port is already used by another program | Yes | Choose another port, or stop that program yourself first. Egg will not stop it. |\n| A program or service already runs from the project folder | Yes, while it runs | Stop it first, or choose another project name. |\n| The same repository already runs by hand from another checkout | Yes, but only for the first production deploy | Stop it yourself first. Egg does not take over a copy that is already running. |\n| Leftovers from an earlier Egg project with this name | No | “It will be reused.” |\n| The listening ports could not be read, a stopped service for the same repository, or a checkout that just sits there | No | Shown as information. A checkout is never touched. |\n\n## Runtimes\n\n| Runtime | Install and build | Default start |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Node.js | Picks pnpm, then yarn, then npm from your lockfile (npm install if there is none) and runs your build script if you have one. The install step uses a frozen lockfile. The build runs the install, the build and then a production-only install on your container, in the new release folder, under the cap, which uses its CPU, memory and disk on every deploy. node_modules is not uploaded. | `npm start` |\n| Python | No build step. On every deploy your container creates a virtual environment in .venv and installs requirements.txt into it, if the file exists, which uses its CPU, memory and disk. This runs under the cap, after your install and build commands, if you set them. | `python3 app.py` |\n| Static site | Builds if there is a build script, then ships only the Output directory. It is served by a short built-in server run with Python 3, or with Node.js if Python is missing. That server answers only GET and HEAD, shows no folder listings, and refuses any path that has a part starting with a dot (such as .env or .git) and any link that points outside the folder. | `Egg’s static server on PORT` |\n| Custom command | No detection and no install on the container. You give the install, build and start commands. | Your command |\n\nThe build uses the Node.js and Python on your container. Your Node.js must match the `engines` range in `package.json`, or be 22.13 or newer when there is no range. For Node.js the install command is `npm ci`, `corepack enable && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` or `corepack enable && yarn install --frozen-lockfile`, and the build command is `npm run build`, `pnpm build` or `yarn build`. The service runs your start command through a login shell, so tools installed with nvm are on the path. Egg names Go, Ruby and Rust as not supported by detection, so use Custom command if you can run a prebuilt binary. Bun is detected but not built yet.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe build runs next to your app, and the Egg machine only clones and packs the source, so the GitHub token never leaves it. The cap exists because a Nest container is small, so the kernel stops a build that goes over it before it can stop the app, and that is why build memory is a setting. A failed build never touches the running release, and container checks look before they touch anything, because the container is yours and may hold things Egg did not make.\n\n## What every app can rely on\n\n- It runs from its release folder with `PORT`, `HOST=0.0.0.0` and `NODE_ENV=production` set. Production runs from the `current` link, and a preview from its own release folder.\n- systemd restarts it three seconds after it exits (`Restart=always`, `RestartSec=3`), so a crash is not the end of the app, and it starts again when the container boots.\n- It sees your environment variables from a file only the service reads. The names `PORT`, `HOST`, `PATH`, `HOME` and `EGG` are reserved, in any letter case.\n- Files in `shared/` are never touched by a deploy, so data the app keeps there survives new releases. Write data there, not into the release folder, which is replaced on every deploy.\n\n## Dockerfile and Compose modes\n\nNative is the default, so your project deploys as a container only once your server passes the Docker check. The check runs when settings open and when you test the connection, and it asks the Docker on your container, not Egg's own, about cgroup limits, free disk at its storage root and the port proxy. When a part fails, the Dockerfile and Compose options stay greyed out with the reason, and nothing changes on your server.\n\n- **Dockerfile mode.** Egg uploads your source, then builds the image on your container with `docker build`, under the same memory, CPU and disk caps as a native build, so every release gets its own image tag. A systemd service starts and watches one named container, so restarts, journal logs, the Restarts count and rollbacks work as they do for a native process.\n- **Compose mode.** The Compose file is read by Docker itself, and only the resolved form Egg checked is ever run. Services must use prebuilt images, host ports must bind to `127.0.0.1` only, and the one service Egg monitors must not publish ports of its own; Egg maps the project's listen port onto it. Every other service, such as a database, starts with `docker compose up -d` and keeps its named volumes across releases. Rollbacks restore the previous release's code and configuration, never the data the stack wrote.\n- **What stays the same.** Environment variables reach the container at run time through the same encrypted path, nothing is baked into the image. A bot in a container mode can still open one optional listen port. Scheduled jobs run inside the running container with `docker exec`. Previews exist for Dockerfile projects; a Compose stack has no branch preview yet. A preview on a Compose project is refused when it is queued, with a message saying to deploy the branch to production instead, so no build time is wasted.\n- **What is refused.** Compose files that build images, set a container name, add depends_on, links, devices or privileged options, mount bind paths or outside volumes, join external networks, or publish ports on other interfaces, are rejected before anything starts, with the first reasons named in the logs. Only a short allowlist of service fields is accepted. A Compose project also cannot use the GitHub Actions build: the image is built on your server.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project name | 1 to 60 characters | “name: Too big: expected string to have <=60 characters” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Address-safe name (slug) | 1 to 40 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens; starts and ends with a letter or digit | “The project name must use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (up to 40).” In the code: server/deploy/layout.ts |\n| Commands | 500 characters, one line | “startCommand: Commands must be a single line” or “startCommand: Too big: expected string to have <=500 characters” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Root directory | Relative, no “..”, 200 characters | “rootDir: Root directory must be a relative path inside the repository”, or at deploy time “The root directory “DIR” does not exist in this repository.” In the code: server/projects.ts server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Branch | 200 characters; letters, digits and . _ - /; no leading hyphen; no “..” | “productionBranch: Branch names may use letters, digits, . _ - and /” or “productionBranch: Invalid branch name” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Port | 1024 to 65535, and not 20000 to 29999; unique on a server | “port: Ports 20000 to 29999 are reserved for previews”, “port: Too small: expected number to be >=1024”, “Port N is already used by another project on this server.” (in Settings: “Port N is already used on this server.”) In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Health check path | Starts with a slash, 200 characters | “healthPath: Must start with /” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Repository address | https, github.com only, no credentials | “Only plain https repository URLs are supported.” or “Repositories on HOST are not supported yet. Supported: github.com.” In the code: server/deploy/build.ts assertCloneUrl |\n| Detection reads | Files up to 200,000 bytes, 3 folders deep, 100 variable names, 20 app folders | The rest is skipped. In the code: server/repo-read.ts server/repo-detect.ts |\n| Container checks while you fill the form | 20 a minute | “Too many checks. Wait N seconds and try again.” In the code: app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts |\n| Environment variables per project | 100 | See Environment variables. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“You already have a project called “name”.”`\n\n  Project names are unique for you, across servers. Pick another name.\n- `“Deploy is turned off because N things on this container are in the way.”`\n\n  The conflict check found a service, folder or port that Egg did not create. It will not stop or change them. The panel above lists each one. Change the project name, server or port, or fix it on your container. The link “Open container status” opens the Container page in a new tab.\n- `“Could not check this container just now: reason. The deploy checks again before it changes anything.”`\n\n  The form could not ask your container. You can still deploy; the check runs again at the start.\n- `“Nothing was changed on your container.” followed by details`\n\n  The same check, run at deploy time, stopped the deployment before it wrote anything. The first three reasons are quoted.\n- `“Set a start command for this project (for example ./my-binary).”`\n\n  A Custom command project has no default start. Fill in the Start command.\n- `“Static sites need Python 3 or Node.js on your Nest container to be served.”`\n\n  Install one of them on the container, or use another runtime.\n- `“Could not read this repository. If it is private, install the GitHub App so it can be read. You can still set everything by hand.”`\n\n  Install the app on the repository. “Could not find the BRANCH branch of REPO.” means the branch name is wrong.\n- `“GitHub did not answer (STATUS). Try again later.” or “Could not reach GitHub to look. Try again in a moment.”`\n\n  GitHub is not answering. The form still works with fields you fill by hand.\n- No folder called “DIR” on the BRANCH branch.\n\n  The root directory does not exist on that branch. Other lines say “That path is a file, not a folder.” or “That root directory is not a valid relative path.”\n- `“The project was created, but NAME was not saved: reason. Press Deploy to try again.”`\n\n  The project exists. Press Deploy again; it does not create a second project.\n- `“A deployment is still running for this project. Cancel it or wait for it to finish, then delete the project.”`\n\n  Cancel the deployment on the Deployments tab, then delete the project. Nothing on the container was touched.\n- `“A deployment is still stopping for this project. Wait for it to finish, then delete the project.”`\n\n  A worker still holds a job for this project. Wait a minute and try again.\n- `409 “Cleanup is in progress for this project. Wait for it to finish or retry it before making changes.”`\n\n  The project is being removed from the container, and new deployments are blocked until that ends. Wait for it, then check whether the project still exists.\n- `“Could not remove the project from your container.”`\n\n  The container did not finish the removal. Test the connection and try again. The records are kept so you can retry.\n- `“Pick a different project to copy from.” or “You can only copy from a project on the same server.”`\n\n  Choose another project from the same server in the Copy list. “There are no other projects on this server to copy from.” means there is none.\n\nPasted URLs do not deploy on push\n\nA project made from a pasted public repository URL stores no repository name, so a push never builds it. Deploy, a deploy hook or the API builds it instead, so import through the GitHub app when you want pushes to deploy.\n\n### Example: Set up recipe-bot and change its health path\n\n#### Given\n\nSam imports `sam-r/recipe-bot`. The “We found” summary says Node.js with `npm`, with the build command left empty because there is no build script, and Sam’s `package.json` has a `start` script. The repository has a `.env.example` naming `DISCORD_TOKEN`.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Import and accept\n\n   Sam presses Use these settings, names the project `recipe-bot`, keeps the production branch `main` and the port `3000`.\n2. Step 2: Fill the variable\n\n   Sam pastes the Discord token into the `DISCORD_TOKEN` field and presses Deploy.\n3. Step 3: Change the health path\n\n   Later Sam opens Settings, Build and Run, sets Health check path to `/health` and presses Save.\n\n#### Result\n\nThe project and the first deployment are created, and `DISCORD_TOKEN` exists for All environments. After the Save, the page reads “Saved. Changes apply to the next deployment.” The next deploy checks `/health` instead of `/`.\n\n**Related:** [Environment variables](/docs/env), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Limits](/docs/limits).\n","build-github-actions":"Use it\n\nPage 9 of 40\n\n# Build with GitHub Actions\n\nGitHub builds the app and sends the finished release to Egg, so your Nest container only runs it. This is the recommended choice for a project made from a GitHub repository.\n\n## What it is and why it is recommended\n\nWith this choice a workflow in your repository installs, builds and packs the app on GitHub's machines. It sends the finished release to Egg. Egg unpacks it on your container, starts it and checks that it answers. Your container never runs the build, so a framework build that needs a lot of memory no longer competes with your app for it.\n\nThis is the default for a new project. Pick **Build on Nest** when you want no dependence on GitHub; see [Build on Nest](/docs/build-on-nest). A project made from a pasted public address has no link to the GitHub app. The new project flow does not offer GitHub Actions for it.\n\nBuilds use your GitHub Actions minutes.\n\n## Setting it up\n\nNew projects take you straight to these steps. For an existing project, open Settings, then **Where Builds Run**. Choose **GitHub Actions** and press Save. Each step opens one at a time, and finished steps fold into one line. Egg stores no progress. It knows whether the credentials exist and whether the last test passed. It asks you about the two steps that only GitHub can confirm.\n\n1. **Make the build credentials.** Press **Make credentials**. Egg shows four values once and keeps only a fingerprint. Making new ones stops the old ones at once.\n2. **Save the secrets on GitHub.** Save each value under the name and in the place the next section lists. Press **I saved all four secrets**. Egg cannot read GitHub secrets, so this step is your confirmation.\n3. **Add the workflow file.** Press **Add to repository**. Egg opens a pull request that adds the file. Merge it on GitHub. Without the GitHub app permissions below, copy or download `egg.yml` and commit it to the production branch yourself.\n4. **Test the connection.** Egg checks its own records and, read only, your repository. Every failed check says what to do. The last section here goes through them.\n5. **Run the first build.** Push a commit, or open the workflow on GitHub and press **Run workflow**. The deployment appears on the project's Deployments page.\n\n## The four secrets\n\nThe workflow reads four secrets. The two production values live in a GitHub Environment called `egg-production`. You limit that environment to the production branch. The two preview values are repository secrets.\n\n| Name | Saved in | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `EGG_UPLOAD_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` | The egg-production environment | Sends a finished production build to Egg. |\n| `EGG_ENV_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` | The egg-production environment | Reads the production build variables. |\n| `EGG_UPLOAD_PREVIEW_TOKEN` | Repository secrets | Sends a finished preview build to Egg. |\n| `EGG_ENV_PREVIEW_TOKEN` | Repository secrets | Reads the preview build variables. |\n\n- To make the environment, open the repository's Settings, then Environments, then New environment. Under Deployment branches and tags, choose Selected branches and tags and add the production branch. You can turn on Required reviewers so a person approves each production build.\n- Preview secrets go under Settings, Secrets and variables, Actions, as repository secrets.\n- **Never save the two production values as repository secrets.** A repository secret can be read by a workflow that someone edits on any branch.\n\n## What the pull request does\n\n**Add to repository** asks the GitHub app to create a branch, commit `.github/workflows/egg.yml` to it and open a pull request into the production branch. If a pull request from an earlier press is still open, Egg updates it. If the production branch already has an identical file, Egg changes nothing and says so. Nothing is merged for you. The workflow only starts working when you merge.\n\nThis needs three permissions on the GitHub app\n\nAdding the workflow by pull request needs Egg's GitHub app to have **Contents**, **Pull requests** and **Workflows** read and write access. Only the person who runs this Egg can grant them, in the app's settings on GitHub. Without them, Egg says so. You copy or download the file instead, and everything else works the same.\n\n## Forks, pull requests and production secrets\n\n- The workflow runs on pushes and on **Run workflow**, not on pull requests. A pull request from a fork therefore never runs it and never receives a secret.\n- Only the job for the production branch can use the `egg-production` environment. A workflow edited on another branch can ask for it. GitHub then refuses the secrets.\n- Anyone who can push a workflow can run code with the preview credentials and with whatever else the repository allows. Treat the people who can edit workflows as trusted operators of this project. Each project has a single owner.\n- The credentials can send a release and read build variables, and nothing else. A production credential cannot deliver a preview build. A preview credential cannot deliver a production one.\n\n## Limits\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at the limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Release size | 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes) | The workflow stops with a message and nothing is sent. |\n| Wait for the release | 60 minutes | Egg stops waiting: “GitHub did not send the build in time. Check the workflow run, then deploy again.” |\n| Build credential lifetime | 90 days | Test connection reports them as expired. Make new ones and replace all four secrets. |\n\nThese numbers are the ones the setup steps show. They come from the code that enforces them.\n\n## When Test connection fails\n\nTest connection lists six checks. A check that could not run because an earlier one failed shows “Not checked”. It never shows “Passed”. Fix the first failure, then test again.\n\n| Check | What failed | What to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Build location | The project is set to build on your container. | Choose GitHub Actions under Where Builds Run and press Save. |\n| Build credentials | Egg has no complete set, or they expired. | Make new credentials, then replace all four secrets on GitHub. |\n| GitHub app | The project is not linked to the app, or the app was uninstalled. | Install the app from Account, then add the project again. |\n| Repository access | The app cannot see the repository. | On GitHub, open the app's settings and add the repository to its access. |\n| Workflow file | There is no file at .github/workflows/egg.yml on the production branch. | Merge the pull request, or commit the copied file to the production branch. |\n| Workflow settings | The file does not match this project's address, production branch, secrets or environment. | Copy the workflow from the setup step over the file. Do this again after changing the runtime, commands or production branch. |\n\nTest connection cannot see your GitHub secrets or environment settings. It does not know whether the last workflow run passed. If the first build fails, open the workflow run on GitHub. A run that cannot send the release usually means a secret is missing. It may be saved in the wrong place. It may be out of date.\n\n**Related resources:** [Build on Nest](/docs/build-on-nest), [GitHub and previews](/docs/github), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","build-on-nest":"Use it\n\nPage 10 of 40\n\n# Build on Nest\n\nEgg builds the app on your Nest container, under a memory and CPU cap, and starts it when the build finishes.\n\n## What it is\n\nNothing depends on GitHub for the build itself. Egg sends the source to your container over SSH. It runs the install and build commands there, then starts the result. It suits small apps and projects that are not on GitHub through the app.\n\n## The memory and CPU cap\n\n- A build runs under a memory cap and a CPU limit, so it cannot take the container from your running app. Both are set on the Container page, under Build memory and Build CPU.\n- The memory cap is never more than your container's memory minus 512 MB. The 512 MB stays free for your app. A container with under 768 MB cannot build.\n- A build that runs longer than 15 minutes is stopped. Your running app is not.\n\n## When to prefer it\n\n- Prefer it for a small app whose build fits comfortably in the cap.\n- Prefer it when you want builds that do not use GitHub Actions minutes.\n- Prefer GitHub Actions for a framework build that runs out of memory here. See [Build with GitHub Actions](/docs/build-github-actions).\n\nSwitching is a saved choice under Where Builds Run in the project's Settings. Egg never changes it for you, so the next deployment uses the new place. The numbers and the messages a build can give are on the [Container page](/docs/container#container-build-memory) and in [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting#troubleshooting-build).\n\n**Related resources:** [Build with GitHub Actions](/docs/build-github-actions), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","deployments":"Use it\n\nPage 11 of 40\n\n# Deployments\n\nEvery build is a deployment, with its own log, commit and port. Production and preview deployments are kept apart.\n\nA deployment is one attempt to build a commit and put it on your container. It starts Queued, ends as Ready, Error or Canceled, and keeps its log, so you can see where it came from. Its source is `manual` (the Deploy button or API), `webhook` (a push or pull request), `hook` (a deploy hook), `redeploy` or `rollback`. You need a project and an access token, which you make on the Developer page so a script can call Egg’s API, and the token needs `deployments:write` to start, redeploy, cancel or roll back a deployment.\n\n## States\n\n| State | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| Queued | Waiting. A project runs one deployment at a time, and the worker runs 2 deployments at once across all projects. |\n| Building | Cloning and packing on the Egg machine, then the checks, the upload, and the install and build on your container. |\n| Deploying | On your container: starting the service and waiting for it to answer. A rollback also shows as Deploying. |\n| Ready | Started and answering. |\n| Error | A step failed. The error text, at most 500 characters, is on the deployment page. |\n| Canceled | You canceled it, or its pull request closed. |\n\nThe lists draw Deploying as Building, so the Compare table shows all six words. In the status filter, Building also lists Queued, and Error also lists Canceled.\n\nIf a production deployment fails after the switch, Egg puts the previous release back and checks it for 20 seconds, so the log says “The previous release is serving traffic again.” or “The previous release did not come back either. Check the logs.” Egg stops and removes a preview that fails to start, so it does not stay behind as a service that keeps restarting.\n\nThe deployment page has a Details card (Created, Duration, Environment, Address, Port and Source, with the branch and commit) and a Build Logs card. A log reads in this order: “Deploying NAME (production) to USER@HOST”, the clone, “Commit abc1234: message”, “Building: COMMAND” (or “No build step for this project.”), “Packed release: N MB”, “Connecting to your Nest container”, “Uploading N MB”, the steps on the container, “Waiting for the app to answer on port N” and a line that starts “Healthy:”. Production ends with “Live on port N. Map your domain to this port in the Nest dashboard.” The Build Logs card starts open while a deployment runs or has failed and collapses when it is Ready, so you can read it in place. It has a count of lines and a **Find in logs…** box, but you cannot download build logs.\n\n## Actions\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Deploy\n\n   Open your project and press the button at the top. It reads **Deploy** until a production release is live and **Redeploy** after that, and either way it queues a new build of the production branch tip, not an older commit. The build runs on your container and restarts your app when it goes live, so a new deployment waits as Queued while another is running.\n2. Step 2: Redeploy one commit\n\n   In the Deployments list, open **Actions for deployment** on a row and choose **Redeploy**, so Egg builds the same commit again in the same environment. If it cannot fetch, check out and verify that exact commit, the deployment fails, because Egg never substitutes the branch tip. The menu also has **View logs**.\n3. Step 3: Roll back\n\n   Press **Instant rollback** on the project page, or choose it from a row’s menu, so `current` points at an earlier Ready production release still on your container and the service restarts. The app is offline while it restarts. Nothing is rebuilt, but Egg still waits up to 45 seconds for the app to answer, so an unhealthy older release puts the live release back and ends the rollback row as Error. The log ends with “Rollback complete. Database changes are not reversed.” The project page button targets the newest Ready production release that is not live, and its tooltip says “Switches back to the previous release. Database changes are not reversed.”\n4. Step 4: Cancel\n\n   Choose **Cancel** on a row that is Queued, Building or Deploying, so a running build or upload stops within about two seconds, because the worker checks for a cancel every two seconds. Egg also checks before each step on your container. Cancel works until Egg starts to switch the release, so after that it is refused with “Activation has started or this deployment is already finished.” and the deployment finishes as Ready, or as Error if the new release does not answer.\n5. Step 5: Restart without building\n\n   On the project page’s Service card, press **Restart** and confirm “Restart the service?”, so Egg restarts the production service without building anything. The app is offline for a few seconds.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Search\n\n   Search with **Search by commit, branch, author or #pr…**, so every word must match the project, branch, commit message, short commit, author, source, environment or pull request (type `#14` or `pr 14`).\n2. Step 2: Sort\n\n   Sort with **Sort deployments** (Newest first, Oldest first, Longest build, Shortest build), so you change only the order shown.\n3. Step 3: Filter\n\n   Filter with **Filter by status**, **Filter by environment**, **Filter by project** and **Filter by time** (Any time, Last 24 hours, Last 7 days, Last 30 days), so Egg answers from the server, and press **Clear filters** to reset them.\n4. Step 4: Act on rows\n\n   Tick rows for **Compare** (exactly two), **Cancel** (unfinished previews) or **Delete previews** (previews not live and at least 24 hours old), so the action applies only to what you ticked. Deleting asks “Delete N previews?” and says Egg stops each preview and removes its files from your container, but never deletes production releases or the live release. The record stays in the list.\n\nThe all-projects Deployments page loads the newest 100 deployments and shows 25 at a time with a **Show N more** button. A single project’s Deployments tab loads only the newest 25, so a long history does not have to load at once, and a **Load older** button (“Loading older…”) fetches the next 25. Search, status, environment and time run on the server, not on the rows already loaded, so a match is found however far back it is. Search waits until you stop typing, and sorting only reorders the rows loaded so far, as the page says under the list. If a page cannot load, the rows already shown stay and the button tries again.\n\nTo keep a record outside the dashboard, open a project’s Deployments tab and press **Export CSV**. The file holds that project’s newest 5,000 deployments with the time (UTC), commit, branch, environment, state, duration in seconds and deployment id, but never variables, logs, ports or addresses. You can make 10 exports a minute, so after that Egg answers “Too many exports. Wait N seconds and try again.”\n\n## Releases and previews on disk\n\nEach release lives in its own folder under `~/.egg/NAME/releases/` on your container, so up to six copies of the code and its installed dependencies use your disk. After every successful deploy, production keeps the five newest Ready releases plus the live one, and a rollback can leave six. Previews, across all branches, keep the two newest Ready ones, so Egg stops and deletes older ones. You cannot roll back to a release that was removed.\n\nA build that fails, runs out of memory, times out or is canceled removes its own release folder, so the running release is not touched. A deployment that fails or is canceled after its build finished leaves its folder until the next successful deploy removes it, while a preview that fails to start is removed at once. If cleanup fails over SSH, the records are still marked cleaned up, so a leftover folder can stay on your container while the page says it is gone, and the Container page lists such leftovers.\n\n## Branch previews\n\nAny branch other than production builds as a preview, with its own service on the lowest free port from 20000 to 29999 on that server, so production keeps running. Nest gives previews no address on its own, and the log says “Preview is running on port N. Map a domain to it in the Nest dashboard to open it.” You build a preview by pushing a branch, opening a pull request, calling a deploy hook with a branch or using the API. Previews use the variables set for All or Preview, and the project page lists them under Active branches, one row per branch.\n\nA failed deploy leaves production alone\n\nIf the new release never answers on its port, Egg switches the previous release back on, so a failed deploy does not leave you on a broken version. If your app crashes at start, the check stops at once with “Service crashed on start” instead of waiting the full 45 seconds.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA release is a folder and going live is moving one link, so rolling back moves it the other way and takes seconds. For the same reason, a rollback cannot restore a cleaned-up release or reverse changes your app made to its own database, because only the code folder changes. A project runs one deployment at a time, so two builds cannot race to switch the same link.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Releases kept in production | 5 Ready, plus the live one | Older ones are deleted and cannot be rolled back to. In the code: server/deploy/layout.ts KEEP_RELEASES |\n| Previews kept | 2 newest Ready | Older previews are stopped and removed. In the code: server/deploy/layout.ts KEEP_PREVIEWS |\n| Preview ports | 20000 to 29999 per server | “No free preview ports on this server.” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Deployments at once, all projects | 2 by default | The rest wait as Queued. A self-hosted worker can change it with WORKER_CONCURRENCY. In the code: instrumentation.ts |\n| Deployments at once, one project | 1 | The others wait as Queued. In the code: server/jobs.ts |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | “The build timed out.” In the code: server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Fetching one commit for a redeploy | 3 minutes | The deployment fails if the requested SHA cannot be fetched, checked out, and verified. The branch tip is never substituted. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Build time | 15 minutes, for install and build together. Fixed. | “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS |\n| Build cap | Build memory (MB) of the server, 512 MB by default, at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when the container has room, and never more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB; Build CPU limit (cores), 2 by default; no swap. The build does not start if the cap would be under 256 MB. | “The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB.” A build that waits for another build on the same container waits up to 30 minutes. In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts containerMemoryCapMb lib/build-memory.ts |\n| Free disk before a build | 2048 MB | “Free up disk space on your container: N MB is free and a build needs at least 2048 MB.” Nothing is uploaded. In the code: server/deploy/container-probe.ts MIN_FREE_DISK_MB |\n| Build memory and CPU setting | Build memory (MB), set per server on the Container page: 512 MB up to the memory the server reports, or 8192 MB when it reports none. Build CPU limit (cores): 0.5 up to the cores the server reports, in 0.25 steps, 2 by default. | A value outside the range is refused. In the code: lib/build-memory.ts DEFAULT_BUILD_CPUS |\n| Source archive | 500 MiB compressed, 2 GiB unpacked, and 200,000 included paths | Packaging stops when any limit is exceeded. The compressed archive is removed. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts MAX_ARCHIVE_BYTES MAX_SOURCE_UNPACKED_BYTES MAX_SOURCE_ENTRIES |\n| One step on your container | 2 minutes; 15 minutes for unpacking and installing | “Command timed out after 120s” (or 900s). In the code: server/deploy/pipeline.ts |\n| Wait for the app to answer | 45 seconds, then 20 seconds for the old release | The release is marked Error and the previous one is restored. In the code: server/deploy/pipeline.ts |\n| Worker lease on a running job | Renewed every 15 seconds. Counted as expired after 60 seconds without renewal | The job keeps blocking its project until the person running Egg recovers it by hand. Egg does not fail it by itself. In the code: server/jobs.ts LEASE_RENEW_MS STALE_AFTER_MS |\n| Log lines per deployment | 20,000, each cut at 4000 characters | “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts |\n| Build log lines loaded on a page | 1000 | A finished deployment shows its first 1000 lines. A running one streams new lines as they arrive. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Live build stream | About 3.75 hours | The stream stops; reload the page. In the code: app/api/deployments/[id]/stream/route.ts |\n| Bulk actions | 50 deployments at a time | Each one runs on its own; one failure does not stop the others. In the code: server/deployments-bulk.ts MAX_BULK |\n| Delete previews | Not live, not cleaned up, not running, 24 hours old or more | “Only old previews that are not live or removed can be deleted in bulk.” In the code: lib/deployments-filter.ts |\n| Deployments page, all projects | Newest 100, shown 25 at a time | Older ones do not appear on the page. Open the project’s Deployments tab for them. In the code: server/queries.ts components/deployments-view.tsx |\n| A project’s Deployments tab | 25 at a time, up to 100 for an API request | Load older brings in the next page. Filters and search run on the server. In the code: server/deployment-page.ts DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE MAX_PAGE_SIZE |\n| CSV export | Newest 5,000 deployments, 10 exports a minute | “Too many exports. Wait N seconds and try again.” In the code: server/export-csv-data.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“Could not clone the repository. Check the branch name and that Egg has access to it.”`\n\n  Check the production branch name, and that the GitHub app is installed on the repository.\n- `“The root directory “DIR” does not exist in this repository.” or “DIR” is outside the repository.”`\n\n  Fix the Root directory in Settings. It must be a folder inside the repository.\n- `“The build failed (exit CODE).”`\n\n  Read the Build Logs card from the bottom. A missing lockfile, a wrong root directory or a missing variable is the usual cause.\n- `“The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  A build went over its cap and was stopped (exit 137). Your app was not. The second sentence is added only when a release was already serving. Raise **Build memory (MB)** on the Container page and deploy again, or make the build lighter. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- `“The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  A build ran past 15 minutes. Cut work from the build. This limit is fixed. “Your app is still running.” is added only when a release was already serving.\n- `“The build timed out.”`\n\n  The clone ran past 5 minutes. The limit is fixed. Check that the repository is not very large, then deploy again.\n- A one-line fix that starts with “Install”, “Free up”, “Egg could not check your container”, “Builds on your container run under” or “Your container cannot apply”\n\n  Egg looked at your container before it uploaded anything and found something missing. Nothing was changed and your running app was not touched. Do what the line says, then deploy again. Every line is listed on the [troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) page.\n- `“Another build has been running on this container for more than 30 minutes, so this one did not start. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  Only one container build runs at a time on a container. Wait for the other build, or cancel it, then deploy again.\n- `“The output folder “DIR” was not created by the build.”`\n\n  A Static site project builds into a folder that does not exist. Check the Output directory and the build command.\n- `“The build output is N MB, over the 524.288 MB limit.”`\n\n  Leave large files out of the build, or ship them another way. Files in `shared/` are not part of a release.\n- `“Nothing was changed on your container.” followed by details`\n\n  The container check found something that is not Egg’s. See Projects for each case.\n- `“PATH is a link, so Egg will not write into it.”, “PATH exists and is not a folder.”, “PATH already exists and was not created by Egg, so it was left alone.” or “UNIT already exists and was not created by Egg, so it was left alone.”`\n\n  Something of yours is in the way. Egg never overwrites it. Rename the project or remove the item yourself.\n- `“Preparing folders failed (exit N).” and similar for Checking the service name, Unpacking the release, Registering the service, Switching the release and Starting the preview`\n\n  That step failed on your container. The log lines above it say why. “Upload is corrupted: checksum mismatch” means the file changed in transit; deploy again.\n- `“The new release did not become healthy on port N. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.”`\n\n  Fix the port or address, or the health path, and deploy again. The last 30 log lines of the service are printed in the build log.\n- A deployment stays Building or Deploying and does not move\n\n  The worker stopped, or it can no longer renew its lease on the job. Egg leaves the job blocking its project until the person running Egg recovers it by hand, because a command on your container may still be running. After that the deployment shows Error with “The worker stopped during this job.” and you can deploy again.\n- `“No free preview ports on this server.”`\n\n  All 10,000 preview ports are in use. Delete old previews.\n- `“That release was removed from your server to save space.”`\n\n  It is outside the five kept. Redeploy that commit instead.\n- `“That deployment is already live.”, “Only production deployments can be rolled back to.” or “That deployment never finished, so there is nothing to roll back to.”`\n\n  Pick a different Ready production deployment.\n- `“That release is no longer on your server (old releases are removed to save disk space).” or “The older release did not become healthy, so nothing was changed.”`\n\n  The rollback row ended as Error and the live release was left or restored. Redeploy the commit.\n- `“There is no earlier release on your server to switch back to.” (the rollback button’s tooltip)`\n\n  There is nothing older to go back to. Redeploy a commit instead.\n- `“Activation has started or this deployment is already finished.”`\n\n  It is too late to cancel: the release is being switched or the deployment ended. Check its state.\n- `“Only previews that are queued or running can be canceled in bulk.” or “Could not remove the preview from your container.”`\n\n  The bulk action skipped that row. Check its state, then try again; the others still run.\n- `“Could not start the deployment”`\n\n  The browser could not queue the build. Press Deploy again, and check your connection.\n\n### Example: Recover from a bad release\n\n#### Given\n\nSam deploys a change to `recipe-bot` that crashes when it reads a missing variable. The previous release is still on the container.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Ship a bad release\n\n   The new deployment ends as Error with “The new release did not become healthy on port 3000…”. The log prints the service’s last 30 lines and “The previous release is serving traffic again.”\n2. Step 2: Fix and push\n\n   Sam fixes the code and pushes. The next deployment reaches Ready.\n3. Step 3: Roll back a bad one\n\n   A week later Sam ships a change that works but behaves badly, and presses Instant rollback.\n\n#### Result\n\nA new deployment appears with the source “rollback” and the state Ready. The log ends with “Rollback complete. Database changes are not reversed.” Any change the bot made to its own database stays as it was.\n\n**Related:** [GitHub and previews](/docs/github), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Logs](/docs/logs), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","env":"Use it\n\nPage 12 of 40\n\n# Environment variables\n\nSet once per project. Values are encrypted, masked in logs, and written to your container when you deploy.\n\nEnvironment variables give your app its secrets and settings, such as a Discord token or database address, without putting them in the repository. Each has a name, a value and a target that says which deployments get it. You need a project, so open its Settings and find the Environment Variables card. You can also fill in names found in your repository while you create the project.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Add a variable\n\n   Type a **Name** and a **Value**, choose where it applies in **Applies to** (All, Production or Preview) and press **Save**, so the list below shows each name with its target in alphabetical order. The button stays off until there is a name.\n2. Step 2: Import names from the repository\n\n   Press **Import names from the repository**, so the button says “Reading the repository…” while Egg reads `.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template` and `.env.local.example` on the production branch. Every name not yet present is added for All with an empty value, and the page says “Added N names with empty values. Use the pencil on each to set its value.” Egg never touches names you already have.\n3. Step 3: Change a value\n\n   Press the pencil (“Change the value of NAME”), type a “New value” and press **Update**. Saving the same name and target again also replaces the value. You cannot read the old one back. Saving a value never changes the variable’s secret flag.\n4. Step 4: Delete a variable\n\n   Press the trash button (“Delete NAME”). The dialog “Delete NAME?” says “Deployments after this one will not have it.” The variable stays in the current release until you deploy again.\n5. Step 5: Apply the change\n\n   A change reaches your container on the next deployment, because Egg writes the values into the new release. To apply it now, press **Redeploy** on the project page, which builds again on the Egg machine and restarts your app. A restart alone does not pick up a change.\n\n| Applies to | Production deployments | Preview deployments |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| All | Yes | Yes |\n| Production | Yes | No |\n| Preview | No | Yes |\n\nWhen you set the same name for `All` and for one environment, the matching Production or Preview value wins, so `All` only supplies a value when no environment-specific value matches.\n\n- The list shows names and targets only, and the browser never shows a value. Each variable has a secret flag, on by default, and the flag is changed through the API. A token with `env:read` gets a value back from `get_env_vars` only when the variable is marked non-secret. Egg stores them encrypted with AES-256-GCM, tied to the project, the name and the target.\n- At deploy time Egg passes your values to the build on your container as ordinary environment variables, so any install or build script, including those of your dependencies, can read them. For the build they are written to a file with permissions 600 that Egg deletes when the build ends. They are also written to a file in the project’s `env/` folder on your container, named after the release, with permissions 600 and never inside the release folder, because a static site serves that folder. They are written as `NAME=\"value\"` lines for systemd. Line breaks in a value become `\\n`. An empty value is left out of the file, so the app sees the variable as unset rather than empty.\n- Build and deploy logs, and the error text of a failed deployment, hide each value as `***`. The base64 form of a value and its percent-encoded form are hidden too. In a multi-line value such as a key file, each line of 8 or more characters is hidden on its own. A value of 2 or 3 characters is hidden only where it stands alone, not inside a longer word or number, and a one-character value is never hidden. Another changed form, such as hex or a hash, is not recognised.\n- Saving a name and target that already exist replaces the value. The same name for All and for one target counts as two variables.\n\nVariables files are not shown in the dashboard or the API\n\nThe Files page and the API refuse to open anything in `env/` or `current.env`, and the `.env` files that releases made before the move still sit in `current/` and `releases/ID/`. The Files page says “This file holds the project’s environment variable values, so it is not shown here. Change the values in the project’s settings.” and the API answers 403. A terminal on your container can read the file, so the Terminal switch is off by default.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nSecret values are write-only, so no page, token or log has to be trusted with them. The cost is that you cannot check a secret value after saving it; you replace it. A value marked non-secret can be read back through `get_env_vars`. Egg writes variables into each release instead of reading them live, so a rollback runs with the variables that release shipped with and a half-edited set never reaches a running app.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Variables per project | 100, counted per name and target | “A project can have up to 100 variables.” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Name | Letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit; 100 characters | “key: Names use letters, digits and underscores, and do not start with a digit” In the code: server/deploy/runtime.ts ENV_KEY |\n| Value | 10,000 characters | “value: Too big: expected string to have <=10000 characters” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Reserved names | PORT, HOST, PATH, HOME, EGG, in any letter case | “key: That name is set by Egg” In the code: server/deploy/runtime.ts RESERVED_ENV |\n| Names found in a repository | 100, from files up to 200,000 bytes | The rest are not listed. In the code: server/repo-detect.ts |\n| Masking | Values of 2 or more characters, and their base64 and percent-encoded forms. 2 or 3 characters only where they stand alone | A one-character value shows in the log as typed. In the code: server/deploy/redact.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“key: Names use letters, digits and underscores, and do not start with a digit”`\n\n  Rename it, for example `DISCORD_TOKEN` and not `1TOKEN` or `discord-token`.\n- `“key: That name is set by Egg”`\n\n  Egg sets `PORT` and `HOST` itself, and `PATH`, `HOME` and `EGG` are kept. Choose a different name, or change the project’s port in Settings.\n- `“A project can have up to 100 variables.”`\n\n  Delete variables you no longer use, or combine related values into one.\n- `“No example env file (such as .env.example) was found in the repository.”`\n\n  Add the names by hand, or commit an example file with names and no values.\n- `“Every name in the repository is already here.”`\n\n  Nothing to add. Set the values with the pencil.\n- `“N added, then it stopped: reason”`\n\n  Some names were added before an error. Read the reason and import again.\n- `“Could not look at the repository.” or “Could not read this repository. If it is private, install the GitHub App so it can be read.”`\n\n  Install the GitHub app on the repository, or add the names by hand.\n- `“A .env file is committed to this repository. Remove it from git and rotate the values.”`\n\n  A warning from the import. Egg never read that file. Anyone with access to the repository can, so change the values.\n- A change did not take effect\n\n  It needs a deployment. Press Redeploy, then check the variable is set for the right environment.\n- A variable is set for All and for one environment\n\n  Keep one row per name where you can. Egg loads both rows, and these docs do not promise which one wins.\n\n## Example: Give recipe-bot its Discord token\n\n### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` reads its Discord token from `DISCORD_TOKEN`. The repository has a `.env.example` with that name and no value. Sam wants a different token for previews.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Import the names\n\n   Sam presses Import names from the repository. `DISCORD_TOKEN` appears for All with no value.\n2. Step 2: Set the value\n\n   Sam presses the pencil beside `DISCORD_TOKEN`, pastes the token as the New value and presses Update.\n3. Step 3: Add a preview token\n\n   Sam types `DISCORD_TOKEN` again, sets Applies to Preview and presses Save. The list now has two rows.\n4. Step 4: Apply it\n\n   Sam presses Redeploy.\n\n### Result\n\nProduction gets the token set for All, and previews get the token set for All and Preview. Egg does not promise which of those two wins for a preview, so Sam deletes the All row and adds a Production row instead. The build log prints the token as `***` if the app ever echoes it. The bot logs in on the new release.\n\n**Related:** [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Security](/docs/security).\n","logs":"Use it\n\nPage 13 of 40\n\n# Logs\n\nTwo kinds: what the build printed, and what your running app prints.\n\nEgg stores one build log per deployment on the Egg machine, showing the clone, install, build and release. It does not store runtime logs, so each time you open or refresh the page Egg reads them from your container over SSH to show what your running app prints. Build logs need a deployment, and runtime logs need a project that has been deployed, covering only the production service.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Read a build log\n\n   Open a deployment, so the **Build Logs** card shows each line. While it runs, new lines stream in and the card says “Waiting for the first log line…” until the first arrives. Use **Find in logs…** to search. A Ready deployment’s log starts collapsed, so open the card to read it.\n2. Step 2: Open the runtime logs\n\n   Open Logs in the sidebar and choose a project, then pick a **Time range** (1 hour, 24 hours or 7 days; the default is 7 days) and a **Level** (All lines, or Warnings and errors). The line under the controls says how many lines are shown, for example “120 lines from recipe-bot, last 7 days.”\n3. Step 3: Search\n\n   Type in **Search logs**, whose hint is “Search the last 1000 lines…”. The match is not case-sensitive and the page waits a moment after you stop typing before it asks your container.\n4. Step 4: Load older lines\n\n   The page opens with the newest 300 lines, so when the journal holds more inside the time range, a **Load older lines** button (“Loading older lines…”) sits above them. Each press adds the 300 lines before the ones you see, using the same time range, level and search, and keeps your place in the list, while a screen reader is told how many lines were added. The button goes away when nothing earlier is in range, but stays when a stretch has no match, so you can keep looking. If a read fails, the lines above stay and the button tries again. Changing the range, level or search starts again from the newest lines.\n5. Step 5: Follow it live\n\n   Tick **Refresh every 5s** to follow the log, so a “Live” marker appears and the page reads quietly in the background and adds only new lines at the bottom when older lines are on screen. Each refresh reads your container over SSH, so untick it when you stop watching, or press **Refresh** when you want to.\n6. Step 6: Keep a copy\n\n   Press **Download** to save exactly the lines on screen, older lines included, as a text file such as `recipe-bot-logs-24h-2026-11-05.txt`, named for the project, the range (`1h`, `24h` or `7d`) and the UTC date. The button is off when there are no lines.\n\n- Runtime logs are your production service’s journal on your container (`journalctl -u egg-NAME.service`), and the page shows up to 300 lines at a time, so Load older lines repeats the read for earlier lines. A search or the Warnings and errors level reads up to 1000 lines first, filters them and keeps the newest 300 that match. A preview’s output is not on this page.\n- Egg decides a line’s level from its text, in this order. A level the program printed itself wins: `[ERROR]`, `level=warn`, a JSON `level` or `severity` field, or a pino number, so `[INFO] failed login` stays Info. Next, a systemd marker such as `<3>`. Then counts of zero are ignored, so “0 errors” and “no warnings” do not count. After that the words error, fatal, critical, panic, exception, failed, failure and traceback, `npm ERR!`, Node codes such as `ERR_X`, class names like `TypeError:`, Python `File \"x\", line N` lines and stack frames are errors. The words warn, warning and deprecated are warnings. Everything else is Info. A stack trace takes the level of the error above it.\n- In the Warnings and errors view each kept line is marked Error or Warning.\n- Build logs hide secret values as `***`, drop colour codes, cut a line at 4000 characters, and stop at 20,000 lines per deployment with the line “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” Build logs have no download button. They stay on the deployment page.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg does not copy runtime output, so it stays in your container’s journal and reading it needs a live connection, with history set by systemd, not Egg. Build output is different: Egg collects it while the build runs and stores it with the deployment, so it is still there when your container is off.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Runtime log lines shown | 300 | The newest 300 after any filter. In the code: components/logs-view.tsx |\n| Lines read for a search or level filter | 1000 for each read, 300 kept | Press Load older lines to search further back, inside the time range. In the code: server/runtime-info.ts |\n| Lines added by Load older lines | 300 at a time | The button goes away when the journal has nothing before the first line. In the code: components/logs-view.tsx lib/log-page.ts |\n| Lines the API can be asked for | 1 to 1000 | Other numbers are clamped. In the code: app/api/projects/[id]/runtime-logs/route.ts |\n| Search text | 200 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/runtime-info.ts |\n| Reading the journal | 30 seconds | “Could not read the logs from your container.” or a timeout message. In the code: server/runtime-info.ts |\n| Automatic refresh | Every 5 seconds | Untick the box to stop. In the code: components/logs-view.tsx |\n| Build log lines per deployment | 20,000 | “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts |\n| Length of one build log line | 4000 characters | The rest of the line is cut off. In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts |\n| Build log lines loaded on a page | 1000 | A finished log shows its first 1000 lines. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Live build stream | About 3.75 hours | The stream stops; reload the page. In the code: app/api/deployments/[id]/stream/route.ts |\n| Journal history | Whatever systemd keeps on your container | Egg does not set it. In the code: Not in Egg |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“Could not read the logs from your container.”`\n\n  The container answered with an error. Press Refresh; if it keeps failing, test the connection on the Connect page.\n- `“Timed out reading the logs. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.”`\n\n  Wait a minute, then press Refresh.\n- `“Nest rejected the server’s key, so the logs could not be read.”`\n\n  The key is not accepted. Paste the current public key in the Nest dashboard and test the connection.\n- `“The server’s host key changed, so the logs were not read. Reconnect the server if you expected this.”`\n\n  See Servers for what a changed host key means and how to reconnect.\n- `“We can’t reach your container right now”`\n\n  Use the retry button, and open the details line for the exact message. Your apps may still be running.\n- `“Nothing printed in this time range. Try a longer one.”`\n\n  The app printed nothing in that time. Pick 24 hours or 7 days.\n- `“No warnings or errors in this time range.”`\n\n  Switch Level to All lines to see everything.\n- `“No lines match “TEXT”.”`\n\n  Each read looks through 1000 lines. If the page offers it, press Load older lines to search further back, or pick a shorter word or a shorter time range.\n- `“Nothing to read yet”`\n\n  You have no projects. Deploy one and what it prints will show up here.\n- A secret appears in a build log\n\n  Masking covers a value as stored, and its base64 and percent-encoded forms. It does not cover a value of one character, or one changed in another way, such as a hash. Do not print secrets from your own code.\n\n## Example: Find why the bot went quiet\n\n### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` goes quiet on Discord. The Service card shows Running.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open the logs\n\n   Sam opens Logs and chooses `recipe-bot`.\n2. Step 2: Narrow them\n\n   Sam sets Level to Warnings and errors and types `rate` in Search logs.\n3. Step 3: Keep the evidence\n\n   Sam presses Download.\n\n### Result\n\nThe page lists the matching lines marked Error or Warning, newest last among the 300 kept. Sam sees Discord’s rate limit message and attaches the downloaded file to a support question.\n\n**Related:** [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","domains":"Use it\n\nPage 14 of 40\n\n# Domains\n\nYour Nest name is free. A custom domain needs a DNS record first. Egg shows a domain as active only after its DNS check passes.\n\nA domain gives your app a name people can type, so three places have to agree: your DNS provider sends the name to Nest, the Nest dashboard maps it to your app’s port, and Egg records both so it can check them. Egg does only the last part, by looking, so it changes nothing in DNS, in Nest or on your container, and its checks run from the Egg machine. You need a connected server and either a domain whose DNS you can edit or your own Nest name, and the form adds the domain to the server you connected first.\n\n- **Your Nest name is free.** `your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, and any name under it such as `app.your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, needs no DNS record and is active as soon as you add it. Other names under `hackclub.app` are refused: “Only your Nest name, NAME.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.”\n- **A custom domain needs a DNS record.** Add a CNAME to `your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, or a TXT record with the value `domain-verification=your-nest-username`, so the domain waits as “Waiting for DNS” until Egg finds the record. This is the same proof Nest asks for when you add the domain on its dashboard.\n- **A subdomain of a domain you already verified** on the same server, such as `api.example.com` when `example.com` is active, needs no new record.\n- **Reserved names are blocked.** Names such as `admin`, `api`, `dashboard`, `status`, `www`, `nest`, `hackclub` and `egg` cannot be used as the name under `hackclub.app`, `hackclub.com` or `hackclub.dev`, or on their own. The message is “That name is reserved. Choose another domain.”\n- **Only active after the DNS check.** Egg shows a domain as active only when it is your Nest name, a subdomain of a verified domain, or its DNS record was found. IP addresses, `localhost` and internal names such as `.local` are refused.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Add the domain\n\n   Open Domains. In **Add a domain**, type the **Domain** (for example `app.example.com`), optionally pick a **Project (optional)** to fill its port, check the **Target port** and press **Add domain**, so a panel headed “Next: finish setup for DOMAIN” lists what to do next.\n2. Step 2: Point DNS at Nest\n\n   At your DNS provider, add one of the two records shown: a CNAME with the “CNAME value”, which is `your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, or a TXT record with the “TXT value”, which is `domain-verification=your-nest-username`. Use the TXT record for a bare domain such as `example.com`, where a CNAME often cannot sit beside the other records. Your Nest name and subdomains of a verified domain skip this step.\n3. Step 3: Map it in the Nest dashboard\n\n   In the Domains page of your Nest dashboard, add the same domain with the same port, so Egg can check it. Nest looks for the same record when you add it, so do step 2 first, because Egg cannot do this for you.\n4. Step 4: Check\n\n   Press **Check**, so the button reads “Checking…” and the status moves from “Waiting for DNS” to “Active” when the record is found, or stays on “Waiting for DNS” with a reason when it is not. DNS changes can take a few minutes, so check again after a short wait.\n\n- **DNS check.** Passes when the domain has a TXT record `domain-verification=username`, or a CNAME to `username.hackclub.app`. For a name under `username.hackclub.app`, or that exact name, an A record is enough. A domain that has only an A record, and is not under your Nest name, gets “No CNAME found yet”. A subdomain of a domain you already verified passes without its own record.\n- **App check.** Only once DNS passes, so Egg sends a GET over HTTPS to `/` and, if your project has a different health path, to that path too. It waits 5 seconds for each, checks the certificate, follows no redirects and reads no body. It shows “Certificate accepted” or “Certificate not verified yet”, then “Answers with HTTP CODE” for any status below 500, or “Reachable but returning CODE” for 500 and above. A 404 therefore still counts as answering. The health path is added in brackets after the message. It only checks names that resolve to public addresses, and never IP addresses.\n- **Edit** (the pencil, “Edit DOMAIN”) changes the project and port. Changing the port resets the status to “Not checked” (“Waiting for DNS” for a custom domain) and says “Saved. Also change the target port to N on the Domains page of your Nest dashboard, then press Check.” Changing a project’s port in its Settings does the same to every domain linked to that project.\n- **Remove** (“Remove DOMAIN”) opens “Remove DOMAIN?” and says “This removes the record from Egg.” It deletes the record in Egg only. Nest and your DNS provider are not changed.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| Waiting for DNS | A custom domain whose record has not been found yet. The record to add sits under the row. |\n| Not checked | A Nest name or a subdomain of a verified domain whose port changed and has not been checked since. |\n| Active | Your Nest name, a subdomain of a verified domain, or a domain whose DNS record was found. The app check may still show a problem. |\n| Needs attention | A domain that was active and no longer passes the check. The row says “DNS no longer points here. Check your record.” Egg never removes the domain. |\n\nEgg rechecks active custom domains whenever you open the Domains page, at most once a minute for each, so if the record has gone the row shows “DNS no longer points here. Check your record.” A failed lookup shows nothing, so fix the record and press **Check**.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg checks DNS and the app instead of changing anything, so the mapping stays in the Nest dashboard, which has no API, and a record in Egg is only a note of your intent. The rules follow Nest’s own, so your Nest name is free, a custom domain proves itself with the same TXT or CNAME record, and Nest’s reserved names are blocked here too. Egg shows a domain as active only after that proof, so a name that is not yours never looks live. The two checks are separate, so DNS proves the name reaches Nest and the app check proves your app answers behind it, letting you tell which side to fix.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Domain | A hostname up to 253 characters, each part up to 63, ending in letters; no IP address or URL | “Enter a domain name such as app.example.com (not an IP address or a URL).” In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| Target port | 1 to 65535 | “Enter a port between 1 and 65535.” In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| Same domain twice | Once across all users. Egg does not say whose it is | “DOMAIN is already added.” In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| Reserved names | 119 names from Nest (admin, api, dashboard, docs, help, status, www and more) plus egg, nest, hackclub, hack-club, nest-deploy, egg-deploy and loadout | “That name is reserved. Choose another domain.” In the code: server/domain-policy.ts |\n| Names under hackclub.app | Only your own Nest name and names under it | “Only your Nest name, NAME.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.” In the code: server/domain-policy.ts |\n| Public domains only | No IP address, localhost, .local, .internal or .home.arpa name | “Use a public domain name such as app.example.com. IP addresses, localhost and internal names cannot be used.” In the code: server/domain-policy.ts |\n| How long a re-check is remembered | 60 seconds for each domain | Opening the page again within a minute shows the same answer. In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| App check wait | 5 seconds for each path | “No answer within 5 seconds.” In the code: server/app-check.ts |\n| Servers the form can use | Your first server, the one you connected first | A second server’s domains cannot be added from this form. In the code: components/domains-view.tsx |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“The CNAME points to X, but it should point to TARGET.” or “The CNAME points to X. It should point to TARGET.”`\n\n  Change the CNAME at your DNS provider to the value shown. The first form means X is another hackclub.app name.\n- `“No CNAME found yet. Create one pointing to TARGET, or add a TXT record with the value domain-verification=NAME. DNS changes can take a few minutes.”`\n\n  Add one of the two records, wait a few minutes, press Check.\n- `“That name is reserved. Choose another domain.”`\n\n  The name is on the reserved list. Pick another name, or use a domain of your own.\n- `“Only your Nest name, NAME.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.”`\n\n  Under `hackclub.app` you can use only your own Nest name and names beneath it. Use a domain of your own for anything else.\n- `“DNS no longer points here. Check your record.”`\n\n  An active domain no longer passes the check. Restore the CNAME or TXT record, then press Check. Egg keeps the domain.\n- `“The name does not resolve yet. Add it in the Nest dashboard first.”`\n\n  For a `.hackclub.app` name, add it on the Nest dashboard’s Domains page.\n- `“DNS could not be checked right now. Try again in a minute.”`\n\n  A lookup failed. Press Check again.\n- `“DNS does not point at your server yet. Press Check to see what it points at.”`\n\n  The default text under a row that needs attention. Press Check for the specific reason.\n- `“The app check runs once DNS points at Nest.”`\n\n  Fix DNS first. The app check only starts after the status is Active.\n- `“The certificate has expired.”, “The certificate is for a different name.” or “The certificate is not trusted.”`\n\n  The domain reaches something else, or the certificate on it is wrong. Check the mapping in the Nest dashboard.\n- `“The connection was refused on port 443.” or “The secure connection could not be set up.”`\n\n  Nothing is listening for the domain yet. Check the Nest dashboard mapping and the target port.\n- `“No answer within 5 seconds.” or “Could not connect.”`\n\n  Your app did not answer in time, or the app is not running. Check the Service card and the logs.\n- `“The name does not resolve to an address yet.”, “The name resolves to a private or internal address, so it was not checked.” or “Only domain names can be checked, not IP addresses.”`\n\n  The app check only runs against public domain names. Fix the DNS record.\n- `“Reachable but returning 503”`\n\n  The app answered with a server error. Read the logs.\n- `“DOMAIN is already added.”`\n\n  The name is already recorded, by you or by another account. A domain can be added only once across all users.\n- `“Project not found”`\n\n  The project is on a different server than the domain’s. Pick a project from the first server, or none.\n\n## Example: Give the bot’s page a domain\n\n### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` now has a small status web page on port `3000`. Sam owns `recipes.example.com` and the Nest username is `sam-r`.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Add the domain\n\n   Sam adds `recipes.example.com` with project `recipe-bot`, which fills port `3000`. The row reads “Waiting for DNS” and shows the records.\n2. Step 2: Set DNS and Nest\n\n   Sam creates a CNAME for `recipes.example.com` pointing to `sam-r.hackclub.app`, then adds the same domain and port in the Nest dashboard.\n3. Step 3: Check\n\n   Sam presses Check, and finds “No CNAME found yet”. Ten minutes later Sam presses Check again.\n\n### Result\n\nThe status reads “Active” and the app check shows “Certificate accepted” and “Answers with HTTP 200”.\n\n**Related:** [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","container":"Use it\n\nPage 15 of 40\n\n# Container, terminal and files\n\nThree switches decide what Egg may do inside your container beyond deploying. All three start off.\n\nYou need a connected server, which is one container in Egg’s list. The switches live in Account, under Container permissions, one card per server named like `sam-r.hackclub.app`. Egg checks the switch on every request, before it opens an SSH session to your container, so turning one off stops the very next call. Turning a switch on or off never restarts an app. Only the Terminal switch closes anything: it closes your open terminals on that server.\n\n## The three switches\n\n| Switch | Default | Fresh sign-in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Terminal** | Off | Needed to turn on |\n| **File changes** | Off | Needed to turn on |\n| **Removing leftovers** | Off | Not needed |\n\n### Turn a switch on\n\n1. Step 1: Open Account\n\n   Find Container permissions and the card for your server.\n\n   In the dashboard: Accountthen Container permissions\n2. Step 2: Flip a switch\n\n   It saves at once and a message names the switch and the server, for example “Terminal turned on”.\n3. Step 3: Confirm if asked\n\n   Terminal and File changes send you to Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied. Turning any switch off never asks. Turning Terminal off closes your open terminals on that server, and says so.\n\nReading is separate from changing. You can browse, read and download files, and read the Container page, with every switch off.\n\nCheck your older servers\n\nAn update once set File changes to off on every server that already existed. Do not assume an older server kept its old setting: open Account and look at each card. A server connected before the switches started off may also still have Removing leftovers on. Turn off what you do not use.\n\n| Switch | What it can destroy |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Terminal** | Anything the container user can touch, including apps and files Egg did not create. It is a root shell, and anyone signed in to your account can use it while it is on. |\n| **File changes** | Anything inside `shared/` for every project on that server, including data your app keeps there. A folder delete is recursive. A change can alter the code your app runs at its next restart, so treat it like terminal access. |\n| **Removing leftovers** | Egg services (`egg-*.service`) that no project uses, and whole folders under `~/.egg` that no project uses, including their `shared/` data. |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nAll three switches start off because each one reaches past “deploy my app”. The server enforces them, not just the page: a request to a feature that is off is refused with a 403 before any SSH connection is opened, and an access token cannot turn a switch on. A switch that is off is the only part of this you do not have to trust Egg about.\n\n## The Container page\n\nContainer in the sidebar opens one SSH connection to your container and runs read-only commands, so it changes nothing there. It shows what is running and what Egg left behind. Pick a server if you have more than one, and press **Refresh** to read it again. A read that takes longer than 60 seconds fails.\n\n- **Disk used** shows the percentage and how much of it is Egg folders.\n- **Projects** shows each project’s service (Running, Starting, Crashed, Stopped, or No service yet), its port, folder size and previews. If another program holds a project’s port you see “Port <n> is held by <x>, not by <project>” with a “Change the port” link. The next deploy of that project fails its health check until the clash is sorted.\n- **Orphans** lists leftovers, described next.\n- **Other ports** lists programs listening that Egg did not start, with “This container only” or “Open to the network”. It is read only. Without `ss` or `netstat` on the container it says so.\n\n### Build memory\n\nBuild memory is the cap on a build. The setting belongs to the server, so every project on that server builds with the same amount. A new server starts at 512 MB. A server that still had the old 1024 MB default saved was lowered to 512 MB; a value you saved yourself was kept. The Build memory card on the Container page has a field, **Build memory (MB)**, and a **Save** button. The allowed range is 512 MB up to the memory your container reported when Egg last read it, at Test connection or during a deploy, or up to 8192 MB when it reported none. The hint under the field shows the range for this server.\n\nA build runs on your container, so it can never use all of its memory. At deploy time Egg lowers the cap to your container’s memory minus 512 MB, which stays free for your app, whatever the field says. A field set to 2048 MB on a container that reports 2048 MB gives a cap of 1536 MB. If that leaves less than 256 MB, the build does not start.\n\nProjects with a build step get at least 1024 MB while they build, if your container has room for it. A build step is a build command, or a build script such as `next build`. The floor applies only when your container’s memory minus 512 MB is at least 1024 MB, so a container that reports 1536 MB or more qualifies and a 1 GB container keeps the setting. It is applied when the build starts: the saved setting does not change, and a plain app or an install alone stays at the setting.\n\n### Change the build memory\n\n1. Step 1: Open Container\n\n   Pick the server from the list if you have more than one.\n\n   In the dashboard: Containerthen Build memory\n2. Step 2: Type a number in Build memory (MB)\n\n   A whole number of MB inside the range in the hint. Save stays off until the number differs from the saved one.\n3. Step 3: Press Save\n\n   A message says “Build memory set to <n> MB”. The next build on that server uses it. A build already running keeps the old limit.\n4. Step 4: Go back to the default\n\n   Press Use 512 MB, then Save. The button shows only while the saved value is not 512.\n\nA value outside the range is refused with “Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.” A build that needs more than the cap stops with “The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB.” When a release was already serving, “Your app is still running.” follows. The deployment shows Error, Egg deletes the new release folder, and the live release keeps running. Raise the limit and deploy again. The setting is changed in the browser only: no access token or agent call can change it. Each change is recorded in Activity as “Changed the build memory limit for”.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA build shares your container with your app, so the default is 512 MB, which covers installs and plain apps, a project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB when the container has room, and the cap always leaves 512 MB for the app. The limit is per server because servers differ: some can use more, and a build that fits a small server should not be given more than that server could ever offer. The top of the range follows what the server reported, so the page does not let you ask for memory the server does not have. A build gets no swap, so the limit is a real one and a build cannot slip past it by swapping.\n\n### Build CPU\n\nThe CPU limit applies to the build. It does not limit the app after it is installed on your Nest container. Each connected server has its own limit: 2 cores by default, adjustable from 0.5 cores in 0.25-core steps up to the core count the Nest container last reported when Egg checked it. If no count was reported, the maximum stays at 2 cores. The default is capped to the reported count when a server has fewer than 2 cores. The build also runs at a low priority, so your app gets the CPU first when it is busy.\n\n### Change the build CPU limit\n\n1. Step 1: Open Container\n\n   Pick the server from the list if you have more than one.\n\n   In the dashboard: Containerthen Build CPU\n2. Step 2: Set Build CPU limit (cores)\n\n   Enter a value in 0.25-core steps within the range shown under the field.\n3. Step 3: Press Save\n\n   The next build on that server uses the new CPU cap. A build already running keeps its current limit.\n4. Step 4: Restore the default\n\n   Press Use 2 cores, or the lower capacity-based default shown by the button, then Save.\n\nA value outside the range is refused with “Build CPU must be a number from 0.5 to <max> cores in 0.25-core steps.” The cap limits the build’s maximum CPU time; it does not reserve cores for a build.\n\n## Removing leftovers\n\nA leftover is a service named `egg-<project>.service` or a preview unit that no project uses, or a folder under `~/.egg` that is not a project. It usually appears after you delete a project with “Also stop the service and delete releases” unticked, or after a failed cleanup.\n\n### Remove a leftover\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on Removing leftovers\n\n   In Account, under Container permissions, for that server.\n2. Step 2: Open Container\n\n   Find the row under Orphans. Its kind is Service, Preview service or Folder.\n3. Step 3: Press Remove\n\n   A service asks “Remove <unit>?”. It stops the service, so anything running under it goes down, turns off starting at boot and deletes its unit file, the small file that tells Linux how to run it. Files in its folder are not touched.\n4. Step 4: Confirm a folder by name\n\n   A folder asks “Delete the folder <name>?” and you type its name, then press Delete folder. This deletes it and everything inside, including shared data, and cannot be undone.\n\nEgg knows what it made by two markers. A service file starts with a comment `egg:managed`, and a project folder holds a file named `.egg-managed`. An older folder without that file counts only if it holds nothing but `releases`, `incoming`, `shared`, `current` and `current.new` and has no `.git`. Anything without a marker is never overwritten, stopped, moved or deleted: a deploy refuses, cleanup refuses and project deletion leaves it.\n\nEvery request is checked again against a fresh read of the container. A row that now belongs to a project, that is a symbolic link, or that has no marker is refused, even if you press Remove. Access tokens and the MCP endpoint cannot remove leftovers at all, and one request removes at most 50 items.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nSomething you set up yourself can have a service whose name also starts with `egg-`, and a folder you made by hand can sit in `~/.egg`. A name alone proves nothing, so Egg trusts only its own marker, and checks it again at the moment you press Remove instead of trusting the list you were looking at.\n\n## Adopting an app that already runs\n\nIf an app of yours already runs on the container, Egg can check what the new project would collide with before it touches anything. In New Project the check runs by itself once the name, server and port are valid, and again at the start of every deploy. It never stops, moves or deletes anything.\n\n- **Blocks the deploy:** a service called `egg-<name>.service` that Egg did not create; the project folder being a link, a file, someone else’s git checkout, or a folder with other files in it; the port used by another program; a program or service already running from the project folder; and, for the first production deploy only, the same repository already running by hand from another checkout.\n- **Good to know, does not block:** a stopped service for the same repository, a checkout that is only sitting there, leftovers from an earlier Egg project of the same name (they are reused), and ports the container would not let it read.\n\nWhen something blocks, the form says “Deploy is turned off because N thing(s) on this container is/are in the way” and links to Open container status. Tick **Start with automatic deploys off** when you already run the repository on this container by hand. The project is created with automatic deploys off, so the first deploy is manual. Nothing turns them on afterwards: when the first deploy works, open Settings, Build and Run and tick **Deploy automatically on push**. This does not import, take over or stop the copy that is already running, and Egg does not import a running service yet. If that copy holds the port, stop it yourself first.\n\n## The terminal\n\nThe terminal is a live shell on your container in the browser, started in `~/.egg/<project>`. It is off until you turn it on for a server, and turning it on needs a Hack Club sign-in from the last 5 minutes. Anything you type runs for real.\n\n### Open a terminal\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on Terminal\n\n   In Account, under Container permissions, then confirm with Hack Club.\n2. Step 2: Open the project’s Terminal tab\n\n   With the switch off the page says “The terminal is turned off for this server.” and links to Account with a Go to Account button.\n3. Step 3: Press Open terminal\n\n   It shows who the shell runs as, for example “Connected as root” on the host name.\n4. Step 4: Type, then press Close terminal\n\n   A “Time left” countdown shows the time before the shell closes. Close terminal ends it.\n\n- It closes after 10 minutes without input. Output does not count as activity, and neither does resizing.\n- It closes after 60 minutes in total, whatever you are doing. A banner warns “This terminal closes in under a minute.”\n- It closes when you sign out, when another of your sessions is ended, when you delete your account, when you switch the terminal off, and when the connection drops or the shell exits.\n- Leaving the page keeps the terminal open until it times out. Come back and press **Resume terminal**, which replays the last 64 KB of output, or **Close it**.\n- The terminal takes every key while it has focus, including Tab. Press Escape, then Tab, to move focus to Close terminal. Copy with Ctrl+Shift+C and paste with Ctrl+V.\n- Only the opening and closing are recorded, with the duration and the reason. Keystrokes and output never are.\n\n| Title on the page | Why | Button |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal closed after a pause | 10 minutes without typing. | Reopen terminal |\n| Terminal reached its time limit | 60 minutes in total. | Reopen terminal |\n| Terminal closed | You pressed Close terminal. | Open a new terminal |\n| The shell ended | The shell exited, for example after typing exit. | Open a new terminal |\n| The connection dropped | The link to your container was lost. | Open a new terminal |\n| The terminal stopped | The shell stopped unexpectedly. | Open a new terminal |\n| This terminal is no longer open | It closed or timed out while you were away. | Open a new terminal |\n\n## The Files tab\n\nThe project’s Files tab browses `~/.egg/<project>`. Reading and downloading need no switch. Creating, uploading, editing, renaming and deleting need File changes on, and work only inside `shared/`. The live release `current/` is always read only, as are `releases/` and `incoming/`. `shared` itself cannot be renamed or deleted. A release’s `.env` is not shown or downloaded.\n\n| Folder | What it is | Can you change it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `shared/` | Survives deploys. Keep uploads and databases here. | Yes, with File changes on. |\n| `current/` | The live release. Replaced on the next deploy. | Never. |\n| `releases/` | Every deployed version, newest last. | No. |\n| `incoming/` | Archives on their way to a release. | No. |\n\n### Change a file\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on File changes\n\n   In Account, then confirm with Hack Club.\n2. Step 2: Open shared/\n\n   Uploads and edits are refused anywhere else. Elsewhere the page says “Uploads are not allowed here” and “Open shared/ first.”\n3. Step 3: Act\n\n   Use New folder or Upload (or drop files on the list), or select a file and press Edit, change it and press Save. Discard throws the edit away. Rename (or F2) and Delete are in the row. Deleting a folder asks you to type its name.\n4. Step 4: Restart if the app needs to re-read it\n\n   A change does not restart the app. Press Restart on the Service card when your app reads the file only at start. The app is unavailable for a moment while it restarts.\n\nCode changes belong in your repository, then a deploy. Files that must last, like data or settings, belong in `shared/`, which deploys never touch. A file you upload over an existing name asks before it replaces it. A link is never written through, and a link to a folder opens as a folder.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\n`current/` is read only even with File changes on because it is the code your app runs: editing it would put code on your container that is in no repository and that the next deploy silently erases. `shared/` is the one place a deploy never touches, so it is the one place a change can safely last.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal idle | 10 minutes | “Terminal closed after a pause”. Anything running in it was stopped. In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.idleMs) |\n| Terminal total | 60 minutes | “Terminal reached its time limit”. In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.maxAgeMs) |\n| Terminals open | 1 per project per person, 5 per account, 50 on the server | “A terminal is already open for this project. Resume or close it first.”, “You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.” or “Too many terminals are open right now. Try again soon.” In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.maxSessionsPerUser maxSessions) |\n| Terminal input | 8 KB a request, 300 requests a minute | “Send at most 8192 bytes at a time.” or “Too many requests for terminal input.” Wait and retry. In the code: server/terminal.ts (inputBytes) server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.terminalInput) |\n| Terminal size | 20 to 400 columns, 5 to 200 rows | “The terminal size must be 20 to 400 columns and 5 to 200 rows.” In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.minCols to maxRows) |\n| Terminal output kept | The last 64 KB | A reload resumes with that much. In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.bufferBytes) |\n| File preview and edit | The first 1 MB | A larger file shows “Too large to preview”. In the code: server/files-paths.ts (READ_LIMIT) |\n| File upload and download | 50 MB | “That file is over the 50 MB limit.” The row says “Over 50 MB”. In the code: server/files-paths.ts (UPLOAD_LIMIT DOWNLOAD_LIMIT) |\n| Folder listing | 3000 entries | More are not shown. In the code: server/files.ts (LIST_LIMIT) |\n| Path | 1024 characters, and 255 bytes in one name | “Path is longer than 1024 characters.” or “A name in the path is longer than 255 bytes.” In the code: server/files-paths.ts (MAX_PATH_LENGTH MAX_SEGMENT_BYTES) |\n| File reads | 120 a minute | “Too many file requests.” Wait and retry. In the code: server/files.ts (FILE_READ_LIMIT) |\n| File changes | 60 a minute | “Too many requests for file changes.” Wait and retry. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.fileWrite) |\n| File operation time | 30 seconds, 120 for upload, download and delete | The request fails with the container’s answer. Try again. In the code: server/files.ts (timeoutMs) |\n| Leftover removal | 50 items a request, 10 requests a minute | “Remove at most 50 items at a time.” In the code: server/inventory.ts (MAX_ORPHANS_PER_REQUEST) server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.orphanRemoval) |\n| Container reads | 60 seconds a read, 120 seconds for a removal | The read fails with the SSH message. Press Refresh. In the code: server/inventory.ts (timeoutMs) |\n| Conflict checks | 20 a minute | “Too many checks. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.” In the code: app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts (LIMIT) |\n| Build memory | 512 MB to the server’s memory, default 512 MB | “Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts (DEFAULT_CONTAINER_BUILD_MEMORY_MB buildMemoryRange) |\n| Build cap on the container | The Build memory setting, raised to at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when the container’s memory minus 512 MB is at least 1024 MB, and never more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB. No build starts under 256 MB. | “The build did not start: your container reports <n> MB of memory, and 512 MB of that is kept for your app, which leaves too little to build in. Use a larger container.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (containerMemoryCapMb CONTAINER_RESERVE_MB MIN_CONTAINER_CAP_MB) |\n| Build CPU | 0.5 cores to the Nest server’s reported core count, in 0.25-core steps; defaults to 2 or the lower reported count | “Build CPU must be a number from 0.5 to <max> cores in 0.25-core steps.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts (DEFAULT_BUILD_CPUS buildCpuRange checkBuildCpu) |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `The terminal is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Terminal in Account. Nothing else is needed to read files or the Container page.\n- `Changing files is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on File changes. Reading and downloading still work without it.\n- `Removing leftovers is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Removing leftovers. Leftovers stay listed while it is off.\n- `Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning this on needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.`\n\n  403. Flip the switch again. It sends you through Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied.\n- `This server has not been verified yet. Run Test connection on the Connect page first.`\n\n  409. The host key is not pinned yet, and a terminal never opens to a host Egg has not checked. Press Test connection.\n- `A terminal is already open for this project. Resume or close it first.`\n\n  409. Only one terminal per project and person. Press Resume terminal, or Close it, then open a new one.\n- `You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.`\n\n  429. One account may hold 5 terminals across all its projects, so one account cannot use up the server’s share. Close one you are not using.\n- `Too many terminals are open right now. Try again soon.`\n\n  503. The server as a whole holds 50 terminals. Wait a little and open yours again.\n- `The terminal was closed before it opened.`\n\n  403. You signed out, or switched Terminal off, while it was still connecting. Open it again.\n- `This project has no folder on your container yet. Deploy it first.`\n\n  404. The folder is created by the first deploy. Deploy once, then open Files.\n- `This location is read only. Files in shared/ can be changed. The live release (current/) cannot.`\n\n  403. Open shared/ and work there. The page shows the same rule as “current/ is the live release and is read only.”\n- `current/ is the live release and is read only. Changing it would change the code your app runs. Make the change in your repository and deploy, or use shared/ for files that last.`\n\n  403. Change the code in your repository and deploy. Put files that must last in shared/.\n- `This file holds the project's environment variable values, so it is not shown here. Change the values in the project's settings.`\n\n  403. The Files tab never opens a variables file. Change the values in the project’s settings, then deploy.\n- `That file holds the project's environment variable values, which are never returned by the API.`\n\n  403. The API refuses those files too. Use env:read to list names.\n- `Egg did not create this, so it was left alone.`\n\n  The leftover has no marker. If you are sure, remove it yourself in a terminal.\n- `This belongs to one of your projects, so it was left alone.`\n\n  It is not a leftover. Delete the project first if you want it gone.\n- `The container’s answer was cut short, so nothing was classified.`\n\n  Press Refresh.\n- `Remove at most 50 items at a time.`\n\n  400. Remove in batches.\n- `Nothing was changed on your container. <details>`\n\n  The conflict check refused. The details say what is in the way: choose another name or port, or stop that program yourself.\n- `Could not read the container.`\n\n  The page could not reach the container. Check it is running, then press Refresh.\n- `Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.`\n\n  400. Type a whole number inside the range in the hint under Build memory (MB). The top of the range is the memory your container reported when Egg last read it, at Test connection or during a deploy.\n- `The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB. Your app is still running.`\n\n  The build was stopped with exit 137. Your app was not. The second sentence is added only when a release was already serving. Raise Build memory (MB), press Save and deploy again, or make the build use less memory. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- `Could not read the build memory.`\n\n  The Build memory card could not load the setting. Reload the page. It does not need the container to be reachable.\n- `Could not save the build memory.`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try Save again.\n\nThe rest of the terminal and file messages, with their fixes, are on the [troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) page.\n\n## Example: Remove a leftover service\n\n### Given\n\nYou deleted a project named old-api with “Also stop the service” unticked, so `egg-old-api.service` still runs.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on Removing leftovers\n\n   In the dashboard: Accountthen Container permissionsthen Removing leftovers\n2. Step 2: Find the row\n\n   Open Container and look under Orphans for egg-old-api.service.\n3. Step 3: Remove it\n\n   Press Remove, then confirm with Remove service.\n\n### Result\n\nA message says the service was removed and the row leaves Orphans. Its folder, if any, is its own row and stays until you delete it by typing its name.\n\n## Example: Keep an upload across deploys\n\n### Given\n\nAn app that reads uploads from a folder, and File changes switched on.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open the Files tab\n\n   Open shared/ in the project.\n2. Step 2: Make a folder and upload\n\n   Press New folder, name it, then press Upload.\n\n### Result\n\nThe files are still there after the next deploy, because `shared/` is never touched by a release.\n\n## Example: Edit a settings file without a deploy\n\n### Given\n\nAn app that reads `shared/config.json` when it starts, and File changes switched on.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open the file\n\n   In the Files tab open shared/ and select config.json.\n2. Step 2: Edit and save\n\n   Press Edit, change the value, press Save.\n3. Step 3: Restart\n\n   Press Restart on the Service card so the app reads it.\n\n### Result\n\nThe app runs with the new setting. The change is in Activity as “Saved a file on” with the path and no contents.\n\n**Related:** [Account](/docs/account), [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","bots-discordjs":"Bots\n\nPage 16 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)\n\nHost a Discord bot written with discord.js: import the repository, add DISCORD_TOKEN, deploy, and let systemd keep it running.\n\nA discord.js bot is a Node.js process that connects to Discord over its gateway. It stays running and does not listen on a port. On Egg it is a **worker** workload: a background process that Egg keeps running instead of publishing a URL. If you do not have a bot yet, follow the [official discord.js guide](https://discordjs.guide/) until one runs locally with `npm start`. A repository that installs with `npm install` and starts with `npm start` deploys here.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL. Egg reads the files and shows what it found under “We found”.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `discord.js` is in the dependencies, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The summary says: “This looks like a Discord bot. It is set up as a background app that needs no port, and DISCORD_TOKEN is offered as an environment variable.” If Egg guessed wrong, the picker below stays editable.\n3. **Add the token.** In the environment section, the row `DISCORD_TOKEN` appears with “suggested for Discord bot (discord.js)” behind it. Paste the bot token from the [Discord developer portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications). Egg saves the value through the normal environment variable path and never shows it again.\n4. **Press Deploy.** There is no port to fill in. When it is live the deployment page says `The worker is running.` The process stays active. Egg checks it every few minutes. If it stops, you get an alert on the channel you set up.\n\n## What Egg does with it\n\nsystemd, the Linux service manager, starts the bot's process. It restarts the process with `Restart=always` if it exits. There is no health path to answer, so Egg checks that the process stays active instead. The Service card on the project page shows the status, the uptime, the memory and a **Restarts** count. A bot that keeps crashing is visible without opening the logs. Read the runtime output on the [Logs page](/docs/logs). Never paste the token into the repository. Use [environment variables](/docs/env).\n\nIf it also serves web pages\n\nSome bots also host a small web dashboard. A worker cannot receive HTTP requests in this version. Keep the bot as a worker and put the dashboard in a separate web project. A Discord webhook or interaction proxy needs its own setup outside the bot for the same reason.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.py)](/docs/bots-discordpy), [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](/docs/bots-slack), [Deploy a Telegram bot](/docs/bots-telegram), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Environment variables](/docs/env).\n","bots-discordpy":"Bots\n\nPage 17 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Discord bot (discord.py)\n\nHost a Discord bot written with discord.py: a requirements.txt with the framework, DISCORD_TOKEN in the environment, and the Python runtime preset.\n\nA discord.py bot is a Python process that stays connected to Discord. It does not listen on a port, so it deploys as a **worker** workload. If you do not have a bot yet, the framework's own repository carries [runnable examples](https://github.com/Rapptz/discord.py/tree/master/examples), from a first bot to slash commands. Grow one into your own repository. A project with a `requirements.txt` and an entry point such as `main.py` deploys here. The install step reads your `requirements.txt` on the container.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `discord.py` is in your `requirements.txt`, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The summary names DISCORD_TOKEN. The picker stays editable if the guess is wrong. The `python` preset is the right runtime. Check that the start command points at your entry point, for example `python3 main.py`.\n3. **Add the token.** The environment section lists `DISCORD_TOKEN` with “suggested for Discord bot (discord.py)” behind it. Paste the bot token from the [Discord developer portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications). Egg saves the value once and never shows it again.\n4. **Press Deploy.** No port, no domain. When it is live the page says `The worker is running.`\n\nPython specifics\n\nEgg reads your `requirements.txt` for package names only. It uses the file to spot the framework. Version pins stay in the file and apply when the container installs them. discord.py needs Python 3.8 or newer, and prefix commands must be registered in the setup hook. An older pin can fail at startup with an import error. Check the runtime logs first when a Python bot does not start.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](/docs/bots-slack), [Deploy a Telegram bot](/docs/bots-telegram), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Logs](/docs/logs).\n","bots-slack":"Bots\n\nPage 18 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)\n\nHost a Slack app built with Bolt for JavaScript in Socket Mode: the workload is a worker, and the token names are listed for you.\n\nA Slack app built with [Bolt for JavaScript](https://github.com/slackapi/bolt-js) can work two ways. With **Socket Mode** it connects outward to Slack. It never needs a public URL, so it deploys as a **worker** workload like any other bot. With an **HTTP receiver**, Slack sends events to a request URL on your app. That needs a public address. A worker cannot receive HTTP requests in this version. Use Socket Mode here.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL. A Bolt app installs with `npm install` and starts with `npm start`.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `@slack/bolt` is in the dependencies, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The picker stays editable if the guess is wrong.\n3. **Add the tokens.** The environment section lists two suggested names from your [Slack app settings](https://api.slack.com/apps): `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN`, which starts with `xoxb-`, and `SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET`. If your code also reads the app-level Socket Mode token, which starts with `xapp-`, add it under the name your code expects, for example `SLACK_APP_TOKEN`.\n4. **Press Deploy.** No port and no request URL in this mode. When it is live the page says `The worker is running.`\n\n## Why Socket Mode\n\nSocket Mode keeps the advantages of a worker workload. systemd restarts the process if it exits. Egg checks that it stays active. Nothing about the container has to be reachable from the internet. Turn it on in the app settings on api.slack.com. Allow the events and interactivity scopes. Paste the app token into the environment under one of the two names above. An HTTP receiver would change the story. The app would have to answer requests on a URL. A separate web project does that here.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Deploy a Telegram bot](/docs/bots-telegram), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Environment variables](/docs/env).\n","bots-telegram":"Bots\n\nPage 19 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Telegram bot\n\nHost a Telegram bot written with Telegraf or python-telegram-bot: import the repository, add TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN, deploy, and let systemd keep the process running.\n\nA Telegram bot is a process that connects to Telegram and waits for updates. It does not listen on a port, so on Egg it is a **worker** workload: a background process that Egg keeps running instead of publishing a URL. Egg reads your files to find the framework. When `telegraf` is in your `package.json` dependencies, or `python-telegram-bot` is in your `requirements.txt`, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The summary names TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN. A project that installs and starts with `npm start`, or with `python3 main.py`, deploys here.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL. Egg reads the files and shows what it found under “We found”.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `telegraf` is in your `package.json` dependencies, or `python-telegram-bot` is in your `requirements.txt`, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The picker stays editable if the guess is wrong. If you do not have a bot yet, follow the [Telegraf documentation](https://telegraf.js.org/) to write one, or start from the framework's own [runnable python-telegram-bot examples](https://github.com/python-telegram-bot/python-telegram-bot/tree/master/examples).\n3. **Add the token.** The environment section lists `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` with “suggested for Telegram bot (telegraf)” behind it for a Telegraf project, or “suggested for Telegram bot (python-telegram-bot)” for a python-telegram-bot project. Paste the bot token from [BotFather on Telegram](https://t.me/BotFather). Egg saves the value through the normal environment variable path and never shows it again.\n4. **Press Deploy.** There is no port to fill in. When it is live the deployment page says `The worker is running.` The process stays active. Egg checks it every few minutes. If it stops, you get an alert on the channel you set up.\n\n## What Egg does with it\n\nsystemd, the Linux service manager, starts the bot's process. It restarts the process with `Restart=always` if it exits. There is no health path to answer, so Egg checks that the process stays active instead. The Service card on the project page shows the status, the uptime, the memory and a **Restarts** count. A bot that keeps crashing is visible without opening the logs. Read the runtime output on the [Logs page](/docs/logs). Never paste the token into the repository. Use [environment variables](/docs/env).\n\nPolling and webhooks\n\nA worker holds no public URL in this version. Use long polling. It is the default in both Telegraf and python-telegram-bot. A webhook app needs a separate web project. The web project receives the updates and forwards them to the bot until workers can expose a URL.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](/docs/bots-slack), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Environment variables](/docs/env).\n","bots-alive":"Bots\n\nPage 20 of 40\n\n# Keep a bot alive\n\nWhat keeps a bot running: systemd restarts, the Restarts count, crash loops and their fixes, and nightly work that belongs in scheduled jobs.\n\nEgg gives a bot the same care a web app gets. It adjusts for a process that does not answer requests. A bot that fails shows no error page to anyone. Read these signals instead.\n\n## Starts, restarts and crash loops\n\n- systemd starts the bot's process with `Restart=always`. If it exits, systemd restarts it within seconds. Most crashes pass unnoticed, which is the point. The first place one shows is the **Restarts** count on the Service card.\n- A stable bot reads `Running` on the Service card with a low, slow-moving Restarts number. A climbing number means the process keeps exiting. Open the [runtime logs](/docs/logs) and look for the error it prints each time it starts.\n- When a bot keeps failing to start, systemd pauses it for a while instead of retrying forever. The status reads `Crashed`. Fix the error. Press **Restart** on the Service card to start the bot again.\n- A missing token is the most common first failure. The process starts, reads an empty DISCORD_TOKEN and exits at once. Set values under [Environment variables](/docs/env) and restart. Values reach the server on the next deploy.\n\n| Signal | Where you see it | What it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A climbing Restarts count | Service card | The process exits as soon as it starts. The runtime logs hold the error it prints each time. |\n| Status reads Crashed and stays there | Service card | systemd has paused the retries. Fix the error, then press Restart to start the unit again. |\n| A “has stopped” message | Your [notification channel](/docs/notifications) | The process did not stay up, or keeps restarting. Egg keeps checking. It sends a second message when it is back. |\n| A memory alert | Your notification channel | The bot crossed its memory limit. A leak grows the memory reading on the Service card over hours. A cache without a cap grows it over days. |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA web app that fails shows an error page, and the health check catches it. A bot that fails shows nothing to anyone. Read the process state, the restart count and the alert instead. A bot is healthy when its process stays up, nothing else.\n\n## Nightly work belongs in scheduled jobs\n\nDo not keep a bot awake just to run tomorrow's task. A crash resets a sleep loop, so do not build the schedule on one. Give the schedule to [Scheduled jobs](/docs/jobs) instead. A job is a systemd timer next to your bot. It runs a command inside the current release, with its environment variables, at the times you set, in UTC. The bot stays quiet in between and needs less memory. It cannot miss a schedule it never had.\n\n## When it stops, hear about it\n\nAdd a [notification webhook](/docs/notifications) before the first bad night. A down alert says a worker “has stopped”. Recovery says it is back up. [monitoring](/docs/monitoring) explains the checks behind them, from failed checks in a row to resource limits. The Restarts count on the Service card and these alerts are the whole picture of a bot's health.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Scheduled jobs](/docs/jobs), [Logs](/docs/logs), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring).\n","github":"Automate\n\nPage 21 of 40\n\n# GitHub and previews\n\nEgg reads your code through a GitHub app that you install on chosen repositories, and builds when you push.\n\nThe app lets Egg read a repository and hear about pushes without holding your GitHub password, and you choose which repositories it can see. You need a GitHub account and a connected server, so Egg can use the app for private repositories, pushes to deploy and pull request comments. Egg clones with a short-lived token that stays on the Egg machine and is never sent to your container. You can import a public repository without the app by pasting its address, but then pushes do not deploy.\n\n## Deploying on push\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Install the app\n\n   On the New project page, the “Connect GitHub” card has **Install the GitHub App**, which opens GitHub so you can choose the account and repositories to share. If the list is empty, come back and press **I already installed it**, which signs you in to GitHub once to find your installations, so the sign-in is used once and not stored. Use **Add another account or repository** to share more later, and when you return the page says “GitHub is connected. Pick a repository below.”\n2. Step 2: Import the repository\n\n   Pick your repository under **GitHub account**, find it with **Search repositories…** and press **Import**, so the list shows the newest pushes first. Leave **Deploy automatically on push** on, as it is by default.\n3. Step 3: Push\n\n   A push to the production branch deploys to production, and a push to any other branch builds a preview, so the deployment shows the source “webhook”, the first line of the commit message (cut at 300 characters) and the author.\n4. Step 4: Open a pull request\n\n   A pull request from a branch of the same repository builds a preview too, and gets a comment. See Pull request previews below.\n\n- Branch deletions, tag pushes and other events are ignored.\n- GitHub delivers each push to `/api/webhooks/github` on Egg’s web address, so GitHub must be able to reach it. An Egg on localhost cannot receive pushes. There, press Deploy or use a deploy hook.\n- Only projects with **Deploy automatically on push** on, whose repository name matches and whose GitHub installation is one the project’s owner linked, are built.\n- The same commit on the same branch is not built twice while one for it is already queued, building or deploying.\n- Every distinct push delivery remains queued, even if it arrives after a different commit. Webhook delivery order does not prove commit order, so Egg does not cancel a queued push based on arrival time.\n- GitHub signs every delivery and Egg checks the signature, so a repeat of a signed body is answered “duplicate delivery” and not run again, using a one-way digest Egg keeps in its database. Pressing Redeliver in GitHub for a push that already worked therefore does nothing, so push a new commit or press Deploy.\n- If you rename the repository on GitHub, Egg follows the rename: the stored name and the clone address change to the new name. That needs the app to receive the Repository event.\n- A project made from a pasted public URL is not built by a push. See Projects and runtimes.\n- If you uninstall the app on GitHub, projects keep their records but lose access to private repositories until you install it again.\n\n| Permission or event | What Egg uses it for | If it is missing |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Read access to code and metadata | Listing repositories, reading files for “We found”, cloning to build. | Private repositories cannot be read. |\n| Push event | Deploying on push. | Pushes do not start builds. |\n| Pull requests: read and write, and the Pull request event | Previews for pull requests and the comment. | Previews of pushed branches still build; the comment is skipped quietly. |\n| Repository event | Following a rename. | After a rename, pushes no longer match the project. |\n| Installation event | Noticing an uninstall. | Projects keep a link to an installation that is gone. |\n\n## Pull request previews\n\n- A pull request from the same repository that is not a draft, and not from the production branch, builds a preview, so the app posts one comment when it finishes and edits that same comment when a later build of the pull request finishes. It only ever edits a comment it wrote itself.\n- The comment says “Preview ready” or “Preview failed”, with the project name, branch, short commit and a link, “Open the deployment”, that only you can open. A ready comment also gives the preview’s port and says Nest only gives it a public address once a domain is mapped to that port. A failed one says “The build log is in Egg.” It never includes error output, the server name or variable values.\n- Closing the pull request, merged or not, cancels builds still running and removes its Ready previews from your container. A build that was still running shows as Canceled with “Pull request closed”. A Ready preview is stopped and removed from the container and is marked as cleaned up.\n- Pull requests from forks and from the production branch are not built. The events handled are opened, reopened, synchronize, ready_for_review and closed.\n\nA draft pull request’s branch still builds a preview\n\nA push to the branch of a draft pull request in the same repository still builds a preview, because it comes from the push event, so only the pull request event skips drafts. No comment is written until you mark the pull request ready for review.\n\nPermissions for the comment\n\nCommenting needs Pull requests read and write access and the Pull request event, so without them previews still build and the comment is skipped quietly. Egg logs a 403 from GitHub on the server, and nothing shows on the deployment.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe app model means Egg never holds your password or a personal token, so it gets a short-lived token for one installation, cached only until a minute before it expires, and sees only what you shared. Previews run on a separate port from production, so a branch under test never replaces the live app.\n\n## Building on GitHub Actions\n\nThe setup steps, limits and troubleshooting are on [Build with GitHub Actions](/docs/build-github-actions), so this is the short version.\n\nA project can build on GitHub and send Egg the finished release instead of building on your container, and your project settings make the workflow file while the GitHub build card shows the steps. The workflow reads four credentials, and Egg shows each value once when you make the build credentials.\n\n- **Production.** `EGG_UPLOAD_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` and `EGG_ENV_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` are secrets of a GitHub Environment named `egg-production` (Settings, Environments), so limit that environment to the production branch. You can also turn on Required reviewers, so a person approves each production build.\n- **Previews.** `EGG_UPLOAD_PREVIEW_TOKEN` and `EGG_ENV_PREVIEW_TOKEN` are repository secrets.\n- **Never save the two production values as repository secrets.** A repository secret can be read by a workflow that someone edits on any branch.\n\nTreat workflow editors as trusted operators\n\nTest connection cannot see your GitHub secrets or environment settings, so check by hand that the production values are in the `egg-production` environment and not in the repository, where anyone who can edit workflows could run code with the preview credentials. A project has a single owner, and Egg does not isolate the Nest account from that.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Repositories listed | 5 pages of 100, newest push first | Repositories beyond that are not listed. Paste the address instead. In the code: server/github/app.ts |\n| Installation owner | The first Egg user to link it | “That GitHub installation is not linked to your account.” In the code: server/github/app.ts |\n| App sign-in token | 9 minutes | Renewed automatically. In the code: server/github/app.ts |\n| Comments read when updating | 100 per pull request | A comment beyond that is not found, and a new one is posted. In the code: server/github/pulls.ts |\n| Repeated deliveries | A digest of each signed body is kept in the database, with no expiry | A repeat of the same body is answered “duplicate delivery”. A push with a new commit has a new body. In the code: server/github/webhook.ts |\n| Webhook payload | 5 MB | “payload too large” In the code: app/api/webhooks/github/route.ts |\n| Pull request events handled | opened, reopened, synchronize, ready_for_review, closed | Other events are ignored. In the code: server/github/webhook.ts |\n| Commit message kept | First line, 300 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/github/webhook.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“No repositories shared with the app yet.”`\n\n  Open **Install the GitHub App** and share at least one repository.\n- `“No repositories match “QUERY”.”`\n\n  The search found nothing. Clear it, or add the repository with Add another account or repository.\n- `“The GitHub App is not configured.” or “The GitHub App is not set up on this installation.”`\n\n  This copy of Egg has no GitHub app set up. Whoever runs it sets the GITHUB_APP_* and GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET variables.\n- `“GitHub could not be connected. Try again.” or “That GitHub link expired. Try again.”`\n\n  Press I already installed it again, in one browser, within 10 minutes.\n- `“The GitHub App is no longer installed on that account.”`\n\n  Install it again on GitHub, then press I already installed it.\n- `“GitHub refused to issue an access token (STATUS).”, “GitHub did not accept the authorization (ERROR).” or “Could not list repositories (STATUS).”`\n\n  GitHub rejected the request. Try again. If it repeats, reinstall the app.\n- `“That GitHub installation is not linked to your account.”`\n\n  Another Egg account linked that installation first. Install the app yourself on your own account.\n- A push did not deploy\n\n  Check Deploy automatically on push, that the project came from the app and not a pasted URL, and that the push was to a branch and not a tag or a deletion.\n- A webhook delivery answers 401 or 503 in GitHub’s list\n\n  401 “bad signature” means the webhook secret in GitHub does not match the one Egg has. 503 “webhook not configured” means Egg has none. Whoever runs Egg sets GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET.\n- No comment on a pull request\n\n  Check that the pull request is not a draft or from a fork, and that the app has Pull requests read and write access. The deploy itself is not affected.\n\n### Example: Try a change in a pull request\n\n#### Given\n\nSam wants to try a new command in `recipe-bot` without touching the live bot.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Push a branch\n\n   Sam pushes branch `new-search`. A preview starts building.\n2. Step 2: Open a pull request\n\n   Sam opens a pull request into `main`.\n3. Step 3: Merge it\n\n   Later Sam merges and closes the pull request.\n\n#### Result\n\nA preview deployment builds on port `20000`. When it finishes the pull request gets one comment, “Preview ready”, with an Open the deployment link. When Sam closes the pull request, the preview is stopped and removed from the container and its deployment is marked as cleaned up. The merge to `main` deploys to production on its own.\n\n**Related:** [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Deploy hooks](/docs/hooks), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","hooks":"Automate\n\nPage 22 of 40\n\n# Deploy hooks\n\nA private URL that starts a deployment when it receives a POST. Use it from CI, a cron job or a script.\n\nUse a deploy hook when the GitHub app cannot help: from CI, a scheduled job, a script on your laptop or a project made from a pasted public address. The URL is the only credential, so the caller needs no access token or cookie, and the call only queues a build. That build runs on the Egg machine, so your app restarts on your container when it goes live. You need a project and a way to send an HTTP POST.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Create the hook\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Deploy Hook**, and press **Create hook**, so the card changes from “No hook yet. Create one to get a URL you can call from CI.”\n2. Step 2: Copy the URL now\n\n   The card shows the URL in a copy field and says “Copy it now. For safety it is not shown again. Anyone with this URL can deploy this project, so treat it like a password.” Egg stores only a hash, and the card also shows a ready-made `curl -X POST` line.\n3. Step 3: Call it\n\n   Send a POST, so an optional JSON body picks a branch, and any branch other than production builds a preview, while no body builds the production branch.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST https://your-egg/api/hooks/deploy/ndh_...\n   curl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"branch\":\"new-search\"}' https://your-egg/api/hooks/deploy/ndh_...\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Replace or revoke\n\n   **Make a new URL** replaces the old one, which stops working at once, and **Revoke** removes the hook, so after either a call to the old URL answers 404. When a hook exists but its URL is hidden, the card says “A hook is active and its URL is hidden. Make a new one to see a URL again; the old one stops working at once.”\n\n- A good call answers 202 with `{ \"deployment\": { \"id\", \"state\", \"branch\", \"env\" } }`, so the state is “queued” and the deployment’s source is “hook”, its author “deploy hook” and its message “Triggered by deploy hook”. Activity records “Deployed through the deploy hook for” the project.\n- The build is of the tip of the branch at the moment it runs. A hook cannot name a commit. If the branch does not exist, the deployment ends as Error with “Could not clone the repository…”.\n- The body can only hold a `branch`, with the same rules as a project branch: letters, digits and . _ - /, up to 200 characters, no leading hyphen and no “..”. Any other field is refused.\n- The URL looks like `/api/hooks/deploy/ndh_` followed by 32 characters. It does not expire. There is no sign-in, no cookie and no origin check on this route; the URL is the only secret.\n- Replacing or revoking a hook from the API needs the `deployments:write` permission.\n\nTreat it like a password\n\nAnyone with the URL can deploy that project. Do not paste it in a public repository or a chat. If it leaks, press Make a new URL.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA hook refuses a second call while a deployment is running instead of queueing it, so a script stuck in a loop cannot fill the queue, and the body holds only a branch, so a leaked URL can only build code already in your repository, never run a command or change a setting.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Hooks per project | One | Make a new URL replaces it. In the code: server/hooks.ts |\n| Running deployments | One per project at a time | 409 “A deployment is already running for this project.” In the code: server/hooks.ts |\n| Request body | 4 KB | 413 “That request is too large.” In the code: app/api/hooks/deploy/[token]/route.ts |\n| Branch name | Same rules as a project branch | 400 with the reason. In the code: server/hooks.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `404 “Unknown hook”`\n\n  The URL is wrong, was replaced, was revoked, or is cut off. A malformed token gives the same answer. Copy the current URL, or make a new one.\n- `409 “A deployment is already running for this project.”`\n\n  A deployment is Queued, Building or Deploying. Call again when it finishes.\n- `400 “Request body must be JSON”`\n\n  Send valid JSON, or no body at all.\n- `400 “Unrecognized key: \"NAME\"”`\n\n  The body holds a field other than `branch`. Remove it.\n- `400 “Branch names may use letters, digits, . _ - and /” or “Invalid branch name”`\n\n  Use a branch name that git accepts and that does not start with a hyphen.\n- `400 “Invalid input: expected string, received number” or “Too small: expected string to have >=1 characters”`\n\n  The branch must be a non-empty string.\n- `413 “That request is too large.”`\n\n  The body is over 4 KB. Send only `{ \"branch\": \"name\" }`.\n- The call returns 202 but the deployment ends as Error\n\n  The call only queues the build. Open the deployment to read why it failed.\n- `“Could not create the hook” or “Could not revoke the hook”`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try again.\n\n## Example: Redeploy every night\n\n### Given\n\nSam wants `recipe-bot` to redeploy every night so it picks up a data file fetched at build time.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Create the hook\n\n   Sam creates the hook and copies the URL.\n2. Step 2: Store it\n\n   Sam stores the URL as a secret in a cron service.\n3. Step 3: Schedule the call\n\n   Sam runs the `curl -X POST` line at 03:00.\n\n### Result\n\nEach night a deployment appears in the list with source “hook”. If a deployment is already running at that time, the call answers 409 and nothing breaks; the next night’s call works.\n\n**Related:** [GitHub and previews](/docs/github), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","jobs":"Automate\n\nPage 23 of 40\n\n# Scheduled jobs\n\nA command that runs on a schedule inside your current release, such as a nightly clean-up or a weekly report.\n\nA scheduled job is a command and a time pattern, so Egg turns each job into a systemd timer on your container. systemd is the Linux service manager, and a timer starts something at set times. Your command runs in the folder of your current release, the deployed version of your app that is live now, and gets the same environment variables as your app, with the project’s virtual environment first on the path for Python. You need a project that has been deployed once, because a job has nothing to run in before that.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Open the card\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Scheduled Jobs**, so the card says “No jobs yet.” when you have no jobs.\n2. Step 2: Fill in the form\n\n   Under **Add a job**, type a **Name**, a **Schedule** and a **Command**, or use the **Common schedules** list to fill in the schedule for you. A line under it says what the schedule means and when it next runs in UTC, or why it is not valid.\n3. Step 3: Press Add job\n\n   The card says “Added. It is armed on your container now.” because Egg saves the timer on your container before it keeps its record, so a job you can see on the card is installed.\n4. Step 4: Check how it went\n\n   Each row shows the schedule in words, the next run and the last result: “Has not run yet”, “Running now”, “Succeeded” or “Failed (exit N)”, with how long ago. Egg reads your container when the card opens and every 30 seconds while the page is visible, so the row stays current.\n\n- Your name is 1 to 60 characters: letters, numbers, spaces, dots, dashes and underscores, starting with a letter or number. The command is one line of up to 500 characters, so for anything longer, put a script in your repository and run it from the command.\n- Egg runs the command with `bash -lc` from the current release folder, so a relative path such as `node scripts/cleanup.js` works. A deploy replaces the current release, so the next run uses the new code.\n- Job names and commands are shown on the card in plain text. Keep secrets in environment variables, not in the command.\n\n## Schedule format\n\nA schedule is five fields separated by spaces: minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, the form cron uses. Cron is the classic Unix scheduler, and all times are UTC, so a job does not depend on your container’s time zone.\n\n| Field | Values |\n| --- | --- |\n| Minute | 0 to 59 |\n| Hour | 0 to 23 |\n| Day of month | 1 to 31 |\n| Month | 1 to 12 |\n| Day of week | 0 to 7. Both 0 and 7 are Sunday, and 1 is Monday. |\n\nEach field takes `*` for every value, a number, a list such as `1,15`, a range such as `9-17` or a step such as `*/15` or `0-30/10`. Four nicknames are also accepted in any letter case: `@hourly`, `@daily` (and `@midnight`), `@weekly` and `@monthly`, so Egg saves `@daily` as its five fields, `0 0 * * *`.\n\n| Schedule | When it runs |\n| --- | --- |\n| `*/15 * * * *` | Every 15 minutes |\n| `0 * * * *` | Every hour, on the hour. Same as @hourly. |\n| `0 */6 * * *` | Every 6 hours, at minute 0 |\n| `30 3 * * *` | Every day at 03:30 UTC |\n| `0 9 * * 1-5` | Monday to Friday at 09:00 UTC |\n| `0 9 * * 1` | Every Monday at 09:00 UTC |\n| `0 4 1 * *` | On the 1st of each month at 04:00 UTC |\n\n| Schedule | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| `0 9 * * MON` | Names such as MON or JAN are not accepted. Use numbers. |\n| `0 0 L * *` | Cron extras such as ?, L and W are not accepted. |\n| `0 0 * * * *` | Six fields, with seconds, are not accepted. There must be exactly five. |\n| `@yearly` | Only @hourly, @daily, @midnight, @weekly and @monthly are accepted. |\n| `0 9 1 * 1` | Both a day of the month and a day of the week are set. Set only one and leave the other as *. |\n| `*/2 * * * *` | It would run every 2 minutes. A job runs at most once every 5 minutes. |\n\n## How a job behaves\n\n- **It gives way to your app.** A job runs at a lower priority than your app, so if your container runs out of memory, Egg stops the job before the app.\n- **Missed runs are not made up.** If your container was off or restarting at the scheduled time, the job waits for its next time, so it does not run a backlog.\n- **It does not overlap itself.** If a run is still going when the next time arrives, Egg skips that time, so a second copy never starts.\n- **It is stopped after 30 minutes.** Egg ends a run that is still going, so the last result reads Failed.\n- **A run can start a few seconds late.** The timer is accurate to within 10 seconds.\n- **It goes when the project goes.** Deleting a project with “Also stop the service and delete releases” ticked also removes its timers from the container. Egg only removes timers that it made.\n- **A job needs a release.** Egg refuses to add or change a job before the project has been deployed once. Deploy the project, then add the job.\n\n## Actions on a job\n\n| Control | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Run now** | Starts the job once, straight away, without waiting for it to finish. The card says “Started NAME. Open its output to see what it printed.” It works on a paused job too. |\n| **Pause** | Stops the timer, so the job no longer runs on its own. The row reads “Paused”. The button changes to Resume. |\n| **Resume** | Arms the timer again. |\n| **Edit** | The pencil. Opens the name, schedule and command in the row. Save puts the change on your container, and Cancel or the Escape key closes it without saving. |\n| **Output** | The terminal icon. Shows what the job printed on its last runs, up to the latest 120 lines, or “It has not printed anything yet.” |\n| **Remove** | The bin. Asks “Remove NAME?” and, on Remove, stops the job and deletes it from your container. |\n\n## Dashboard routes\n\nThe card uses these routes, which answer only to your signed-in browser, so they are not part of the public `/api/v1` API and an access token cannot call them. `:id` is the project id and `:jobId` the job id.\n\n| Route | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/projects/:id/jobs` | Lists the jobs. With ?status=1 it also reads each job’s last result from your container. |\n| `POST /api/projects/:id/jobs` | Adds a job. The body holds name, schedule and command, and may hold enabled. Answers 201. |\n| `PATCH /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId` | Changes any of name, schedule, command and enabled. |\n| `DELETE /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId` | Removes the job from your container and from Egg. |\n| `POST /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId/run` | Starts the job once. Answers 202. |\n| `GET /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId/logs` | Returns the job’s recent output as a list of lines. |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nCron reads a schedule with both a day of the month and a day of the week as “either”, while systemd reads it as “both”, so Egg refuses the mix instead of guessing which one you meant. The 5 minute floor, the low priority and the 30 minute stop exist because your container is small and shared with your app, so a job does not starve it. Egg skips missed runs, so a restart does not trigger a burst of old work.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Jobs per project | 5 | 409 “A project can have 5 jobs. Remove one to add another.” In the code: server/scheduled-jobs.ts MAX_JOBS_PER_PROJECT |\n| Time between runs | At least 5 minutes | The schedule is refused with “Jobs run at most once every 5 minutes so they do not starve your app.” In the code: lib/cron-schedule.ts MIN_GAP_MINUTES |\n| Run time | 30 minutes | systemd stops the run and the last result reads Failed. In the code: server/deploy/jobs-remote.ts JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS |\n| Name | 1 to 60 characters | The form says which characters are allowed. In the code: server/deploy/jobs-remote.ts JOB_NAME |\n| Command | 500 characters, one line | “Keep the command under 500 characters. Put longer scripts in a file in your repository.” In the code: server/scheduled-jobs.ts jobInput |\n| Schedule | 100 characters | “That schedule is too long.” In the code: lib/cron-schedule.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `409 “Deploy the project once first. A job runs inside the current release, and there is none yet.”`\n\n  Deploy the project, then add the job.\n- `“Set either the day of the month or the day of the week, not both.”`\n\n  Leave one of the two fields as `*`.\n- `“That would run every N minutes.”`\n\n  Choose a schedule with at least 5 minutes between runs.\n- `409 “A project can have 5 jobs. Remove one to add another.”`\n\n  Remove a job you no longer need.\n- `409 “A service with that name already exists on your container and was not made by Egg, so it was left alone.”`\n\n  Egg never replaces a unit it did not write. Remove or rename the unit on your container yourself.\n- `“Not on your container. Pause and resume it to put it back.”`\n\n  The timer is gone from the container, for example after someone removed it. Pause the job and press Resume to install it again. If Run now answers “That job is not on your container any more”, save the job again.\n- `504 “Your container did not answer in time.” or 502 “Could not reach your container.”`\n\n  The container may be stopped or starting. Try again in a minute. The list still shows what Egg saved.\n- The job shows Failed (exit N)\n\n  Open the output to read what it printed. Run the same command in the Terminal from the current release folder to see the error.\n\n## Example: Clean up old uploads every night\n\n### Given\n\nSam’s `recipe-bot` is deployed and has a script `scripts/cleanup.js` that deletes old uploads.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Fill in the form\n\n   Sam types the name `Nightly clean-up`, picks “Every day at 03:00” from Common schedules and types `node scripts/cleanup.js`.\n2. Step 2: Add the job\n\n   Sam presses Add job and sees “Added. It is armed on your container now.”\n3. Step 3: Try it\n\n   Sam presses Run now, waits a moment, then opens the output.\n\n### Result\n\nThe row reads “Every day at 03:00 UTC” with the next run time, and the output shows what the script printed. After a deploy, the next night’s run uses the new code.\n\n**Related:** [Deploy hooks](/docs/hooks), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Limits](/docs/limits).\n","notifications":"Automate\n\nPage 24 of 40\n\n# Notifications\n\nMessages to a Discord or Slack channel from an incoming webhook, when a deployment finishes or an alert fires.\n\nA notification tells you a deployment finished, so nobody has to watch the dashboard. Each project has one webhook address, and every finished deployment, Ready or Error, can post a short message there, sent by Egg from the Egg machine to Discord or Slack. Nothing runs on your container. You need an incoming webhook URL from Discord or Slack, which also carries the alerts described under Monitoring and uptime.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Make the webhook\n\n   In Discord, make a webhook for a channel, or in Slack, make an incoming webhook, then copy its URL.\n2. Step 2: Save it\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Notifications**. Paste the **Webhook URL**, choose **Send**(Every deployment, or Failures only) and press **Save**, so the page says “Saved. Send a test message to check it.” The Save button stays off while the URL box is empty, so to change only **Send** later, paste the URL again.\n3. Step 3: Send a test\n\n   Press **Send test message**, so Egg posts “Test from Egg for PROJECT” and the card says “Sent. Check the channel.” The button appears once you save a URL.\n4. Step 4: Remove it\n\n   Press **Remove** to delete the saved URL, so messages stop at once.\n\n- Egg accepts https only, with no port, query, fragment or credentials, so Discord hosts are `discord.com`, `discordapp.com` and both with a `ptb.` or `canary.` prefix, on a path `/api/webhooks/ID/TOKEN` where ID is digits. Slack is `hooks.slack.com` on a path `/services/X/Y/Z`.\n- The URL is stored encrypted and never shown again. The card says “Sending to Discord for every finished deployment. The URL is hidden. Paste a new one to replace it.” (or “for failed deployments only”, or “Slack”). Saving a new URL replaces the old one.\n- A message reads “PROJECT is live in production” (or “a preview”) with the branch, short commit, the commit message in quotes up to 120 characters and the build time, or “PROJECT failed to deploy to production” (or “a preview”) with the branch, short commit and the error, up to 200 characters. The last line is a link to the deployment. Discord messages are sent so that they cannot ping anyone.\n- Failures only hides the “is live” messages. Canceled deployments never send. A failed post is only logged and never changes the deployment.\n- The Alerts card on the same page can also turn off “Deployment results”, and pause every alert for a while. Both stop these messages too. The deployment still appears in the alert history.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg accepts only Discord and Slack addresses of the exact shape those services use, and refuses redirects, so a saved address cannot send Egg’s request somewhere else, and a failed post is dropped rather than retried, because a broken channel must never hold up or fail a deployment.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Webhook per project | One | Saving a new URL replaces it. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| URL length | 500 characters | The server refuses it. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| Time to post | 5 seconds | The post is dropped and logged; redirects are refused. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| Commit message in a message | 120 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| Error text in a message | 200 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“That is not a valid URL.”`\n\n  Paste the full address, starting with https://.\n- `“Use the plain https webhook URL from Discord or Slack.”`\n\n  Remove any port, query string, or fragment from the URL.\n- `“Only Discord and Slack incoming webhook URLs are supported.”`\n\n  Check the host and path against the list above.\n- `“Add a webhook URL first.”`\n\n  Save a URL before pressing Send test message.\n- `“Discord answered STATUS.” or “Slack answered STATUS.”`\n\n  The service refused the post. The webhook may be deleted or the channel gone. Make a new webhook.\n- `“Could not save”, “Could not send” or “Could not remove”`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try again.\n- Messages stop arriving\n\n  Check the Alerts card: the “Deployment results” channel and an active pause also stop deployment messages. The events still appear in the history. Then check Send is not set to Failures only.\n\n## Example: Hear about failures in Discord\n\n### Given\n\nSam has a private Discord server with a `#bot-deploys` channel.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Make a webhook\n\n   Sam makes a webhook in the `#bot-deploys` channel.\n2. Step 2: Save it\n\n   Sam pastes it into Notifications, chooses Failures only and presses Save.\n3. Step 3: Send a test\n\n   Sam presses Send test message.\n\n### Result\n\nThe channel shows “Test from Egg for recipe-bot”. From now on it only hears about failed deployments, and about alerts if those are on.\n\n## Signed webhooks for your own code\n\nDiscord and Slack are for people, so to tell a program, use **Webhooks** in Account. Egg sends a signed JSON message to an https address when a deployment starts, succeeds or fails, covering every project on your account.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Add the address\n\n   Open Account, find **Webhooks**, paste the address under **Add a webhook address** and press **Add webhook**, so the button reads “Adding…” while it works.\n2. Step 2: Copy the secret\n\n   The card shows the signing secret once, starting with `whsec_`, under “Signing secret for HOST”, so copy it before you press **I have copied it**. Egg cannot show it again.\n3. Step 3: Send a test\n\n   Press **Send test** on the new row. The toast says “Test message delivered”, and the row lists the delivery under **Recent deliveries**. A test is one attempt, with no retries, and carries `\"test\": true`.\n4. Step 4: Check the signature in your code\n\n   Every request has an `X-Egg-Signature` header of the form `t=TIME,v1=HEX`, so the value is an HMAC-SHA256 of `TIME.BODY` keyed with your secret. Refuse a request that does not match, or whose time is more than 300 seconds from yours.\n\n   Node.js\n\n   ```\n   import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from \"node:crypto\";\n\n   export function isFromEgg(secret, header, body) {\n     const m = /^t=(\\d+),v1=([0-9a-f]{64})$/.exec(header ?? \"\");\n     if (!m || Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - Number(m[1])) > 300) return false;\n     const want = createHmac(\"sha256\", secret).update(m[1] + \".\" + body).digest();\n     return timingSafeEqual(want, Buffer.from(m[2], \"hex\"));\n   }\n   ```\n5. Step 5: Pause, replace or delete\n\n   **Pause** (then **Resume**) stops messages without deleting the webhook. **New secret** opens “Replace the secret?”, and **Replace secret** makes the old secret stop working at once and shows the new one once. **Delete** removes the webhook and its history.\n\nThe body is JSON with `event` (`deployment.started`, `deployment.succeeded`, `deployment.failed` or `webhook.test`), `project` with its id and slug, `deployment` with its id, branch, short commit and state, and `time` as an ISO date, so it never carries variables, logs, commit messages, errors or links. The headers `X-Egg-Event` and `X-Egg-Delivery` name the event and this delivery. A canceled deployment sends nothing, and the started message goes out when the build begins.\n\n### Limits\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Webhooks per account | 10 | “You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.” In the code: server/webhooks.ts |\n| Address | https, standard port, a full domain name, no credentials, no #, 500 characters at most | The form says what to change, for example “The URL must start with https://.” or “Use a domain name, not an IP address.” In the code: server/webhook-net.ts |\n| Where it can point | A public address | A private or internal address is refused when the message is sent, as “points at a private address”. In the code: server/webhook-net.ts |\n| Time to answer | 5 seconds | The attempt counts as failed with “timed out after 5 seconds”. Redirects are not followed. In the code: server/webhook-net.ts |\n| Attempts | 3, with pauses of 2 and 10 seconds | A failed delivery is listed with a short reason, such as “receiver answered 500” or “could not connect”. In the code: server/webhooks.ts |\n| Signature age | 300 seconds | Your code should refuse an older timestamp. In the code: server/webhook-sign.ts |\n| Delivery history | 30 days | Older rows are removed. In the code: server/webhooks.ts |\n\n**Related:** [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Deploy hooks](/docs/hooks), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","api":"Automate\n\nPage 25 of 40\n\n# Access tokens and the API\n\nMake a token, give it only the permissions you need, and call Egg’s API from a script or an AI agent.\n\nYou make tokens on the Developer page. A token is a bearer secret that starts with `egg_`; send it in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. You call the JSON API under `/api/v1`, you point an agent at `/api/mcp`, and you read the descriptions at `/api/v1/openapi.json` and `/api/v1/agent-spec.json`. The agent spec is a short list of operations built from the same document as the OpenAPI reference, so the two cannot disagree on names. Your token can do nothing its permissions do not name (the API and its code call them scopes), and a few things it can never do at all. Egg runs every call on Egg’s machine. The calls marked “Container session” in the table below also open an SSH connection to your container, so they use a little of its CPU and Egg limits them to 10 a minute for each token.\n\nKeep your token out of public code\n\nAnyone who reads a token you publish can act as you until you revoke it.\n\n## Making a token\n\n### Make a token\n\n1. Step 1: Open Developer\n\n   Open Developer and press Create token. The New token dialog opens.\n\n   In the dashboard: Developerthen Access tokensthen Create token\n2. Step 2: Name it\n\n   Type 1 to 60 characters. Pick a name you will recognise later: Activity shows it for everything the token does.\n3. Step 3: Tick Permissions\n\n   Tick only what you need. Read only ticks every read permission. Deploy ticks projects:read, deployments:read, deployments:write and logs:read.\n4. Step 4: Choose Expires after\n\n   Pick 7, 30, 90 or 365 days; 90 is the default. If you tick a terminal or file permission, you can pick only 7 days.\n5. Step 5: Copy the secret\n\n   Copy it when the dialog says “Copy it now. It is shown once.” Keep it in a secret store, not in code.\n\nCopy the secret before you close the dialog\n\nEgg stores only a hash of it, so no one can show you the secret again.\n\nThe list groups tokens as Active, Expired and Revoked, and flags an active token that ends within 7 days. Each shows when it was last used, at most once an hour, or “Never”. Each active token has Edit and Revoke. Edit changes the name, the permissions and the expiry, counted from now, and takes effect on the next request. Revoke asks you to confirm with “Revoke token”: anything using the token stops working at once and any terminal it opened closes. A revoked token is final. Dropping the deploy permission warns that deploys will stop working for anything that uses the token.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe secret is shown once because only its SHA-256 is stored, so it cannot be shown again, even to Egg. An expired, revoked or unknown token is rejected with the same message so nobody can use the API to find out which tokens once existed. Names matter because Activity shows them: a log that says “a token” cannot tell you which script did it.\n\n## What a token can do\n\n| Permission | What it allows | Calls |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `projects:read` | Read projects, their settings and status page. | list_projects, get_project, get_status_page |\n| `projects:write` | Create, change and delete projects and services, including build and start commands. | create_project, update_project, delete_project, delete_service, deploy_from_github_url |\n| `deployments:read` | Read deployments, build output and deployment metrics. | list_deployments, get_deployment, get_deployment_logs, get_deployment_metrics |\n| `deployments:write` | Deploy, redeploy, cancel and roll back, and replace or revoke the deploy hook. | deploy_branch, deploy_from_github_url, redeploy, cancel_deployment, rollback_deployment, rotate_deploy_hook, revoke_deploy_hook |\n| `logs:read` | Read runtime logs and crash reports. | get_logs, list_crash_reports, get_crash_report |\n| `env:read` | List variable names, and read the values of variables marked non-secret. | list_env_vars, get_env_vars |\n| `env:write` | Set and remove variables, and change whether a variable is secret. | set_env_var, delete_env_var, set_env_var_secret |\n| `domains:read` | Read domains. | list_domains |\n| `domains:write` | Add, check and remove domains. | add_domain, verify_domain, remove_domain |\n| `notifications:read` | Read notification settings and send a test message. | get_notifications, test_notification |\n| `alerts:read` | Read alert settings and history. | get_alerts |\n| `analytics:read` | Read visitor analytics. | get_analytics |\n| `activity:read` | Read your activity log and a project’s change feed. | get_activity, get_changes |\n| `resources:read` | Read CPU, memory and disk, now and over time, and a project’s resource limits and usage. | get_server_usage, get_resource_history, get_project_resources, get_resource_quota |\n| `container:read` | List servers and inspect one project’s containers and health. Read only. | list_servers, get_container_inventory, list_project_containers, get_project_container, get_project_health |\n| `files:read` | List and read files in a project’s folder. | list_files, read_file, download_file |\n| `files:write` | Create, change, rename and delete files in a project’s shared/ folder. | write_file, make_folder, rename_file, delete_file |\n| `terminal:use` | Open and use a shell on the container. | terminal_status, terminal_open, terminal_send, terminal_read, terminal_resize, terminal_close |\n| `webhooks:read` | Read your webhooks and their recent deliveries, never their secrets. | GET /webhooks routes |\n| `webhooks:write` | Create, change, delete and test webhooks, and replace a webhook secret. | POST, PATCH, DELETE /webhooks routes |\n\nYou get write permissions for projects, deployments, variables, domains, files and webhooks, and `terminal:use` lets you run commands. Notifications, alerts, analytics, resources, activity, the container and logs are read only, and no permission changes notification or alert settings: those stay in the browser. You can call `test_notification` with only `notifications:read`, but it posts a real message to your channel. Send it only when you ask for it, and tell an agent to do the same.\n\n## Terminal, files and the 7-day rule\n\nYou switch terminal and file changes on for a server before a token can use them, and Egg asks you to sign in with Hack Club again when you do. A token reaches them only while that switch is on. A token holding `terminal:use` or `files:write` expires within 7 days, whatever length you picked, and the form says so as you tick the permission. Send no expiry and it gets 7 days. Adding either permission to a longer-lived token is refused unless the same edit sets the expiry to 7 days.\n\nSome things stay with you in the browser. You cannot use a token to remove leftovers, change the container permissions, manage your account or sessions, or create other tokens. Files in `current/` stay read only, and a release’s `.env` is never returned.\n\nA leaked terminal token is short lived\n\nWhoever holds it can run commands on your container until its 7 days end or you switch the terminal off.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA terminal can run any command and a file change can alter the code your app runs, so a leaked token with either is the worst case. Capping its life at 7 days, and tying it to a switch you turned on with a recent Hack Club sign-in, keeps that case short. The token does not sign in again: it can use the terminal until its 7 days end or you switch the terminal off.\n\n## The API and agents\n\nYou call the REST API under `/api/v1` with your token as a bearer header. Read the full reference at `/api/v1/openapi.json`, or fetch the shorter `/api/v1/agent-spec.json` when you want only the operation ids, methods, paths and parameters. Start with `/api/v1/me`: it needs no permission, so any valid token can call it. It returns your token’s name, permissions and expiry and how many servers and projects you have, and never an email address.\n\n- **Requests.** Send JSON. If a body field the call does not know is present, Egg refuses it with an “Unknown field” message that lists the allowed ones, so a typo cannot silently do nothing. A value in the path wins over a body field of the same name.\n- **Answers.** You get `200`, or `201` for something new, or `202` for a deployment that is queued and will finish later. An error is always `{ \"error\": { \"code\", \"message\" } }`. Authenticated answers are never cached.\n- **Secrets are write-only.** Variable values marked secret, notification URLs and token secrets are never returned. A variable value comes back only from `get_env_vars`, and only when it is marked non-secret. A deploy hook URL comes back once, from the call that makes it.\n- **Cross-origin use.** The API allows any origin and never reads cookies, so a page on another site can call it only with a token it already holds. Keep your token out of a public page.\n\nYou point an agent at `/api/mcp` instead. It speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 over POST: one message per request, no sessions and no batches. It takes the same bearer token and never reads cookies. Each tool is one API call, with the same scopes, limits and rules, and no way to reach leftover removal, permission switches, your account or token management. It answers `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` and `tools/call`. A tool that fails comes back as a result with `isError` set and text that starts with the error code. A `GET` or `DELETE` to the address gets a 405.\n\n### Connect an agent\n\n1. Step 1: Make a token\n\n   Give your agent only the scopes it needs, and keep the secret in a store it can read. Use the steps above if you have not made a token yet.\n2. Step 2: Point the client at the agent endpoint\n\n   Use an HTTP MCP client with one line of configuration: the address, the token in an Authorization header, and no other settings.\n\n   MCP client config\n\n   ```\n   {\n     \"mcpServers\": {\n       \"egg\": {\n         \"type\": \"http\",\n         \"url\": \"https://your-egg/api/mcp\",\n         \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer egg_...\" }\n       }\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Call get_me first\n\n   It needs no permission. The answer tells your agent which scopes the token has and how many projects and servers you have, so it can plan its next calls.\n4. Step 4: Narrow the tool list if you want to\n\n   Add ?toolsets= or ?scope= to the address, as described below, to cut down what the agent sees. Leave both out to see every tool your token allows.\n5. Step 5: Use the compact server when context is tight\n\n   Point the client at /api/mcp/compact instead. It offers three tools instead of one per operation, with the same token, scopes and limits.\n\n### Narrow the tools your agent sees\n\nYou narrow the list with two query parameters, on `/api/mcp` and on `/api/mcp/compact`. Set `?toolsets=` to a comma-separated list such as `toolsets=core,projects,deployments`. The `core` group is always included, and leaving the parameter out shows every group. Set `?scope=read` to keep only tools that change nothing, or `?scope=deploy` to add deployment changes and nothing else. Your token’s own scopes still apply, and the server checks them again on every call: narrowing can only hide tools, never add ones your token could not already run.\n\nA mistyped toolset or scope is refused\n\nEgg answers 400 with the list of valid names instead of quietly showing a different set of tools.\n\n### Use the compact server\n\nYou use `/api/mcp/compact` when the client has a small context window. It answers the same `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` and `tools/call` methods but offers three tools: `search_api` finds operations by keyword, `describe_operation` returns one operation’s schema, and `call_api` runs it. A destructive operation does not run until you send its id again as `confirm`. Each result is capped at 24,000 characters.\n\nA destructive call waits for your confirmation\n\nWithout `confirm` set to the operation’s own id, the call changes nothing on Egg.\n\nYou see deploys, redeploys, rollbacks, cancels and file or terminal actions made with a token in [Activity](/docs/activity), named for the token that made them.\n\n| Tool | Method and path | Permission | Allowance |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `get_me` | `GET /me` | None | Reads |\n| `list_projects` | `GET /projects` | `projects:read` | Reads |\n| `get_project` | `GET /projects/{projectId}` | `projects:read` | Reads |\n| `create_project` | `POST /projects` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `update_project` | `PATCH /projects/{projectId}` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `delete_project` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `delete_service` | `DELETE /services/{serviceId}` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `get_status_page` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/status-page` | `projects:read` | Reads |\n| `list_deployments` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/deployments` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `deploy_branch` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/deployments` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `get_deployment` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `get_rollout` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}/rollout` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `get_deployment_logs` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}/logs` | `deployments:read` | Container session |\n| `get_deployment_metrics` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}/metrics` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `deploy_from_github_url` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/deploy-from-github-url` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `redeploy` | `POST /deployments/{deploymentId}/redeploy` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `cancel_deployment` | `POST /deployments/{deploymentId}/cancel` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `rollback_deployment` | `POST /deployments/{deploymentId}/rollback` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `rotate_deploy_hook` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/hook/rotate` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `revoke_deploy_hook` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/hook` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `get_logs` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/logs` | `logs:read` | Reads |\n| `list_crash_reports` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/crash-reports` | `logs:read` | Reads |\n| `get_crash_report` | `GET /crash-reports/{crashId}` | `logs:read` | Reads |\n| `list_env_vars` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/env` | `env:read` | Reads |\n| `get_env_vars` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/env/values` | `env:read` | Reads |\n| `set_env_var` | `PUT /projects/{projectId}/env/{key}` | `env:write` | Changes |\n| `set_env_var_secret` | `PATCH /projects/{projectId}/env/{key}/{target}/secret` | `env:write` | Changes |\n| `delete_env_var` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/env/{key}` | `env:write` | Changes |\n| `list_domains` | `GET /domains` | `domains:read` | Reads |\n| `add_domain` | `POST /domains` | `domains:write` | Changes |\n| `verify_domain` | `POST /domains/{domainId}/verify` | `domains:write` | Changes |\n| `remove_domain` | `DELETE /domains/{domainId}` | `domains:write` | Changes |\n| `get_notifications` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/notify` | `notifications:read` | Reads |\n| `test_notification` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/notify/test` | `notifications:read` | Changes |\n| `get_alerts` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/alerts` | `alerts:read` | Reads |\n| `get_analytics` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/analytics` | `analytics:read` | Reads |\n| `get_activity` | `GET /activity` | `activity:read` | Reads |\n| `get_changes` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/changes` | `activity:read` | Reads |\n| `get_server_usage` | `GET /servers/{serverId}/usage` | `resources:read` | Reads |\n| `get_resource_history` | `GET /servers/{serverId}/usage/history` | `resources:read` | Container session |\n| `get_project_resources` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/resources` | `resources:read` | Container session |\n| `get_resource_quota` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/resources/quota` | `resources:read` | Container session |\n| `list_servers` | `GET /servers` | `container:read` | Reads |\n| `get_container_inventory` | `GET /servers/{serverId}/inventory` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `list_project_containers` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/containers` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `get_project_container` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/containers/{containerId}` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `get_project_health` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/health` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `list_files` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/files` | `files:read` | Container session |\n| `read_file` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/files/content` | `files:read` | Container session |\n| `download_file` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/files/download` | `files:read` | Container session |\n| `write_file` | `PUT /projects/{projectId}/files/content` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `make_folder` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/files/folders` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `rename_file` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/files/rename` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `delete_file` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/files` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `terminal_status` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/terminal` | `terminal:use` | Reads |\n| `terminal_open` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/terminal` | `terminal:use` | Container session |\n| `terminal_send` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}/input` | `terminal:use` | Terminal input |\n| `terminal_read` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}/output` | `terminal:use` | Reads |\n| `terminal_resize` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}/resize` | `terminal:use` | Changes |\n| `terminal_close` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}` | `terminal:use` | Changes |\n\nYou reach webhooks as plain routes under `/api/v1/webhooks` rather than tools: `GET` and `POST` on `/webhooks`, `GET`, `PATCH` and `DELETE` on `/webhooks/{webhookId}`, and `POST` on `/webhooks/{webhookId}/rotate` and `/webhooks/{webhookId}/test`. To create one you send `url`, an optional `events` list of `deployment.started`, `deployment.succeeded` and `deployment.failed`, and an optional `projectId`. The answer carries the signing secret once. The body is 4 KB at most, and the test call uses the container-session allowance. Webhooks are not in the OpenAPI description or the agent tool list. How your receiver checks the `X-Egg-Signature` header, what a delivery carries and how failed deliveries are tried again are on the [Notifications](/docs/notifications) page.\n\n## Examples and the TypeScript client\n\nYou can start from the ready-made examples in the repository; each says which permissions it needs. Every example uses a placeholder like `egg_...`, so keep your real token in an environment variable or a secret store. The TypeScript client is one file with no dependencies: copy it into your project and call its typed methods.\n\n- [TypeScript client](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/web/src/lib/sdk/egg.ts). One file, no dependencies. Typed methods for projects, deployments, logs, domains, environment names, analytics, usage, alerts and the status page. Runs on Node 20 and in the browser. Depends on the method. Each one names its permission.\n- [Node script](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/node/tail-logs.mjs). Lists your projects and follows a build log until it finishes. Needs projects:read and deployments:read.\n- [Python script](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/python/tail_logs.py). The same, with only the standard library. Needs projects:read and deployments:read.\n- [Static dashboard](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/dashboard/index.html). One HTML file. Paste a token and see your projects and page views. The token stays in the page's memory and is never saved. Needs projects:read and analytics:read.\n- [GitHub Actions workflow](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/github-actions/deploy-on-release.yml). Deploys when you publish a release, through a deploy hook. No token. It uses the project's deploy hook URL.\n- [MCP config](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/tree/main/examples/mcp). Connect Claude Desktop, Cursor or Claude Code to your projects. Whatever you want the agent to do. The first call, get_me, needs none.\n- [Egg command line](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/developers/cli.md) (the script is [egg-cli.mjs](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/web/scripts/egg-cli.mjs)) covers 153 commands for projects, deployments, environments, jobs, services, domains, notifications, observability, GitHub, organizations, context, and local Egg information. For example, run `egg whoami`, `egg projects list`, `egg deploy create <project>`, `egg deploy logs <deployment-id> --follow`, `egg env names <project>`, `egg domains list`, or `egg status`. Remote commands read `EGG_TOKEN` and `EGG_URL` from the environment and never print the token; offline context and information commands do not use a token. Destructive commands require exact confirmation, and a mismatch exits 2. Exit codes are 0 for done, 1 for other failures, 2 for wrong use, 3 for authentication, 4 for not found, 6 for rate limits, 7 for server errors, and 8 for timeouts. The CLI still lacks alert metric rules, status-page management, environment pull, several observability commands, Compose stack creation, and other operations listed in its command reference.\n\n### Common calls\n\n1. Step 1: Check the token\n\n   It needs no permission. The answer shows what your token may do and when it ends.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" https://your-egg/api/v1/me\n   ```\n2. Step 2: List projects and find an id\n\n   You need `projects:read`.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" https://your-egg/api/v1/projects\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Deploy a branch\n\n   You need `deployments:write`. Leave the body out to deploy the production branch; any other branch becomes a preview. The answer is 202 with the queued deployment. The build then runs on your container, and a production deploy restarts the app on your container.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"branch\":\"pricing-page\"}' \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/deployments\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Give each write a key\n\n   Send Idempotency-Key with a fresh value on every write. Use a new UUID for each action you want to happen once. The key belongs to your token, and Egg keeps the answer for 24 hours. A repeat with the same key and the same request returns the kept answer with Idempotent-Replayed: true. A different request with that key gets 422.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     -H \"Idempotency-Key: 7f1c2e6a-4b5d-4c8e-9a1f-3d2b0e5c7a90\" \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/deployments\n   ```\n5. Step 5: Watch it finish\n\n   You need `deployments:read`. A 202 means the deploy is queued, not live. Call the rollout endpoint with `wait=1`: Egg reads the state every 2 seconds for up to 60 seconds and returns early at ready, error or canceled. The answer says `done` and `waitedMs`. If `done` is false, call again.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     \"https://your-egg/api/v1/deployments/DEPLOYMENT_ID/rollout?wait=1\"\n   ```\n6. Step 6: Read why it failed\n\n   You need `deployments:read`. Pass the last line’s id as `after` to continue. It uses the container-session allowance of 10 a minute.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     \"https://your-egg/api/v1/deployments/DEPLOYMENT_ID/logs?after=0&limit=200\"\n   ```\n7. Step 7: Set a variable\n\n   You need `env:write`. `target` is `all`, `production` or `preview`. The value reaches the app at the next deploy. Setting a value never changes its secret flag, and the value comes back only from `get_env_vars`, when the variable is marked non-secret.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X PUT -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"value\":\"sk_test_123\",\"target\":\"production\"}' \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/env/STRIPE_KEY\n   ```\n8. Step 8: Roll back\n\n   You need `deployments:write`. Pass the id of an earlier production deployment whose release is still on your container. Nothing is rebuilt: the service restarts on that older release, so the app is unavailable for a moment.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/deployments/DEPLOYMENT_ID/rollback\n   ```\n9. Step 9: Wait out a 429\n\n   A rate-limited call answers 429 with a Retry-After header. Wait that many seconds, then repeat the call.\n\n   Node.js\n\n   ```\n   async function call(path, init) {\n     const res = await fetch(\"https://your-egg/api/v1\" + path, {\n       ...init,\n       headers: { Authorization: \"Bearer \" + process.env.NEST_TOKEN, ...init?.headers },\n     });\n     if (res.status === 429) {\n       await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, Number(res.headers.get(\"retry-after\") ?? 5) * 1000));\n       return call(path, init);\n     }\n     const body = await res.json();\n     if (!res.ok) throw new Error(body.error.code + \": \" + body.error.message);\n     return body;\n   }\n   ```\n10. Step 10: Use the typed client\n\n    Copy the one file into your project. Each method names its permission and throws an error that carries the status, the code and Retry-After.\n\n    TypeScript\n\n    ```\n    import { Egg } from \"./egg\";\n\n    const nest = new Egg({ token: process.env.NEST_TOKEN!, baseUrl: \"https://your-egg\" });\n\n    const me = await nest.me();\n    console.log(me.token.scopes);\n\n    const { projects } = await nest.listProjects();\n    for (const p of projects) console.log(p.name, p.state);\n    ```\n11. Step 11: Call a tool over MCP\n\n    You send one JSON-RPC message per POST. Start with get_me, and add ?toolsets= or ?scope= to the address to narrow the tool list.\n\n    Terminal\n\n    ```\n    curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n      -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n      -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"get_me\",\"arguments\":{}}}' \\\n      https://your-egg/api/mcp\n    ```\n\nEgg holds idempotency keys in memory\n\nA restart of Egg forgets them. Treat the key as protection against a lost connection, not as a long-term record.\n\n## Addresses for tools and agents\n\n| For | Address |\n| --- | --- |\n| API calls | `https://your-egg/api/v1` |\n| The full reference | `https://your-egg/api/v1/openapi.json` |\n| A short index for agents | `https://your-egg/api/v1/agent-spec.json` |\n| AI agents | `https://your-egg/api/mcp` |\n| Agents with a small context window | `https://your-egg/api/mcp/compact` |\n\nReplace `your-egg` with the address of your Egg. Every address takes the same token in an `Authorization: Bearer` header, and none of them reads cookies. The two description documents are public and hold no data. You add `?toolsets=` or `?scope=` to either agent address to narrow the tools; the client configuration is in the steps above.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reads | 120 a minute per token | 429 with a Retry-After header. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.read) |\n| Changes | 30 a minute | 429. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.write) |\n| Calls that open a session to your container (build logs, resource history, project resources, the inventory, opening a terminal, files) | 10 a minute | 429. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.ssh) |\n| Terminal input | 300 a minute | 429. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.input) |\n| Invalid tokens | 60 a minute per address | 429 “Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens.” In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.badToken) |\n| Request body | 16 KB (4 MB for a file write, 64 KB for terminal input and for MCP, 4 KB for webhooks) | 413 “The request body is too large.” In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES) server/api-v1-shell.ts app/api/mcp/route.ts server/webhooks-http.ts |\n| File write and download | 1 MB and 2 MB | 413 “The API writes files of up to 1 MB. Upload larger files in the dashboard.” In the code: server/api-v1-shell.ts |\n| Active tokens | 25 | 400 “You can have up to 25 active tokens. Revoke one first.” In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (MAX_TOKENS_PER_USER) |\n| Token life | 7, 30, 90 or 365 days; 7 with terminal or files:write | 400 naming the 7 day rule. In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (createTokenInput SHORT_LIVED_MAX_DAYS) |\n| List sizes | Deployments 1 to 100, runtime log lines 1 to 1000, build log lines 1 to 1000, activity 1 to 100, analytics 1 to 90 days, resource history 1 to 168 hours | 400 for a value outside the range. In the code: server/api-v1.ts server/api-v1-more.ts (query fields) |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Send an access token as an Authorization: Bearer egg_... header.`\n\n  401. Add the header. A cookie is never accepted here.\n- `That access token is not valid.`\n\n  401. Missing, revoked, expired and unknown tokens all look the same on purpose. Make a new one on the Developer page.\n- `This token does not have the <scope> permission.`\n\n  403. The message names it. Press Edit on the token on the Developer page and tick it. The change applies to the next request.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 60 tokens that did not check out from one address in a minute. Fix the token your script sends.\n- `Too many requests for this token. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header. Reads are 120 a minute, changes 30, container sessions 10, terminal input 300.\n- `No such item, or it belongs to someone else.`\n\n  404. Check the id. Another account’s items answer the same way.\n- `The request body is too large.`\n\n  413. 16 KB for most calls, 4 MB for a file write, 64 KB for MCP and 4 KB for webhooks.\n- `Request body must be JSON`\n\n  400. Send valid JSON, or no body.\n- `Request body must be a JSON object`\n\n  400. Send { … }, not a list or a bare value.\n- `Unknown field \"<name>\". Allowed: ...`\n\n  400. Unknown body fields are refused so a typo cannot silently do nothing. The message lists the allowed ones.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Pick 7 days, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Pick 7 days, or untick Use the terminal and Change files.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Set the expiry to 7 days in the same change, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Raised when you edit a token. Set Expires after to 7 days in the same edit.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `400` | The request was invalid. The message says which field. |\n| `401` | Missing or invalid token. The response has a WWW-Authenticate: Bearer header. |\n| `403` | The token lacks the permission, or the switch for terminal, files or leftovers is off. |\n| `404` | No such item, or it belongs to someone else. |\n| `405` | The agent endpoint takes POST only. |\n| `409` | The item is in a state that does not allow this. |\n| `413` | The request body or the file is too large. |\n| `429` | Too many requests. Wait the seconds in Retry-After. |\n| `502` | Your container could not be reached. |\n| `503` | Too many terminals are open on the service right now. |\n\n### Command line messages\n\n- `Set EGG_TOKEN to your personal access token (it starts with egg_).`\n\n  Exit 2. Export EGG_TOKEN with a token from the Developer page.\n- `Set EGG_URL to your Egg address, such as https://deploy.example.com.`\n\n  Exit 2. Export EGG_URL with the address of your Egg.\n- `Unknown command \"<name>\".`\n\n  Exit 2. Run egg --help for the list.\n- `Unknown option <name>.`\n\n  Exit 2. The options are --json, --follow, --branch and --help.\n- `egg <command> needs <argument>.`\n\n  Exit 2. Add the project slug or deployment id the command asks for.\n- `No project with the slug \"<slug>\". Run egg projects list to see yours.`\n\n  Exit 1. Check the slug against the list.\n- `Error: <message>`\n\n  Exit 1. The API refused the call. The message is the one from the tables above. With --json the error is printed as an object with status, code and message.\n\n## Example: List your projects with curl\n\n### Given\n\nA token with projects:read, kept in an environment variable.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Call the projects endpoint\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   export NEST_TOKEN=egg_...\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" https://your-egg/api/v1/projects\n   ```\n\n### Result\n\nA JSON list of your projects. A token without the permission gets 403 naming it.\n\n## Example: Deploy from a script and wait\n\n### Given\n\nA token with projects:read, deployments:read and deployments:write, and a project whose id you found with the call above.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Start the deploy\n\n   POST to the project’s deployments endpoint with no body, and send a fresh Idempotency-Key so a lost connection cannot start a second deploy.\n2. Step 2: Watch the rollout\n\n   Call the rollout endpoint with wait=1. It reads the state every 2 seconds for up to 60 seconds and returns when the deploy is ready, error or canceled. Call again if done is false.\n3. Step 3: On error, read the log\n\n   Fetch the deployment’s logs and print the last lines.\n\n### Result\n\nThe POST answers 202 with the queued deployment. When the rollout says done and the state is ready, the release is live. Activity shows “Started a deployment of” with the token’s name.\n\n**Related resources:** [Security](/docs/security), [Limits](/docs/limits), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Notifications](/docs/notifications).\n","api-resources":"Automate\n\nPage 26 of 40\n\n# Projects through the API\n\nCreate a project on a server connected to Egg, then deploy it through the API.\n\n## What it does\n\n`POST /api/v1/projects` creates a project record from a public HTTPS Git repository on a server you have connected to Egg. It does not build or deploy anything. The build and the app run on that server when you deploy.\n\nDeploy the production branch with `POST /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/deployments`. Change settings with `PATCH /api/v1/projects/{projectId}`. Creating and changing a project need `projects:write`. Deploying needs `deployments:write`.\n\nPushes do not deploy an API project\n\nA project made through the API has no GitHub App link. A push to its repository does nothing, and private repositories are not supported. Deploy with the API, or with a deploy hook.\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Field | Required | What to send |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `serverId` | Yes | The id of a server connected to Egg. List the ids with `GET /api/v1/servers`. |\n| `name` | Yes | A display name, up to 60 characters. |\n| `repoUrl` | For source projects | A public HTTPS Git repository URL on a supported host, such as https://github.com/owner/name.git. |\n| `runtime` | For source projects | One of node, python, static, command, go, rust, ruby, php or java. |\n| `slug` | No | Lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, up to 40. Defaults to the name. |\n| `productionBranch` | No | The branch that deploys to production. Defaults to main. |\n| `port` | No | The port the app listens on, from 1024 to 65535. Ports 20000 to 29999 are reserved for previews. No other project on the server may use the port. |\n\nThe request returns `409` when the slug or the port is already in use.\n\n**Deploy from a GitHub URL.** `POST /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/deploy-from-github-url` sets the project's repository and production branch, then queues a production deploy in one call. It needs `deployments:write` and `projects:write`. The body takes a github.com URL in the https, `.git` or `git@` form, with an optional `/tree/<branch>`. Egg uses that branch when present, or the repository's default branch otherwise. Credentials in the URL are never shown.\n\n**Delete a service.** `DELETE /api/v1/services/{serviceId}` needs `projects:write`. Its JSON body must include `confirmName`, exactly matching the service name. Without the optional `?removeFromServer=true` query, Egg deletes its project record and the app keeps running on the container, now unmanaged. With it, Egg also stops the service and removes its releases and previews; the shared data folder stays. The TypeScript client call is `deleteService(serviceId, confirmName)`; pass `{ removeFromServer: true }` as its third argument to remove those files too. In the CLI, run `egg services delete <service> --confirm <name> [--remove-from-server]`. The confirmation must exactly match the service name.\n\n## Example\n\nSet `EGG_URL` to your Egg address and `EGG_TOKEN` to a token with `projects:write`. Then run the two requests in order.\n\n1. **Create the project.** Replace `SERVER_ID` and the repository URL. The response contains the project record. Copy its id.\n2. **Deploy the production branch.** Replace `PROJECT_ID` with the id from step 1. The request needs `deployments:write`.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"serverId\": \"SERVER_ID\",\n    \"name\": \"My app\",\n    \"repoUrl\": \"https://github.com/OWNER/REPOSITORY.git\",\n    \"runtime\": \"node\",\n    \"port\": 3000\n  }'\n```\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/deployments\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\"\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Configuration through the API](/docs/api-configuration), [Monitoring and notifications API](/docs/api-monitoring), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n","api-configuration":"Automate\n\nPage 27 of 40\n\n# Configuration through the API\n\nSet project variables and deployment settings with the API.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe API lists variable names, adds or replaces variables and removes selected keys. Values are encrypted at rest. Each variable has a secret flag, on by default. Only a variable marked non-secret returns its value, from `GET /env/values`. Secret values are write-only: no endpoint returns them.\n\nEach variable has a target. `production` covers the production branch, `preview` covers branch previews and `all` covers both. A changed variable reaches the app on the next deploy.\n\nAll four operations use `/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/env`. The method decides what happens.\n\n| Method | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET` | Lists names and targets, one page at a time. Needs env:read. Pass limit, then the nextCursor from a response as cursor. |\n| `POST` | Adds or replaces the variables you send. Other names and targets stay as they are. Needs env:write. |\n| `PUT` | Replaces every variable and target on the project. Names you leave out are deleted. Needs env:write and confirmName. |\n| `DELETE` | Removes the named keys, for every target or for one target. Needs env:write and confirmName. |\n\n| Method and path | Scope | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/env/values` | `env:read` | Returns the value of each variable marked non-secret. Secret variables are left out. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/env/{key}/{target}/secret` | `env:write` | Sets the secret flag for one variable and target. Send secret: false to let get_env_vars return its value. Saving a value never changes the flag. |\n\n## Parameters\n\n- **projectId.** The project id from `GET /api/v1/projects`.\n- **envVars.** An array of objects with `key`, `value` and `target`. A key and target pair may appear once in a request.\n- **target.** `all`, `production` or `preview`.\n- **confirmName.** The exact project name. Required for `PUT` and `DELETE`, because those calls can remove variables. The name must match, or the call is refused.\n- **keys.** For `DELETE`, one to 100 variable names. Each name may appear once. Add `target` to remove only that target.\n\nA write response lists the key and target of each entry. It does not include the value. Send an `Idempotency-Key` header on writes that you might retry. Use a value you have not used before for each change. Egg keeps the first answer for 24 hours. A repeat with the same key and the same request returns that answer without changing anything twice. The same key with a different request is refused with `422`.\n\n## Example\n\nMerge one production variable. Set `EGG_URL` to your Egg address, `EGG_TOKEN` to a token with `env:write`, `PROJECT_ID` to the project id and `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY` to a new value.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/env\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -H \"Idempotency-Key: $IDEMPOTENCY_KEY\" \\\n  --data '{\"envVars\":[{\"key\":\"PUBLIC_ORIGIN\",\"value\":\"https://app.example.com\",\"target\":\"production\"}]}'\n```\n\nThe response confirms the key and target. The request and response shapes for every endpoint are in the OpenAPI document at `/api/v1/openapi.json`.\n\n## Deployment settings\n\nThese endpoints change how a deployment builds, checks its health and pulls its image. Each call needs `projects:write`. Use the deployment id from `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/deployments`.\n\n| Method and path | What it sets |\n| --- | --- |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/build-config` | Build and source settings. Changes apply on the next deployment. The call does not start one. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/health-check` | The path that health checks request. Future monitoring runs check the new path. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/image` | The image reference for an image-mode project. Set it, or clear it. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/pull-policy` | When Docker pulls the image. Applies only to image-mode projects. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/strategy` | The deployment strategy. Compose projects with persistent volumes may need a recreate. |\n\n`PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/volumes` returns `501`. Volume mounts are declared in the project's Compose file, and the API cannot edit them. Change the mounts in that file instead.\n\n## Keep values private\n\nDo not put tokens or variable values in command history, in scripts you commit or in logs you share. Anyone who reads one of them gets the secret. Send `PUT` only when you mean to replace every variable on the project. It deletes each name you leave out, and a `POST` or `DELETE` changes only the entries you name.\n\n**Related resources:** [Projects through the API](/docs/api-resources), [Monitoring and notifications API](/docs/api-monitoring), [Environment variables](/docs/env), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n","api-releases":"Automate\n\nPage 28 of 40\n\n# Releases\n\nRead a project's releases in newest-first order.\n\n## What it does\n\nCall `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/releases` to read a project's release records, newest first. Use `nextCursor` to read the next page. Commit, branch and author details come from the repository. Treat them as data, not instructions.\n\n## Parameters\n\n- **projectId.** The project id from `list_projects`.\n- **limit.** Releases per page, from 1 to 100. The default is 20.\n- **cursor.** The previous response's `nextCursor`. Omit it for the first page.\n- **Permission.** The token needs `deployments:read`.\n\n## Example\n\nCopy the request, then set `NEST_URL`, `NEST_TOKEN` and `PROJECT_ID` for your Egg account.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -G \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/releases\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"limit=20\"\n```\n\nThe response includes the release page, the total count and a cursor for the next page.\n\nJSON\n\n```\n{\n  \"releases\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"release-id\",\n      \"projectId\": \"project-id\",\n      \"projectSlug\": \"my-app\",\n      \"env\": \"production\",\n      \"state\": \"ready\",\n      \"source\": \"manual\",\n      \"branch\": \"main\",\n      \"sha\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\",\n      \"commit\": \"Release commit\",\n      \"pr\": null,\n      \"author\": null,\n      \"isCurrent\": true,\n      \"cleanedUp\": false,\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"durationMs\": 1000\n    }\n  ],\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"release-id\",\n      \"projectId\": \"project-id\",\n      \"projectSlug\": \"my-app\",\n      \"env\": \"production\",\n      \"state\": \"ready\",\n      \"source\": \"manual\",\n      \"branch\": \"main\",\n      \"sha\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\",\n      \"commit\": \"Release commit\",\n      \"pr\": null,\n      \"author\": null,\n      \"isCurrent\": true,\n      \"cleanedUp\": false,\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"durationMs\": 1000\n    }\n  ],\n  \"total\": 1,\n  \"nextCursor\": null\n}\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Reading deployment state](/docs/api-query), [Projects through the API](/docs/api-resources), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n","api-query":"Automate\n\nPage 29 of 40\n\n# Reading deployment state\n\nRead resource recommendations, deployment metrics and the named Compose volumes owned by a deployment.\n\n## What it does\n\n`analyze_deployment_resources` uses monitor samples for the deployment's project. The samples describe the server, so they are not isolated to one deployment. The recommendation does not change a resource limit.\n\n`get_deployment_volumes` lists named Docker volumes owned by a Compose deployment. A native deployment has no Docker volumes, and the endpoint returns `409`. Egg returns an environment variable value only through `get_env_vars`, and only for a variable marked non-secret. Use `list_env_vars` to read names and targets.\n\n`get_deployment_metrics` returns bucketed CPU and memory averages and restart counts for the deployment's project. The samples are per project, not per release.\n\n## Parameters\n\nBoth endpoints take one path parameter, `deploymentId`, which is the deployment's id.\n\n| Endpoint | Scope |\n| --- | --- |\n| `analyze_deployment_resources` | `resources:read` |\n| `get_deployment_volumes` | `deployments:read` |\n| `get_deployment_metrics` | `deployments:read` |\n\n## Examples\n\nSet `NEST_URL`, `NEST_TOKEN` and `DEPLOYMENT_ID`, then copy the command that matches your question.\n\nResource recommendation\n\n```\ncurl --fail-with-body \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/deployments/$DEPLOYMENT_ID/resource-analysis\"\n```\n\nCompose volumes\n\n```\ncurl --fail-with-body \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/deployments/$DEPLOYMENT_ID/deployment-volumes\"\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Releases](/docs/api-releases), [Monitoring and notifications API](/docs/api-monitoring), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n","api-monitoring":"Automate\n\nPage 30 of 40\n\n# Monitoring and notifications API\n\nRead project containers and health, deployment logs, resource signals and visitor analytics. Manage signed notification channels and their delivery history.\n\n## What it does\n\nMonitoring endpoints return what Egg records for a project: container details, deployment health and output, the service journal, resource samples and counted page views.\n\nPage views are not every HTTP request. Egg does not collect a web server access log, so `/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/request-stats` counts recorded page views only. `/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/attack-signals` lists repeated client-side error groups. Treat them as leads to check, not proof of an attack.\n\nA notification channel sends signed deployment events to a public HTTPS address you choose. Each channel has an event filter. The signing secret comes back once, from the call that creates the channel. No later read returns it, so keep a copy. Each delivery records its event, status (`pending`, `success` or `failed`), attempts and last error code. It does not store the response body.\n\n## Endpoints and scopes\n\n| Endpoint | Scope | What it returns |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/logs/search` | `logs:read` | Service journal lines, filtered by text, level (info, warn or error) and a time range of 1h, 24h or 7d, with a page limit. |\n| `GET /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/build-logs` | `deployments:read` | get_build_logs: build output for one deployment, one page at a time. |\n| `GET /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/logs` | `deployments:read` | get_deployment_logs: build and deploy output for one deployment, oldest first and paged. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/containers` | `container:read` | Production Docker containers for one project, including Compose services. Native systemd projects return an empty list. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/containers/{containerId}` | `container:read` | Safe details for one container in that project's set, including image and creation time. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/health` | `container:read` | The project's existing cached systemd and HTTP health check. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/request-stats` | `analytics:read` | Recorded page views, visitors, top pages and referrers for 1 to 90 days. Defaults to 30. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/anomalies` | `analytics:read` | Client-error and resource anomalies for 1 to 90 days. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/attack-signals` | `analytics:read` | Repeated client-side error groups to investigate. |\n| `GET /api/v1/notification-channels` | `webhooks:read` | Each channel with its receiver host and event filter. Signing secrets are never returned. |\n| `GET /api/v1/notification-channels/{channelId}/deliveries` | `webhooks:read` | Recent sends for one channel, 1 to 100 at a time. Defaults to 20. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/crash-reports` | `logs:read` | Crash reports for the project. Each keeps up to 200 journal lines from the release that was live, with known secrets masked. |\n| `GET /api/v1/crash-reports/{crashId}` | `logs:read` | One crash report, with the same journal lines. |\n| `GET /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/metrics` | `deployments:read` | Bucketed CPU and memory averages and restart counts for the deployment’s project. Samples are per project, not per release. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/resources/quota` | `resources:read` | Limits and current usage. A value Egg has not measured is null, never zero. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/changes` | `activity:read` | The project’s change feed, newest first. Environment variable key names appear in deletion and replacement summaries; values never appear. |\n\nThe container endpoints are read only and project scoped. `restartCount` uses systemd's restart counter for the main container and Docker's counter for Compose auxiliaries; `status.restarts` on the health response is systemd's count. Health reuses the existing cached systemd and HTTP check and returns a `cached` flag. These endpoints do not offer Docker exec into containers or per-container log streaming. Use `get_deployment_logs` for build and deploy output. They do not provide a cluster-wide container listing.\n\nIn `get_changes`, an item can include `deploymentId` for API deploys (including a GitHub URL deploy), redeploys, rollbacks, cancellations, project restarts, deployment lifecycle actions and preview deletions when the id belongs to this project. A project-linked domain removal appears in that project's feed. Deletions and replacements show environment variable key names, including for shared environment sets; values never appear.\n\nEach request needs the scope in its row. Use `channelId` from the channel list, and `deliveryId` from the deliveries list. Creating a channel needs `webhooks:write`. The full list of parameters is in the OpenAPI document at `/api/v1/openapi.json`.\n\n## Example\n\nSet `EGG_URL` to your Egg address, `EGG_TOKEN` to a token with `analytics:read` and `PROJECT_ID` to the project id. List the last 30 days of recorded page views:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/request-stats?days=30\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\"\n```\n\nList notification channels with `webhooks:read`. The response shows each receiver and its event filter, and no signing secrets:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/notification-channels\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\"\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Logs](/docs/logs), [Reading deployment state](/docs/api-query), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n","api-agent-safety":"Automate\n\nPage 31 of 40\n\n# Agent safety rules\n\nFind an operation, read its inputs, and call scheduled-job or project actions safely.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe compact MCP endpoint at `/api/mcp/compact` offers three tools. `search_api` finds operations the token can use. `describe_operation` returns the route, scope and input schema. `call_api` runs one operation.\n\nThe full MCP endpoint at `/api/mcp` exposes each operation as its own tool. Both endpoints apply the token's scopes and any toolset filter in the URL, such as `?toolsets=core`. Batch requests are refused, so send one message per request.\n\nSet `NEST_URL` to your Egg address and `NEST_TOKEN` to the token. Then run a search:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -X POST \"$NEST_URL/api/mcp/compact\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n    \"params\": {\n      \"name\": \"search_api\",\n      \"arguments\": { \"query\": \"scheduled jobs\", \"limit\": 5 }\n    }\n  }'\n```\n\n## Parameters\n\n- `search_api`: send `query`. Add `limit` from 1 to 50; the default is 10. Results include only operations this token can run in its toolset.\n- `describe_operation`: send an `operationId` returned by search. The result lists the fields a call needs.\n- `call_api`: send `operationId` and an `arguments` object. Some routes also require an exact resource name in their arguments.\n\n## Scheduled jobs\n\nScheduled jobs use `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/jobs` to list jobs and the same path with `POST` to create one. Here is a list request:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -X GET \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/jobs\"\n```\n\nThe job tools are `list_jobs`, `create_job`, `get_job`, `update_job`, `delete_job`, `list_job_runs`, `get_job_run`, `get_job_run_logs`, `run_job`, `cancel_job_run` and `replace_job_steps`.\n\nEgg stores one command per job and keeps only its latest status, not a run history. A job accepts exactly one step. The run id is the job's own id. Cancel pauses the job's timer. It does not stop a run that has already started.\n\n## Destructive actions\n\nAsk the user before you delete or change a live resource. A permanent delete cannot be taken back. Through `call_api`, Egg refuses an operation flagged as destructive until `confirm` repeats its operation ID exactly. Examples include `delete_project`, `delete_service`, `delete_job`, `run_job` and `cancel_job_run`. Project deletion also needs `confirmName` set to the exact project name:\n\nJSON\n\n```\n{\n  \"operationId\": \"delete_project\",\n  \"confirm\": \"delete_project\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"projectId\": \"PROJECT_ID\",\n    \"confirmName\": \"Recipe bot\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDeleting a service with `delete_service` also needs `confirmName`, exactly matching the service name. The optional `removeFromServer` flag stops the service and removes its releases and previews from the container; the shared data folder stays.\n\nDeleting a notification channel needs `confirmName` set to the exact channel host, such as `hooks.example.com`. Egg records the channel delete in the audit log with the token that made it.\n\nReplacing or deleting environment variables with `set_env_vars` or `delete_env_vars` needs `confirmName` set to the exact project name. Secret values are write-only, so an agent cannot read one back. Only a variable marked non-secret comes back, from `get_env_vars`.\n\n**Related resources:** [The API and agents](/docs/api#api-mcp), [What a token can do](/docs/api#api-scopes), [Making a token](/docs/api#api-tokens).\n","monitoring":"Watch\n\nPage 32 of 40\n\n# Monitoring and uptime\n\nWhat your app is doing now, and what it has been doing.\n\nYou need a deployed project and a running worker, so Egg can check your app. You also need a webhook under Notifications for messages when something goes wrong.\n\n## The Service card\n\nOnce something is live, the Service card appears on your project page, showing Status (Running, Starting, Crashed, Stopped, Not deployed or Unknown), Uptime, Memory, Restarts and Health check (“HTTP CODE in MS ms”, “No answer” or “Not checked”). It reads systemd on your container and sends one real request to the health path with a 3 second limit.\n\n- It looks again every 30 seconds while your tab is visible, so Egg reuses a reading that is under 30 seconds old and opening the page does not always open a new connection to your container. A line under the card says when the reading was taken: “Checked” with the time, “Readings are reused for 30 seconds; Check now asks the container again.”\n- **Check now** always asks your container, whatever the age of the last reading.\n- **Restart** restarts the production service after you confirm “Restart the service?”. It builds nothing.\n\nWhen Egg sees a production service fail, it keeps up to 200 lines of its journal against the current release, once per crash, with known secrets masked. A token with `logs:read` lists them with `list_crash_reports` and reads one with `get_crash_report`.\n\n## Resource use\n\nThe Resource use card on your project page shows this one service’s memory and CPU over the last 24 hours, so it says “Collecting data.” until it has two readings. CPU is a share of one core, so it can pass 100. Egg leaves a point blank when there is no earlier reading, the gap is over 30 minutes or the counter went backwards after a restart. The Resources page in the sidebar shows load, memory and disk for the whole server over 24 hours or 7 days, with a table of each project’s memory, CPU and restarts.\n\nThe worker samples every connected server every 5 minutes by default and keeps 7 days, so it writes project samples only while the service is running.\n\nThrough the API, `get_deployment_metrics` (`deployments:read`) returns bucketed CPU and memory averages and restart counts for the deployment’s project. The samples are per project, not per release. `get_resource_quota` (`resources:read`) returns the limits and current usage of the project. A value Egg has not measured is`null`, never zero.\n\n## Uptime checks and alerts\n\nEvery 5 minutes the worker opens one SSH connection to each connected server, so for each project with a live production release and no deployment in progress it runs a short read-only command on your container. The command asks systemd about the service and makes one request to the app on 127.0.0.1, so a project is up when its service runs and the health path answers with a status from 1 to 499. Without `curl` on your container, running is enough, and a server it cannot reach is skipped rather than reported as down.\n\nEgg declares a project down after a number of failed checks in a row, 2 by default, so you get one message when it goes down and one when it answers again. The project header shows “Answering”, “Not answering since TIME” or “Not checked yet”, and links to the alert history. Each project sets its own alerts.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Open the Alerts card\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Alerts**. For any message to be sent, you must save a webhook in the Notifications card.\n2. Step 2: Set “When to warn”\n\n   Set **Disk full (%)**, **Memory used (%)**, **CPU load per core (%)** and **Failed checks before down**, plus **Slow response limit (ms)** if you want one.\n3. Step 3: Choose the channels\n\n   Under **Send to the webhook**, tick any of The app stops answering, The app answers again, Disk, memory or CPU warnings and Deployment results. All four are on by default.\n4. Step 4: Save\n\n   Press **Save** at the bottom of the card, so a message says “Alert settings saved”.\n5. Step 5: Pause when you need quiet\n\n   Under **Pause all alerts**, choose “For 1 hour”, “For 24 hours” or “Until I resume” and press **Pause**, so it takes effect at once without Save and the card shows “Alerts are paused”. Press **Resume alerts** to end it, because while paused nothing is sent but the history still records the event with the badge “Not sent, paused”.\n\n| Setting | Range | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Disk full (%) | Whole number, 1 to 100 | 90 |\n| Memory used (%) | Whole number, 1 to 100 | 90 |\n| CPU load per core (%) | Whole number, 1 to 100. The 1 minute load divided by the core count. | 90 |\n| Failed checks before down | Whole number, 1 to 10 | 2 |\n| Slow response limit (ms) | Empty (off), or 50 to 60000. A slower answer counts as a failed check. | Off |\n\n- A resource alert fires once when a value reaches its limit and once more when it falls back below it, so nothing is sent in between. Disk, memory and CPU are for the whole server, checked against each project’s own limits.\n- History keeps the newest 50 alerts for 90 days, with the badges “Still happening”, “Resolved” and “Not sent, paused”. Open alerts are kept past 90 days.\n- The “Needs attention” card on the Projects page lists apps not answering, projects whose latest deployment failed, a disk, memory or CPU load at or over the lowest limit you set (90% by default), and projects with alerts paused. It was called the Alerts card in older docs.\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Check interval | 5 minutes by default | A very short outage can be missed. |\n| Failed checks before down | 1 to 10, default 2 | You pick the number from a list, so there is no message. |\n| Resource limits | 1 to 100 each, default 90 | “The disk limit must be a whole number from 1 to 100.” or “The disk limit must be from 1 to 100.” (and memory, CPU) |\n| Slow response limit | 50 to 60000 ms, or empty | “The response time limit must be a whole number from 50 to 60000.” or “The response time limit must be from 50 to 60000.” |\n| Samples kept | 7 days | Older readings are deleted; the charts show 24 hours or 7 days. |\n| Service card refresh | Every 30 seconds, with a 20 second read. A reading under 30 seconds old is reused; Check now is never reused | “Could not read the service from your container.” |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“The service is STATE.” / “The service is running but nothing answered on its health path.”`\n\n  The down message says why. Fix the app, or the health path in Settings, Build and Run.\n- `“The health path took MS ms to answer, over your limit of N ms.”`\n\n  Raise the Slow response limit, or make the page faster.\n- `“The health path answered HTTP CODE.”`\n\n  A 500 or above counts as down. A 404 does not, because the check treats 1 to 499 as healthy.\n- `“Could not load alert settings”`\n\n  Press **Try again** on the Alerts card.\n- No messages at all\n\n  Check that a webhook is saved, the channel is ticked, alerts are not paused, and the app has failed as many checks in a row as your setting.\n\n### Example: Get warned before memory runs out\n\n#### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` sometimes runs out of memory on Sam’s container. Sam has a webhook saved.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Set the limits\n\n   Sam sets Memory used (%) to `80` and Failed checks before down to `3`.\n2. Step 2: Keep the channels\n\n   Sam leaves the four boxes under Send to the webhook ticked and presses Save.\n\n#### Result\n\nWhen the server passes 80% memory, the channel gets one message like “Memory on the server reached 82% (your limit is 80%).” A second arrives when it falls back below. If the bot stops answering for three checks in a row, about 15 minutes, a “recipe-bot is not answering” message follows.\n\n**Related:** [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Status page](/docs/status-page), [Logs](/docs/logs).\n","status-page":"Watch\n\nPage 33 of 40\n\n# Status page\n\nA public page that says whether one app is answering.\n\nYou need a deployed project and a running worker, so the uptime checks can supply the figures. Egg serves the page from its own stored data, so a visitor never reaches your container through it.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Publish it\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Public Status Page** (the section list at the top of Settings calls it Status Page), and press **Publish status page**, so it stops saying “Not published.”\n2. Step 2: Share the link\n\n   Copy the link from **Status page URL**, which is `/status/KEY` on your Egg address, so the page asks search engines not to index it. The key is 12 random URL-safe characters, and the page reflects the worker’s checks every few minutes.\n3. Step 3: Take it down\n\n   Press **Unpublish**, so Egg deletes the key and the old link stops working. Publishing again makes a new key.\n\n- Anyone with the link sees the project name, a state card, the share of the last 30 days your app was answering, a 30 day strip and the latest 10 outages under “Recent outages”, but nothing else: no repository, port, server or commit.\n- The state card reads “Operational” (“The app is answering.”), “Not responding” (“The app has stopped answering.”) or “No data yet” (“Checks have not run for this app yet.”). The percentage is withheld until a check has run.\n- The strip has one square per UTC day, with a key beneath it. Green is “Answered all day”, amber “Down for part of a day”, red “Down for most of a day” (more than half of it) and grey “No checks yet”. Hover a square to see the minutes, or open “Show each day as text” for a table of every day. With no outages the list says “None in the last 30 days.”, or “Nothing recorded yet.” before any check has run.\n- A slow answer over your Slow response limit counts as down here too, because it fails the check.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Window | 30 days | Older days are not shown. |\n| How long the page is kept before it is read again | 60 seconds, dropped early when the app’s state changes or the page is unpublished | A change can take up to a minute to show on a page that is already open. In the code: server/status-page-cache.ts |\n| Recent outages listed | 10 | Older outages are not listed. |\n| Check interval | 5 minutes | An outage shorter than that can be missed. |\n| Status pages per project | One | Publishing again makes a new key. |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“No data yet”`\n\n  No check has run yet. Wait for the next 5 minute pass.\n- The link shows a not found page\n\n  The page was unpublished, or you published again and the key changed. Copy the new link.\n- The page says Not responding but the app works\n\n  The check uses your health path. Make sure it answers with a status below 500, within your slow response limit if you set one.\n- An outage is missing\n\n  It was shorter than the 5 minute check interval, or the server could not be reached at all, which is not reported as the app being down.\n\n## Example: Let friends check the bot\n\n### Given\n\nSam’s friends ask whether `recipe-bot` is down.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Publish\n\n   Sam presses Publish status page.\n2. Step 2: Share\n\n   Sam pastes the link into the Discord server’s pinned message.\n\n### Result\n\nThe page shows “Operational”, the 30 day share and a strip of green squares. A night when Sam redeployed twice shows one amber square.\n\n**Related:** [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Analytics](/docs/analytics).\n","analytics":"Watch\n\nPage 34 of 40\n\n# Analytics\n\nPage views for your site, counted by a small script. Nest does not share its web server logs, so the numbers have to come from the page itself.\n\nYou need a deployed project that serves web pages and access to the head of those pages, so a bot with no web page has nothing to count. Each visitor’s browser sends the count to Egg’s web address, not your container, so your container does no extra work and Egg must be reachable from your visitors. An Egg on localhost cannot count visitors on a public site.\n\n## Setting it up\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Turn it on\n\n   Open your project’s Analytics tab, find **Count visits** and press **Turn on analytics**.\n2. Step 2: Paste the snippet\n\n   The card is now titled **Tracking snippet**, so copy its snippet into the head of every page you want counted.\n\n   HTML\n\n   ```\n   <script defer data-key=\"nda_...\" src=\"https://your-egg/api/analytics/script?key=nda_...\"></script>\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Redeploy and visit\n\n   Redeploy so the page has the snippet, which builds again and restarts your app, then open the page in a normal browser window.\n4. Step 4: Switch off when done\n\n   Press **Turn off** to remove the key, so existing numbers stay until they age out after 90 days, unless you chose 30 or 60 under “Keep the numbers for”.\n\n## What you see\n\nYou see “Page views” and “Visitors” for the last 30 days, “Page views per day” as a bar for each day and your top 10 pages under “Top pages”, so once Referrers is on you also see the top 10 sites under “Where visitors came from”. Turning on Campaigns adds “Campaigns”, and Record visitor countries adds a map under “Where visitors are”. Visitors are counted per day, so someone who comes on two days counts twice, and percentages, shares and the median appear only with at least 50 visits behind them. Below that you see counts and “Not enough data”.\n\nOnce analytics is on, the “What else to record” card adds ten things and a choice of how long to keep the numbers. Each is a checkbox that is off until you tick it and saves at once, with “Saved.” beneath, so with all of them off the script records only page views. Route details default to `/`, so to include a route, set a safe template such as `data-egg-path=\"/users/:id\"` on the page’s `<html>` element. Never put a visitor’s name, token or other personal value in that attribute. Under Settings, “Analytics defaults” has eight of them, all off, and Campaigns and Record visitor countries are not among them, because you set them up for each project. The checkboxes decide what a project starts with the first time you turn analytics on for it, so projects that already have analytics are not changed.\n\n- **Clicks** (“Top clicks”). Only elements you explicitly mark with a `data-egg-track` name are counted, so Egg never reads visible text or accessible labels. Do not put personal or changing values in the tracking name, because Egg never records input values or form content and stores only the project, the UTC day and the label.\n- **Time on page** (a card of the same name, with a Median). How long the page was open and visible, in four groups: under 10 seconds, 10 to 60 seconds, 1 to 5 minutes, over 5 minutes. It is sent when the visitor leaves.\n- **Funnel.** Two to six steps in order. For each, pick Page and type a path starting with `/`, or pick Tracked name. Press **Add step** for more, then **Save steps**. A visitor counts at a step only after reaching the ones before it, on the same UTC day.\n- **Referrers** (“Where visitors came from”). The script sends only the host of the referring site, never its full address or query string, so it does this once per page load and a link between pages of your own site is not a referrer. The host is stored with the page view and kept as sent, so `www.` stays, and turning Referrers off deletes the hosts already stored for your project.\n- **Campaigns** (“Campaigns”) starts off, so you must save at least one allowed value before you can turn it on. Under Allowed campaign values you list the exact `utm_source`, `utm_medium` and `utm_campaign` values you use, so the script reads only those three parameters from the address, and only a value that is on its list, spelled exactly as listed with capital letters included, is sent and stored. Any other value is dropped, and no other parameter is read. Each list holds up to 20 values of up to 40 characters: letters, numbers, dot, dash and underscore. Type a value, press **Add**, then press **Save allowed values**. Turning Campaigns off deletes the campaign values already stored for your project but keeps your lists, so taking a value off a list stops it being recorded while page views already stored with it stay until they age out.\n- **Speed metrics** (“Speed”). Your browser sends five measurements once per page view, when the visitor leaves or switches tab: Largest Contentful Paint, Cumulative Layout Shift, Interaction to Next Paint, First Contentful Paint and Time to First Byte. Times are in milliseconds (ms) and shown in ms or seconds (s), so layout shift is a score with no unit. The table shows the median and 75th percentile for each and labels the 75th percentile Good, Needs work or Poor with Google’s thresholds: LCP 2.5 s and 4 s, CLS 0.1 and 0.25, INP 200 ms and 500 ms, FCP 1.8 s and 3 s, TTFB 800 ms and 1800 ms. Egg does not collect exception messages, because they can contain credentials or personal data.\n- **Custom events** (“Events”). A click on an element with `data-egg-track` is recorded by name. Add `data-egg-value` for a value of up to 40 characters, letters, numbers, spaces and - _ . : only, such as a plan name. A value that breaks the rule is dropped and the event still counts. It stores the name, the value and the day, no visitor id. A funnel step can use the same name.\n- **Live view** (“On the site now”). You see page views in the last 5 minutes and, with Retention on, distinct visitors in that window, so with Retention off it says “Unique visitors need retention on”. While a page is open and visible, the script sends a small signal every 60 seconds, which Egg deletes after an hour, and it sends the signal only when both are on.\n- **Retention** (“Returning visitors”). Egg stores a random id in your visitor’s browser, not a cookie, in local storage under the name `_egg_id`, so a visitor can remove it by clearing site data. Egg keeps only an HMAC (a keyed hash) of it with the project, never the id, and the salt comes from the server secret and changes every 90 days, so someone who returns after a change counts as new. Turning retention off deletes the stored codes and the script removes the id from the browser. To turn it off, clear the Retention checkbox under What else to record on your project’s Analytics tab, and leave it cleared under Analytics defaults in Settings. The card shows visitors in the last 7 days, then of those who first came 7 to 14 days ago how many returned on a later day within 7 days, and of those who first came 30 to 37 days ago how many returned within 30 days. A group under 20 visitors shows “Not enough data yet”.\n- **Record visitor countries** (“Where visitors are”, a map with a table of the same numbers), looks up the visitor’s IP address in a country database that ships with Egg, in the same request that records the page view, so the address is not sent to any other service. Only the two-letter country code, such as `FR`, is stored with the page view. Egg does not store or log the IP address, and keeps no city, region or position. An address that is local or private, or not in the database, has no country, so nothing is stored for it. The map covers the last 30 days, and a country with fewer than 10 page views is shown as “fewer than 10”, with larger counts rounded to two significant figures. Turning it off deletes the countries already stored for your project. This product includes GeoLite2 data created by MaxMind, available from https://www.maxmind.com.\n\n**Keep the numbers for** sets how long Egg keeps page views, events, speed measurements and error records before deleting them: 30, 60 or 90 days for your project. A project keeps 90 days until you change it. Egg always deletes live view signals after an hour.\n\n## Privacy\n\n- By default Egg omits route details and represents them as `/`, so to report routes your site must explicitly provide a safe template in `data-egg-path`. No cookies. Everything else is a switch that starts off.\n- Egg stores no IP address or browser details, so a visitor is a shortened hash of the address, browser, project and date, salted with the server secret. It changes daily, so nobody can be followed across days.\n- Egg counts nothing for browsers that send Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, on localhost or for known bots, so the collector drops requests that carry either signal.\n- The page path is a template your site provides, so the script does not send the browser’s raw address path and legacy snippets without the template marker are stored as `/`. It reduces the referrer to its host.\n- The script stores nothing in your browser unless Retention is on, and then only one random id in local storage. No IP address is stored by any feature, and Egg does not log it, so the address only limits how fast one visitor can send and feeds the daily visitor hash, apart from one country lookup in the same request while Record visitor countries is on.\n- Campaign values are recorded only while Campaigns is on, and only values on the project’s allowed list. Any other value is dropped before it is stored.\n- Turning off Referrers, Campaigns, Retention or Record visitor countries deletes what that switch stored for the project. The other switches stop recording when turned off, and what they already stored stays until it ages out.\n- On a copy of Egg you run yourself behind a reverse proxy, set `TRUST_PROXY=1`, so Egg reads the proxy headers. It is off by default, and without it every visitor counts as one address, so the daily visitor hash tells people apart only by browser and everyone shares the send limits. Turn it on only when every request comes through a proxy you run that sets those headers itself, because with it off Egg has no visitor address and Record visitor countries stores no country either.\n- Egg removes analytics history after 30, 60 or 90 days, as you choose for each project under “Keep the numbers for”, and after 90 days where you have not chosen, so cleanup runs from the app every 15 minutes, independently of the build worker and uptime checks. Backups may keep older database contents under their own retention, and a setting change reaches visitors within about 5 minutes because the script is cached.\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| History kept | 30, 60 or 90 days for each project, 90 by default | Older rows are deleted by the app cleanup task. |\n| Percentages and median | At least 50 visits | “Not enough data” |\n| Funnel steps | 2 to 6 | “A funnel needs at least 2 steps” or “A funnel has at most 6 steps” |\n| Tracked name | Letters, digits, spaces and - _ . : up to 40 characters | “A tracked name uses letters, numbers, spaces and - _ . : and is at most 40 characters” |\n| Page step | Starts with / | “A page step starts with \"/\", like /pricing” |\n| Page views | 1 per second per project and address. With TRUST_PROXY off, all visitors share one address | Extra views are dropped. In the code: server/analytics.ts |\n| Every other report (clicks, time, goals, events, speed, live signal) | 30 per 10 seconds per project and address. With TRUST_PROXY off, all visitors share one address | Extra events are dropped. In the code: server/analytics-events.ts |\n| Path and referrer | 200 and 100 characters | The rest is cut off. |\n| Allowed campaign values | Up to 20 each for source, medium and campaign, of up to 40 characters: letters, numbers, dot, dash or underscore | “Use short static labels with letters, numbers, dot, dash or underscore.” |\n| Campaign values recorded | Only values on the allowed list, spelled exactly | Any other value is dropped. |\n| Event value | Letters, digits, spaces and - _ . : up to 40 characters | The value is dropped. The event still counts. |\n| Speed figures | At least 50 page views per measurement | “Not enough data” |\n| Live view | Last 5 minutes, signal every 60 seconds, deleted after 1 hour | Older signals are deleted. |\n| Returning visitors | At least 20 visitors in the group | “Not enough data yet” |\n| Retention code | Changes every 90 days | A visitor after a change counts as new. |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“Nothing counted yet” (or “Analytics is off”)`\n\n  Check the snippet is in the head of a page that was redeployed, that your browser is not sending Do Not Track, and that you are not on localhost.\n- `“Not enough data”`\n\n  The percentages need 50 visits behind them. The counts are still shown.\n- `“Not enough data yet” under Returning visitors`\n\n  The group needs at least 20 visitors. Retention only counts visitors from the day it was turned on.\n- `“Unique visitors need retention on”`\n\n  Turn on Retention under What else to record. Without the random id, visitors cannot be told apart for 5 minutes.\n- `“Could not save” or “Could not change analytics”`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try again.\n- `“Allow at least one safe campaign label before turning campaigns on.” or Campaigns cannot be ticked`\n\n  Add a value to at least one of the three lists and press Save allowed values. Then turn Campaigns on.\n- A campaign you use does not appear under Campaigns\n\n  Only values on the allowed list are recorded, spelled exactly the same, capital letters included. Add the value and save, then visit again.\n- `“Where visitors are” has no countries`\n\n  Turn on Record visitor countries. Views from before it was on have no country. Local and private addresses have none, and on a copy you run yourself, TRUST_PROXY must be on to give Egg the visitor’s address.\n- A funnel does not turn on, or “A funnel needs at least 2 steps”\n\n  Fill in at least 2 steps and press Save steps. A funnel with fewer counts as off.\n\n### Example: Count visits to the bot’s page\n\n#### Given\n\nSam adds a small web page to `recipe-bot` that lists recipes, with a Subscribe button.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Turn it on\n\n   Sam turns on analytics and pastes the snippet in the page head.\n2. Step 2: Record clicks\n\n   Sam ticks Clicks under What else to record and marks the button `data-egg-track=\"subscribe\"`.\n3. Step 3: Redeploy\n\n   Sam redeploys.\n\n#### Result\n\nAfter a week the page shows page views per day, the top pages, and “subscribe” under Top clicks. Sam sees no personal data, because none was stored.\n\n**Related:** [Status page](/docs/status-page), [Domains](/docs/domains), [Security](/docs/security).\n","activity":"Watch\n\nPage 35 of 40\n\n# Activity\n\nA newest-first log of what you changed and what the monitor saw, with a search, two filters and a CSV export.\n\nOpen Activity in the sidebar. Entries are grouped by day in UTC, with Today and Yesterday named. Each row says what happened, to which project, server or domain, and what did it. A change made with an access token shows the token’s name, so you can tell a script from yourself. A project, token or server that has since been deleted still shows by the name it had, marked as gone. The log is kept in Egg’s database, so reading, searching and exporting it never contacts your container and changes nothing.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nDays are cut at midnight UTC so the log reads the same wherever you are and two people on one account see the same groups. Two entries a second apart on either side of midnight UTC land under different days. The build log on a deployment page shows your browser’s time, which is the only place that does.\n\n## What the log records\n\nThe log covers your own actions and the monitor’s. Examples of the wording: “Created project”, “Changed settings for”, “Saved environment variable on”, “Started a deployment of”, “Redeployed”, “Rolled back”, “Canceled a deployment of”, “Deployed through the deploy hook for”, “Turned on analytics for”, “Published a status page for”, “Detected downtime on”, “Detected recovery of”, “Created an access token”, “Opened a terminal on”, “Saved a file on”, “Created a webhook”, “Replaced the SSH key for”, “Changed the build memory limit for”, “Disconnected”, “Changed container permissions for” and “Removed a leftover from the container”.\n\n| Kind | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Projects** | Creating, changing, restarting and deleting projects. Saving and removing variables. Turning analytics on or off or changing what it records. Publishing or unpublishing a status page. |\n| **Deployments** | Starting, redeploying, rolling back and canceling deployments. Deleting previews. Creating, revoking and using a deploy hook. |\n| **Domains** | Adding, changing and removing a domain. |\n| **Servers** | Replacing the SSH key, changing the build memory limit, changing container permissions, removing leftovers and disconnecting a server. A disconnect entry records how many projects were kept or deleted. |\n| **Access and tokens** | Creating, editing and revoking access tokens. Signing out other browsers. Confirming sign-in with Hack Club. Creating, changing, testing and deleting webhooks. |\n| **Monitoring** | “Detected downtime on” and “Detected recovery of”. |\n| **Terminal and files** | Opening and closing a terminal, and saving, creating, renaming and deleting files. |\n\nAn entry that fits none of these shows only under All. Terminal and file entries record that something happened, with a path or a duration. They never record file contents or what you typed or saw. A detail that looks like a secret, by its name or its value, is dropped before an entry is shown or exported.\n\n## Searching and filtering\n\n### Find an entry\n\n1. Step 1: Search\n\n   Type in “Search by action, project or detail”. Every word you type has to match.\n2. Step 2: Pick a kind\n\n   Choose All, Projects, Deployments, Domains, Servers, Access and tokens, Monitoring, or Terminal and files.\n3. Step 3: Pick a date range\n\n   Choose Last 24 hours, Last 7 days, Last 30 days or All time.\n4. Step 4: Load more\n\n   Press Load more at the bottom to read the next 50.\n5. Step 5: Start again\n\n   Press Clear filters to go back to All kinds, All time and no search.\n\nThe filters are in the address, so a filtered list can be bookmarked or shared with yourself on another device. With Kind on All and nothing in the search box, three or more neighbouring entries of one kind fold into one row such as “4 token and access changes”, and every entry is still inside it. Search and a chosen Kind show every row.\n\n## Exporting to CSV\n\nPress **Export CSV** at the top of the page to download what the list shows. The file follows the search, kind and date range you have set, newest first. Its columns are Time (UTC), Kind, Action, Description, Target, Details, Access token and Entry id. Secrets are left out exactly as they are on the page, and a value that starts with `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` gets a leading quote so a spreadsheet shows it as text. The response carries an `x-export-truncated` header that is `true` when more entries matched than the file holds.\n\n### Reading it from a script\n\nA token with `activity:read` can read the same log with `GET /api/v1/activity`, with the same kinds and ranges. `kind` is one of `all`, `projects`, `deployments`, `domains`, `servers`, `access`, `monitoring` and `terminal`. `range` is `24h`, `7d`, `30d` or `all`. `limit` is 1 to 100 and defaults to 50. The answer has `items`, `nextCursor` and `total`. Pass `nextCursor` back as `cursor` for the next page.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"https://your-egg/api/v1/activity?kind=deployments&range=24h&limit=20\"\n```\n\nA token with `activity:read` can also read the change feed with `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/changes`, or the `get_changes` tool. It is a newest-first list of one project’s deploys, rollbacks, redeploys, domain changes and build-config changes. An item can include `deploymentId` for API deploys, redeploys, rollbacks, cancels, project restarts, deployment lifecycle actions and preview deletions when the id belongs to the project. A project-linked domain removal appears in that project’s feed. Environment deletion and replacement summaries show key names, including for shared environment sets; values never appear.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Entries per page | 50 | Press Load more for the next 50. In the code: server/activity.ts (PAGE_SIZE) |\n| Search depth | The newest 5000 | A search reads in batches of 200 and says “Searched the latest 5,000 entries.” when it stopped at the limit. Narrow the date range instead. In the code: server/activity-query.ts (SCAN_LIMIT SCAN_BATCH) |\n| Search text | 80 characters | Anything longer is cut. In the code: server/activity.ts (MAX_QUERY) |\n| CSV export size | 5000 entries | The file stops there and x-export-truncated is true. Narrow the date range or search to export the rest. In the code: server/export-csv.ts (EXPORT_ROW_LIMIT) |\n| CSV export rate | 10 a minute per account | “Too many exports. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.” In the code: server/export-csv-data.ts (EXPORT_RATE) |\n| API page | 1 to 100, default 50 | A value outside the range is a 400. In the code: server/api-v1-more.ts (activity query) |\n| Detail length | 200 characters | A longer detail is cut with an ellipsis. In the code: server/activity.ts (MAX_VALUE) |\n| Retention | No pruning | Egg does not delete old entries. Deleting your account removes all of them. In the code: server/account.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `No activity yet`\n\n  Nothing has been recorded for your account yet. Make a change and it appears.\n- `Nothing in this range yet`\n\n  Pick a longer date range, or set Kind back to All. Press Clear filters.\n- `Could not load more`\n\n  Press Try again. If it keeps failing, reload the page. Your filters stay as they were.\n- `Searched the latest 5,000 entries.`\n\n  An older match may exist. Narrow the date range and search again.\n- `Too many exports. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 10 exports a minute for your account.\n- `A row shows the action name instead of a sentence`\n\n  The entry is of a kind this version has no wording for. Its time, target and details are still right.\n\n## Example: Find who deployed a project\n\n### Given\n\nA project named recipe-api that went live at an odd hour.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open Activity\n\n   Type recipe-api in the search box.\n2. Step 2: Narrow it\n\n   Set Kind to Deployments and Date range to Last 24 hours.\n\n### Result\n\nRows such as “Deployed through the deploy hook for recipe-api” or “Redeployed recipe-api” show the time and what started it, with the token’s name when a script did it.\n\n## Example: Keep a record of one month\n\n### Given\n\nYou want every token and sign-in change from the last 30 days in a spreadsheet.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Set the filters\n\n   Set Kind to Access and tokens and Date range to Last 30 days.\n2. Step 2: Export\n\n   Press Export CSV.\n\n### Result\n\nA file named for activity downloads with one row per entry, newest first. The Access token column names the token that made a change, and is empty for changes you made in the browser.\n\n**Related:** [Account](/docs/account), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring).\n","account":"Watch\n\nPage 36 of 40\n\n# Account\n\nYour Hack Club sign-in, the Settings page, the browsers that are signed in, the cards on the Account page, and how to delete your account.\n\nOpen Account from the account menu. It has cards for Profile, GitHub, Container permissions, Webhooks, Signed-in browsers and Delete account. How Egg looks and what new projects start with are on a separate page, Settings, which is in the same menu.\n\n- **Profile** shows “Shown as” (your display name and character, with a “Change in Settings” link), your Hack Club name, and your Email, or “Not shared” when there is no email. The Hack Club name and email are read only: change them at Hack Club. The name and character you are shown as are changed in Settings.\n- **GitHub** lists the accounts where the GitHub app is installed, each with a “Manage on GitHub” link. With none installed it says “Not connected yet. Import a project to install the app.”\n- **Container permissions** are covered on the [container](/docs/container) page.\n- **Webhooks** send a signed message to your own code when a deployment starts, succeeds or fails. They are covered on the [notifications](/docs/notifications) page.\n\n## The Settings page\n\nSettings holds your own choices. They are saved to your account as you make them, with a “Saved at” time under each card, and they follow you to other devices. The look is also kept in a browser cookie called `egg_prefs`, so a page is drawn the right way before it loads. Nothing on this page changes a project that already exists.\n\n| Card and setting | Choices | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Profile, Display name | Up to 40 characters. Shown in the menu and on the Account page. It is not used in any address, and two people can share a name. Control and invisible characters are refused. | Empty: your Hack Club name is shown |\n| Profile, Character | One of eight small pixel characters: Egg, Cat, Ghost, Robot, Frog, Alien, Owl or Mushroom. You cannot upload a picture. | Egg |\n| Appearance, Match my device | On or off. On, Egg uses your light theme while your device is light and your dark theme while it is dark, and switches by itself when the device does. Off, one theme stays whatever the device does. | On |\n| Appearance, Light theme | Shown while Match my device is on. One of the light themes: Egg Light, GitHub Light, VS Code Light+, Inspired by Catppuccin Latte, Inspired by Rosé Pine Dawn, Inspired by Solarized Light, Inspired by Ayu Light or Inspired by One Light, or a light theme of your own. | Egg Light |\n| Appearance, Dark theme | Shown while Match my device is on. One of the dark themes: Egg Dark, GitHub Dark, VS Code Dark+, Nord, Gruvbox Dark, Inspired by Dracula, Inspired by One Dark, Inspired by Tokyo Night, Inspired by Catppuccin Mocha, Inspired by Rosé Pine, Inspired by Monokai, Inspired by Ayu Dark or Inspired by Solarized Dark, or a dark theme of your own. | Egg Dark |\n| Appearance, Theme | Shown while Match my device is off. Any one theme, listed as Light themes and Dark themes, that stays as you picked it. | Egg Dark |\n| Appearance, Your themes | New theme opens an editor with a name, light or dark, and eight colours: page background, card surface, border, text, muted text, primary, link and danger. Each colour is a picker and a hex field. A sample page and the contrast checks update as you type. Edit, Export and Delete are on each saved theme, and Import reads a theme file. Up to 20 themes. | None |\n| Appearance, Density | Comfortable or Compact | Comfortable |\n| Deploys, Default branch | A valid git branch name of up to 100 characters. It is filled in as the production branch when you add a project from a pasted repository URL. A repository you pick from GitHub keeps its own default branch. | `main` |\n| Analytics defaults | Eight checkboxes: Clicks, Time on page, Funnel, Referrers, Speed and errors, Custom events, Live view and Retention. They decide which analytics features a project starts with the first time you turn analytics on for it. | All off |\n| Preferences, Motion | Device follows your system setting. Reduce turns animation off. Allow keeps transitions even if your device asks for less. | Device |\n\n- **Themes** are each light or dark, as in VS Code, and none has a second version. A theme sets every colour together: backgrounds, cards, borders, text, links, buttons, the selected item, the focus ring and the status colours. Themes apply in the signed-in pages; the public site keeps the Egg look. Egg Light and Egg Dark are the two defaults. Themes named “Inspired by” take their colours from well-known palettes and are not made or endorsed by those projects.\n- **Your own themes** are private to your account and follow you to every device. A colour is only ever a hex value such as `#1a2b3c` (3, 4, 6 or 8 digits); nothing else is accepted. Egg builds the rest of the theme from your eight colours. Text on the page and on a card must reach 4.5:1 contrast, or the theme is not saved, and Egg names the pair, shows the ratio and suggests the nearest colour that passes. Weaker pairs (button text, danger text, links, the focus ring at 3:1) only warn. An exported file holds the name, light or dark, and the colours, and nothing about you; an import over 8 KB or with unknown keys is refused. If you delete a theme you are using, Egg Light or Egg Dark takes its place.\n- **Earlier choices** are moved once. An account set to Light or Dark keeps that as one fixed theme, and one set to System now has Match my device on. An old accent becomes the nearest theme (Blue becomes GitHub Light, Nord becomes Nord, Amber and Gruvbox become Gruvbox Dark, and the rest become Egg Light). You can pick another at any time.\n- **Analytics defaults** only apply to a project the first time analytics is turned on for it. A project that already has analytics is not changed, and every project can still change its own switches. A Funnel switched on by default does nothing until the project has two steps. Retention stores a random id in the visitor’s browser, so leave it off unless you want returning visitors. Campaigns and Record visitor countries are not in this list: you turn them on for each project, and Campaigns also needs a list of allowed values. See [Analytics](/docs/analytics).\n- **Default branch** changes only the starting value of the form. You can still type another branch on each project.\n\n## Signing in and out\n\n### Sign in\n\n1. Step 1: Open the login page\n\n   Its heading is “Log in to Egg”.\n2. Step 2: Press Continue with Hack Club\n\n   Approve at Hack Club.\n3. Step 3: Land on Projects\n\n   Egg sets a session cookie that lasts 30 days.\n\nThe account menu has **Sign out**. It ends this browser’s session and closes every terminal your account has open. Some switches ask you to confirm with Hack Club again. That is a second sign-in that counts for 5 minutes, and it must be the same Hack Club account. A copy of Egg running in local mode has no Hack Club account, so it skips the confirmation.\n\nHack Club is the way to sign in until you add another. The **Sign in with a password** card on Account can also turn on sign-in with your email and a password. It is off until you turn it on. You can turn it on only within 5 minutes of a Hack Club sign-in. If your last one is older, Egg answers “Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning on password sign-in needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.” Sign in with Hack Club again, then retry. The account must share an email address with Egg, the password needs at least 12 characters, and Egg shows a recovery key during setup, which you confirm you saved before the password is accepted. You cannot turn it off if it would leave you with no way to sign in.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg never stores your Hack Club password. Hack Club proves who you are, and Egg keeps only a hash of its own session token. If you turn on a password, Egg keeps only a salted hash of it and of the recovery key, never the text itself. The 5 minute rule means a borrowed session cookie cannot add a password that would outlive the cookie. The second confirmation also exists because turning on a root shell is the one change that should not ride on a session cookie someone else may have borrowed from a laptop left open.\n\n## Signed-in browsers\n\nThe Signed-in browsers card lists each session with a label such as “Chrome on Windows” and when it was signed in. This one has a “This browser” badge. Press **Sign out** on another to end it, or **Sign out everywhere else** to end them all. Ending another session closes every terminal your account has open, because a terminal belongs to the account and not to one browser.\n\n## Deleting your account\n\n### Delete your account\n\n1. Step 1: Open the Delete account card\n\n   It is at the bottom of Account.\n2. Step 2: Type your name\n\n   Type your Hack Club name exactly as Profile shows it under Hack Club name. Spaces at the start and end are ignored.\n3. Step 3: Press Delete account\n\n   Your terminals close first, then your records are deleted.\n\nThis deletes your projects, servers, domains, keys, access tokens, webhooks, sessions and your activity log from Egg, and it cannot be undone. Nothing on your container is removed. Apps already there keep running, but nothing manages them any more. Delete the key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard as well, so Egg’s old public key does not stay on your container.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session | 30 days | Sign in again. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (SESSION_DAYS) |\n| Fresh sign-in | 5 minutes | It matters only for turning on Terminal, File changes or password sign-in. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (REAUTH_WINDOW_MS) |\n| Sign-in handshake | 10 minutes | Start again from the login page, in one browser. In the code: app/api/auth/login/route.ts (maxAge) |\n| Sign-in attempts | 20 a minute per address | A redirect to the login page. Wait a minute. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.signIn) |\n| Servers | 5 per account | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts (MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER) |\n| Access tokens | 25 active | Revoke one to make another. In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (MAX_TOKENS_PER_USER) |\n| Webhooks | 10 per account | “You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.” In the code: server/webhooks.ts (MAX_WEBHOOKS_PER_USER) |\n| Display name | 40 characters | “The display name can be at most 40 characters” In the code: server/preferences.ts |\n| Default branch | 100 characters, a valid git branch name | “That is not a valid git branch name” In the code: server/preferences.ts lib/preferences.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Hack Club sign-in is not set up on this installation yet.`\n\n  The button is off. On your own copy, set HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID and HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET.\n- `You did not approve the sign-in. Try again when you are ready.`\n\n  Press Continue with Hack Club and approve.\n- `That sign-in link was not valid. Please try again.`\n\n  Start again from the login page.\n- `The sign-in took too long or came from another browser. Please try again.`\n\n  Start and finish in one browser within 10 minutes.\n- `Hack Club could not sign you in. Please try again in a moment.`\n\n  Wait a minute and try again.\n- `Too many sign-in attempts. Wait a minute and try again.`\n\n  More than 20 sign-in attempts in a minute from one address. Wait a minute. On your own copy this only counts attempts when TRUST_PROXY is on.\n- `Something went wrong signing in.`\n\n  Try again. If it keeps happening, open an issue on GitHub.\n- `That is the session you are using. Sign out instead.`\n\n  Use Sign out in the account menu for this browser.\n- `Session not found`\n\n  That session already ended. Reload the page.\n- `Type your name exactly to confirm.`\n\n  Copy your Hack Club name from the Profile card.\n- `Could not delete the account`\n\n  Nothing was deleted. Try again.\n- `Could not sign that browser out`\n\n  Try again.\n- `Could not sign the others out`\n\n  Try again.\n- `A switch stays off after confirming`\n\n  You signed in with a different Hack Club account, so you land back on Account with no message. Sign in with the same account and flip the switch again.\n\n## Example: End a lost laptop’s session\n\n### Given\n\nYou signed in on a laptop you no longer have.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open Account\n\n   Find the other row under Signed-in browsers.\n2. Step 2: Press Sign out\n\n   Use the button on that row.\n\n### Result\n\nThe row disappears and any terminal that session had open closes. This browser stays signed in.\n\n## Example: Start clean after a scare\n\n### Given\n\nYou think a token or a cookie may have leaked.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Sign out everywhere else\n\n   Press it on the Signed-in browsers card. Every other session ends and your terminals close.\n2. Step 2: Revoke the tokens\n\n   On the Developer page, press Revoke on each token you do not recognise.\n3. Step 3: Read Activity\n\n   Set Kind to Access and tokens and look for entries you did not make.\n\n### Result\n\nOnly this browser stays signed in, no token works, and Activity shows what happened and when.\n\n**Related:** [Activity](/docs/activity), [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n","security":"Reference\n\nPage 37 of 40\n\n# Security\n\nHow Egg keeps your SSH key, your builds and your secrets, what each part can reach, and what the people who run it can and cannot see.\n\nEvery statement on this page is enforced by the server, and every number is the value the code uses. The Limits table at the end says where each one is set. To report a vulnerability, use the [security page](/security), not a public issue.\n\nWords used on this page. Your **container** is the Nest account Egg deploys into. A **server** is one connected container in Egg’s list. **Egg’s machine** is the computer that runs Egg: Egg’s own host, or your computer if you run a copy yourself. The **worker** is the part of Egg that takes queued deployments and runs them. A **build** installs and builds your project on your container, in a new release folder, under a **cap**, a memory and CPU limit that the kernel enforces. **Sealed** means encrypted, so it cannot be read without `APP_SECRET`. A **webhook** is a message one program sends to a web address when something happens. GitHub sends Egg one on every push, and Egg sends one to your own address if you add it under Webhooks. An **access token** is a secret you make on the Developer page for scripts and agents. A **session** is one signed-in browser.\n\n## The SSH key and the host key\n\nPress **Create key** on the Connect page. Egg makes one ed25519 key for that server and for nothing else. Then you paste the public half into the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard. The private half is sealed with AES-256-GCM before it is stored in Egg’s database. The sealing key comes from the `APP_SECRET` setting, and each sealed value is tied to its own record, so a value copied into another row does not open. The private key is never shown, and no API call returns it.\n\nThis key is root on your container\n\nSSH into Nest lands in a root shell inside your own container, so the key can do anything you can do there. The Connect page says so before you add it. Connect only a container you are happy to deploy to.\n\nEgg does not trust Nest’s SSH host on sight. The first time you press **Test connection**, Egg reaches the host, reads its host key, hangs up and shows the SHA-256 fingerprint in a “Confirm host key” box. Nothing is sent to your container yet. Check the fingerprint against the one Nest shows for your server, then press **Confirm host key**. Only then is the fingerprint pinned, which means stored and required from then on. Until it is pinned, no deploy, file change or terminal connects to the server. After that, if the host presents any other key, Egg stops before it sends anything and the connection fails.\n\n**New key** deletes Egg’s old private key at once and keeps the pin. The server goes back to “Waiting for the key”, and deploys, files and terminals fail until you paste the new public key into the Nest dashboard and press **Test connection**. Apps that are already running are not touched. To clear a pin, press **Disconnect** and connect the server again. That asks you to confirm the fingerprint again.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nNest cannot hand Egg a narrower login than yours, so the key is as strong as your own. A key made for one server means you can end Egg’s access by deleting that one key in the Nest dashboard, without touching anything else. The pin exists because SSH trusts whatever answers the first time. After you confirm the fingerprint, a different host is refused before any file or variable is sent. Egg cannot know the right fingerprint for you, so check it against the one Nest shows for your server. If you confirm a fingerprint without checking it and something sat between Egg and Nest at that moment, its key is the one that gets pinned.\n\nDisconnect asks about the server’s projects\n\nDisconnect closes the terminals opened with this server’s key and deletes Egg’s copy of the private key. The public key stays on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard until you delete it there. When the server has projects, a box asks whether to delete them as well: “Also delete the project on this server”, or “Also delete the N projects on this server”. Left unticked, which is the default, the projects are kept, the server shows as disconnected, and connecting the same Nest username again reattaches them. Ticked, the projects and their deployments and logs are deleted from Egg. Nothing on the container is removed either way, and running apps keep running.\n\n### Take access back\n\n1. Step 1: Delete the key in Nest\n\n   Egg’s access ends at once. Your apps keep running, because they are ordinary services in your container (systemd, the Linux service manager) and do not need Egg to run.\n\n   In the dashboard: Nest dashboardthen SSH Keysthen Delete the key\n2. Step 2: Check it\n\n   Press **Test connection** on the Connect page. It should report “Could not connect”.\n3. Step 3: Tidy up\n\n   Press **Disconnect** when you are done with the server, or **New key** to start again with a fresh key.\n\n## Where builds run\n\nA build turns your repository into files your app can run. Egg does it in four steps. Three of them happen on your container, so this page says what they use.\n\n### Where each step runs\n\n1. Step 1: Clone and pack the source, on Egg’s machine\n\n   The worker downloads a shallow copy of your branch from github.com (the latest commit only) into a work folder and packs the source into one archive, a compressed .tar.gz file. Nothing is installed or built there. The GitHub token is used for the clone and is never sent to your container. The clone stops after 5 minutes. The work folder is deleted when the deployment ends.\n2. Step 2: Check your container, then upload and unpack\n\n   Before anything is uploaded, Egg looks at your container: the Node.js version (the project’s engines range, or 22.13 or newer), the package manager the lockfile needs, Python 3 and venv for a Python project, systemd-run, timeout, and at least 2048 MB of free disk. If a check fails, the deployment ends with one line that says what to fix, and nothing is uploaded. If all pass, Egg uploads the source over SSH and unpacks it into a new release, a folder that holds one deployed version of your app. The running release is not touched.\n3. Step 3: Install and build, on your container, under a cap\n\n   Your install and build commands run in the new release folder, then the production install. All of it runs under the cap, a hard memory and CPU limit that the kernel enforces. The table below lists it. Your app keeps serving the whole time. Your project’s variables are written to a file with mode 600 for the build, and Egg deletes that file when the build ends. If the build fails, runs out of memory, times out or is canceled, Egg deletes the new folder and the running release stays.\n4. Step 4: Switch, on your container\n\n   Egg writes the release’s variables file, points the service at the new release and restarts it, so the app is unavailable for a moment. Then it waits up to 45 seconds for the app to answer.\n\nIf the clone, the checks, the build or the upload fails, the deployment shows Error and your container keeps running its current release. If the new release does not answer after the restart, a production deploy puts the previous release back and shows Error.\n\nThe cap is set like this:\n\n| Setting | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Memory | The server’s Build memory (MB), 512 MB by default, set on the Container page. A project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB while it builds, if your container has room for it (its memory minus 512 MB is at least 1024 MB). Installs use the setting. It is never more than your container’s memory minus 512 MB, which stays free for your app. If that leaves less than 256 MB, the build does not start. There is no swap, so the limit is real. |\n| CPU | The server’s Build CPU limit (cores), 2 by default, as a quota of 100% for each core. The build also runs at a low priority, so your app gets the CPU first when it is busy. |\n| Processes | At most 4096. |\n| Time | 15 minutes, then the build is stopped. This limit cannot be changed. |\n| Builds at once | One at a time on a container. A second build waits for up to 30 minutes. |\n| If memory runs out | The kernel is told to stop the build before your app, so a build that goes over its cap is the one that dies. |\n| What it can see | Everything your Nest user can see on your container. The cap limits memory and CPU. It does not hide your files from the build, which runs as the same user as your app. |\n| Network | Normal access from your container, because installing packages needs it. |\n| What it is given | Your project’s variables for a production or a preview deployment, and `CI=true`. |\n\n- **What is checked first.** The repository must be a plain `https` URL on `github.com`. A branch name may use letters, digits, dots, underscores, hyphens and slashes, and may not start with a hyphen or contain `..`, so it cannot be read as a Git option. The root and output folders are resolved to their real paths, so a link inside a repository cannot pull in a file from Egg’s machine.\n- **What is limited.** The packed source may be 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes). A larger one fails the deployment before anything is uploaded. The work folder is deleted when the deployment ends. A deployment stores at most 20,000 log lines, and then one line says “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” The build carries on. Colour codes and control characters are removed from each line, and a line is cut at 4000 characters.\n- **Pull requests from forks are never built**, because that would run a stranger’s code next to your variables.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA build runs code from a repository, so it has to run somewhere. Egg runs it on your container, a machine you already control and already run your own code on, and keeps strangers’ build code off the machine that holds everyone’s data. The cap protects your running app from a build that uses too much memory or CPU. It does not protect your files from the build. That is a deliberate trade, and it is why you should build only code you wrote or trust, and why variables should hold only what the build needs. The build has network access, because a build that cannot install packages is no build.\n\n## Variables and secrets\n\n- **Environment variables** are sealed with AES-256-GCM, tied to the project, the name and where the value applies. The list shows names only. The browser routes never send a value back, and the API returns one only through `get_env_vars` for a variable marked non-secret. Each variable has a secret flag, on by default. At deploy time they are written to a `.env` file on your container with mode 600.\n- **Log masking.** A value is replaced by `***` in build and deploy logs and in a deployment’s error text. The base64 form of a value (padded, unpadded or URL-safe) and its percent-encoded form are replaced too. A value of 2 or 3 characters is masked only where it stands alone, not inside a longer word or number. A value of one character is never masked, because it would garble every line. A value that spans several lines, such as a PEM key, is also masked line by line for each line of 8 or more characters. Any other change to a value, such as hex or a hash, is not recognised. Do not print secrets.\n- **Deploy hooks, access tokens and sessions.** Only a SHA-256 of each is stored. A hook URL and a token secret are shown once, when you make them.\n- **Notification webhook URLs and webhook signing secrets** are sealed. A Discord or Slack URL is never shown again. A signing secret starts with `whsec_` and is shown once.\n- **Analytics keys** are the exception: a key starting with `nda_` sits in your page source on purpose, and can only add a page view to its own project.\n\nWhere a value can still be read\n\nThe Files tab and the API both refuse `current/.env` and each release’s `.env`, by name and after links are followed, so a link in `shared/` cannot reach them. A terminal can still read them, because a shell reads anything its user can. That is why the terminal is off by default and why an access token with the terminal permission lives 7 days at most. Treat the terminal switch, and your Egg sign-in, as the key to your variables.\n\n## Sign-in and requests\n\n- **Sign-in.** Hack Club Auth proves who you are. Egg asks only for `openid`, `profile` and `email`, and none of that reaches your container. The sign-in handshake must finish within 10 minutes, in the browser that began it.\n- **Session.** A random token in an `httpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax` cookie called `egg_session`, marked `Secure` in production. It lasts 30 days. Only a hash is stored. Signing in again replaces the session you were using, so an old cookie does not live on.\n- **Requests.** Every change from the browser must come from this site, because the `Origin` header is checked. A write with no `Origin` that the browser marks as cross-site is refused too. The API and the MCP endpoint never read cookies. They take a bearer token only. A JSON request from the dashboard is read up to 256 KB and a larger one is refused with 413.\n- **Sensitive switches.** The Terminal and File changes switches need a Hack Club sign-in from the last 5 minutes before they turn on. Turning one off never asks.\n- **Guessing.** One IP address may make 20 sign-in attempts a minute, and 60 requests a minute with access tokens that are not valid. Past that the answer is 429. Egg reads the address from the proxy headers only when the `TRUST_PROXY` setting is on. It is off by default, so with it off there is no address to count and these two limits do not apply. Turn it on only when every request reaches Egg through a reverse proxy you run.\n- **GitHub events.** A message from GitHub is accepted only with a valid `X-Hub-Signature-256` signature over the raw body. The body is capped at 5 MB, and a request with no length or a missing or malformed signature is refused before its body is read. Each message has an id, called a delivery id, and Egg remembers it for 1 hour, so a replayed delivery does nothing. A deploy hook takes a body of 4 KB at most.\n- **Browser headers.** Every page is sent with `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and a policy that forbids framing, `nosniff`, a strict referrer policy and a permissions policy that turns off the camera, microphone, location and payment. In production it also sends `Strict-Transport-Security` for two years. The content policy still allows inline scripts, because Next.js writes the ones that start a page.\n\n## Who can see what\n\n- **You** can see everything that belongs to your account and nothing that belongs to anyone else. Another account’s project, server or deployment answers as if it did not exist.\n- **The people who run Egg** have no admin screen in the product. What shows through the site is the public stats page, which gives totals only: a count under 10 shows as “fewer than 10”, a larger one keeps two significant figures, and a percentage or median appears only with at least 50 items behind it.\n- **They also host the database and hold the secret.** `APP_SECRET` seals your SSH key and your variables. The sealing protects a copied database or a backup. It does not stop whoever holds both the database and the secret. If that is not acceptable, run your own copy.\n- **The public** can see three things you turn on: a status page, the analytics script and its collector, and the comment on a pull request. A status page shows the project name, whether the app answers, a 30-day strip and the latest 10 outages, and nothing else. The collector answers 204 whether or not a key exists, so keys cannot be probed.\n- **Your receivers**, the addresses you add under Webhooks, get a signed message when a deployment starts, succeeds or fails. It never carries variables, logs, commit messages or errors. Egg refuses to send to a private or internal address and does not follow redirects.\n- **The log of your actions** records what you did, with names and paths. It never records the contents of a file, a variable’s value, or what you typed or saw in the terminal, and anything that looks like a secret is dropped before an entry is shown.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session | 30 days | The cookie and the stored session both expire. Sign in again. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (SESSION_DAYS) |\n| Fresh sign-in | 5 minutes | Older than that, turning on Terminal or File changes sends you to Hack Club again, and turning on password sign-in is refused until you sign in again. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (REAUTH_WINDOW_MS) |\n| Sign-in handshake | 10 minutes | A sign-in that takes longer fails. Start again from the login page, in one browser. In the code: app/api/auth/login/route.ts (maxAge) |\n| Sign-in attempts | 20 a minute per address | A redirect to the login page. Wait a minute. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.signIn) |\n| Invalid access tokens | 60 a minute per address | 429 with Retry-After. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.badToken) |\n| Token with terminal or file changes | 7 days at most | The token form refuses a longer expiry. In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (SHORT_LIVED_MAX_DAYS) |\n| Terminals | 5 per account, 50 on the server | “You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.” In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS) |\n| Servers per account | 5 | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts (MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER) |\n| GitHub delivery | 5 MB, id remembered 1 hour | A larger body or a repeated id is dropped. In the code: app/api/webhooks/github/route.ts server/github/webhook.ts (DELIVERY_TTL_MS) |\n| Deploy hook body | 4 KB | 413. In the code: app/api/hooks/deploy/[token]/route.ts (MAX_BODY_BYTES) |\n| Dashboard JSON request | 256 KB | 413 “That request is too large.” In the code: server/body-limit.ts (JSON_BODY_LIMIT) |\n| Host key | Confirmed by you on the first Test connection | Nothing connects until it is confirmed. A different key later stops the connection. In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | “The build timed out.” Nothing changes on your container. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts (clone timeoutMs) |\n| Build time | 15 minutes. It cannot be changed. | The build is stopped and the deployment fails with “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” The new release folder is deleted and the live release keeps running. In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) |\n| Free disk before a build | 2048 MB | The deployment ends before the upload with “Free up disk space on your container: <n> MB is free and a build needs at least 2048 MB.” In the code: server/deploy/container-probe.ts (MIN_FREE_DISK_MB) |\n| Build output | 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes), measured on the packed archive | The deployment fails with the size in the message, before the upload. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts (MAX_ARCHIVE_BYTES) |\n| Unpack the source on your container | 15 minutes | The deployment fails and the live release keeps running. In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (unpackSourceScript) |\n| Build log | 20,000 lines | One line says the rest was dropped. The build carries on. In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts (MAX_LOG_ROWS) |\n| Build memory | 512 MB by default, 512 MB up to the server’s memory. A project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB while it builds, if the container has room. A build never gets more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB, and does not start under 256 MB. | “Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts server/deploy/container-build.ts (containerMemoryCapMb) |\n| Build CPU | 2 cores by default, 0.5 to the Nest server’s reported cores in 0.25-core steps | “Build CPU must be a number from 0.5 to <max> cores in 0.25-core steps.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts server/deploy/container-build.ts (cpuQuotaPercent) |\n| Notification post | 5 seconds | The post is dropped and logged. Redirects are refused. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `The host key of <host> changed (was <old>, now <new>). Nothing was sent. If you expected this, reconnect the server.`\n\n  Stop. Something else answered where Nest should be, or Nest changed its key. If you expected it, Disconnect and add the server again, and choose whether to keep its projects.\n- `Nest rejected the key. Check that you pasted the public key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and that the username is exactly your Nest username.`\n\n  Paste the public key again. After New key, the old one stops working.\n- `Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning this on needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.`\n\n  403. Flip the switch again. It sends you through Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied.\n- `<name> is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.`\n\n  409. You kept the projects when you disconnected. Connect the same Nest username again from Connect and run Test connection.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Many invalid tokens came from your address. Fix the token your script sends, then wait.\n\n## Example: End Egg’s access\n\n### Given\n\nA server connected as sam-r, with two projects running.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Delete the key in Nest\n\n   In the dashboard: Nest dashboardthen SSH Keysthen Delete the key\n2. Step 2: Test the connection\n\n   In Egg, open Connect and press **Test connection**.\n\n### Result\n\nThe server shows “Could not connect” with “Nest rejected the key.” Both apps still answer, because their services are still running on your container.\n\n## Example: See a secret masked\n\n### Given\n\nA project with a variable `STRIPE_KEY` set to `sk_live_abc123`, and a build command of `echo \"key is $STRIPE_KEY\"`.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Deploy\n\n   Press Deploy and open the deployment’s log.\n2. Step 2: Find the line\n\n   Look for the line the echo printed.\n\n### Result\n\nThe log shows `key is ***`. The same happens if the build prints the base64 form of the value. Had the value been printed as a hash or in hex, the log would have shown it.\n\n**Related:** [Servers](/docs/servers), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Environment variables](/docs/env), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n","selfhost":"Reference\n\nPage 38 of 40\n\n# Running your own\n\nEgg is open source. You can run your own copy, with your own database, secret and worker.\n\nYou need Node.js and pnpm. This page lists what the code reads. The [self-hosting guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md) walks through a private install on your own computer and a server run with Docker Compose, the [customising guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/customising.md) lists the files to change for the name and the wording, and the [backend setup guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/backend-setup.md) has the Hack Club and GitHub registration steps in full.\n\n## Starting your own copy\n\n### Start it locally\n\n1. Step 1: Install and set up\n\n   From the `web` folder, `pnpm run setup` writes `.env.local` with a fresh `APP_SECRET`. It never overwrites an existing file. Use `pnpm run setup`, not `pnpm setup`: pnpm has a built-in command of that name that does something else.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   cd web\n   pnpm install\n   pnpm run setup\n   ```\n2. Step 2: Pick a way to sign in\n\n   Setup writes `LOCAL_MODE=1`, so the login page gets a **Sign in on this computer** button and you need no external account. On first use, enter the generated `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET` from `.env.local`and choose a password; use that password for later sign-ins. Or set the Hack Club values to use Hack Club sign-in. Development sign-in is off by default.\n3. Step 3: Start it\n\n   `pnpm egg start` creates `.env.local` if it is missing, checks the computer, builds Egg itself the first time, which makes that first start slower and uses your computer’s CPU and disk, starts it on the port in `APP_URL` and opens the browser. Setup turns on `RUN_WORKER=1`, so the queue runs in the same process. Ctrl+C stops it. Add `--dev` to run the development server instead.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   pnpm egg start\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Try deploys without a Nest account\n\n   Run `pnpm fake-nest` in a second terminal. Set `NEST_HOST=127.0.0.1` and `NEST_PORT=2222`, plus the `UNIT_DIR` value it prints, before you add the server. It is for trying things out only, never a substitute for a real Nest container. On Windows it runs its commands in WSL. The first Test connection still asks you to confirm the host key.\n\n`pnpm run doctor` only checks the computer and starts nothing. A cross stops Egg from starting. A warning does not: for example, a missing optional tool is shown as a warning, and Egg itself starts without it. You can also run `pnpm build` and then `pnpm start` yourself. The database changes apply the first time a new version opens the file.\n\nYour data is one file, `web/data/egg.db`. It stays on disk when Egg stops. Back it up together with `APP_SECRET`, because the file cannot open your sealed keys without it.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nLocal mode is off unless you set `LOCAL_MODE=1`. It requires a private `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET` of at least 32 characters to set or recover the owner password. Keep the recovery key private. It also refuses to start unless `APP_URL`is localhost, `127.0.0.1` or `::1`. `LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE=1` exists for a copy behind a VPN or an access proxy you control, where something else decides who can reach the site.\n\n## Start on login\n\n`pnpm run service:install` writes one file that starts Egg when you sign in to your computer. It is optional. Nothing starts until you run the command it prints. Run `pnpm egg start` once first, so a build exists, then press Ctrl+C. `pnpm run service:remove` stops the service and deletes the file.\n\n- **macOS.** A LaunchAgent at `~/Library/LaunchAgents/app.egg.web.plist`. Turn it on with `launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u)` and that file. The log is `~/Library/Logs/egg.log`.\n- **Linux.** A systemd user unit at `~/.config/systemd/user/egg.service`. Turn it on with `systemctl --user enable --now egg`. To start at boot without signing in, run `loginctl enable-linger`.\n- **Windows.** A script at `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Egg\\egg-task.ps1`. Run it to register a Scheduled Task named Egg that runs `pnpm start` at sign-in.\n\n## Settings Egg reads\n\n| Setting | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `APP_SECRET` | Required, 32 or more characters. Seals SSH keys and variables. Back it up: losing or changing it makes stored keys unreadable. |\n| `APP_URL` | The public address. Used for sign-in redirects, hook URLs and links. Default http://localhost:3000. |\n| `DATABASE_URL` | The SQLite file. Default file:./data/egg.db. Migrations apply on startup. |\n| `HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID, HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET` | From a Hack Club Auth app. Without them the Continue with Hack Club button is off. |\n| `LOCAL_MODE` | 1 or true enables password-based owner sign-in without an external account. Off by default. Refused unless APP_URL is localhost, 127.0.0.1 or ::1. |\n| `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET` | Required in local mode; a private random recovery key of at least 32 characters for first-time setup and password reset. Setup generates it for new installations. |\n| `LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE` | 1 lets LOCAL_MODE run on another address. Use it only behind a VPN or access proxy you control. |\n| `TRUST_PROXY` | Off by default. 1 or true makes Egg believe X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP for the visitor’s address. Set it to 1 only when every request reaches Egg through a reverse proxy you run that sets those headers itself. Without it, every visitor counts as one address for analytics, and the sign-in and invalid-token limits do not apply. |\n| `DEV_LOGIN` | 1 shows a fake sign-in in pnpm dev. It is ignored in a production build. |\n| `GITHUB_APP_ID, GITHUB_APP_SLUG, GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID, GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET, GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY, GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | All six are needed for private repositories, the repository picker and deploys on push. The private key may use \\n escapes. |\n| `RUN_WORKER` | 1 runs the queue inside the web process. Anything else means no builds run there. Use pnpm worker. |\n| `WORKER_CONCURRENCY` | Deployments at once. Default 2. One project never builds twice at a time. Read by the worker, not by config.ts. |\n| `DEPLOY_WORK_DIR` | Where builds are cloned. Default ./data/work. |\n| `MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS` | How often uptime and usage are checked. Default 300000. 0 turns the checks off. |\n| `NEST_HOST, NEST_PORT, UNIT_DIR` | Nest’s SSH host (hackclub.app, 22) and where systemd units are written (/etc/systemd/system). Change only for testing. |\n\nBuild memory is not a setting in the file. It is per server, on that server’s Container page. See [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container).\n\n## The worker that runs builds\n\nThe worker takes queued deployments and runs the uptime monitor. It polls every second and never runs two jobs of one project at once. With `RUN_WORKER=1` it lives inside the web process. For production you can set `RUN_WORKER=0` and run `pnpm worker` on another machine. It finishes running jobs when it gets SIGINT or SIGTERM. Every project needs a worker, because the worker clones the repository and runs the container build over SSH. The build itself runs on the project’s own container, so the machine that runs the worker needs no Docker.\n\nWhat a deployment costs the worker’s machine: a clone and a packed copy of the source in `DEPLOY_WORK_DIR`, kept until the deployment ends, and an upload over SSH. Two deployments can run at once by default. Set `WORKER_CONCURRENCY=1` to allow one at a time. The install and build use the container’s memory and CPU, under the cap set for that server.\n\nA worker renews a lease on its running job every 15 seconds. If the lease is 60 seconds old, Egg logs a warning and leaves the job running, because commands it started on your container may still be changing files. That deployment keeps its project blocked until you recover it by hand. See [Recovering a stale deployment](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md) in the self-hosting guide.\n\nWithout a worker, nothing builds\n\nIf `RUN_WORKER` is not `1` and no `pnpm worker` is running, deployments stay queued, and uptime checks and resource history do not run.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe queue is a table in the database, and a job is claimed with one conditional update, so two workers can share it and neither takes the same job. Builds are kept off the web process on purpose when you can: a long build then cannot slow the pages. One process is still enough for one person, which is why setup turns the embedded worker on.\n\n## The database and backups\n\nThe database is one SQLite file at `DATABASE_URL`. Users, servers, projects, deployments, logs, jobs and history are all in it. Back up the file together with `APP_SECRET`: the file alone cannot open your sealed keys, and the secret alone has nothing to open. Keep them in different places if the backup leaves your machine. Stop Egg before you copy the file, and copy any `-wal` or `-shm` files beside it. Foreign keys are on, so deleting a server record deletes its projects.\n\n## Hack Club and GitHub apps\n\n- **Hack Club Auth.** Make an app at `auth.hackclub.com/developer/apps` with the redirect URI `<APP_URL>/api/auth/callback`.\n- **GitHub App.** Callback and setup URL `<APP_URL>/api/github/callback` with “Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation” on, webhook URL `<APP_URL>/api/webhooks/github`, repository permissions Contents and Metadata read only, Pull requests read and write, and the Push and Pull request events.\n\n## Run on a Linux server with Docker Compose\n\nTo run Egg on a Linux server, use Docker Compose. You need Docker and Docker Compose, a domain that points at the server, and ports 80 and 443 open. Caddy sits in front of the app, and gets and renews HTTPS by itself on ports 80 and 443, and 443/udp for HTTP/3. The app is not reachable on any other port.\n\n### Start it on a server\n\n1. Step 1: Get the code and the settings file\n\n   Clone the repository and copy the example file to .env in the repository root.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   git clone https://github.com/jeremy341/egg.git\n   cd egg\n   cp deploy/.env.example .env\n   ```\n2. Step 2: Set the address and the secret\n\n   In `.env`, set `APP_URL` (starting with https://), `APP_HOST` (the same name without https://) and `APP_SECRET`. Make the secret with the command below and back it up.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   openssl rand -base64 32\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Make the data folder\n\n   The Docker containers that run Egg use user 1000, so that user must own the data folder. The path must match `EGG_DATA_DIR`.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   sudo mkdir -p /srv/egg/data && sudo chown -R 1000:1000 /srv/egg/data\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Start it\n\n   From `web/`, `pnpm run compose:up` does the same.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   docker compose up -d --build\n   ```\n\nNothing is built on this server\n\nThe `worker` service in the Compose file runs every deployment. It clones the repository, packs the source and sends it to the project’s Nest container, where the install and build run under a cap. So no service here needs Docker access or extra privileges, and the worker starts with the rest. Without a running worker, deployments stay queued.\n\nThe example file sets `TRUST_PROXY=1`, because every request comes through Caddy. Do not publish the app port yourself. `sh deploy/backup.sh` (or `pnpm run backup`) makes a consistent copy while Egg runs, gzips it into `backups/` and keeps the newest 14. `sh deploy/upgrade.sh` backs up first, pulls, rebuilds and restarts. Database changes apply when the app starts.\n\nSecurity on a server: a signed-in JSON request is limited to 256 KB. One address may make 20 sign-in attempts a minute and 60 requests a minute with unrecognised tokens, counted only when `TRUST_PROXY` is on. The content policy still allows inline scripts, and the nonce fix is open. See [Security](/docs/security).\n\n## What works where\n\n| System | Support |\n| --- | --- |\n| Windows | Full. pnpm fake-nest needs WSL. |\n| Linux | Full. |\n| macOS | The same as Linux. |\n\nThe CI workflow runs the typecheck, lint, tests and the dependency audit on Linux and macOS. The audit fails the Linux run on a high finding and only reports it on macOS.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Concurrent deployments | 2 by default | Set WORKER_CONCURRENCY to change it. In the code: instrumentation.ts (WORKER_CONCURRENCY) |\n| Build time | 15 minutes, on the container. It cannot be changed. | “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) |\n| Build memory and CPU | 512 MB (at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step, when the container has room) and 2 cores by default for each server; both are configurable | The kernel stops the build at its cap. With memory, exit 137 and “The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (systemdRunArgs) lib/build-memory.ts |\n| Uptime check | Every 300000 ms by default | Set MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS to 0 to stop checks. In the code: server/jobs.ts (MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS) |\n| Secret length | 32 characters at least | “APP_SECRET must be at least 32 characters” In the code: server/config.ts (APP_SECRET) |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Invalid environment configuration`\n\n  The first request or the worker start checks every setting, and the lines under it name the one that is wrong. Fix it, or run pnpm run setup.\n- `APP_SECRET must be at least 32 characters`\n\n  Use a longer secret. pnpm run setup makes one.\n- `LOCAL_MODE signs anyone in as the owner, so APP_URL must be localhost or 127.0.0.1. Set APP_URL to a local address, or set LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE=1 if you control who can reach the site.`\n\n  Set APP_URL=http://localhost:3000, or read the override above before you set LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE.\n- `The recovery key is incorrect.`\n\n  Enter the LOCAL_MODE_SECRET from your private .env.local file.\n- `The login page has no Sign in on this computer button`\n\n  LOCAL_MODE is not 1 or true, or Egg was not restarted after the change. Edit .env.local and restart.\n- `The GitHub App is not configured.`\n\n  Set all six GITHUB_APP settings and GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET.\n- `webhook not configured`\n\n  The webhook route answers 503 until GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET is set.\n- `Hack Club sign-in is not set up on this installation yet.`\n\n  Set HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID and HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET, or use local mode.\n- `Egg did not start. Fix the lines marked with a cross, then run this again.`\n\n  The doctor found a problem that stops Egg. Read the Fix line under each cross. pnpm run doctor shows the same list.\n- `Port 3000 is already in use`\n\n  Another program uses the port in APP_URL. Stop it, or change the port in APP_URL in .env.local.\n- `Deployments stay Queued on a server`\n\n  The worker service is not running. Run docker compose ps, then docker compose up -d. Read its output with docker compose logs worker.\n- `Deployments stay Queued`\n\n  No worker is running. Set RUN_WORKER=1, or start pnpm worker.\n\n## Example: Move to a new machine\n\n### Given\n\nA copy running on one machine with a database file and an .env.local.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Stop it\n\n   Stop the web process and any worker.\n2. Step 2: Copy two things\n\n   Copy the database file and the APP_SECRET value to the new machine.\n3. Step 3: Set and start\n\n   Set DATABASE_URL and APP_SECRET, then start it.\n\n### Result\n\nYour servers and variables open as before. With a different APP_SECRET, stored keys cannot be read and you would have to make new keys.\n\n## Example: Run a private copy on your laptop\n\n### Given\n\nA laptop with Node.js and pnpm, and a Nest container to deploy to.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Set up\n\n   Run pnpm install and pnpm run setup in the web folder.\n2. Step 2: Check local mode\n\n   Setup wrote LOCAL_MODE=1 and LOCAL_MODE_SECRET to .env.local. Add a random secret of at least 32 characters to older files.\n3. Step 3: Start\n\n   Run pnpm egg start. It checks the computer, builds the first time and opens the browser.\n4. Step 4: Sign in\n\n   Use LOCAL_MODE_SECRET from .env.local to set your password on first use, then sign in with that password.\n\n### Result\n\nYou land on Projects as the owner. Connect your Nest username, paste the key into the Nest dashboard, press Test connection, and deploy as usual.\n\n**Related:** [Security](/docs/security), [Limits](/docs/limits), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n","limits":"Reference\n\nPage 39 of 40\n\n# Limits\n\nEvery number Egg enforces, grouped by what it limits, with the place in the code that sets it.\n\nEach table has a third column with the setting or constant that holds the number. Paths are inside `web/src`. If a number here ever disagrees with what you see, the code is right and the page is the bug.\n\n## Projects and deploys\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Servers per account | 5. | `MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER, server/servers.ts` |\n| Project name | 1 to 60 characters. | `createProjectInput, server/projects.ts` |\n| Project slug | Lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, 1 to 40 characters, not starting or ending with a hyphen. | `SLUG, server/deploy/layout.ts` |\n| Port | 1024 to 65535, and not 20000 to 29999, which previews use. | `port, server/projects.ts` |\n| Commands | 500 characters each, on one line. | `command, server/projects.ts` |\n| Root directory | 200 characters, relative, no .. | `rootDir, server/projects.ts` |\n| Branch name | 200 characters. Letters, digits, dots, underscores, hyphens and slashes, not starting with a hyphen. | `branch, server/projects.ts` |\n| Deployments at once | One per project. Two at a time overall by default. One build at a time on a container. | `WORKER_CONCURRENCY, instrumentation.ts, server/deploy/build-lock.ts` |\n| Clone | 5 minutes, on Egg’s machine. | `server/deploy/build.ts` |\n| Build | 15 minutes, on your container. It cannot be changed. | `BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, server/deploy/container-build.ts` |\n| Build output | 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes), measured on the packed archive. | `MAX_ARCHIVE_BYTES, server/deploy/build.ts` |\n| Build cap | On your container, for each build: a CPU cap (2 cores by default), 4096 processes, no swap, and memory (512 MB by default, at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when the container has room, never more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB). The CPU cap and memory are set for each server. | `systemdRunArgs, server/deploy/container-build.ts` |\n| Build memory | 512 MB up to the server’s reported memory, or 8192 MB when it reports none. Default 512 MB. | `lib/build-memory.ts` |\n| Free disk before a build | 2048 MB. | `MIN_FREE_DISK_MB, server/deploy/container-probe.ts` |\n| Build log | 20,000 lines, 4000 characters each. | `MAX_LOG_ROWS, server/deploy/logger.ts` |\n| Health wait | 45 seconds after the switch. The service restarts at the switch, so the app is unavailable for a moment. A failed production deploy puts the previous release back. | `healthTimeoutSeconds, server/deploy/pipeline.ts` |\n| Production releases kept | The 5 newest ready ones, plus the live one. | `KEEP_RELEASES, server/deploy/layout.ts` |\n| Previews kept | The 2 newest. | `KEEP_PREVIEWS, server/deploy/layout.ts` |\n| Preview ports | 20000 to 29999, one per preview. | `PREVIEW_PORT_MIN, PREVIEW_PORT_MAX, server/projects.ts` |\n| Environment variables | 100 per project, names up to 100 characters, values up to 10,000. | `envInput, server/projects.ts` |\n| Bulk cancel or delete of previews | 50 per request. | `MAX_BULK, server/deployments-bulk.ts` |\n\n## Container features\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal | Closes after 10 minutes without input or 60 minutes in total. | `TERMINAL_LIMITS, server/terminal.ts` |\n| Terminals open | 1 per project per person, 5 per account, 50 on the server. | `TERMINAL_LIMITS, server/terminal.ts` |\n| Terminal input | 8 KB a request, 300 requests a minute. | `inputBytes, RATE_LIMITS.terminalInput` |\n| Terminal output kept | The last 64 KB. | `bufferBytes, server/terminal.ts` |\n| File preview and edit | The first 1 MB. | `READ_LIMIT, server/files-paths.ts` |\n| File upload and download | 50 MB. | `UPLOAD_LIMIT, DOWNLOAD_LIMIT, server/files-paths.ts` |\n| Folder listing | 3000 entries. | `LIST_LIMIT, server/files.ts` |\n| File reads | 120 a minute. | `FILE_READ_LIMIT, server/files.ts` |\n| File changes | 60 a minute. | `RATE_LIMITS.fileWrite, server/rate-limit.ts` |\n| Leftover removal | 50 items a request, 10 requests a minute. | `MAX_ORPHANS_PER_REQUEST, server/inventory.ts` |\n| Conflict checks | 20 a minute. | `app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts` |\n| Scheduled jobs | 5 per project. At most one run every 5 minutes. A run is stopped after 30 minutes. | `MAX_JOBS_PER_PROJECT, server/scheduled-jobs.ts; MIN_GAP_MINUTES, lib/cron-schedule.ts; JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, server/deploy/jobs-remote.ts` |\n\n## Access tokens and the API\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Active tokens | 25 per account. Revoked and expired ones do not count. | `MAX_TOKENS_PER_USER, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Token name | 1 to 60 characters. | `createTokenInput, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Token life | 7, 30, 90 or 365 days. 90 is the default. | `createTokenInput, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Terminal or file changes | A token holding either lives 7 days at most. | `SHORT_LIVED_MAX_DAYS, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Reads | 120 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.read, server/api-v1-kit.ts` |\n| Changes | 30 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.write` |\n| Calls that open a session to your container | 10 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.ssh` |\n| Terminal input through the API | 300 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.input` |\n| Invalid tokens | 60 a minute per address. Counted only when TRUST_PROXY is on. | `RATE_LIMITS.badToken, server/rate-limit.ts` |\n| Request body | 16 KB. A file write takes 4 MB, terminal input 64 KB, webhooks 4 KB and the MCP endpoint 64 KB. | `DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES, server/api-v1-kit.ts` |\n| File write through the API | 1 MB. A download is 2 MB. | `server/api-v1-shell.ts` |\n| List sizes | Deployments 1 to 100, runtime log lines 1 to 1000, build log lines 1 to 1000, activity 1 to 100, analytics 1 to 90 days, resource history 1 to 168 hours. | `query fields in server/api-v1*.ts` |\n| Webhooks | 10 per account. | `MAX_WEBHOOKS_PER_USER, server/webhooks.ts` |\n\n## Watching and history\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Uptime checks | Every 5 minutes by default. Down after 2 failed checks in a row, which you can set from 1 to 10 per project. | `MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS, server/jobs.ts` |\n| Alert limits | Disk, memory and CPU 1 to 100, default 90. Slow response 50 to 60000 ms. | `server/notify.ts` |\n| Resource history | Sampled every 5 minutes, kept 7 days, shown for 24 hours or 7 days. | `SAMPLE_KEEP_MS, server/monitor.ts` |\n| Runtime logs | Up to 300 lines shown, and 300 more each time you press Load older lines. A search or the Warnings and errors level reads up to 1000 lines for each read. | `components/logs-view.tsx, lib/log-page.ts` |\n| Alert history | The newest 50 shown, kept 90 days. | `ALERT_KEEP_MS, server/notify.ts` |\n| Analytics history | 30, 60 or 90 days for each project, 90 by default. | `retentionDays, server/analytics-config.ts; prunePageviews, server/analytics.ts` |\n| Funnel | 2 to 6 steps. | `MIN_STEPS, MAX_STEPS, server/analytics-config.ts` |\n| Analytics events | 30 per 10 seconds per project and address. Page views are 1 per second. With TRUST_PROXY off every visitor shares one address. | `server/analytics-events.ts, server/analytics.ts` |\n| Analytics fields | Allowed campaign values up to 20 each for source, medium and campaign, 40 characters each, event value 40, error message 200, 5 errors per page view. Speed and returning-visitor figures need 50 page views and 20 visitors. | `server/analytics-fields.ts, server/analytics-retention.ts` |\n| Live view and retention | A 5 minute window. Signals are kept 1 hour. The retention code changes every 90 days. | `server/analytics.ts (LIVE_WINDOW_MS, LIVE_KEEP_MS, RETENTION_PERIOD_MS)` |\n| Status page | 30 days and the latest 10 outages. | `server/status-page.ts` |\n| Webhook delivery | 3 attempts, 5 seconds each, history kept 30 days. | `MAX_ATTEMPTS, DELIVERY_RETENTION_MS, server/webhooks.ts` |\n| Deployments page | All projects: the newest 100, shown 25 at a time. One project’s tab: 25 at a time with Load older, and search and filters run on the server. | `server/queries.ts, server/deployment-page.ts, components/deployments-view.tsx` |\n| Activity | 50 a page. A search reads the newest 5000. An export is 5000 entries and 10 a minute. | `PAGE_SIZE, SCAN_LIMIT, EXPORT_ROW_LIMIT` |\n\n## Sign-in\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session | 30 days. | `SESSION_DAYS, server/auth/session.ts` |\n| Fresh sign-in | 5 minutes. | `REAUTH_WINDOW_MS, server/auth/session.ts` |\n| Sign-in handshake | 10 minutes. | `app/api/auth/login/route.ts` |\n| Sign-in attempts | 20 a minute per address. Counted only when TRUST_PROXY is on. | `RATE_LIMITS.signIn, server/rate-limit.ts` |\n| Dashboard JSON request | 256 KB. | `JSON_BODY_LIMIT, server/body-limit.ts` |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEach allowance is counted against the thing that can misbehave. An access token has its own counts, so one noisy script cannot use up the allowance of another script or of you in the browser. Terminal and file requests are counted per account because the container behind them is shared by every project you have. Bad tokens are counted per address, because a token that does not check out has no account to count against. The counters live in the memory of the running process, so a restart of Egg clears them, and a copy of Egg that runs several processes counts in each one.\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Too many requests for this token. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. A token went over its reads, changes, container sessions or terminal input. Wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 60 tokens that did not check out came from one address in a minute. Fix the token your script sends.\n- `Too many requests for terminal input. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 300 input requests a minute for your account.\n- `Too many file requests. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Listing, reading and downloading are 120 a minute.\n- `Too many requests for file changes. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Creating, writing, renaming and deleting are 60 a minute.\n- `Too many exports. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 10 CSV exports a minute.\n- `Too many checks. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 20 conflict checks a minute.\n- `You can connect up to 5 servers.`\n\n  Disconnect one you no longer use.\n- `You can have up to 25 active tokens. Revoke one first.`\n\n  Revoke a token you no longer use.\n- `You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.`\n\n  Delete a webhook you no longer use.\n- `A project can have up to 100 variables.`\n\n  Delete a variable you do not need.\n\nYour container’s size\n\nHow much CPU, memory and disk your Nest container has is set by Nest, not by Egg. Test connection and the Container page show what it has.\n\n## Example: Poll a deploy without hitting a limit\n\n### Given\n\nA script that starts a deploy and waits for it to finish.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Start it\n\n   One POST to the deployments endpoint is one of your 30 changes a minute.\n2. Step 2: Poll\n\n   List the project’s deployments every 2 seconds. That is 30 reads a minute, a quarter of the 120 allowed.\n3. Step 3: Back off\n\n   On a 429, wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header, then carry on.\n\n### Result\n\nThe script finishes a normal build without a 429. Fetching the build log counts against the smaller 10 a minute allowance, so ask for it once the deploy is done.\n\n**Related:** [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container).\n","troubleshooting":"Reference\n\nPage 40 of 40\n\n# Troubleshooting\n\nOne entry per message Egg shows, with the fix. Find the heading for what you were doing.\n\nMessages are quoted as the product prints them. A part in angle brackets, such as `<n>`, is filled in with a name or a number. Where a message comes with a status code, the code is the first thing in the fix.\n\n## Test connection fails\n\n- `Nest rejected the key. Check that you pasted the public key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and that the username is exactly your Nest username.`\n\n  Paste the public key again, exactly as shown. After New key, the old one is dead. Check the username.\n- `Timed out connecting to Nest. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.`\n\n  Wait a minute and press Test connection again.\n- `Could not reach the Nest SSH host (<reason>).`\n\n  The reason is the network error. Try again later.\n- `Confirm host key`\n\n  The first Test connection stops here on purpose. Check the fingerprint against the one Nest shows for your server, then press Confirm host key.\n- `Confirm host key: open the Connect page, check the fingerprint of this server and confirm it first.`\n\n  409. A deploy, file change or terminal was tried before the host key was confirmed. Open Connect, press Test connection, then Confirm host key.\n- `The host key of <host> changed (was <old>, now <new>). Nothing was sent. If you expected this, reconnect the server.`\n\n  This stops on purpose. Disconnect and add the server again. The box asks whether to keep or delete the server’s projects. You confirm the new fingerprint on the next Test connection.\n- `SSH error: <message>`\n\n  Anything else. Read the message, then try again.\n- `That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.`\n\n  Use letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen, starting with a letter or digit, up to 32 characters.\n- `<name> is already connected.`\n\n  One server per Nest username. Use the one you have.\n- `<name> is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.`\n\n  409. You disconnected it and kept its projects. Connect the same username again.\n- `You can connect up to 5 servers.`\n\n  Disconnect one you no longer use.\n\nAfter a successful test, the list of issues may show warnings such as “curl is missing. Health checks fall back to a plain TCP connection check.” They are shown for your information and do not block a deploy.\n\n## The build fails\n\n- `Could not clone the repository. Check the branch name and that Egg has access to it.`\n\n  Check the production branch in Settings and, for a private repository, that the GitHub app is installed on it.\n- `The root directory \"<dir>\" does not exist in this repository.`\n\n  Fix Root directory in Settings, Build and Run.\n- `The build failed (exit <code>).`\n\n  Read the log above it. The usual causes are a missing lockfile or build script, the wrong root directory, or a command that needs a variable you have not set.\n- `The build timed out.`\n\n  The clone took longer than 5 minutes. The limit is fixed. Nothing changes on your container.\n- `The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped. Your app is still running.`\n\n  The build on your container ran past 15 minutes. Make the build faster. This limit is fixed. “Your app is still running.” is added only when a release was already serving. Egg deletes the new release folder.\n- `The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB. Your app is still running.`\n\n  Exit 137. The build went over its cap and was stopped, and your app was not. Raise Build memory (MB) on the Container page and deploy again, or use less memory in the build. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- `The build did not start: your container reports <n> MB of memory, and 512 MB of that is kept for your app, which leaves too little to build in. Use a larger container.`\n\n  The cap would be under 256 MB. Egg keeps 512 MB of your container’s memory for your app, so a container with under 768 MB cannot build.\n- `Another build has been running on this container for more than 30 minutes, so this one did not start. Your app is still running.`\n\n  One build runs at a time on a container. Wait for the other build to finish or cancel it, then deploy again.\n- `Egg could not check your container before building, so nothing was uploaded. Check that the container is running and try again.`\n\n  The check before the upload got no answer. Check the Container page and Test connection, then deploy again.\n- `Free up disk space on your container: <n> MB is free and a build needs at least 2048 MB.`\n\n  Delete files you do not need on your container, then deploy again. Nothing was uploaded.\n- `Builds on your container run under a memory cap with systemd-run, which is not installed here, so the build did not start. Install systemd.`\n\n  Your container has no systemd-run, which the cap needs. Install systemd.\n- `Your container cannot apply a memory cap to a build (it needs systemd with cgroup v2 and the memory controller), so the build did not start rather than run without a limit.`\n\n  Egg does not run a build without a limit. Use a container with systemd, cgroup v2 and the memory controller.\n- `Install coreutils on your container: sudo apt install coreutils (the build needs the timeout command).`\n\n  Run the command on your container, then deploy again.\n- `Install Node.js <version> or newer on your container (nodejs.org), then deploy again.`\n\n  No Node.js was found. <version> is 22.13 when the project has no engines range. With a range, the line says: Install Node.js on your container (this project needs <range>), then deploy again.\n- `Node.js <installed> on your container does not match what this project needs (<wanted>). Install a matching Node.js on your container (nodejs.org), then deploy again.`\n\n  <wanted> is the engines range in package.json, or >=22.13 when there is none.\n- `Install npm on your container: sudo apt install npm (installing Node.js from nodejs.org includes it).`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again.\n- `This project uses <pnpm or yarn>, which needs corepack. On your container run: npm install -g corepack`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again.\n- `Install Python 3 on your container: sudo apt install python3`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again.\n- `Install python3-venv on your container: sudo apt install python3-venv`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again. On Debian and Ubuntu venv is a separate package.\n- `Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>. Change it on the Container page.`\n\n  The saved build memory no longer fits the range, for example after the server reported less memory. Open Container, set Build memory (MB) inside the range and press Save.\n- `The output folder \"<dir>\" was not created by the build.`\n\n  Set Output directory to the folder your build really writes.\n- `The build output is <n> MB, over the 524.288 MB limit.`\n\n  Leave out what the app does not need to run. The limit is 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes), measured on the packed archive. Nothing was uploaded to your container.\n- `Set a start command for this project (for example ./my-binary).`\n\n  A Custom command project needs a Start command.\n- `Static sites need Python 3 or Node.js on your Nest container to be served.`\n\n  Install one of them on the container.\n- `A deployment stays on Building or Deploying and nothing moves`\n\n  The worker that owned it stopped. Egg does not free the job on its own, because commands it started on your container may still be running, and that project cannot start another deployment. Only the person who runs Egg can fix it: see Recovering a stale deployment in the self-hosting guide.\n- `No free preview ports on this server.`\n\n  All of 20000 to 29999 are in use. Delete old previews.\n- `Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.`\n\n  The log holds 20,000 lines. Make the build print less. The build itself carried on.\n\n## It built, but the app never answered\n\n- `The new release did not become healthy on port <port>. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.`\n\n  Listen on PORT and on 0.0.0.0, not only localhost. The log shows the last 30 lines of the app’s output. For a production deploy, the previous release is put back, so the app is unavailable for a short time while the new release starts and again while the old one returns.\n- `Service crashed on start`\n\n  The service failed before the wait ended. Read the output printed under it.\n- `The previous release did not come back either. Check the logs.`\n\n  Open Logs and read the runtime lines, then redeploy or use Instant rollback to an earlier release.\n\nA health path that answers with anything from 200 to 499 counts as healthy, so a 404 passes. Only 500 and above fails.\n\n## A deploy is blocked by something on the container\n\n### The conflict check\n\n- `Nothing was changed on your container. <details>`\n\n  The conflict check refused. The details say what is in the way. Choose another name or port, or stop that program yourself first.\n- `A service called egg-<slug>.service already exists and Egg did not create it. It will not be replaced. Choose another project name.`\n\n  Rename the project.\n- `Port <n> is already in use by <process>. Egg will not stop it. Choose another port, or stop that program yourself first.`\n\n  Pick another port, or stop that program.\n- `<path> already exists and was not created by Egg, so it was left alone.`\n\n  Pick another name, or move that folder yourself.\n- `Could not check this container just now: <reason>. The deploy checks again before it changes anything.`\n\n  Try again in a moment.\n- `Too many checks. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. The check is 20 a minute for your account.\n\n## A project will not create\n\n### Project messages\n\n- `You already have a project called \"<slug>\".`\n\n  Pick another name.\n- `Port <n> is already used by another project on this server.`\n\n  Pick another port. In Settings the same refusal reads “Port <n> is already used on this server.”\n- `Ports 20000 to 29999 are reserved for previews`\n\n  Choose a port from 1024 to 19999 or 30000 to 65535.\n- `The project name must use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (up to 40).`\n\n  Fix the name. The slug is at most 40 characters.\n- `Only plain https repository URLs are supported.`\n\n  Use an https URL. Only github.com is supported.\n- `The project was created, but <NAME> was not saved: <reason>. Press Deploy to try again.`\n\n  The project exists. Fix the variable in Settings and deploy.\n- `Could not remove the project from your container.`\n\n  Your records are kept so you can try again.\n\nA project made from a pasted public URL does not deploy on push. It builds from Deploy, a deploy hook or the API. Import it through the GitHub app for push deploys.\n\n## A rollback or cancel is refused\n\n### Rollback and cancel messages\n\n- `That release was removed from your server to save space.`\n\n  Only 5 production releases are kept. Deploy the commit again.\n- `That deployment is already live.`\n\n  Pick an earlier one.\n- `Only production deployments can be rolled back to.`\n\n  Choose a production deployment.\n- `That deployment never finished, so there is nothing to roll back to.`\n\n  Choose one that was Ready.\n- `The older release did not become healthy, so nothing was changed.`\n\n  The release that was live is still live. Check the older release’s logs.\n- `That deployment is already finished.`\n\n  Cancel works only while a deployment is Queued, Building or Deploying.\n- `Only old previews that are not live or removed can be deleted in bulk.`\n\n  409. Pick previews that are finished and not the current one.\n- `A cancel by API returns 409 once the release has been switched.`\n\n  The live release keeps running. Check the deployment’s state.\n\n## A variable will not save\n\n### Variable messages\n\n- `Names use letters, digits and underscores, and do not start with a digit`\n\n  Rename the variable.\n- `That name is set by Egg`\n\n  PORT, HOST, PATH, HOME and EGG are reserved.\n- `A project can have up to 100 variables.`\n\n  Delete one you do not need.\n- `No example env file (such as .env.example) was found in the repository.`\n\n  Add .env.example, or type the names by hand.\n\nA saved change reaches your app at the next deployment. Redeploy to apply it. A variable with an empty value is left out of the app’s environment rather than set to an empty string.\n\n## A domain stays on “Needs attention”\n\n### Domain messages\n\n- `No CNAME found yet. Create one pointing to <target>, or add a TXT record with the value domain-verification=<name>. DNS changes can take a few minutes.`\n\n  Add the CNAME or the TXT record at your DNS provider, wait, and press Check.\n- `The CNAME points to <x>. It should point to <target>.`\n\n  Change the CNAME value.\n- `The name does not resolve yet. Add it in the Nest dashboard first.`\n\n  Add the domain and port on the Domains page of the Nest dashboard.\n- `DNS could not be checked right now. Try again in a minute.`\n\n  Press Check again.\n- `That name is reserved. Choose another domain.`\n\n  The name is on the reserved list, for example admin, api, dashboard, status or www. Pick another name or a domain of your own.\n- `Only your Nest name, <name>.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.`\n\n  Under hackclub.app only your own Nest name and names beneath it are allowed.\n- `Use a public domain name such as app.example.com. IP addresses, localhost and internal names cannot be used.`\n\n  Use a real public domain name.\n- `DNS no longer points here. Check your record.`\n\n  A domain that was active no longer passes the check. Restore the CNAME or TXT record and press Check. Egg keeps the domain.\n- `<domain> is already added.`\n\n  A domain can be added once across all accounts.\n- `Certificate not verified yet`\n\n  The app check found no trusted certificate. Wait for Nest to issue one, then press Check again.\n\n## A deploy hook answers 409\n\n### Deploy hook messages\n\n- `A deployment is already running for this project.`\n\n  The hook answers 409 while any deployment of the project is queued, building or deploying. Try again when it finishes.\n- `Unknown hook`\n\n  The URL is wrong or was replaced. Make a new URL.\n- `Request body must be JSON`\n\n  Send valid JSON, or no body. The only field is branch.\n\n## No comment appears on a pull request\n\nThe pull request must come from the same repository, must not be a draft, and its branch must not be your production branch. The GitHub app needs Pull requests read and write and the Pull request event. When a comment fails the deploy is unaffected and nothing is shown to you. A pull request from a fork is never built.\n\n## Alerts or notifications do not arrive\n\n### Notification and alert messages\n\n- `Add a webhook URL first.`\n\n  Save a Discord or Slack webhook URL in Notifications.\n- `Use the plain https webhook URL from Discord or Slack.`\n\n  Remove ports, queries and credentials.\n- `Only Discord and Slack incoming webhook URLs are supported.`\n\n  Use a discord.com, discordapp.com or hooks.slack.com URL.\n- `Discord answered <status>.`\n\n  Slack gives the same message with its own name. The webhook was deleted or the URL is wrong. Make a new one.\n- `Failed checks must be a whole number from 1 to 10.`\n\n  Fix the number in Settings, Alerts.\n- `The response time limit must be at least 50 ms.`\n\n  Use 50 to 60000, or leave it empty to turn the limit off.\n- `Could not load alert settings`\n\n  Press Try again.\n\nIf the test arrives but alerts do not, check three things. An app is reported down only after 2 failed checks in a row by default. Alerts may be paused for the project. And the channel you want may be switched off.\n\n### Webhook messages\n\n- `The URL must start with https://.`\n\n  A signed webhook goes to an https address only.\n- `Use the standard https port (443).`\n\n  Remove the port from the address.\n- `Use a domain name, not an IP address.`\n\n  Use a full public domain name.\n- `Use a full public domain name.`\n\n  A name with no dot, such as localhost, is refused.\n- `Remove the user name and password from the URL.`\n\n  Credentials in an address are never accepted.\n- `Remove the # part from the URL.`\n\n  Take off the fragment.\n- `You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.`\n\n  409. Delete a webhook you no longer use.\n- `Test message delivered`\n\n  The receiver answered with a 2xx status. The delivery is listed under Recent deliveries.\n\nA delivery that fails shows a short code instead of a message from your receiver, because the response is never stored. `http_500` (or another `http_` status) means your receiver answered that status. `timeout` means it did not answer within 5 seconds. `redirect` means it answered with a redirect, which is not followed. `blocked_address` means the name points at a private or internal address. `dns_failed` means the name did not resolve. `network` means the connection failed. `interrupted` means a restart cut the delivery off. A status of 500 and above, 429, 408, a timeout, a network failure and a failed lookup are tried again up to 3 times in all, with pauses of 2 and 10 seconds. Any other 4xx status is not.\n\n## The terminal, files or leftovers are refused\n\nThese refusals are about the terminal, files and leftovers. The [container](/docs/container) page explains the switches.\n\n### Terminal messages\n\n- `The terminal is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Terminal in Account. Nothing else is needed to read files or the Container page.\n- `Changing files is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on File changes. Reading and downloading still work without it.\n- `Removing leftovers is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Removing leftovers. Leftovers stay listed while it is off.\n- `Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning this on needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.`\n\n  403. Flip the switch again. It sends you through Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied.\n- `This server has not been verified yet. Run Test connection on the Connect page first.`\n\n  409. The host key is not pinned yet, and a terminal never opens to a host Egg has not checked. Press Test connection.\n- `A terminal is already open for this project. Resume or close it first.`\n\n  409. Only one terminal per project and person. Press Resume terminal, or Close it, then open a new one.\n- `You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.`\n\n  429. One account may hold 5 terminals across all its projects, so one account cannot use up the server’s share. Close one you are not using.\n- `Too many terminals are open right now. Try again soon.`\n\n  503. The server as a whole holds 50 terminals. Wait a little and open yours again.\n- `The terminal was closed before it opened.`\n\n  403. You signed out, or switched Terminal off, while it was still connecting. Open it again.\n- `Could not open a terminal: <reason>`\n\n  502. Egg could not reach your container. The reason is the SSH error. Check the container is running, then try again.\n- `Could not open a terminal on your container.`\n\n  502. The same, with no detail from the connection. Try again in a minute.\n- `The terminal size must be 20 to 400 columns and 5 to 200 rows.`\n\n  400. Only scripts and the API see this: the page sends a size that fits.\n- `Send at most 8192 bytes at a time.`\n\n  413. One input request carries at most 8 KB. Split the text.\n- `That is too much input at once.`\n\n  413. The whole request was over 64 KB, which is room for 8 KB of text after JSON escaping.\n- `Too many requests for terminal input. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 300 input requests a minute for your account. Wait for the seconds in Retry-After.\n- `Terminal session not found`\n\n  404. The session ended, or the id is wrong, or it belongs to someone else. Open a new one.\n\n### File messages\n\n- `This project has no folder on your container yet. Deploy it first.`\n\n  404. The folder is created by the first deploy. Deploy once, then open Files.\n- `This location is read only. Files in shared/ can be changed. The live release (current/) cannot.`\n\n  403. Open shared/ and work there. The page shows the same rule as “current/ is the live release and is read only.”\n- `current/ is the live release and is read only. Changing it would change the code your app runs. Make the change in your repository and deploy, or use shared/ for files that last.`\n\n  403. Change the code in your repository and deploy. Put files that must last in shared/.\n- `This file holds the project's environment variable values, so it is not shown here. Change the values in the project's settings.`\n\n  403. The Files tab never opens a variables file. Change the values in the project’s settings, then deploy.\n- `That file holds the project's environment variable values, which are never returned by the API.`\n\n  403. The API refuses those files too. Use env:read to list names.\n- `Nothing exists at that path.`\n\n  404. Check the spelling, or refresh the folder.\n- `That path is not a folder.`\n\n  409. You asked to list a file. Open its folder instead.\n- `That path is not a file.`\n\n  409. You asked to read or download a folder.\n- `That path is a folder.`\n\n  409. You tried to write over a folder. Pick a file name.\n- `That path is a symbolic link. Files are not written through links.`\n\n  409. A link could lead outside shared/, so it is never written through. Write to the real path.\n- `Something with that name already exists.`\n\n  409. Pick another name. An upload to an existing file asks whether to replace it.\n- `That path leaves the project folder.`\n\n  403. A path must stay inside the project’s folder, links included.\n- `That file is over the 50 MB limit.`\n\n  413. Uploads and downloads stop at 50 MB. Put larger files on the container by SSH.\n- `The upload arrived incomplete. Nothing was saved.`\n\n  The connection broke part way. Upload again.\n- `Too many file requests. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Listing, reading and downloading are 120 a minute for your account.\n- `Too many requests for file changes. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Creating, writing, renaming and deleting are 60 a minute for your account.\n- `Enter a name.`\n\n  A new name cannot be empty.\n- `A name cannot contain a slash.`\n\n  A rename or new folder takes one name. Open the folder first.\n- `That name is not allowed.`\n\n  “.” and “..” are not names.\n- `That is the same path.`\n\n  A rename must change the name.\n- `A folder cannot move into itself.`\n\n  Pick a destination outside the folder you are moving.\n- `This container's tools are too limited to list files.`\n\n  502. The container lacks tools Egg uses to list a folder. Test connection lists what is missing.\n- `Your container could not finish that. Try again.`\n\n  502. The container failed part way. Try again, and check Container for disk space.\n\n## The API answers 401, 403 or 429\n\n### API and token messages\n\n- `Send an access token as an Authorization: Bearer egg_... header.`\n\n  401. Add the header. A cookie is never accepted here.\n- `That access token is not valid.`\n\n  401. Missing, revoked, expired and unknown tokens all look the same on purpose. Make a new one on the Developer page.\n- `This token does not have the <scope> permission.`\n\n  403. The message names it. Press Edit on the token on the Developer page and tick it. The change applies to the next request.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 60 tokens that did not check out from one address in a minute. Fix the token your script sends.\n- `Too many requests for this token. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header. Reads are 120 a minute, changes 30, container sessions 10, terminal input 300.\n- `No such item, or it belongs to someone else.`\n\n  404. Check the id. Another account’s items answer the same way.\n- `The request body is too large.`\n\n  413. 16 KB for most calls, 4 MB for a file write, 64 KB for MCP and 4 KB for webhooks.\n- `Request body must be JSON`\n\n  400. Send valid JSON, or no body.\n- `Request body must be a JSON object`\n\n  400. Send { … }, not a list or a bare value.\n- `Unknown field \"<name>\". Allowed: ...`\n\n  400. Unknown body fields are refused so a typo cannot silently do nothing. The message lists the allowed ones.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Pick 7 days, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Pick 7 days, or untick Use the terminal and Change files.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Set the expiry to 7 days in the same change, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Raised when you edit a token. Set Expires after to 7 days in the same edit.\n- `You can have up to 25 active tokens. Revoke one first.`\n\n  400. Revoke a token you no longer use. Expired and revoked ones do not count.\n- `Give the token a name.`\n\n  400. A name is 1 to 60 characters.\n- `Pick at least one permission.`\n\n  400. A token with no permissions can do nothing.\n- `Send at least one field to change.`\n\n  400. An edit must change the name, the permissions or the expiry.\n- `This token is revoked and cannot be edited.`\n\n  409. A revoked token is final. Make a new one.\n- `Token not found`\n\n  404. It was deleted, or it is not yours.\n- `content is not valid base64.`\n\n  400. With encoding set to base64, send standard base64 text.\n- `The API writes files of up to 1 MB. Upload larger files in the dashboard.`\n\n  413. Use the Files tab for bigger files.\n- `That file is over the 2 MB limit for this endpoint. Download larger files in the dashboard.`\n\n  413. The API download returns at most 2 MB, as base64.\n- `This endpoint only accepts POST.`\n\n  405. The MCP address takes POST only.\n\n### Agent endpoint messages\n\n- `Parse error`\n\n  400. The body was not JSON.\n- `Not a JSON-RPC 2.0 message.`\n\n  400. Send { \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 1, \"method\": ..., \"params\": ... }.\n- `Batches are not supported. Send one message per request.`\n\n  400. Send one message at a time.\n- `Method not found: <method>`\n\n  Use initialize, ping, tools/list or tools/call.\n- `Unknown tool: <name>`\n\n  That tool is not in this connection’s list. Call tools/list to see what your token can run; a tool can also be hidden by the toolset or scope you picked on the address.\n- `Unknown toolset \"<name>\". Choose from: ...`\n\n  400. Pick a name from the list in the message. core is added for you.\n- `Unknown scope \"<name>\". Use read or deploy.`\n\n  400. Use read or deploy.\n- `This operation changes live systems and has not run. To run it, call call_api again with confirm set to \"<operationId>\".`\n\n  The compact server held the call. Send the same call again with confirm set to that id.\n- `tools/call needs a tool name.`\n\n  Send params with a name.\n- `Tool arguments must be an object.`\n\n  Send arguments as { … }.\n- `<code>: <message>`\n\n  A tool that fails comes back as a result with isError set, and the text starts with the error code, for example forbidden: This token does not have the files:write permission.\n\n## Sign-in fails\n\nThe sign-in messages and their fixes are on the [account](/docs/account) page.\n\n## Monitoring, the status page and analytics\n\n### What you see\n\n- `Could not read the service from your container.`\n\n  The Service card could not reach the container within 20 seconds. Press Check now, and check the container is running.\n- `The service is running but nothing answered on its health path.`\n\n  The app starts but the health path gives no answer. Fix the path in Settings, Build and Run, or the app.\n- `The health path answered HTTP <code>.`\n\n  A status of 500 or above counts as down. A 404 does not.\n- `The health path took <ms> ms to answer, over your limit of <n> ms.`\n\n  Raise the Slow response limit, or make the page faster.\n- `No data yet`\n\n  A status page says this until the first check has run. Wait for the next 5 minute pass.\n- `Not responding`\n\n  The app has stopped answering, by the same check as the alerts.\n- `Not enough data`\n\n  Percentages and medians need 50 visits behind them, and speed figures need 50 page views. The counts are shown.\n- `Not enough data yet`\n\n  Returning visitors need at least 20 visitors in the group, counted from the day Retention was turned on.\n- `Unique visitors need retention on`\n\n  Turn on Retention under What else to record. Without the random id in the browser, visitors cannot be told apart.\n- `A funnel needs at least 2 steps`\n\n  Add a second step, or leave the funnel empty.\n- `A funnel has at most 6 steps`\n\n  Remove a step.\n- `Nothing is counted on a page`\n\n  The snippet is missing or the page was not redeployed. Nothing is counted for Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, on localhost, or for known bots.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEach entry quotes the message word for word so you can search this page for the text you saw. The status code leads the fix because it tells you what kind of problem it is: a 4xx means the request needs to change, a 429 means wait for the Retry-After seconds, and a 502 or 503 means Egg could not reach your container or has no room right now, so check the container and try again.\n\n## Example: Fix a build that stops with exit 137\n\n### Given\n\nA project named recipe-api whose deployment fails with “The build needed more memory than the cap of 1024 MB. Your app is still running.”\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open Container\n\n   Pick the server that holds recipe-api, then find the Build memory card.\n\n   In the dashboard: Containerthen Build memory\n2. Step 2: Raise the number\n\n   Read the range in the hint under Build memory (MB). Type 1536 if the hint allows it, then press Save.\n3. Step 3: Deploy again\n\n   Open the project and press Redeploy, or push a commit.\n\n### Result\n\nThe message “Build memory set to 1536 MB” appears, the next build runs with that limit and finishes, and Activity lists “Changed the build memory limit for” the server. The cap is at most your container’s memory minus 512 MB, so on a container that reports less than 2048 MB, make the build use less instead.\n\nStill stuck?\n\nLook at [your dashboard](/projects) or open an issue on [GitHub](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg). Leave real keys, tokens and variable values out of it.\n"};
export const FULL = "# Egg docs\n\nStart\n\nPage 1 of 40\n\n# How it works\n\nEgg builds your GitHub repository and runs it on your Nest container as a service. Egg is independent, so it is not an official Hack Club product.\n\nNest gives you a small Linux container that you reach over SSH. Egg connects with a key you control, uploads your finished build and runs it as a systemd service on a port, so Nest’s proxy can map your domain to that port. You no longer do that work by hand in a terminal.\n\nYou need a Hack Club account, a Nest container and a GitHub repository. Your container has nothing installed ahead of time.\n\nThese docs use each word in one way only.\n\n- **Container.** Your Nest Linux container, the one you log in to with SSH. Your app runs here.\n- **Server.** Egg’s menus call a Nest container you have connected to Egg a server. One server is one Nest username.\n- **Egg machine.** The computer that runs the Egg website and its worker. If you use someone else’s Egg, it is their computer. If you run Egg yourself, it is yours. It clones your repository and packs the source.\n- **Worker.** The part of Egg that takes queued deployments and runs them.\n- **Build.** The install and build of your project. It runs on your container, in a new release folder, under a cap.\n- **Cap.** A hard limit on memory and CPU that the kernel enforces on a build, so a build that goes over it stops. Your app keeps running.\n- **Archive.** The finished build packed into one compressed file, `release.tar.gz`, for upload.\n- **Release.** One uploaded build, in its own folder on your container.\n- **Production branch.** The git branch whose pushes go live. A branch is a named line of commits in your repository.\n- **Preview.** A build of any other branch. It runs as a second service on its own port, so production keeps running.\n\n## What happens on a push\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: GitHub tells Egg about the push\n\n   GitHub sends the push to Egg with a signed request. Only matching projects with **Deploy automatically on push** on get a deployment, so GitHub must reach Egg’s web address. An Egg on localhost never hears about pushes. There, press **Deploy** or use a deploy hook.\n2. Step 2: The deployment joins a queue\n\n   The worker picks up two queued deployments at a time by default, across all projects. A project never runs two at once, so the second waits as Queued. With no worker running, every deployment stays Queued.\n3. Step 3: The worker clones your repository\n\n   This step runs on the Egg machine. Egg clones the newest commit of the branch into a work folder there and packs the source into an archive, so the GitHub token stays on the Egg machine. Nothing is installed or built here, and the Egg machine spends no memory or CPU on your build.\n4. Step 4: The build runs on your container\n\n   Egg checks your container first: the Node.js version, the package manager your lockfile needs, Python 3 and venv for a Python project, systemd-run, timeout, and at least 2048 MB of free disk. A failed check ends the deployment with one line that says what to fix, so nothing is uploaded. When all checks pass, Egg uploads the source over SSH and unpacks it into a new release folder next to the running release. Your install, build and production install commands run there, under the cap.\n   The memory cap is the server’s **Build memory (MB)**, 512 MB by default, and never more than your container’s memory minus 512 MB. A project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB while it builds, if your container has room for it. The CPU cap is **Build CPU limit (cores)**. The build gets no swap and runs at a low CPU priority, so your app wins when the CPU is busy. It stops after 15 minutes. Your running app keeps serving the whole time. One build runs at a time on a container, and another waits for up to 30 minutes. If the build fails, runs out of memory, times out or is canceled, Egg deletes the new folder, so the running release stays in place.\n5. Step 5: The service switches over\n\n   Egg writes your environment variables to a file that only the service reads. For production, the `current` link points at the new release and the service restarts, so the app is offline until the new release answers. A preview starts its own service on its own port, and production is not touched.\n6. Step 6: Egg waits for an answer\n\n   Egg asks your app on its port, from your container, for up to 45 seconds. If it never answers, the deployment is Error, so Egg switches back to an earlier production release, when one exists, and checks it for 20 seconds. A first production deployment has nothing to go back to: the service stays registered and systemd keeps restarting it every 3 seconds. Fix the app and deploy again.\n\nA push to your production branch goes live. A push to any other branch builds a preview on its own port, so production keeps running. When a deployment ends, the worker deletes its folder on the Egg machine, whether it worked or not. That folder held the clone and the source archive.\n\nWhat it does not do\n\nEgg does not run databases or give a preview a public address on its own, and does not promise that any application works on Nest. It runs the runtimes listed under Projects and runtimes, and any start command that listens on the `PORT` it is given. A Dockerfile alone does nothing, so Egg stays in native process mode until a check of your server confirms Docker works there.\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Deployments per project at once | One | The others wait as Queued. |\n| Deployments running at once, all projects | 2 by default | The rest wait. A self-hosted worker can change this. |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | The deployment fails with “The build timed out.” |\n| Build time | 15 minutes, for install and build together. It cannot be changed. | The deployment fails with “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” |\n| Build cap | The server’s Build memory (MB), 512 MB by default, at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when your container has room for it, and never more than your container’s memory minus 512 MB. Build CPU limit (cores), 2 by default. No swap. | A build that needs more memory is stopped with “The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB.” A build that needs more CPU only runs slower. If the cap would be under 256 MB, the build does not start. |\n| Build output | 500 MiB (524.288 MB), measured on the archive | The deployment fails with the size it found. |\n| Wait for the app to answer | 45 seconds | The deployment is marked Error and the previous release is put back, if there is one. |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- A push did not start a deployment\n\n  Open the project’s Settings, Build and Run, and check that **Deploy automatically on push** is ticked. A project made from a pasted public repository URL is never built by a push, because it does not store the repository name GitHub sends. Press Deploy, or use a deploy hook. GitHub must also be able to reach Egg’s web address.\n- `“The new release did not become healthy on port N. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.”`\n\n  The app started but nothing answered on its port. Read the last log lines, fix the port or the address it binds to, and deploy again.\n- `“The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  A build went over its cap and was stopped. Your app was not. The second sentence is added only when a release was already serving. Raise **Build memory (MB)** on the Container page and deploy again, or make the build lighter. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- A deployment stays Building or Deploying and does not move\n\n  The worker stopped or lost its link to the database. Egg does not free the deployment by itself, because a command it started on your container may still be running. Nothing else deploys for this project until the person running Egg follows “Recovering a stale deployment” in the self-host guide. After that the deployment shows Error and you can deploy again.\n\n### Example: Push a fix to a bot\n\n#### Given\n\nSam has a Discord bot for sharing recipes in a repository called `sam-r/recipe-bot`. It runs on Node.js, and a Nest container called `sam-r` is already connected.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Push to the production branch\n\n   Sam pushes a fix to `main` from a laptop.\n2. Step 2: Open the Deployments tab\n\n   Sam opens the project’s Deployments tab and watches the new row.\n\n#### Result\n\nA new deployment appears as Queued, then Building, then Deploying, then Ready. The Build Logs card shows the clone, the install and the build, and ends with the app answering on its port. The bot restarts with the fix and no one logged in to the container.\n\n**Related:** [Quick start](/docs/quickstart), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [GitHub and previews](/docs/github).\n\n\n---\n\nStart\n\nPage 2 of 40\n\n# Getting started\n\nFollow the four chapters of the guided first-project tour in text: connect your container, import a repository, watch the first deployment and give it an address.\n\nThis page follows the tour in order. Each item gives you the words the tour uses, then the button to press on the real screen, so you can read along as you work.\n\nYou need three things before you start:\n\n- **A Nest container and your Nest username.** You use the name you log in with.\n- **A GitHub repository.** You can import a public one by its address. A private one, and deploys on every push, need the GitHub app.\n- **An app that listens on a port.** Egg sets `PORT` and `HOST` for it, so a static site needs no code for this.\n\nNot on Nest?\n\nIf you run your own copy of Egg, or deploy to a server you own, some words differ, so the address chapter is the one to watch. Read [Run Egg yourself](/docs/run-yourself) first.\n\n## 1. Connect your container\n\nIn this chapter you connect your container, so Egg can reach it and check what it can run.\n\n1. **Put your app on Nest.** Egg builds your GitHub repository and runs it on your Nest container. It takes about five minutes. Egg signs in to your container over SSH with a key made only for it.\n2. **Connect your container first.** Egg needs a way in. This sets it up. On Projects, with no server yet, press **Connect a server**. That opens Connect.\n3. **Your Nest username.** The name you log in with, as in ssh username@hackclub.app. Then press Create key. Type it in **Nest username**. The status reads “Waiting for the key”.\n4. **Add the key in Nest.** Copy it, open the Nest dashboard, and paste it on the SSH Keys page. Then come back. Copy the **Public key** and press **Open the Nest dashboard**.\n5. **Test the connection.** Egg connects once and checks what your container can run. Press **Test connection**.\n6. **Check the fingerprint.** Compare it with the one Nest shows for your server. Confirm only if they match. Egg refuses any other key afterwards. This appears the first time, in the “Confirm host key” box. Press **Confirm host key** yourself; nothing confirms it for you.\n7. **Connected.** Next, pick a repository. The status turns to “Connected”.\n\n## 2. Import a repository\n\nIn this chapter you point a project at your repository, so Egg can read it and you can check the settings.\n\n1. **Pick your repository.** Search, then press one. This is the version when the GitHub app is already installed: find it under **GitHub account** and press **Import**.\n2. **Connect GitHub.** This lets Egg read your repositories and deploy on every push. This is the version when the app is set up on this Egg but not installed for you. Press **Install the GitHub App**, share the repository, then come back.\n3. **Paste a repository URL.** A public one works without any setup. This is the version when this Egg has no GitHub app at all, as in a copy you run yourself. Paste an address like `https://github.com/owner/name` and press **Continue**. Pushes will not deploy on their own.\n4. **Check what Egg found.** It guessed the name, runtime and port from your repository. Change anything that is wrong. The port starts at `3000`. Your app has to listen on that port; see the note below.\n5. **Something is already on your container.** Items marked as blocking stop the deploy until you deal with them. Egg never changes anything it did not create. This appears only when the page lists things already on the container, under “What is already on this container”.\n6. **Deploy.** Egg builds on your container and starts the app. You can watch it happen. Press **Deploy**. You land on the deployment page.\n\nYour app has to listen on the right port\n\nEgg starts your app with `PORT` set to the port you chose and `HOST` set to `0.0.0.0`. If your app binds only to `localhost`, the health check fails.\n\n## 3. Watch the first deployment\n\nIn this chapter you start a release, so a build runs on your container.\n\n1. **Watch it build.** This is the live output from your container. A first build usually takes a few minutes. You can leave this page; the tour will not block you. It is the **Build Logs** card.\n2. **Your app is running.** It listens on port N inside your container. It has no web address yet. The next step gives it one. The state reads Ready. N is the port you chose.\n3. **The build failed.** Read the last lines of the log to see why. The troubleshooting page lists the usual causes. The state reads Error and your earlier release keeps running or is put back.\n\nIf the build fails, read the last lines of the log, then open the [troubleshooting page](/docs/troubleshooting#troubleshooting-build) for the usual causes.\n\n## 4. Give it an address\n\nIn this chapter you give the app a web address, so you can open it in a browser.\n\n1. **Choose an address.** Use your-nest-name.hackclub.app (or a subdomain of it), which Nest hands out with no DNS setup, or a domain you own. Pick this project; the port is the one it listens on. Open Domains, fill in **Add a domain** and press **Add domain**.\n2. **Do this in Nest.** Egg cannot change Nest’s routing, so this one step is yours. The steps below are exact: open the Domains page in the Nest dashboard and enter the same domain and port. Then come back and press Check. The steps are in the panel headed “Next: finish setup for” and your domain.\n3. **Check it.** Egg looks at DNS and tells you whether it points here. Press **Check**. The status moves from “Waiting for DNS” to “Active”.\n4. **Your app is live at the address you chose.** What next: add environment variables, set up a deploy hook, turn on analytics. The deployment page then shows the address.\n\nThe first page below walks the same path with every button, limit and error message. The others cover each part in full.\n\n**Related:** [Quick start](/docs/quickstart), [Servers](/docs/servers), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Domains](/docs/domains).\n\n\n---\n\nStart\n\nPage 3 of 40\n\n# Quick start\n\nFrom nothing to a running app on your Nest container, in four steps. Every button name here matches the one on screen.\n\nEgg signs in to your Nest container with a key made only for it and clones your GitHub repository on the Egg machine, the computer that runs Egg, so your container gets a copy of the source. Your container builds it there under a memory and CPU cap and runs it as a service. This page is the shortest path through that, and each part has its own page with the full detail.\n\nYou need four things before you start:\n\n- **A Hack Club account.** You sign in with Hack Club, so Hack Club only confirms who you are.\n- **A Nest container and your Nest username.** This is the name you log in with, as in `ssh username@hackclub.app`.\n- **A GitHub repository.** You import a public one by its address, so a private one, and automatic deploys on push, need the Egg GitHub app installed on it.\n- **An app that listens on a port.** See the warning below. A static site is served for you, so it needs no code for this.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Sign in\n\n   Open the log in page and press **Continue with Hack Club**. Approve the sign-in at Hack Club and you land on Projects, where a new account sees “Connect your Nest container first” with a **Connect a server** button. The sign-in lasts 30 days, so the handshake with Hack Club has to finish within 10 minutes in one browser.\n2. Step 2: Connect your server\n\n   Open Connect, titled “Connect a Nest server”. Under **Add your server**, type your Nest username in **Nest username** and press **Create key**, so the status reads “Waiting for the key”. Copy the **Public key**, press **Open the Nest dashboard** and paste the key on the SSH Keys page there. Come back and press **Test connection**. The first time, check the fingerprint in the “Confirm host key” box and press **Confirm host key**, so the status turns to “Connected” and a “Reached your container” panel appears.\n3. Step 3: Import a repository\n\n   Press **New project**, which opens “Let’s build something new”. Press **Install the GitHub App**, share the repository, then pick it under **GitHub account** and press **Import**. To use a public repository without the app, paste its address under “Or use a public repository URL” and press **Continue**, so the page says “Looking at the repository…” while Egg reads it. Then press **Use these settings** under “We found” to fill in the runtime and commands it detected. That button only appears when a runtime was recognised.\n4. Step 4: Check the form and deploy\n\n   Check **Nest server**, **Project name**, **Application preset**, **Production branch**, **Root directory** and **Port your app listens on**. The port starts at `3000`. Fill in any **Environment variables** the example env file asked for, so an empty field is skipped. Watch the “What is already on this container” panel: a line with the badge “Blocks the deploy” keeps the button off until you fix it. Press **Deploy**, so the button reads “Starting deployment…” and you land on the deployment page.\n5. Step 5: Watch the first deployment\n\n   The **Build Logs** card streams the clone on the Egg machine, then the checks, upload, install, build and start on your container. When the state reads Ready, your app is running on your container, and the log ends with “Live on port N. Map your domain to this port in the Nest dashboard.” If you imported through the GitHub app, pushes to the production branch now deploy on their own, so a pasted public address does not. If the state reads Error, read the log from the bottom and see What can go wrong below. Your earlier release keeps running or is put back.\n\nYour app has to listen on the right port\n\nEgg starts your app with `PORT` set to the port you chose and `HOST` set to `0.0.0.0`, so listen on both. An app that binds only to `localhost` cannot be reached and fails the health check after 45 seconds. A bot that opens no port still needs a tiny web server, so the check has something to answer.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg judges a release by asking your app a question on its port: it requests the health check path, which is `/` unless you change it, and a status from 200 to 499 counts as up. A program that never opens a port cannot answer, so the check fails and the previous release is put back. Egg waits for an answer before it calls a deployment Ready, so Ready means “answering”, not just “started”.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Servers per account | 5 | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Nest username | Letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen; 1 to 32 characters; starts with a letter or digit | “That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Project name | 1 to 60 characters. Its address-safe form is lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, up to 40 | “The project name must use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (up to 40).” In the code: server/projects.ts server/deploy/layout.ts |\n| Port | 1024 to 65535, and not 20000 to 29999 | 20000 to 29999 is kept for previews. The form allows a lower port; the server refuses it with “port: Too small: expected number to be >=1024”. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Public repository address | https://github.com/owner/name | “Enter a GitHub repository URL such as https://github.com/owner/name” In the code: components/new-project-flow.tsx |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | The deployment fails with “The build timed out.” In the code: server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Build time | 15 minutes by default | The deployment fails with “The build timed out.” In the code: server/config.ts BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS |\n| Wait for the app to answer | 45 seconds | The deployment ends as Error and the previous release is put back, if there is one. In the code: server/deploy/pipeline.ts |\n| Container checks while you fill the form | 20 a minute | “Too many checks. Wait N seconds and try again.” In the code: app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“Hack Club sign-in is not set up on this installation yet.”`\n\n  This copy of Egg has no Hack Club app configured, so the button is off. Only the person running it can fix that.\n- `“You did not approve the sign-in. Try again when you are ready.”`\n\n  You pressed cancel at Hack Club. Press Continue with Hack Club again.\n- `“The sign-in took too long or came from another browser. Please try again.”`\n\n  Start again from the log in page, in one browser, and finish within 10 minutes.\n- `“That sign-in link was not valid. Please try again.” or “Hack Club could not sign you in. Please try again in a moment.”`\n\n  Try again. If it repeats, Hack Club may be having a problem.\n- `“Connect a server first”`\n\n  You have no connected server yet, so there is nowhere to deploy. Press **Connect a server**.\n- The Connect page shows “Could not connect”\n\n  Read the red message under the server name. Most often the public key was not pasted on the SSH Keys page, or the username is not exactly your Nest username. The Servers page lists each message.\n- `“Could not read this repository. If it is private, install the GitHub App so it can be read. You can still set everything by hand.”`\n\n  The repository is private or the name is wrong. Install the GitHub app on it, or set the fields yourself.\n- `“Port N is already used by another project on this server.”`\n\n  Each project on a server needs its own port. Pick another one.\n- `“Deploy is turned off because N things on this container are in the way.”`\n\n  The container already holds a service, folder or program that Egg did not create. It will not stop or change it. Change the project name or port, or deal with it yourself. Projects has the full explanation.\n- `“The project was created, but NAME was not saved: reason. Press Deploy to try again.”`\n\n  The project exists. Press Deploy again; it does not create a second one.\n- `“The new release did not become healthy on port N. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.”`\n\n  The app started but nothing answered. Read the last log lines, fix the port or the address, and deploy again.\n\n## Example: Put recipe-bot online\n\n### Given\n\nSam has `sam-r/recipe-bot`, a Node.js Discord bot with a `start` script. The bot only talks out to Discord, so Sam adds a small web server that answers on the port Egg gives it. Sam’s Nest username is `sam-r`.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Add a tiny web server\n\n   Sam adds this line to `index.js` and pushes it: `http.createServer((_, res) => res.end(\"ok\")).listen(process.env.PORT, \"0.0.0.0\")`.\n2. Step 2: Sign in and connect\n\n   Sam presses Continue with Hack Club, then creates a key for `sam-r`, pastes it in Nest and presses Test connection.\n3. Step 3: Import\n\n   Sam presses New project, installs the GitHub app on `recipe-bot`, presses Import and presses Use these settings. The summary said Node.js with npm.\n4. Step 4: Keep the defaults\n\n   Sam keeps the production branch `main` and the port `3000`, and presses Deploy.\n\n### Result\n\nThe deployment goes Queued, Building, Deploying, Ready. The Build Logs card ends with “Live on port 3000. Map your domain to this port in the Nest dashboard.” The project’s Service card shows Running, and the Logs page shows the line the bot prints when it logs in to Discord.\n\n**Related:** [Servers](/docs/servers), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nStart\n\nPage 4 of 40\n\n# Run Egg yourself\n\nEgg is open source, so you can run your own copy with or without a Hack Club account. This is the short version.\n\nThe full guide is in the repository: [docs/selfhost/README.md](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md). It gives the exact steps for Windows, macOS and Linux, every setting, the GitHub app setup and a troubleshooting list. This page keeps to the choices that matter first.\n\n## Pick your setup\n\n| Setup | Who it is for | Sign-in | Start it with |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| On this computer | One person, using Egg privately. | A local password. No outside account. | `pnpm egg start` |\n| On your own server | You, or a few people you trust, at a domain with HTTPS. | Hack Club, or local mode for one owner behind your own access control. | `docker compose up -d --build` |\n| A shared instance | Several people, each with their own account. | Hack Club only. Without Hack Club there is one account. | The same, with the Hack Club values set |\n| A trial with a stand-in server | You want to see a deploy work with no Nest account. | Any of the above. | `pnpm fake-nest` |\n\nYou need Node.js 22.13 or newer, pnpm and Git on the computer that runs Egg. That computer only clones the repository, packs the source and uploads it. The install and the build run on the server your app is deployed to, so Egg needs no Docker for a local copy. The server uses Docker Compose and Caddy, which gets and renews HTTPS by itself.\n\nWindows, macOS and Linux all work, but only Linux and macOS are in the automated checks. `pnpm fake-nest` needs WSL on Windows.\n\n## Signing in without Hack Club\n\nLocal mode lets you run without Hack Club. Follow these steps:\n\n1. `pnpm run setup` writes `.env.local` with `LOCAL_MODE=1` and a private `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET`. `pnpm egg start` does this for you if the file is missing.\n2. On your first visit, the login page asks for that secret as the **recovery key**, and for a new password of at least 12 characters. Press **Set password and sign in**.\n3. Later you enter the password and press **Sign in on this computer**. If you forget it, use **Reset with the recovery key**.\n\nEgg stores only a salted hash of your password. Local mode makes one account, called Owner, and refuses to run when `APP_URL` is not localhost unless you set `LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE=1`, so use that only behind a VPN or an access proxy you control.\n\n- **Hack Club sign-in.** Set `HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID` and `HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET`, so anyone with a Hack Club account who can open your site gets their own account. Egg has no allow list.\n- **Email and password.** The card on Account turns it on only within 5 minutes of a Hack Club sign-in, so it cannot be turned on without Hack Club. The local owner has no email address and cannot use it.\n- **Confirming again.** Turning on Terminal or File changes needs a sign-in from the last 5 minutes. With local mode and no Hack Club, Egg counts you as signed in, so it does not ask. If you set Hack Club up as well, the local owner is sent to Hack Club, which cannot confirm it, so the switch stays off. Do not mix the two.\n\nOne account without Hack Club\n\nA production copy with no Hack Club has exactly one account, the local owner, so a second person cannot sign in. Set up Hack Club Auth, or run one copy of Egg for each person.\n\n## Where your apps run\n\nEgg reaches the server your apps run on by SSH, with a key made only for it. Egg was made for Hack Club Nest, so a server you own is not tested.\n\n- **Nest.** Enter your Nest username, paste the public key into the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and press Test connection.\n- **Your own server.** Set `NEST_HOST` and `NEST_PORT` in `.env.local` before you add the server, use the SSH user as the username, and add the public key to that user’s `authorized_keys`. Egg writes system service files and runs `systemctl` without sudo, so it expects root and a running systemd. Not tested on other hosts.\n- **Addresses.** The Domains page follows Nest’s rules, so on your own server put a reverse proxy in front of the app and keep its port closed to the internet.\n\nGitHub is optional, so a public repository deploys from its address with no setup. Private repositories and deploys on push need a GitHub app that you create yourself. GitHub must be able to reach `/api/webhooks/github` on your Egg to send pushes, so a copy on localhost needs a tunnel for that, or you deploy by hand.\n\nRotate values used with public static sites\n\nA public static deployment could once serve its `.env` file, so a value could be downloaded from `/.env`. If you ran one, rotate those values at their source and deploy again. Variables now live in the project’s `env/` folder on the server, outside every release, and the static server refuses any path that starts with a dot.\n\n## The full guide\n\nRead [the full guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md) for each system, every setting and every error message. [What does not work yet without Hack Club or Nest](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/design/self-host-gaps.md) is ranked, and [Customising](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/customising.md) covers the name, colours, ports and build memory.\n\n**Related:** [Run Egg locally](/docs/run-locally), [Running your own](/docs/selfhost).\n\n\n---\n\nStart\n\nPage 5 of 40\n\n# Run Egg locally\n\nSet up a working copy of Egg on your computer, start the development server, sign in, add sample data and run the checks.\n\nRun every command on this page in the `web` folder, unless it says otherwise. To use a copy without changing it, read [Run Egg yourself](/docs/run-yourself).\n\nWork through these steps in order:\n\n1. Check your Node.js version and turn on corepack.\n2. Clone the repository and install the dependencies.\n3. Create `web/.env.local` with `pnpm run setup`.\n4. Start the development server and sign in.\n5. Seed sample data, then run the checks and the production build.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nYou need Node.js 22.13 or newer and Git. The repository pins pnpm 11.20.0 in `package.json`, so that pnpm needs Node 22.13. This block checks your Node version, turns on corepack, which comes with Node and installs the pinned pnpm, then prints the pnpm version.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\nnode -v\ncorepack enable\npnpm -v\n```\n\nIf your computer blocks corepack, install pnpm from pnpm.io at the pinned version.\n\n## Install\n\nYou download the code and install its dependencies here.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ngit clone https://github.com/jeremy341/egg.git\ncd egg/web\npnpm install\n```\n\n## The env file\n\nThis block creates `web/.env.local` with a new `APP_SECRET` and `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET`, so it never overwrites a file that exists. Type `pnpm run setup`, not `pnpm setup`, which is a different pnpm command.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm run setup\n```\n\nThe file starts with the variables below. These are the ones the code reads for a local copy. The Hack Club and GitHub app values are optional and stay empty. See [Running your own](/docs/selfhost) for every setting.\n\n.env.local\n\n```\nAPP_SECRET=<32 or more random characters>\nLOCAL_MODE_SECRET=<32 or more random characters>\nAPP_URL=http://localhost:3000\nDATABASE_URL=file:./data/egg.db\nLOCAL_MODE=1\nDEV_LOGIN=0\nRUN_WORKER=1\n```\n\n- `APP_SECRET` seals the SSH keys and variables in your database, so back it up with `web/data/egg.db`.\n- `APP_URL` is the address you open in the browser, and `pnpm egg start` uses its port for the server.\n- `RUN_WORKER=1` runs the deployment queue inside the same process.\n\n## Start the dev server\n\nThis block starts the development server. It listens on `127.0.0.1` at port 3000, the Next.js default, and reloads when you edit code. Open `http://localhost:3000/login` in your browser, so you reach the dashboard and not the public home page at `/`.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm dev\n```\n\nThis block checks the computer first, then starts the server on the port in `APP_URL` and opens the browser. Ctrl+C stops either way of starting it.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm egg start --dev\n```\n\nThis block only checks the computer: Node.js, pnpm, `.env.local`, `APP_SECRET`, the port and the data folder. It starts nothing.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm run doctor\n```\n\n## Development sign-in\n\nDevelopment sign-in lets you in as a made-up user, so you need no Hack Club account. Add this line to `.env.local` and restart the server.\n\n.env.local\n\n```\nDEV_LOGIN=1\n```\n\n- You then see a **Development sign-in** form with a name (`dev` by default, 40 characters at most) and a **Sign in** button. Egg stores the user as `dev:` plus the lowercase name.\n- It works only in development, so in a production build the form is hidden and the route answers 404, whatever `DEV_LOGIN` says.\n- With `LOCAL_MODE=1` on, as `pnpm run setup` leaves it, the form also asks for the local password, so set one first with **Sign in on this computer** and the recovery key from `.env.local`.\n- **Origin rule.** Egg refuses a browser write whose `Origin` is not the origin of `APP_URL` or of the address the request came to, so it answers 403 “Cross-origin request blocked”. Open the site at the address in `APP_URL`, and change `APP_URL` if you use another port. After sign-in Egg redirects to `APP_URL` followed by `/projects`.\n- `pnpm run doctor` counts `DEV_LOGIN` as a way to sign in only with `--dev`.\n\n## Demo data\n\nThis block fills your local database with sample projects, deployments and charts for the `dev` user, so you can see a full dashboard. Sign in with the development form and the name `dev`. Every sample row id starts with `demo-`.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm seed:demo\n```\n\nThe sample server points at `127.0.0.1` port 1, so pages that read it live, such as the Service card and runtime logs, show a connection error. This block removes exactly the rows the script made.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm seed:demo --reset\n```\n\n## Tests and lint\n\nThis block runs every test once, with Vitest as the test runner.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm test\n```\n\nThis block runs the tests in one file, so replace the path with any test file you have.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm test src/server/config.test.ts\n```\n\nThis block runs the linter, then the TypeScript check, which writes no files.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm lint\npnpm typecheck\n```\n\n## The production build\n\nThis block makes the production build and starts it on `127.0.0.1`, at port 3000 unless `PORT` is set. Development sign-in is off in this build, so sign in with local mode or Hack Club.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\npnpm build\npnpm start\n```\n\n`pnpm egg start` does the same with the computer check first. It builds the first time by itself and uses the port in `APP_URL`.\n\n**Related:** [Run Egg yourself](/docs/run-yourself), [Running your own](/docs/selfhost).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 6 of 40\n\n# Servers\n\nA server is one Nest account. You can connect up to five, and each holds its own projects and usage.\n\nConnecting a server gives Egg one way into your Nest container, and nothing else. You make a key and paste its public half into the Nest dashboard, so Egg can deploy, restart and read logs. Egg connects from the Egg machine over SSH. You need a Nest account and your Nest username, the name you log in with, for example `sam-r`.\n\n## How the connection works\n\nEgg makes a dedicated ed25519 key for each server and uses it for nothing else. The key’s comment reads `egg:` followed by your username. You paste the public half into the Nest dashboard, so Nest’s bastion routes the connection to your container as `root`, just like your own login. The page warns you: “This key gives Egg the same access as your own login, because SSH into Nest lands in a root shell inside your container. Only connect a container you are happy to deploy to.”\n\nEgg seals the private key with AES-256-GCM, tied to that server, before storing it in Egg’s database on the Egg machine, and never shows it. The first time you press Test connection, Egg reaches the host, reads its host key, hangs up and shows the SHA-256 fingerprint under “Confirm host key”. Nothing reaches the server until you press **Confirm host key**, so Egg records the fingerprint and stops any other host key before it sends anything. Until then, no deploy, file change or terminal connects. The address is `hackclub.app` on port 22, and a self-hosted copy can change both with `NEST_HOST` and `NEST_PORT`.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA key made for one server lets you end Egg’s access without touching your own login, so deleting the key in the Nest dashboard stops access at once. Egg pins the host key because it sends your environment variables and your build to whatever answers, so a different answer stops the connection instead of handing your data over.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Create the key\n\n   Open Connect. Under **Add your server** (the heading reads “Add another server” once you have one), type your Nest username in **Nest username** and press **Create key**, so the status reads “Waiting for the key” and the card shows the **Public key**.\n2. Step 2: Paste it into Nest\n\n   Copy the **Public key** field with its copy button. Press **Open the Nest dashboard** to open `dashboard.hackclub.app` in a new tab, then paste the key on the SSH Keys page.\n3. Step 3: Test the connection\n\n   Back on Connect, press **Test connection**, so the button reads “Connecting…”. The first time, the page stops at a “Confirm host key” box that says “This is the first connection to NAME.hackclub.app” and shows a fingerprint. Check it against the one Nest shows for your server, then press **Confirm host key** (“Confirming…”), so Egg reads your container. On success the status reads “Connected” and a “Reached your container” panel lists System, Logged in as, Node.js, Python, systemd and Key fingerprint. That last row is the fingerprint of the key Egg made, not of Nest’s host key. A missing tool shows as “not installed”.\n4. Step 4: Read the warnings\n\n   Under the panel, red and amber boxes list anything missing on your container. The table below says what each one means.\n\n## Testing, rotating and removing\n\n- **Test connection** opens an SSH session, asks you to confirm the host key the first time, and reads your container’s operating system, user, Node.js and Python versions, tools, systemd, CPU, memory and disk. It only reads, so it changes nothing on your container and you can press it at any time. A failure saves the message and the status becomes “Could not connect”; the next success clears it.\n- **New key** replaces the key pair. A dialog titled “Generate a new key?” says “The old key stops working once you remove it in the Nest dashboard, and you must add the new one.” Confirm with **Generate key**. Egg uses the new private key from that moment, so deploys fail with “Nest rejected the key” until you paste the new public key and test again. The server goes back to “Waiting for the key” and the saved reading is cleared. The pinned host key is kept. Remove the old public key in the Nest dashboard to end its access. The change is recorded in Activity.\n- **Disconnect** opens “Disconnect NAME?”, with the text “This closes its terminals and removes our key for it. Your running apps are not touched. Remove the key from the Nest dashboard to end our access.” If the server has projects, the dialog also shows a checkbox, “Also delete the project on this server” or “Also delete the N projects on this server”, which starts unticked. Unticked, the projects are kept: “Their apps keep running, but Egg stops managing them until you connect this server again.” Ticked, they are deleted with their deployments, logs, environment variables, hook and status page, and the apps still keep running on the container. Either way Egg’s key is removed, any terminal open on that server closes, and the change is recorded in Activity.\n\nComing back after a disconnect\n\nA disconnected server drops out of the list and no longer counts toward the limit of five. Its kept projects stay in Egg but cannot be deployed or read: any attempt answers “NAME is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.” To bring them back, add the same Nest username again on Connect. You get a new key and a new host key to confirm, and the kept projects attach to it. Egg never touches the apps on your container while the server is disconnected.\n\n| Message | Shown as | What it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| systemd is not available in this container, so Egg cannot keep your apps running or restart them. | Error | Apps run as systemd services. Without it nothing can be started or restarted. |\n| SSH did not log in as root. Nest normally does; without root, installing the service files may fail. | Warning | Writing the service file needs root. |\n| tar is not installed. | Error | Releases are unpacked on the container with tar. |\n| sha256sum is missing, so uploads cannot be verified. | Warning | The upload still goes through, without the checksum comparison. |\n| curl is missing. Health checks fall back to a plain TCP connection check. | Warning | The health check only tests that the port accepts a connection, not what it answers. |\n| Neither Node.js nor Python 3 is installed. Only projects that ship their own binary can run. | Warning | Node.js projects install their production dependencies on the container, Python projects create a virtual environment there, and static sites need one of the two to be served. |\n| Only N MB of disk is free. Releases are kept for rollback, so deploys may fail. | Warning | Shown when under 1024 MB is free. |\n\nThese are warnings about your container, so Egg only shows them and does not stop a deploy because of one. Fix the ones that match what you want to run. After a test, the Projects page also shows a usage card with CPU load, memory and disk from that reading, or “No reading yet. Press Test connection on the Connect page.” when there is none. It shows the first connected server.\n\nTaking access back\n\nDelete the key in your Nest dashboard, so access ends at once. Your apps keep running because they are ordinary services in your container.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Servers per account | 5, not counting disconnected ones | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER |\n| Nest username | Letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen; 1 to 32 characters; starts with a letter or digit | “That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Servers per Nest username | One per account | “NAME is already connected.” In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Connect timeout | 20 seconds | “Timed out connecting to Nest. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.” In the code: server/ssh/connection.ts |\n| Connection read | 30 seconds | The test fails and the Connect page shows the error. In the code: server/ssh/probe.ts |\n| Host key | Pinned when you confirm it on the first Test connection | Until then nothing connects: a deploy answers “Confirm host key: open the Connect page, check the fingerprint of this server and confirm it first.” Any other key stops the connection. Only Disconnect, then adding the server again, clears the pin. In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Free disk before a warning | 1024 MB | An amber warning after a test. In the code: server/ssh/probe.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“Nest rejected the key. Check that you pasted the public key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and that the username is exactly your Nest username.”`\n\n  Paste the key again, copying the whole Public key field, and check the username. After New key, this is expected until the new key is pasted.\n- `“Timed out connecting to Nest. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.”`\n\n  Wait, then press Test connection again.\n- `“Could not reach the Nest SSH host (reason).”`\n\n  The connection failed before login. Try again in a minute, and check that Nest itself is up.\n- `“Confirm host key: open the Connect page, check the fingerprint of this server and confirm it first.”`\n\n  The server has no confirmed host key yet, so a deploy, file change or terminal will not connect to it. Open Connect, press **Test connection** and then **Confirm host key**.\n- `“The host key of HOST changed (was OLD, now NEW). Nothing was sent. If you expected this, reconnect the server.”`\n\n  Egg refuses on purpose. If you know the host key changed, press **Disconnect** and add the server again. Leave the project checkbox unticked and the projects reattach, because Disconnect clears the pinned host key.\n- `“SSH error: message”`\n\n  Anything else the SSH library reported. The message is the clue; try the test again once.\n- `“That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.”`\n\n  Use the name you log in to Nest with, without `@hackclub.app`.\n- `“You can connect up to 5 servers.” or “NAME is already connected.”`\n\n  Disconnect one you no longer use, or use the server you already added.\n- `“NAME is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.”`\n\n  The server was disconnected and its projects kept. Add the same Nest username again on Connect.\n\n### Example: Connect sam-r and replace the key\n\n#### Given\n\nSam’s Nest username is `sam-r`. Sam has not connected a server yet. Two months later Sam shares a laptop and wants a fresh key.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Create the key\n\n   Sam opens Connect, types `sam-r` and presses Create key.\n2. Step 2: Paste it in Nest\n\n   Sam pastes the public key on the Nest dashboard’s SSH Keys page.\n3. Step 3: Test and confirm\n\n   Sam presses Test connection, checks the fingerprint in the “Confirm host key” box and presses Confirm host key.\n4. Step 4: Replace the key later\n\n   Sam presses New key, then Generate key, pastes the new public key in Nest, removes the old one there and presses Test connection again.\n\n#### Result\n\nThe first test shows “Reached your container” with the system, Node.js and systemd rows, and the status reads “Connected”. After the replacement the status goes to “Waiting for the key” and back to “Connected”. The host key was not asked for again, because it was confirmed the first time. Sam’s projects and apps were not touched.\n\n**Related:** [Quick start](/docs/quickstart), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Security](/docs/security), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 7 of 40\n\n# Keys\n\nKeys are the SSH keys Egg makes for your projects and for you. Each one is a pair: the public half can be shared, and Egg keeps the private half sealed in its database.\n\nThe Keys page lists the keys you make for a project and for yourself. The key Egg makes for each server is listed there too, with a copy button for its public half and a **New key** action. That server key is described on the Servers page.\n\n## The three kinds\n\n- **GitHub deploy key.** A read-only key that Egg installs on the GitHub repository linked to one project, through the Egg GitHub App. You paste nothing. Once GitHub confirms it, the row shows an “On GitHub” badge.\n- **Git key.** A key for a git host you run yourself. Egg makes the pair, you paste the public half into your host, and the private half is shown once.\n- **Personal key.** A key that belongs to your account rather than a project, for a script or a tool you use outside Egg. Its private half is shown once, too.\n\n## GitHub deploy keys\n\nA deploy key can read the repository and nothing else: it is read-only, so it cannot push. Egg adds it when the project is linked to a repository. Until it is, Egg refuses to add one and says a repository is needed.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA read-only key cannot change your code, even if it leaks. Revoking a deploy key removes it from GitHub before Egg marks it revoked, so the key cannot keep opening the repository after you have revoked it.\n\n## Git keys\n\nUse a git key for a git host that is not on GitHub, such as a server you run yourself. Add it to a project from the Keys page, and name it if you want. Copy its public half into your host’s SSH key settings. The private half is shown once, in the same dialog, so copy it before you press **I have copied it**. Egg keeps it sealed, and the page never shows it again.\n\n## Personal keys\n\nPress **Generate key** in the Personal keys card and give the key a name. Egg shows the private and the public half once. Copy both, then press **I have copied it**. A personal key is not tied to a project, and revoking it in Egg does not change anything outside Egg.\n\n## Adding, replacing and revoking\n\n- **Add key** is on each project’s group. It opens “Add a key to NAME”. Pick **GitHub deploy key** or **Git key**, name a git key if you want, and press **Add key**. A deploy key then reads “Added to GitHub automatically.”\n- **Replace key** is on a GitHub deploy key. The dialog says Egg makes a new read-only key, installs it on GitHub and removes the old one there. If GitHub would not remove the old key, the page says so, and you must delete the old deploy key in the repository’s settings on GitHub yourself.\n- **Revoke** asks first. A deploy key is removed from GitHub before it is revoked here, and if GitHub cannot be reached nothing changes and the key stays active. A git key or a personal key is only marked as revoked, so remove its public half from your host or from anything else that trusts it.\n- Revoked keys move under **Revoked** with the date they were revoked, and they cannot be used again.\n\n## Limits and problems\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Key name | 1 to 60 characters, on one line | “Give the key a name.” or “Keep the name to one line.” In the code: server/ssh-keys.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- The private key is gone.\n\n  It was shown once. Revoke that key and add a new one; Egg cannot show the private half again.\n- Revoking a deploy key is refused, or the key stays active.\n\n  Egg could not remove the deploy key from GitHub, so it kept the key. Check that the Egg GitHub App is still installed on the repository, then try again.\n- Replacing a deploy key says the old key is still on GitHub.\n\n  The new key is in place, but GitHub did not let Egg remove the old one. Delete the old deploy key in the repository’s settings on GitHub.\n- Adding a deploy key says a repository is needed.\n\n  The project is not linked to a GitHub repository yet. Link one to the project, then add the key.\n\n**Related:** [Servers](/docs/servers), [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 8 of 40\n\n# Projects and runtimes\n\nA project ties one GitHub repository to one app on one server.\n\nA project holds everything Egg needs to build and run one app: its repository and branch, Nest server, runtime, commands, port, and the variables, hook and alerts that go with it. You need a connected server. A private repository, and automatic deploys on push, need the Egg GitHub app installed on it.\n\n## Settings\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Pick a repository\n\n   Press **New project**. Pick the account under **GitHub account**, find your repository with **Search repositories…** and press **Import**. Or paste a public address under “Or use a public repository URL” and press **Continue**, so it has to look like `https://github.com/owner/name`. A pasted address starts on your Default branch from Settings, which is `main` until you change it. With no server connected, the page shows “Connect a server first”.\n2. Step 2: Read what Egg found\n\n   The “We found” summary lists the package manager, runtime, install, build and start commands and output folder Egg read from your repository, with notes under them. Nothing is applied until you press **Use these settings**, so it fills **Application preset** and the four command and folder fields but leaves the name, branch, root directory and port alone. If the repository holds several apps, “Apps in this repository” shows a button for each folder, and pressing one sets the root directory.\n3. Step 3: Fill in the fields\n\n   Choose **Nest server**, **Project name**, **Application preset** (Node.js, Python, Static site or Custom command), **Production branch**, **Root directory** and **Port your app listens on**. Leave **Install command (optional)**, **Start command (optional)**, **Build command (optional)** and, for static sites, **Output directory** empty to use the defaults. The project name is also your container’s folder and service name (`egg-NAME.service`), so the form only accepts lowercase letters, digits and hyphens and says “Use lowercase letters, numbers and hyphens.” otherwise.\n4. Step 4: Fill in variables and deploy\n\n   If the repository has an example env file, an **Environment variables** group shows one empty field per name. A name with a value becomes a variable for All environments; an empty one is skipped. Press **Deploy**. The project is created, the values are saved and the first deployment is queued.\n5. Step 5: Change settings later\n\n   Open the project, Settings, **Build and Run**, change a field and press **Save**, so the page says “Saved. Changes apply to the next deployment.” You cannot change the server or the repository, so to move an app you make a new project.\n\n| Setting | What it does | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Production branch | A push here deploys to production. Any other branch builds a preview. | The repository’s default branch; a pasted address starts at your Default branch from Settings (main unless you changed it) |\n| Root directory | The folder inside the repository to build, for monorepos. For a subfolder, a help line shows what GitHub has there: package.json, Lockfile, requirements.txt and Start script. | `.` |\n| Runtime | How to install, build and run it. See Runtimes. | Node.js |\n| Install command, Build command, Start command | Override a command. Each is one line of at most 500 characters. | Detected from the runtime |\n| Output directory | Static sites only: the folder to serve. | The root |\n| Port | The port the app listens on. Unique on a server. | `3000` |\n| Health check path | The path requested to decide the app is up. Starts with a slash. It is not on the New project form, so set it in Settings. | `/` |\n| Deploy automatically on push | Build on every push to a branch of the repository. | On |\n\nThe “We found” step reads your repository from GitHub before Egg creates anything. Here is what it looks at.\n\n- **Language.** A `package.json` means Node.js, so otherwise `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml` means Python. Egg names a `go.mod`, `Gemfile` or `Cargo.toml` as not supported yet, with a hint to try the Custom command preset. It names a `Dockerfile` so you can find it, but your project still starts in native process mode, so a Dockerfile counts only when you choose Dockerfile mode and your server passed its Docker check.\n- **Package manager.** The `packageManager` field in `package.json`, else a lockfile (`pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `bun.lock` or `bun.lockb`), else npm. More than one lockfile gives a warning to delete the ones you do not use. Bun is detected but not built yet. The build itself picks the package manager from the lockfile only, so keep the lockfile and the field in agreement.\n- **Static site.** A Vite or Astro project with no `start` script is suggested as a Static site with output folder `dist`. A Node.js project with no `start` script gets the note “package.json has no \"start\" script. Add one, or set a start command yourself.”\n- **Monorepo.** Workspaces, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, or `package.json` files under `packages/*` and `apps/*` give the note “This looks like a monorepo. Set the root directory to the app you want to deploy.” Up to 20 folders are offered as buttons.\n- **Variable names.** Only `package.json` and the example env files `.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template` and `.env.local.example` are read, and only for their names. Values are discarded. At most 100 names are listed, and invalid or reserved names are dropped. A committed `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production` or `.env.production.local` is never read, and gives the warning “A .env file is committed to this repository. Remove it from git and rotate the values.”\n- **Size.** A file over 200,000 bytes is skipped, paths deeper than 3 folders under the root are ignored, and a huge repository adds “This repository is very large, so some files may not have been looked at.” GitHub gets 10 seconds for the file list and 8 seconds for each file.\n\nThese are the other controls on the form and in Settings.\n\n- **Copy build settings from another project** fills the form from a project on the same server: runtime, install, build and start commands, output directory, root directory and health path. Choose a project and press **Copy**. The page says “Copied from NAME. Review the fields below, then press Save. Nothing is saved yet.” Variables, hooks, notifications, the port and the repository are not copied.\n- **Start with automatic deploys off** is for a repository you already run on this container by hand. The project is created with automatic deploys off, and the form says “The first deploy is manual, and automatic deploys start only after you confirm that first deploy works.” Nothing turns them on for you: tick **Deploy automatically on push** in Settings when the first manual deploy works. This option does not import, take over or stop the copy that is already running, and Egg does not import a running service yet. The container check still runs, and if your old process holds the port you stop it yourself first.\n- **The container check.** Once the name, server and port are valid, the form waits 600 milliseconds and then asks your container, read only, whether the project would collide with something there. The result is the “What is already on this container” panel. A line with the badge “Blocks the deploy” turns the Deploy button off. “Made by Egg” and “Good to know” lines are information only. The same check runs again at the start of every deploy, before anything is written.\n- **Show on Egg Homepage** is off for every project. It is separate from the public status page. Turning it on lists the project’s latest deployments on the public Egg homepage: the first line of the commit message, the short commit, the branch, the state, Production or Preview, the time and the repository name, each linking to the commit on GitHub. Build logs, your server, ports and environment variables are never shown. Only the owner can turn it on, and only for a repository imported through the GitHub App that GitHub says is public: Egg asks GitHub when you press **Show on homepage** and again each time the list is built, at most once a minute. A private or unconfirmed repository is refused with a message, and one that turns private leaves the homepage within a minute. Press **Stop showing** to take the project off.\n- **Delete Project** asks you to type the project name. With “Also stop the service and delete releases on NAME.hackclub.app” ticked, which is the default, the app goes offline. The service, the production and preview releases and the `current` link are removed from the container, but only if Egg created them. The `shared/` folder, `~/.egg/NAME/shared`, is kept, so data your app stored there is still on the container. Unticked, only the records go and the app keeps running. A project with a deployment that is Queued, Building or Deploying cannot be deleted. With the box ticked, Egg checks this first and answers 409 before it touches anything on the container, so a refused delete changes nothing. While the removal runs, new deployments for the project are refused too, whether they come from a push, a hook, the API or the Deploy button. If the removal fails, the block is lifted and the records are kept so you can try again.\n\n| It finds | Blocks the deploy? | What to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A service egg-NAME.service that Egg did not create, or one that systemd already has | Yes | Choose another project name. Egg will not replace it. |\n| The folder ~/.egg/NAME is a link, a file, a git checkout that is not Egg’s, or holds other files | Yes | Choose another project name, or clear the folder yourself. |\n| The port is already used by another program | Yes | Choose another port, or stop that program yourself first. Egg will not stop it. |\n| A program or service already runs from the project folder | Yes, while it runs | Stop it first, or choose another project name. |\n| The same repository already runs by hand from another checkout | Yes, but only for the first production deploy | Stop it yourself first. Egg does not take over a copy that is already running. |\n| Leftovers from an earlier Egg project with this name | No | “It will be reused.” |\n| The listening ports could not be read, a stopped service for the same repository, or a checkout that just sits there | No | Shown as information. A checkout is never touched. |\n\n## Runtimes\n\n| Runtime | Install and build | Default start |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Node.js | Picks pnpm, then yarn, then npm from your lockfile (npm install if there is none) and runs your build script if you have one. The install step uses a frozen lockfile. The build runs the install, the build and then a production-only install on your container, in the new release folder, under the cap, which uses its CPU, memory and disk on every deploy. node_modules is not uploaded. | `npm start` |\n| Python | No build step. On every deploy your container creates a virtual environment in .venv and installs requirements.txt into it, if the file exists, which uses its CPU, memory and disk. This runs under the cap, after your install and build commands, if you set them. | `python3 app.py` |\n| Static site | Builds if there is a build script, then ships only the Output directory. It is served by a short built-in server run with Python 3, or with Node.js if Python is missing. That server answers only GET and HEAD, shows no folder listings, and refuses any path that has a part starting with a dot (such as .env or .git) and any link that points outside the folder. | `Egg’s static server on PORT` |\n| Custom command | No detection and no install on the container. You give the install, build and start commands. | Your command |\n\nThe build uses the Node.js and Python on your container. Your Node.js must match the `engines` range in `package.json`, or be 22.13 or newer when there is no range. For Node.js the install command is `npm ci`, `corepack enable && pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` or `corepack enable && yarn install --frozen-lockfile`, and the build command is `npm run build`, `pnpm build` or `yarn build`. The service runs your start command through a login shell, so tools installed with nvm are on the path. Egg names Go, Ruby and Rust as not supported by detection, so use Custom command if you can run a prebuilt binary. Bun is detected but not built yet.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe build runs next to your app, and the Egg machine only clones and packs the source, so the GitHub token never leaves it. The cap exists because a Nest container is small, so the kernel stops a build that goes over it before it can stop the app, and that is why build memory is a setting. A failed build never touches the running release, and container checks look before they touch anything, because the container is yours and may hold things Egg did not make.\n\n## What every app can rely on\n\n- It runs from its release folder with `PORT`, `HOST=0.0.0.0` and `NODE_ENV=production` set. Production runs from the `current` link, and a preview from its own release folder.\n- systemd restarts it three seconds after it exits (`Restart=always`, `RestartSec=3`), so a crash is not the end of the app, and it starts again when the container boots.\n- It sees your environment variables from a file only the service reads. The names `PORT`, `HOST`, `PATH`, `HOME` and `EGG` are reserved, in any letter case.\n- Files in `shared/` are never touched by a deploy, so data the app keeps there survives new releases. Write data there, not into the release folder, which is replaced on every deploy.\n\n## Dockerfile and Compose modes\n\nNative is the default, so your project deploys as a container only once your server passes the Docker check. The check runs when settings open and when you test the connection, and it asks the Docker on your container, not Egg's own, about cgroup limits, free disk at its storage root and the port proxy. When a part fails, the Dockerfile and Compose options stay greyed out with the reason, and nothing changes on your server.\n\n- **Dockerfile mode.** Egg uploads your source, then builds the image on your container with `docker build`, under the same memory, CPU and disk caps as a native build, so every release gets its own image tag. A systemd service starts and watches one named container, so restarts, journal logs, the Restarts count and rollbacks work as they do for a native process.\n- **Compose mode.** The Compose file is read by Docker itself, and only the resolved form Egg checked is ever run. Services must use prebuilt images, host ports must bind to `127.0.0.1` only, and the one service Egg monitors must not publish ports of its own; Egg maps the project's listen port onto it. Every other service, such as a database, starts with `docker compose up -d` and keeps its named volumes across releases. Rollbacks restore the previous release's code and configuration, never the data the stack wrote.\n- **What stays the same.** Environment variables reach the container at run time through the same encrypted path, nothing is baked into the image. A bot in a container mode can still open one optional listen port. Scheduled jobs run inside the running container with `docker exec`. Previews exist for Dockerfile projects; a Compose stack has no branch preview yet. A preview on a Compose project is refused when it is queued, with a message saying to deploy the branch to production instead, so no build time is wasted.\n- **What is refused.** Compose files that build images, set a container name, add depends_on, links, devices or privileged options, mount bind paths or outside volumes, join external networks, or publish ports on other interfaces, are rejected before anything starts, with the first reasons named in the logs. Only a short allowlist of service fields is accepted. A Compose project also cannot use the GitHub Actions build: the image is built on your server.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Project name | 1 to 60 characters | “name: Too big: expected string to have <=60 characters” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Address-safe name (slug) | 1 to 40 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens; starts and ends with a letter or digit | “The project name must use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (up to 40).” In the code: server/deploy/layout.ts |\n| Commands | 500 characters, one line | “startCommand: Commands must be a single line” or “startCommand: Too big: expected string to have <=500 characters” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Root directory | Relative, no “..”, 200 characters | “rootDir: Root directory must be a relative path inside the repository”, or at deploy time “The root directory “DIR” does not exist in this repository.” In the code: server/projects.ts server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Branch | 200 characters; letters, digits and . _ - /; no leading hyphen; no “..” | “productionBranch: Branch names may use letters, digits, . _ - and /” or “productionBranch: Invalid branch name” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Port | 1024 to 65535, and not 20000 to 29999; unique on a server | “port: Ports 20000 to 29999 are reserved for previews”, “port: Too small: expected number to be >=1024”, “Port N is already used by another project on this server.” (in Settings: “Port N is already used on this server.”) In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Health check path | Starts with a slash, 200 characters | “healthPath: Must start with /” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Repository address | https, github.com only, no credentials | “Only plain https repository URLs are supported.” or “Repositories on HOST are not supported yet. Supported: github.com.” In the code: server/deploy/build.ts assertCloneUrl |\n| Detection reads | Files up to 200,000 bytes, 3 folders deep, 100 variable names, 20 app folders | The rest is skipped. In the code: server/repo-read.ts server/repo-detect.ts |\n| Container checks while you fill the form | 20 a minute | “Too many checks. Wait N seconds and try again.” In the code: app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts |\n| Environment variables per project | 100 | See Environment variables. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“You already have a project called “name”.”`\n\n  Project names are unique for you, across servers. Pick another name.\n- `“Deploy is turned off because N things on this container are in the way.”`\n\n  The conflict check found a service, folder or port that Egg did not create. It will not stop or change them. The panel above lists each one. Change the project name, server or port, or fix it on your container. The link “Open container status” opens the Container page in a new tab.\n- `“Could not check this container just now: reason. The deploy checks again before it changes anything.”`\n\n  The form could not ask your container. You can still deploy; the check runs again at the start.\n- `“Nothing was changed on your container.” followed by details`\n\n  The same check, run at deploy time, stopped the deployment before it wrote anything. The first three reasons are quoted.\n- `“Set a start command for this project (for example ./my-binary).”`\n\n  A Custom command project has no default start. Fill in the Start command.\n- `“Static sites need Python 3 or Node.js on your Nest container to be served.”`\n\n  Install one of them on the container, or use another runtime.\n- `“Could not read this repository. If it is private, install the GitHub App so it can be read. You can still set everything by hand.”`\n\n  Install the app on the repository. “Could not find the BRANCH branch of REPO.” means the branch name is wrong.\n- `“GitHub did not answer (STATUS). Try again later.” or “Could not reach GitHub to look. Try again in a moment.”`\n\n  GitHub is not answering. The form still works with fields you fill by hand.\n- No folder called “DIR” on the BRANCH branch.\n\n  The root directory does not exist on that branch. Other lines say “That path is a file, not a folder.” or “That root directory is not a valid relative path.”\n- `“The project was created, but NAME was not saved: reason. Press Deploy to try again.”`\n\n  The project exists. Press Deploy again; it does not create a second project.\n- `“A deployment is still running for this project. Cancel it or wait for it to finish, then delete the project.”`\n\n  Cancel the deployment on the Deployments tab, then delete the project. Nothing on the container was touched.\n- `“A deployment is still stopping for this project. Wait for it to finish, then delete the project.”`\n\n  A worker still holds a job for this project. Wait a minute and try again.\n- `409 “Cleanup is in progress for this project. Wait for it to finish or retry it before making changes.”`\n\n  The project is being removed from the container, and new deployments are blocked until that ends. Wait for it, then check whether the project still exists.\n- `“Could not remove the project from your container.”`\n\n  The container did not finish the removal. Test the connection and try again. The records are kept so you can retry.\n- `“Pick a different project to copy from.” or “You can only copy from a project on the same server.”`\n\n  Choose another project from the same server in the Copy list. “There are no other projects on this server to copy from.” means there is none.\n\nPasted URLs do not deploy on push\n\nA project made from a pasted public repository URL stores no repository name, so a push never builds it. Deploy, a deploy hook or the API builds it instead, so import through the GitHub app when you want pushes to deploy.\n\n### Example: Set up recipe-bot and change its health path\n\n#### Given\n\nSam imports `sam-r/recipe-bot`. The “We found” summary says Node.js with `npm`, with the build command left empty because there is no build script, and Sam’s `package.json` has a `start` script. The repository has a `.env.example` naming `DISCORD_TOKEN`.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Import and accept\n\n   Sam presses Use these settings, names the project `recipe-bot`, keeps the production branch `main` and the port `3000`.\n2. Step 2: Fill the variable\n\n   Sam pastes the Discord token into the `DISCORD_TOKEN` field and presses Deploy.\n3. Step 3: Change the health path\n\n   Later Sam opens Settings, Build and Run, sets Health check path to `/health` and presses Save.\n\n#### Result\n\nThe project and the first deployment are created, and `DISCORD_TOKEN` exists for All environments. After the Save, the page reads “Saved. Changes apply to the next deployment.” The next deploy checks `/health` instead of `/`.\n\n**Related:** [Environment variables](/docs/env), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Limits](/docs/limits).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 9 of 40\n\n# Build with GitHub Actions\n\nGitHub builds the app and sends the finished release to Egg, so your Nest container only runs it. This is the recommended choice for a project made from a GitHub repository.\n\n## What it is and why it is recommended\n\nWith this choice a workflow in your repository installs, builds and packs the app on GitHub's machines. It sends the finished release to Egg. Egg unpacks it on your container, starts it and checks that it answers. Your container never runs the build, so a framework build that needs a lot of memory no longer competes with your app for it.\n\nThis is the default for a new project. Pick **Build on Nest** when you want no dependence on GitHub; see [Build on Nest](/docs/build-on-nest). A project made from a pasted public address has no link to the GitHub app. The new project flow does not offer GitHub Actions for it.\n\nBuilds use your GitHub Actions minutes.\n\n## Setting it up\n\nNew projects take you straight to these steps. For an existing project, open Settings, then **Where Builds Run**. Choose **GitHub Actions** and press Save. Each step opens one at a time, and finished steps fold into one line. Egg stores no progress. It knows whether the credentials exist and whether the last test passed. It asks you about the two steps that only GitHub can confirm.\n\n1. **Make the build credentials.** Press **Make credentials**. Egg shows four values once and keeps only a fingerprint. Making new ones stops the old ones at once.\n2. **Save the secrets on GitHub.** Save each value under the name and in the place the next section lists. Press **I saved all four secrets**. Egg cannot read GitHub secrets, so this step is your confirmation.\n3. **Add the workflow file.** Press **Add to repository**. Egg opens a pull request that adds the file. Merge it on GitHub. Without the GitHub app permissions below, copy or download `egg.yml` and commit it to the production branch yourself.\n4. **Test the connection.** Egg checks its own records and, read only, your repository. Every failed check says what to do. The last section here goes through them.\n5. **Run the first build.** Push a commit, or open the workflow on GitHub and press **Run workflow**. The deployment appears on the project's Deployments page.\n\n## The four secrets\n\nThe workflow reads four secrets. The two production values live in a GitHub Environment called `egg-production`. You limit that environment to the production branch. The two preview values are repository secrets.\n\n| Name | Saved in | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `EGG_UPLOAD_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` | The egg-production environment | Sends a finished production build to Egg. |\n| `EGG_ENV_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` | The egg-production environment | Reads the production build variables. |\n| `EGG_UPLOAD_PREVIEW_TOKEN` | Repository secrets | Sends a finished preview build to Egg. |\n| `EGG_ENV_PREVIEW_TOKEN` | Repository secrets | Reads the preview build variables. |\n\n- To make the environment, open the repository's Settings, then Environments, then New environment. Under Deployment branches and tags, choose Selected branches and tags and add the production branch. You can turn on Required reviewers so a person approves each production build.\n- Preview secrets go under Settings, Secrets and variables, Actions, as repository secrets.\n- **Never save the two production values as repository secrets.** A repository secret can be read by a workflow that someone edits on any branch.\n\n## What the pull request does\n\n**Add to repository** asks the GitHub app to create a branch, commit `.github/workflows/egg.yml` to it and open a pull request into the production branch. If a pull request from an earlier press is still open, Egg updates it. If the production branch already has an identical file, Egg changes nothing and says so. Nothing is merged for you. The workflow only starts working when you merge.\n\nThis needs three permissions on the GitHub app\n\nAdding the workflow by pull request needs Egg's GitHub app to have **Contents**, **Pull requests** and **Workflows** read and write access. Only the person who runs this Egg can grant them, in the app's settings on GitHub. Without them, Egg says so. You copy or download the file instead, and everything else works the same.\n\n## Forks, pull requests and production secrets\n\n- The workflow runs on pushes and on **Run workflow**, not on pull requests. A pull request from a fork therefore never runs it and never receives a secret.\n- Only the job for the production branch can use the `egg-production` environment. A workflow edited on another branch can ask for it. GitHub then refuses the secrets.\n- Anyone who can push a workflow can run code with the preview credentials and with whatever else the repository allows. Treat the people who can edit workflows as trusted operators of this project. Each project has a single owner.\n- The credentials can send a release and read build variables, and nothing else. A production credential cannot deliver a preview build. A preview credential cannot deliver a production one.\n\n## Limits\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at the limit |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Release size | 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes) | The workflow stops with a message and nothing is sent. |\n| Wait for the release | 60 minutes | Egg stops waiting: “GitHub did not send the build in time. Check the workflow run, then deploy again.” |\n| Build credential lifetime | 90 days | Test connection reports them as expired. Make new ones and replace all four secrets. |\n\nThese numbers are the ones the setup steps show. They come from the code that enforces them.\n\n## When Test connection fails\n\nTest connection lists six checks. A check that could not run because an earlier one failed shows “Not checked”. It never shows “Passed”. Fix the first failure, then test again.\n\n| Check | What failed | What to do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Build location | The project is set to build on your container. | Choose GitHub Actions under Where Builds Run and press Save. |\n| Build credentials | Egg has no complete set, or they expired. | Make new credentials, then replace all four secrets on GitHub. |\n| GitHub app | The project is not linked to the app, or the app was uninstalled. | Install the app from Account, then add the project again. |\n| Repository access | The app cannot see the repository. | On GitHub, open the app's settings and add the repository to its access. |\n| Workflow file | There is no file at .github/workflows/egg.yml on the production branch. | Merge the pull request, or commit the copied file to the production branch. |\n| Workflow settings | The file does not match this project's address, production branch, secrets or environment. | Copy the workflow from the setup step over the file. Do this again after changing the runtime, commands or production branch. |\n\nTest connection cannot see your GitHub secrets or environment settings. It does not know whether the last workflow run passed. If the first build fails, open the workflow run on GitHub. A run that cannot send the release usually means a secret is missing. It may be saved in the wrong place. It may be out of date.\n\n**Related resources:** [Build on Nest](/docs/build-on-nest), [GitHub and previews](/docs/github), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 10 of 40\n\n# Build on Nest\n\nEgg builds the app on your Nest container, under a memory and CPU cap, and starts it when the build finishes.\n\n## What it is\n\nNothing depends on GitHub for the build itself. Egg sends the source to your container over SSH. It runs the install and build commands there, then starts the result. It suits small apps and projects that are not on GitHub through the app.\n\n## The memory and CPU cap\n\n- A build runs under a memory cap and a CPU limit, so it cannot take the container from your running app. Both are set on the Container page, under Build memory and Build CPU.\n- The memory cap is never more than your container's memory minus 512 MB. The 512 MB stays free for your app. A container with under 768 MB cannot build.\n- A build that runs longer than 15 minutes is stopped. Your running app is not.\n\n## When to prefer it\n\n- Prefer it for a small app whose build fits comfortably in the cap.\n- Prefer it when you want builds that do not use GitHub Actions minutes.\n- Prefer GitHub Actions for a framework build that runs out of memory here. See [Build with GitHub Actions](/docs/build-github-actions).\n\nSwitching is a saved choice under Where Builds Run in the project's Settings. Egg never changes it for you, so the next deployment uses the new place. The numbers and the messages a build can give are on the [Container page](/docs/container#container-build-memory) and in [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting#troubleshooting-build).\n\n**Related resources:** [Build with GitHub Actions](/docs/build-github-actions), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 11 of 40\n\n# Deployments\n\nEvery build is a deployment, with its own log, commit and port. Production and preview deployments are kept apart.\n\nA deployment is one attempt to build a commit and put it on your container. It starts Queued, ends as Ready, Error or Canceled, and keeps its log, so you can see where it came from. Its source is `manual` (the Deploy button or API), `webhook` (a push or pull request), `hook` (a deploy hook), `redeploy` or `rollback`. You need a project and an access token, which you make on the Developer page so a script can call Egg’s API, and the token needs `deployments:write` to start, redeploy, cancel or roll back a deployment.\n\n## States\n\n| State | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| Queued | Waiting. A project runs one deployment at a time, and the worker runs 2 deployments at once across all projects. |\n| Building | Cloning and packing on the Egg machine, then the checks, the upload, and the install and build on your container. |\n| Deploying | On your container: starting the service and waiting for it to answer. A rollback also shows as Deploying. |\n| Ready | Started and answering. |\n| Error | A step failed. The error text, at most 500 characters, is on the deployment page. |\n| Canceled | You canceled it, or its pull request closed. |\n\nThe lists draw Deploying as Building, so the Compare table shows all six words. In the status filter, Building also lists Queued, and Error also lists Canceled.\n\nIf a production deployment fails after the switch, Egg puts the previous release back and checks it for 20 seconds, so the log says “The previous release is serving traffic again.” or “The previous release did not come back either. Check the logs.” Egg stops and removes a preview that fails to start, so it does not stay behind as a service that keeps restarting.\n\nThe deployment page has a Details card (Created, Duration, Environment, Address, Port and Source, with the branch and commit) and a Build Logs card. A log reads in this order: “Deploying NAME (production) to USER@HOST”, the clone, “Commit abc1234: message”, “Building: COMMAND” (or “No build step for this project.”), “Packed release: N MB”, “Connecting to your Nest container”, “Uploading N MB”, the steps on the container, “Waiting for the app to answer on port N” and a line that starts “Healthy:”. Production ends with “Live on port N. Map your domain to this port in the Nest dashboard.” The Build Logs card starts open while a deployment runs or has failed and collapses when it is Ready, so you can read it in place. It has a count of lines and a **Find in logs…** box, but you cannot download build logs.\n\n## Actions\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Deploy\n\n   Open your project and press the button at the top. It reads **Deploy** until a production release is live and **Redeploy** after that, and either way it queues a new build of the production branch tip, not an older commit. The build runs on your container and restarts your app when it goes live, so a new deployment waits as Queued while another is running.\n2. Step 2: Redeploy one commit\n\n   In the Deployments list, open **Actions for deployment** on a row and choose **Redeploy**, so Egg builds the same commit again in the same environment. If it cannot fetch, check out and verify that exact commit, the deployment fails, because Egg never substitutes the branch tip. The menu also has **View logs**.\n3. Step 3: Roll back\n\n   Press **Instant rollback** on the project page, or choose it from a row’s menu, so `current` points at an earlier Ready production release still on your container and the service restarts. The app is offline while it restarts. Nothing is rebuilt, but Egg still waits up to 45 seconds for the app to answer, so an unhealthy older release puts the live release back and ends the rollback row as Error. The log ends with “Rollback complete. Database changes are not reversed.” The project page button targets the newest Ready production release that is not live, and its tooltip says “Switches back to the previous release. Database changes are not reversed.”\n4. Step 4: Cancel\n\n   Choose **Cancel** on a row that is Queued, Building or Deploying, so a running build or upload stops within about two seconds, because the worker checks for a cancel every two seconds. Egg also checks before each step on your container. Cancel works until Egg starts to switch the release, so after that it is refused with “Activation has started or this deployment is already finished.” and the deployment finishes as Ready, or as Error if the new release does not answer.\n5. Step 5: Restart without building\n\n   On the project page’s Service card, press **Restart** and confirm “Restart the service?”, so Egg restarts the production service without building anything. The app is offline for a few seconds.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Search\n\n   Search with **Search by commit, branch, author or #pr…**, so every word must match the project, branch, commit message, short commit, author, source, environment or pull request (type `#14` or `pr 14`).\n2. Step 2: Sort\n\n   Sort with **Sort deployments** (Newest first, Oldest first, Longest build, Shortest build), so you change only the order shown.\n3. Step 3: Filter\n\n   Filter with **Filter by status**, **Filter by environment**, **Filter by project** and **Filter by time** (Any time, Last 24 hours, Last 7 days, Last 30 days), so Egg answers from the server, and press **Clear filters** to reset them.\n4. Step 4: Act on rows\n\n   Tick rows for **Compare** (exactly two), **Cancel** (unfinished previews) or **Delete previews** (previews not live and at least 24 hours old), so the action applies only to what you ticked. Deleting asks “Delete N previews?” and says Egg stops each preview and removes its files from your container, but never deletes production releases or the live release. The record stays in the list.\n\nThe all-projects Deployments page loads the newest 100 deployments and shows 25 at a time with a **Show N more** button. A single project’s Deployments tab loads only the newest 25, so a long history does not have to load at once, and a **Load older** button (“Loading older…”) fetches the next 25. Search, status, environment and time run on the server, not on the rows already loaded, so a match is found however far back it is. Search waits until you stop typing, and sorting only reorders the rows loaded so far, as the page says under the list. If a page cannot load, the rows already shown stay and the button tries again.\n\nTo keep a record outside the dashboard, open a project’s Deployments tab and press **Export CSV**. The file holds that project’s newest 5,000 deployments with the time (UTC), commit, branch, environment, state, duration in seconds and deployment id, but never variables, logs, ports or addresses. You can make 10 exports a minute, so after that Egg answers “Too many exports. Wait N seconds and try again.”\n\n## Releases and previews on disk\n\nEach release lives in its own folder under `~/.egg/NAME/releases/` on your container, so up to six copies of the code and its installed dependencies use your disk. After every successful deploy, production keeps the five newest Ready releases plus the live one, and a rollback can leave six. Previews, across all branches, keep the two newest Ready ones, so Egg stops and deletes older ones. You cannot roll back to a release that was removed.\n\nA build that fails, runs out of memory, times out or is canceled removes its own release folder, so the running release is not touched. A deployment that fails or is canceled after its build finished leaves its folder until the next successful deploy removes it, while a preview that fails to start is removed at once. If cleanup fails over SSH, the records are still marked cleaned up, so a leftover folder can stay on your container while the page says it is gone, and the Container page lists such leftovers.\n\n## Branch previews\n\nAny branch other than production builds as a preview, with its own service on the lowest free port from 20000 to 29999 on that server, so production keeps running. Nest gives previews no address on its own, and the log says “Preview is running on port N. Map a domain to it in the Nest dashboard to open it.” You build a preview by pushing a branch, opening a pull request, calling a deploy hook with a branch or using the API. Previews use the variables set for All or Preview, and the project page lists them under Active branches, one row per branch.\n\nA failed deploy leaves production alone\n\nIf the new release never answers on its port, Egg switches the previous release back on, so a failed deploy does not leave you on a broken version. If your app crashes at start, the check stops at once with “Service crashed on start” instead of waiting the full 45 seconds.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA release is a folder and going live is moving one link, so rolling back moves it the other way and takes seconds. For the same reason, a rollback cannot restore a cleaned-up release or reverse changes your app made to its own database, because only the code folder changes. A project runs one deployment at a time, so two builds cannot race to switch the same link.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Releases kept in production | 5 Ready, plus the live one | Older ones are deleted and cannot be rolled back to. In the code: server/deploy/layout.ts KEEP_RELEASES |\n| Previews kept | 2 newest Ready | Older previews are stopped and removed. In the code: server/deploy/layout.ts KEEP_PREVIEWS |\n| Preview ports | 20000 to 29999 per server | “No free preview ports on this server.” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Deployments at once, all projects | 2 by default | The rest wait as Queued. A self-hosted worker can change it with WORKER_CONCURRENCY. In the code: instrumentation.ts |\n| Deployments at once, one project | 1 | The others wait as Queued. In the code: server/jobs.ts |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | “The build timed out.” In the code: server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Fetching one commit for a redeploy | 3 minutes | The deployment fails if the requested SHA cannot be fetched, checked out, and verified. The branch tip is never substituted. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts |\n| Build time | 15 minutes, for install and build together. Fixed. | “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS |\n| Build cap | Build memory (MB) of the server, 512 MB by default, at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when the container has room, and never more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB; Build CPU limit (cores), 2 by default; no swap. The build does not start if the cap would be under 256 MB. | “The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB.” A build that waits for another build on the same container waits up to 30 minutes. In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts containerMemoryCapMb lib/build-memory.ts |\n| Free disk before a build | 2048 MB | “Free up disk space on your container: N MB is free and a build needs at least 2048 MB.” Nothing is uploaded. In the code: server/deploy/container-probe.ts MIN_FREE_DISK_MB |\n| Build memory and CPU setting | Build memory (MB), set per server on the Container page: 512 MB up to the memory the server reports, or 8192 MB when it reports none. Build CPU limit (cores): 0.5 up to the cores the server reports, in 0.25 steps, 2 by default. | A value outside the range is refused. In the code: lib/build-memory.ts DEFAULT_BUILD_CPUS |\n| Source archive | 500 MiB compressed, 2 GiB unpacked, and 200,000 included paths | Packaging stops when any limit is exceeded. The compressed archive is removed. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts MAX_ARCHIVE_BYTES MAX_SOURCE_UNPACKED_BYTES MAX_SOURCE_ENTRIES |\n| One step on your container | 2 minutes; 15 minutes for unpacking and installing | “Command timed out after 120s” (or 900s). In the code: server/deploy/pipeline.ts |\n| Wait for the app to answer | 45 seconds, then 20 seconds for the old release | The release is marked Error and the previous one is restored. In the code: server/deploy/pipeline.ts |\n| Worker lease on a running job | Renewed every 15 seconds. Counted as expired after 60 seconds without renewal | The job keeps blocking its project until the person running Egg recovers it by hand. Egg does not fail it by itself. In the code: server/jobs.ts LEASE_RENEW_MS STALE_AFTER_MS |\n| Log lines per deployment | 20,000, each cut at 4000 characters | “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts |\n| Build log lines loaded on a page | 1000 | A finished deployment shows its first 1000 lines. A running one streams new lines as they arrive. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Live build stream | About 3.75 hours | The stream stops; reload the page. In the code: app/api/deployments/[id]/stream/route.ts |\n| Bulk actions | 50 deployments at a time | Each one runs on its own; one failure does not stop the others. In the code: server/deployments-bulk.ts MAX_BULK |\n| Delete previews | Not live, not cleaned up, not running, 24 hours old or more | “Only old previews that are not live or removed can be deleted in bulk.” In the code: lib/deployments-filter.ts |\n| Deployments page, all projects | Newest 100, shown 25 at a time | Older ones do not appear on the page. Open the project’s Deployments tab for them. In the code: server/queries.ts components/deployments-view.tsx |\n| A project’s Deployments tab | 25 at a time, up to 100 for an API request | Load older brings in the next page. Filters and search run on the server. In the code: server/deployment-page.ts DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE MAX_PAGE_SIZE |\n| CSV export | Newest 5,000 deployments, 10 exports a minute | “Too many exports. Wait N seconds and try again.” In the code: server/export-csv-data.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“Could not clone the repository. Check the branch name and that Egg has access to it.”`\n\n  Check the production branch name, and that the GitHub app is installed on the repository.\n- `“The root directory “DIR” does not exist in this repository.” or “DIR” is outside the repository.”`\n\n  Fix the Root directory in Settings. It must be a folder inside the repository.\n- `“The build failed (exit CODE).”`\n\n  Read the Build Logs card from the bottom. A missing lockfile, a wrong root directory or a missing variable is the usual cause.\n- `“The build needed more memory than the cap of N MB. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  A build went over its cap and was stopped (exit 137). Your app was not. The second sentence is added only when a release was already serving. Raise **Build memory (MB)** on the Container page and deploy again, or make the build lighter. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- `“The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  A build ran past 15 minutes. Cut work from the build. This limit is fixed. “Your app is still running.” is added only when a release was already serving.\n- `“The build timed out.”`\n\n  The clone ran past 5 minutes. The limit is fixed. Check that the repository is not very large, then deploy again.\n- A one-line fix that starts with “Install”, “Free up”, “Egg could not check your container”, “Builds on your container run under” or “Your container cannot apply”\n\n  Egg looked at your container before it uploaded anything and found something missing. Nothing was changed and your running app was not touched. Do what the line says, then deploy again. Every line is listed on the [troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) page.\n- `“Another build has been running on this container for more than 30 minutes, so this one did not start. Your app is still running.”`\n\n  Only one container build runs at a time on a container. Wait for the other build, or cancel it, then deploy again.\n- `“The output folder “DIR” was not created by the build.”`\n\n  A Static site project builds into a folder that does not exist. Check the Output directory and the build command.\n- `“The build output is N MB, over the 524.288 MB limit.”`\n\n  Leave large files out of the build, or ship them another way. Files in `shared/` are not part of a release.\n- `“Nothing was changed on your container.” followed by details`\n\n  The container check found something that is not Egg’s. See Projects for each case.\n- `“PATH is a link, so Egg will not write into it.”, “PATH exists and is not a folder.”, “PATH already exists and was not created by Egg, so it was left alone.” or “UNIT already exists and was not created by Egg, so it was left alone.”`\n\n  Something of yours is in the way. Egg never overwrites it. Rename the project or remove the item yourself.\n- `“Preparing folders failed (exit N).” and similar for Checking the service name, Unpacking the release, Registering the service, Switching the release and Starting the preview`\n\n  That step failed on your container. The log lines above it say why. “Upload is corrupted: checksum mismatch” means the file changed in transit; deploy again.\n- `“The new release did not become healthy on port N. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.”`\n\n  Fix the port or address, or the health path, and deploy again. The last 30 log lines of the service are printed in the build log.\n- A deployment stays Building or Deploying and does not move\n\n  The worker stopped, or it can no longer renew its lease on the job. Egg leaves the job blocking its project until the person running Egg recovers it by hand, because a command on your container may still be running. After that the deployment shows Error with “The worker stopped during this job.” and you can deploy again.\n- `“No free preview ports on this server.”`\n\n  All 10,000 preview ports are in use. Delete old previews.\n- `“That release was removed from your server to save space.”`\n\n  It is outside the five kept. Redeploy that commit instead.\n- `“That deployment is already live.”, “Only production deployments can be rolled back to.” or “That deployment never finished, so there is nothing to roll back to.”`\n\n  Pick a different Ready production deployment.\n- `“That release is no longer on your server (old releases are removed to save disk space).” or “The older release did not become healthy, so nothing was changed.”`\n\n  The rollback row ended as Error and the live release was left or restored. Redeploy the commit.\n- `“There is no earlier release on your server to switch back to.” (the rollback button’s tooltip)`\n\n  There is nothing older to go back to. Redeploy a commit instead.\n- `“Activation has started or this deployment is already finished.”`\n\n  It is too late to cancel: the release is being switched or the deployment ended. Check its state.\n- `“Only previews that are queued or running can be canceled in bulk.” or “Could not remove the preview from your container.”`\n\n  The bulk action skipped that row. Check its state, then try again; the others still run.\n- `“Could not start the deployment”`\n\n  The browser could not queue the build. Press Deploy again, and check your connection.\n\n### Example: Recover from a bad release\n\n#### Given\n\nSam deploys a change to `recipe-bot` that crashes when it reads a missing variable. The previous release is still on the container.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Ship a bad release\n\n   The new deployment ends as Error with “The new release did not become healthy on port 3000…”. The log prints the service’s last 30 lines and “The previous release is serving traffic again.”\n2. Step 2: Fix and push\n\n   Sam fixes the code and pushes. The next deployment reaches Ready.\n3. Step 3: Roll back a bad one\n\n   A week later Sam ships a change that works but behaves badly, and presses Instant rollback.\n\n#### Result\n\nA new deployment appears with the source “rollback” and the state Ready. The log ends with “Rollback complete. Database changes are not reversed.” Any change the bot made to its own database stays as it was.\n\n**Related:** [GitHub and previews](/docs/github), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Logs](/docs/logs), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 12 of 40\n\n# Environment variables\n\nSet once per project. Values are encrypted, masked in logs, and written to your container when you deploy.\n\nEnvironment variables give your app its secrets and settings, such as a Discord token or database address, without putting them in the repository. Each has a name, a value and a target that says which deployments get it. You need a project, so open its Settings and find the Environment Variables card. You can also fill in names found in your repository while you create the project.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Add a variable\n\n   Type a **Name** and a **Value**, choose where it applies in **Applies to** (All, Production or Preview) and press **Save**, so the list below shows each name with its target in alphabetical order. The button stays off until there is a name.\n2. Step 2: Import names from the repository\n\n   Press **Import names from the repository**, so the button says “Reading the repository…” while Egg reads `.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template` and `.env.local.example` on the production branch. Every name not yet present is added for All with an empty value, and the page says “Added N names with empty values. Use the pencil on each to set its value.” Egg never touches names you already have.\n3. Step 3: Change a value\n\n   Press the pencil (“Change the value of NAME”), type a “New value” and press **Update**. Saving the same name and target again also replaces the value. You cannot read the old one back. Saving a value never changes the variable’s secret flag.\n4. Step 4: Delete a variable\n\n   Press the trash button (“Delete NAME”). The dialog “Delete NAME?” says “Deployments after this one will not have it.” The variable stays in the current release until you deploy again.\n5. Step 5: Apply the change\n\n   A change reaches your container on the next deployment, because Egg writes the values into the new release. To apply it now, press **Redeploy** on the project page, which builds again on the Egg machine and restarts your app. A restart alone does not pick up a change.\n\n| Applies to | Production deployments | Preview deployments |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| All | Yes | Yes |\n| Production | Yes | No |\n| Preview | No | Yes |\n\nWhen you set the same name for `All` and for one environment, the matching Production or Preview value wins, so `All` only supplies a value when no environment-specific value matches.\n\n- The list shows names and targets only, and the browser never shows a value. Each variable has a secret flag, on by default, and the flag is changed through the API. A token with `env:read` gets a value back from `get_env_vars` only when the variable is marked non-secret. Egg stores them encrypted with AES-256-GCM, tied to the project, the name and the target.\n- At deploy time Egg passes your values to the build on your container as ordinary environment variables, so any install or build script, including those of your dependencies, can read them. For the build they are written to a file with permissions 600 that Egg deletes when the build ends. They are also written to a file in the project’s `env/` folder on your container, named after the release, with permissions 600 and never inside the release folder, because a static site serves that folder. They are written as `NAME=\"value\"` lines for systemd. Line breaks in a value become `\\n`. An empty value is left out of the file, so the app sees the variable as unset rather than empty.\n- Build and deploy logs, and the error text of a failed deployment, hide each value as `***`. The base64 form of a value and its percent-encoded form are hidden too. In a multi-line value such as a key file, each line of 8 or more characters is hidden on its own. A value of 2 or 3 characters is hidden only where it stands alone, not inside a longer word or number, and a one-character value is never hidden. Another changed form, such as hex or a hash, is not recognised.\n- Saving a name and target that already exist replaces the value. The same name for All and for one target counts as two variables.\n\nVariables files are not shown in the dashboard or the API\n\nThe Files page and the API refuse to open anything in `env/` or `current.env`, and the `.env` files that releases made before the move still sit in `current/` and `releases/ID/`. The Files page says “This file holds the project’s environment variable values, so it is not shown here. Change the values in the project’s settings.” and the API answers 403. A terminal on your container can read the file, so the Terminal switch is off by default.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nSecret values are write-only, so no page, token or log has to be trusted with them. The cost is that you cannot check a secret value after saving it; you replace it. A value marked non-secret can be read back through `get_env_vars`. Egg writes variables into each release instead of reading them live, so a rollback runs with the variables that release shipped with and a half-edited set never reaches a running app.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Variables per project | 100, counted per name and target | “A project can have up to 100 variables.” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Name | Letters, digits and underscores, not starting with a digit; 100 characters | “key: Names use letters, digits and underscores, and do not start with a digit” In the code: server/deploy/runtime.ts ENV_KEY |\n| Value | 10,000 characters | “value: Too big: expected string to have <=10000 characters” In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Reserved names | PORT, HOST, PATH, HOME, EGG, in any letter case | “key: That name is set by Egg” In the code: server/deploy/runtime.ts RESERVED_ENV |\n| Names found in a repository | 100, from files up to 200,000 bytes | The rest are not listed. In the code: server/repo-detect.ts |\n| Masking | Values of 2 or more characters, and their base64 and percent-encoded forms. 2 or 3 characters only where they stand alone | A one-character value shows in the log as typed. In the code: server/deploy/redact.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“key: Names use letters, digits and underscores, and do not start with a digit”`\n\n  Rename it, for example `DISCORD_TOKEN` and not `1TOKEN` or `discord-token`.\n- `“key: That name is set by Egg”`\n\n  Egg sets `PORT` and `HOST` itself, and `PATH`, `HOME` and `EGG` are kept. Choose a different name, or change the project’s port in Settings.\n- `“A project can have up to 100 variables.”`\n\n  Delete variables you no longer use, or combine related values into one.\n- `“No example env file (such as .env.example) was found in the repository.”`\n\n  Add the names by hand, or commit an example file with names and no values.\n- `“Every name in the repository is already here.”`\n\n  Nothing to add. Set the values with the pencil.\n- `“N added, then it stopped: reason”`\n\n  Some names were added before an error. Read the reason and import again.\n- `“Could not look at the repository.” or “Could not read this repository. If it is private, install the GitHub App so it can be read.”`\n\n  Install the GitHub app on the repository, or add the names by hand.\n- `“A .env file is committed to this repository. Remove it from git and rotate the values.”`\n\n  A warning from the import. Egg never read that file. Anyone with access to the repository can, so change the values.\n- A change did not take effect\n\n  It needs a deployment. Press Redeploy, then check the variable is set for the right environment.\n- A variable is set for All and for one environment\n\n  Keep one row per name where you can. Egg loads both rows, and these docs do not promise which one wins.\n\n## Example: Give recipe-bot its Discord token\n\n### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` reads its Discord token from `DISCORD_TOKEN`. The repository has a `.env.example` with that name and no value. Sam wants a different token for previews.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Import the names\n\n   Sam presses Import names from the repository. `DISCORD_TOKEN` appears for All with no value.\n2. Step 2: Set the value\n\n   Sam presses the pencil beside `DISCORD_TOKEN`, pastes the token as the New value and presses Update.\n3. Step 3: Add a preview token\n\n   Sam types `DISCORD_TOKEN` again, sets Applies to Preview and presses Save. The list now has two rows.\n4. Step 4: Apply it\n\n   Sam presses Redeploy.\n\n### Result\n\nProduction gets the token set for All, and previews get the token set for All and Preview. Egg does not promise which of those two wins for a preview, so Sam deletes the All row and adds a Production row instead. The build log prints the token as `***` if the app ever echoes it. The bot logs in on the new release.\n\n**Related:** [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Security](/docs/security).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 13 of 40\n\n# Logs\n\nTwo kinds: what the build printed, and what your running app prints.\n\nEgg stores one build log per deployment on the Egg machine, showing the clone, install, build and release. It does not store runtime logs, so each time you open or refresh the page Egg reads them from your container over SSH to show what your running app prints. Build logs need a deployment, and runtime logs need a project that has been deployed, covering only the production service.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Read a build log\n\n   Open a deployment, so the **Build Logs** card shows each line. While it runs, new lines stream in and the card says “Waiting for the first log line…” until the first arrives. Use **Find in logs…** to search. A Ready deployment’s log starts collapsed, so open the card to read it.\n2. Step 2: Open the runtime logs\n\n   Open Logs in the sidebar and choose a project, then pick a **Time range** (1 hour, 24 hours or 7 days; the default is 7 days) and a **Level** (All lines, or Warnings and errors). The line under the controls says how many lines are shown, for example “120 lines from recipe-bot, last 7 days.”\n3. Step 3: Search\n\n   Type in **Search logs**, whose hint is “Search the last 1000 lines…”. The match is not case-sensitive and the page waits a moment after you stop typing before it asks your container.\n4. Step 4: Load older lines\n\n   The page opens with the newest 300 lines, so when the journal holds more inside the time range, a **Load older lines** button (“Loading older lines…”) sits above them. Each press adds the 300 lines before the ones you see, using the same time range, level and search, and keeps your place in the list, while a screen reader is told how many lines were added. The button goes away when nothing earlier is in range, but stays when a stretch has no match, so you can keep looking. If a read fails, the lines above stay and the button tries again. Changing the range, level or search starts again from the newest lines.\n5. Step 5: Follow it live\n\n   Tick **Refresh every 5s** to follow the log, so a “Live” marker appears and the page reads quietly in the background and adds only new lines at the bottom when older lines are on screen. Each refresh reads your container over SSH, so untick it when you stop watching, or press **Refresh** when you want to.\n6. Step 6: Keep a copy\n\n   Press **Download** to save exactly the lines on screen, older lines included, as a text file such as `recipe-bot-logs-24h-2026-11-05.txt`, named for the project, the range (`1h`, `24h` or `7d`) and the UTC date. The button is off when there are no lines.\n\n- Runtime logs are your production service’s journal on your container (`journalctl -u egg-NAME.service`), and the page shows up to 300 lines at a time, so Load older lines repeats the read for earlier lines. A search or the Warnings and errors level reads up to 1000 lines first, filters them and keeps the newest 300 that match. A preview’s output is not on this page.\n- Egg decides a line’s level from its text, in this order. A level the program printed itself wins: `[ERROR]`, `level=warn`, a JSON `level` or `severity` field, or a pino number, so `[INFO] failed login` stays Info. Next, a systemd marker such as `<3>`. Then counts of zero are ignored, so “0 errors” and “no warnings” do not count. After that the words error, fatal, critical, panic, exception, failed, failure and traceback, `npm ERR!`, Node codes such as `ERR_X`, class names like `TypeError:`, Python `File \"x\", line N` lines and stack frames are errors. The words warn, warning and deprecated are warnings. Everything else is Info. A stack trace takes the level of the error above it.\n- In the Warnings and errors view each kept line is marked Error or Warning.\n- Build logs hide secret values as `***`, drop colour codes, cut a line at 4000 characters, and stop at 20,000 lines per deployment with the line “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” Build logs have no download button. They stay on the deployment page.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg does not copy runtime output, so it stays in your container’s journal and reading it needs a live connection, with history set by systemd, not Egg. Build output is different: Egg collects it while the build runs and stores it with the deployment, so it is still there when your container is off.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Runtime log lines shown | 300 | The newest 300 after any filter. In the code: components/logs-view.tsx |\n| Lines read for a search or level filter | 1000 for each read, 300 kept | Press Load older lines to search further back, inside the time range. In the code: server/runtime-info.ts |\n| Lines added by Load older lines | 300 at a time | The button goes away when the journal has nothing before the first line. In the code: components/logs-view.tsx lib/log-page.ts |\n| Lines the API can be asked for | 1 to 1000 | Other numbers are clamped. In the code: app/api/projects/[id]/runtime-logs/route.ts |\n| Search text | 200 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/runtime-info.ts |\n| Reading the journal | 30 seconds | “Could not read the logs from your container.” or a timeout message. In the code: server/runtime-info.ts |\n| Automatic refresh | Every 5 seconds | Untick the box to stop. In the code: components/logs-view.tsx |\n| Build log lines per deployment | 20,000 | “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts |\n| Length of one build log line | 4000 characters | The rest of the line is cut off. In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts |\n| Build log lines loaded on a page | 1000 | A finished log shows its first 1000 lines. In the code: server/projects.ts |\n| Live build stream | About 3.75 hours | The stream stops; reload the page. In the code: app/api/deployments/[id]/stream/route.ts |\n| Journal history | Whatever systemd keeps on your container | Egg does not set it. In the code: Not in Egg |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“Could not read the logs from your container.”`\n\n  The container answered with an error. Press Refresh; if it keeps failing, test the connection on the Connect page.\n- `“Timed out reading the logs. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.”`\n\n  Wait a minute, then press Refresh.\n- `“Nest rejected the server’s key, so the logs could not be read.”`\n\n  The key is not accepted. Paste the current public key in the Nest dashboard and test the connection.\n- `“The server’s host key changed, so the logs were not read. Reconnect the server if you expected this.”`\n\n  See Servers for what a changed host key means and how to reconnect.\n- `“We can’t reach your container right now”`\n\n  Use the retry button, and open the details line for the exact message. Your apps may still be running.\n- `“Nothing printed in this time range. Try a longer one.”`\n\n  The app printed nothing in that time. Pick 24 hours or 7 days.\n- `“No warnings or errors in this time range.”`\n\n  Switch Level to All lines to see everything.\n- `“No lines match “TEXT”.”`\n\n  Each read looks through 1000 lines. If the page offers it, press Load older lines to search further back, or pick a shorter word or a shorter time range.\n- `“Nothing to read yet”`\n\n  You have no projects. Deploy one and what it prints will show up here.\n- A secret appears in a build log\n\n  Masking covers a value as stored, and its base64 and percent-encoded forms. It does not cover a value of one character, or one changed in another way, such as a hash. Do not print secrets from your own code.\n\n## Example: Find why the bot went quiet\n\n### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` goes quiet on Discord. The Service card shows Running.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open the logs\n\n   Sam opens Logs and chooses `recipe-bot`.\n2. Step 2: Narrow them\n\n   Sam sets Level to Warnings and errors and types `rate` in Search logs.\n3. Step 3: Keep the evidence\n\n   Sam presses Download.\n\n### Result\n\nThe page lists the matching lines marked Error or Warning, newest last among the 300 kept. Sam sees Discord’s rate limit message and attaches the downloaded file to a support question.\n\n**Related:** [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 14 of 40\n\n# Domains\n\nYour Nest name is free. A custom domain needs a DNS record first. Egg shows a domain as active only after its DNS check passes.\n\nA domain gives your app a name people can type, so three places have to agree: your DNS provider sends the name to Nest, the Nest dashboard maps it to your app’s port, and Egg records both so it can check them. Egg does only the last part, by looking, so it changes nothing in DNS, in Nest or on your container, and its checks run from the Egg machine. You need a connected server and either a domain whose DNS you can edit or your own Nest name, and the form adds the domain to the server you connected first.\n\n- **Your Nest name is free.** `your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, and any name under it such as `app.your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, needs no DNS record and is active as soon as you add it. Other names under `hackclub.app` are refused: “Only your Nest name, NAME.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.”\n- **A custom domain needs a DNS record.** Add a CNAME to `your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, or a TXT record with the value `domain-verification=your-nest-username`, so the domain waits as “Waiting for DNS” until Egg finds the record. This is the same proof Nest asks for when you add the domain on its dashboard.\n- **A subdomain of a domain you already verified** on the same server, such as `api.example.com` when `example.com` is active, needs no new record.\n- **Reserved names are blocked.** Names such as `admin`, `api`, `dashboard`, `status`, `www`, `nest`, `hackclub` and `egg` cannot be used as the name under `hackclub.app`, `hackclub.com` or `hackclub.dev`, or on their own. The message is “That name is reserved. Choose another domain.”\n- **Only active after the DNS check.** Egg shows a domain as active only when it is your Nest name, a subdomain of a verified domain, or its DNS record was found. IP addresses, `localhost` and internal names such as `.local` are refused.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Add the domain\n\n   Open Domains. In **Add a domain**, type the **Domain** (for example `app.example.com`), optionally pick a **Project (optional)** to fill its port, check the **Target port** and press **Add domain**, so a panel headed “Next: finish setup for DOMAIN” lists what to do next.\n2. Step 2: Point DNS at Nest\n\n   At your DNS provider, add one of the two records shown: a CNAME with the “CNAME value”, which is `your-nest-username.hackclub.app`, or a TXT record with the “TXT value”, which is `domain-verification=your-nest-username`. Use the TXT record for a bare domain such as `example.com`, where a CNAME often cannot sit beside the other records. Your Nest name and subdomains of a verified domain skip this step.\n3. Step 3: Map it in the Nest dashboard\n\n   In the Domains page of your Nest dashboard, add the same domain with the same port, so Egg can check it. Nest looks for the same record when you add it, so do step 2 first, because Egg cannot do this for you.\n4. Step 4: Check\n\n   Press **Check**, so the button reads “Checking…” and the status moves from “Waiting for DNS” to “Active” when the record is found, or stays on “Waiting for DNS” with a reason when it is not. DNS changes can take a few minutes, so check again after a short wait.\n\n- **DNS check.** Passes when the domain has a TXT record `domain-verification=username`, or a CNAME to `username.hackclub.app`. For a name under `username.hackclub.app`, or that exact name, an A record is enough. A domain that has only an A record, and is not under your Nest name, gets “No CNAME found yet”. A subdomain of a domain you already verified passes without its own record.\n- **App check.** Only once DNS passes, so Egg sends a GET over HTTPS to `/` and, if your project has a different health path, to that path too. It waits 5 seconds for each, checks the certificate, follows no redirects and reads no body. It shows “Certificate accepted” or “Certificate not verified yet”, then “Answers with HTTP CODE” for any status below 500, or “Reachable but returning CODE” for 500 and above. A 404 therefore still counts as answering. The health path is added in brackets after the message. It only checks names that resolve to public addresses, and never IP addresses.\n- **Edit** (the pencil, “Edit DOMAIN”) changes the project and port. Changing the port resets the status to “Not checked” (“Waiting for DNS” for a custom domain) and says “Saved. Also change the target port to N on the Domains page of your Nest dashboard, then press Check.” Changing a project’s port in its Settings does the same to every domain linked to that project.\n- **Remove** (“Remove DOMAIN”) opens “Remove DOMAIN?” and says “This removes the record from Egg.” It deletes the record in Egg only. Nest and your DNS provider are not changed.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| Waiting for DNS | A custom domain whose record has not been found yet. The record to add sits under the row. |\n| Not checked | A Nest name or a subdomain of a verified domain whose port changed and has not been checked since. |\n| Active | Your Nest name, a subdomain of a verified domain, or a domain whose DNS record was found. The app check may still show a problem. |\n| Needs attention | A domain that was active and no longer passes the check. The row says “DNS no longer points here. Check your record.” Egg never removes the domain. |\n\nEgg rechecks active custom domains whenever you open the Domains page, at most once a minute for each, so if the record has gone the row shows “DNS no longer points here. Check your record.” A failed lookup shows nothing, so fix the record and press **Check**.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg checks DNS and the app instead of changing anything, so the mapping stays in the Nest dashboard, which has no API, and a record in Egg is only a note of your intent. The rules follow Nest’s own, so your Nest name is free, a custom domain proves itself with the same TXT or CNAME record, and Nest’s reserved names are blocked here too. Egg shows a domain as active only after that proof, so a name that is not yours never looks live. The two checks are separate, so DNS proves the name reaches Nest and the app check proves your app answers behind it, letting you tell which side to fix.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Domain | A hostname up to 253 characters, each part up to 63, ending in letters; no IP address or URL | “Enter a domain name such as app.example.com (not an IP address or a URL).” In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| Target port | 1 to 65535 | “Enter a port between 1 and 65535.” In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| Same domain twice | Once across all users. Egg does not say whose it is | “DOMAIN is already added.” In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| Reserved names | 119 names from Nest (admin, api, dashboard, docs, help, status, www and more) plus egg, nest, hackclub, hack-club, nest-deploy, egg-deploy and loadout | “That name is reserved. Choose another domain.” In the code: server/domain-policy.ts |\n| Names under hackclub.app | Only your own Nest name and names under it | “Only your Nest name, NAME.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.” In the code: server/domain-policy.ts |\n| Public domains only | No IP address, localhost, .local, .internal or .home.arpa name | “Use a public domain name such as app.example.com. IP addresses, localhost and internal names cannot be used.” In the code: server/domain-policy.ts |\n| How long a re-check is remembered | 60 seconds for each domain | Opening the page again within a minute shows the same answer. In the code: server/domains.ts |\n| App check wait | 5 seconds for each path | “No answer within 5 seconds.” In the code: server/app-check.ts |\n| Servers the form can use | Your first server, the one you connected first | A second server’s domains cannot be added from this form. In the code: components/domains-view.tsx |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“The CNAME points to X, but it should point to TARGET.” or “The CNAME points to X. It should point to TARGET.”`\n\n  Change the CNAME at your DNS provider to the value shown. The first form means X is another hackclub.app name.\n- `“No CNAME found yet. Create one pointing to TARGET, or add a TXT record with the value domain-verification=NAME. DNS changes can take a few minutes.”`\n\n  Add one of the two records, wait a few minutes, press Check.\n- `“That name is reserved. Choose another domain.”`\n\n  The name is on the reserved list. Pick another name, or use a domain of your own.\n- `“Only your Nest name, NAME.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.”`\n\n  Under `hackclub.app` you can use only your own Nest name and names beneath it. Use a domain of your own for anything else.\n- `“DNS no longer points here. Check your record.”`\n\n  An active domain no longer passes the check. Restore the CNAME or TXT record, then press Check. Egg keeps the domain.\n- `“The name does not resolve yet. Add it in the Nest dashboard first.”`\n\n  For a `.hackclub.app` name, add it on the Nest dashboard’s Domains page.\n- `“DNS could not be checked right now. Try again in a minute.”`\n\n  A lookup failed. Press Check again.\n- `“DNS does not point at your server yet. Press Check to see what it points at.”`\n\n  The default text under a row that needs attention. Press Check for the specific reason.\n- `“The app check runs once DNS points at Nest.”`\n\n  Fix DNS first. The app check only starts after the status is Active.\n- `“The certificate has expired.”, “The certificate is for a different name.” or “The certificate is not trusted.”`\n\n  The domain reaches something else, or the certificate on it is wrong. Check the mapping in the Nest dashboard.\n- `“The connection was refused on port 443.” or “The secure connection could not be set up.”`\n\n  Nothing is listening for the domain yet. Check the Nest dashboard mapping and the target port.\n- `“No answer within 5 seconds.” or “Could not connect.”`\n\n  Your app did not answer in time, or the app is not running. Check the Service card and the logs.\n- `“The name does not resolve to an address yet.”, “The name resolves to a private or internal address, so it was not checked.” or “Only domain names can be checked, not IP addresses.”`\n\n  The app check only runs against public domain names. Fix the DNS record.\n- `“Reachable but returning 503”`\n\n  The app answered with a server error. Read the logs.\n- `“DOMAIN is already added.”`\n\n  The name is already recorded, by you or by another account. A domain can be added only once across all users.\n- `“Project not found”`\n\n  The project is on a different server than the domain’s. Pick a project from the first server, or none.\n\n## Example: Give the bot’s page a domain\n\n### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` now has a small status web page on port `3000`. Sam owns `recipes.example.com` and the Nest username is `sam-r`.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Add the domain\n\n   Sam adds `recipes.example.com` with project `recipe-bot`, which fills port `3000`. The row reads “Waiting for DNS” and shows the records.\n2. Step 2: Set DNS and Nest\n\n   Sam creates a CNAME for `recipes.example.com` pointing to `sam-r.hackclub.app`, then adds the same domain and port in the Nest dashboard.\n3. Step 3: Check\n\n   Sam presses Check, and finds “No CNAME found yet”. Ten minutes later Sam presses Check again.\n\n### Result\n\nThe status reads “Active” and the app check shows “Certificate accepted” and “Answers with HTTP 200”.\n\n**Related:** [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nUse it\n\nPage 15 of 40\n\n# Container, terminal and files\n\nThree switches decide what Egg may do inside your container beyond deploying. All three start off.\n\nYou need a connected server, which is one container in Egg’s list. The switches live in Account, under Container permissions, one card per server named like `sam-r.hackclub.app`. Egg checks the switch on every request, before it opens an SSH session to your container, so turning one off stops the very next call. Turning a switch on or off never restarts an app. Only the Terminal switch closes anything: it closes your open terminals on that server.\n\n## The three switches\n\n| Switch | Default | Fresh sign-in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Terminal** | Off | Needed to turn on |\n| **File changes** | Off | Needed to turn on |\n| **Removing leftovers** | Off | Not needed |\n\n### Turn a switch on\n\n1. Step 1: Open Account\n\n   Find Container permissions and the card for your server.\n\n   In the dashboard: Accountthen Container permissions\n2. Step 2: Flip a switch\n\n   It saves at once and a message names the switch and the server, for example “Terminal turned on”.\n3. Step 3: Confirm if asked\n\n   Terminal and File changes send you to Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied. Turning any switch off never asks. Turning Terminal off closes your open terminals on that server, and says so.\n\nReading is separate from changing. You can browse, read and download files, and read the Container page, with every switch off.\n\nCheck your older servers\n\nAn update once set File changes to off on every server that already existed. Do not assume an older server kept its old setting: open Account and look at each card. A server connected before the switches started off may also still have Removing leftovers on. Turn off what you do not use.\n\n| Switch | What it can destroy |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Terminal** | Anything the container user can touch, including apps and files Egg did not create. It is a root shell, and anyone signed in to your account can use it while it is on. |\n| **File changes** | Anything inside `shared/` for every project on that server, including data your app keeps there. A folder delete is recursive. A change can alter the code your app runs at its next restart, so treat it like terminal access. |\n| **Removing leftovers** | Egg services (`egg-*.service`) that no project uses, and whole folders under `~/.egg` that no project uses, including their `shared/` data. |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nAll three switches start off because each one reaches past “deploy my app”. The server enforces them, not just the page: a request to a feature that is off is refused with a 403 before any SSH connection is opened, and an access token cannot turn a switch on. A switch that is off is the only part of this you do not have to trust Egg about.\n\n## The Container page\n\nContainer in the sidebar opens one SSH connection to your container and runs read-only commands, so it changes nothing there. It shows what is running and what Egg left behind. Pick a server if you have more than one, and press **Refresh** to read it again. A read that takes longer than 60 seconds fails.\n\n- **Disk used** shows the percentage and how much of it is Egg folders.\n- **Projects** shows each project’s service (Running, Starting, Crashed, Stopped, or No service yet), its port, folder size and previews. If another program holds a project’s port you see “Port <n> is held by <x>, not by <project>” with a “Change the port” link. The next deploy of that project fails its health check until the clash is sorted.\n- **Orphans** lists leftovers, described next.\n- **Other ports** lists programs listening that Egg did not start, with “This container only” or “Open to the network”. It is read only. Without `ss` or `netstat` on the container it says so.\n\n### Build memory\n\nBuild memory is the cap on a build. The setting belongs to the server, so every project on that server builds with the same amount. A new server starts at 512 MB. A server that still had the old 1024 MB default saved was lowered to 512 MB; a value you saved yourself was kept. The Build memory card on the Container page has a field, **Build memory (MB)**, and a **Save** button. The allowed range is 512 MB up to the memory your container reported when Egg last read it, at Test connection or during a deploy, or up to 8192 MB when it reported none. The hint under the field shows the range for this server.\n\nA build runs on your container, so it can never use all of its memory. At deploy time Egg lowers the cap to your container’s memory minus 512 MB, which stays free for your app, whatever the field says. A field set to 2048 MB on a container that reports 2048 MB gives a cap of 1536 MB. If that leaves less than 256 MB, the build does not start.\n\nProjects with a build step get at least 1024 MB while they build, if your container has room for it. A build step is a build command, or a build script such as `next build`. The floor applies only when your container’s memory minus 512 MB is at least 1024 MB, so a container that reports 1536 MB or more qualifies and a 1 GB container keeps the setting. It is applied when the build starts: the saved setting does not change, and a plain app or an install alone stays at the setting.\n\n### Change the build memory\n\n1. Step 1: Open Container\n\n   Pick the server from the list if you have more than one.\n\n   In the dashboard: Containerthen Build memory\n2. Step 2: Type a number in Build memory (MB)\n\n   A whole number of MB inside the range in the hint. Save stays off until the number differs from the saved one.\n3. Step 3: Press Save\n\n   A message says “Build memory set to <n> MB”. The next build on that server uses it. A build already running keeps the old limit.\n4. Step 4: Go back to the default\n\n   Press Use 512 MB, then Save. The button shows only while the saved value is not 512.\n\nA value outside the range is refused with “Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.” A build that needs more than the cap stops with “The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB.” When a release was already serving, “Your app is still running.” follows. The deployment shows Error, Egg deletes the new release folder, and the live release keeps running. Raise the limit and deploy again. The setting is changed in the browser only: no access token or agent call can change it. Each change is recorded in Activity as “Changed the build memory limit for”.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA build shares your container with your app, so the default is 512 MB, which covers installs and plain apps, a project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB when the container has room, and the cap always leaves 512 MB for the app. The limit is per server because servers differ: some can use more, and a build that fits a small server should not be given more than that server could ever offer. The top of the range follows what the server reported, so the page does not let you ask for memory the server does not have. A build gets no swap, so the limit is a real one and a build cannot slip past it by swapping.\n\n### Build CPU\n\nThe CPU limit applies to the build. It does not limit the app after it is installed on your Nest container. Each connected server has its own limit: 2 cores by default, adjustable from 0.5 cores in 0.25-core steps up to the core count the Nest container last reported when Egg checked it. If no count was reported, the maximum stays at 2 cores. The default is capped to the reported count when a server has fewer than 2 cores. The build also runs at a low priority, so your app gets the CPU first when it is busy.\n\n### Change the build CPU limit\n\n1. Step 1: Open Container\n\n   Pick the server from the list if you have more than one.\n\n   In the dashboard: Containerthen Build CPU\n2. Step 2: Set Build CPU limit (cores)\n\n   Enter a value in 0.25-core steps within the range shown under the field.\n3. Step 3: Press Save\n\n   The next build on that server uses the new CPU cap. A build already running keeps its current limit.\n4. Step 4: Restore the default\n\n   Press Use 2 cores, or the lower capacity-based default shown by the button, then Save.\n\nA value outside the range is refused with “Build CPU must be a number from 0.5 to <max> cores in 0.25-core steps.” The cap limits the build’s maximum CPU time; it does not reserve cores for a build.\n\n## Removing leftovers\n\nA leftover is a service named `egg-<project>.service` or a preview unit that no project uses, or a folder under `~/.egg` that is not a project. It usually appears after you delete a project with “Also stop the service and delete releases” unticked, or after a failed cleanup.\n\n### Remove a leftover\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on Removing leftovers\n\n   In Account, under Container permissions, for that server.\n2. Step 2: Open Container\n\n   Find the row under Orphans. Its kind is Service, Preview service or Folder.\n3. Step 3: Press Remove\n\n   A service asks “Remove <unit>?”. It stops the service, so anything running under it goes down, turns off starting at boot and deletes its unit file, the small file that tells Linux how to run it. Files in its folder are not touched.\n4. Step 4: Confirm a folder by name\n\n   A folder asks “Delete the folder <name>?” and you type its name, then press Delete folder. This deletes it and everything inside, including shared data, and cannot be undone.\n\nEgg knows what it made by two markers. A service file starts with a comment `egg:managed`, and a project folder holds a file named `.egg-managed`. An older folder without that file counts only if it holds nothing but `releases`, `incoming`, `shared`, `current` and `current.new` and has no `.git`. Anything without a marker is never overwritten, stopped, moved or deleted: a deploy refuses, cleanup refuses and project deletion leaves it.\n\nEvery request is checked again against a fresh read of the container. A row that now belongs to a project, that is a symbolic link, or that has no marker is refused, even if you press Remove. Access tokens and the MCP endpoint cannot remove leftovers at all, and one request removes at most 50 items.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nSomething you set up yourself can have a service whose name also starts with `egg-`, and a folder you made by hand can sit in `~/.egg`. A name alone proves nothing, so Egg trusts only its own marker, and checks it again at the moment you press Remove instead of trusting the list you were looking at.\n\n## Adopting an app that already runs\n\nIf an app of yours already runs on the container, Egg can check what the new project would collide with before it touches anything. In New Project the check runs by itself once the name, server and port are valid, and again at the start of every deploy. It never stops, moves or deletes anything.\n\n- **Blocks the deploy:** a service called `egg-<name>.service` that Egg did not create; the project folder being a link, a file, someone else’s git checkout, or a folder with other files in it; the port used by another program; a program or service already running from the project folder; and, for the first production deploy only, the same repository already running by hand from another checkout.\n- **Good to know, does not block:** a stopped service for the same repository, a checkout that is only sitting there, leftovers from an earlier Egg project of the same name (they are reused), and ports the container would not let it read.\n\nWhen something blocks, the form says “Deploy is turned off because N thing(s) on this container is/are in the way” and links to Open container status. Tick **Start with automatic deploys off** when you already run the repository on this container by hand. The project is created with automatic deploys off, so the first deploy is manual. Nothing turns them on afterwards: when the first deploy works, open Settings, Build and Run and tick **Deploy automatically on push**. This does not import, take over or stop the copy that is already running, and Egg does not import a running service yet. If that copy holds the port, stop it yourself first.\n\n## The terminal\n\nThe terminal is a live shell on your container in the browser, started in `~/.egg/<project>`. It is off until you turn it on for a server, and turning it on needs a Hack Club sign-in from the last 5 minutes. Anything you type runs for real.\n\n### Open a terminal\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on Terminal\n\n   In Account, under Container permissions, then confirm with Hack Club.\n2. Step 2: Open the project’s Terminal tab\n\n   With the switch off the page says “The terminal is turned off for this server.” and links to Account with a Go to Account button.\n3. Step 3: Press Open terminal\n\n   It shows who the shell runs as, for example “Connected as root” on the host name.\n4. Step 4: Type, then press Close terminal\n\n   A “Time left” countdown shows the time before the shell closes. Close terminal ends it.\n\n- It closes after 10 minutes without input. Output does not count as activity, and neither does resizing.\n- It closes after 60 minutes in total, whatever you are doing. A banner warns “This terminal closes in under a minute.”\n- It closes when you sign out, when another of your sessions is ended, when you delete your account, when you switch the terminal off, and when the connection drops or the shell exits.\n- Leaving the page keeps the terminal open until it times out. Come back and press **Resume terminal**, which replays the last 64 KB of output, or **Close it**.\n- The terminal takes every key while it has focus, including Tab. Press Escape, then Tab, to move focus to Close terminal. Copy with Ctrl+Shift+C and paste with Ctrl+V.\n- Only the opening and closing are recorded, with the duration and the reason. Keystrokes and output never are.\n\n| Title on the page | Why | Button |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal closed after a pause | 10 minutes without typing. | Reopen terminal |\n| Terminal reached its time limit | 60 minutes in total. | Reopen terminal |\n| Terminal closed | You pressed Close terminal. | Open a new terminal |\n| The shell ended | The shell exited, for example after typing exit. | Open a new terminal |\n| The connection dropped | The link to your container was lost. | Open a new terminal |\n| The terminal stopped | The shell stopped unexpectedly. | Open a new terminal |\n| This terminal is no longer open | It closed or timed out while you were away. | Open a new terminal |\n\n## The Files tab\n\nThe project’s Files tab browses `~/.egg/<project>`. Reading and downloading need no switch. Creating, uploading, editing, renaming and deleting need File changes on, and work only inside `shared/`. The live release `current/` is always read only, as are `releases/` and `incoming/`. `shared` itself cannot be renamed or deleted. A release’s `.env` is not shown or downloaded.\n\n| Folder | What it is | Can you change it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `shared/` | Survives deploys. Keep uploads and databases here. | Yes, with File changes on. |\n| `current/` | The live release. Replaced on the next deploy. | Never. |\n| `releases/` | Every deployed version, newest last. | No. |\n| `incoming/` | Archives on their way to a release. | No. |\n\n### Change a file\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on File changes\n\n   In Account, then confirm with Hack Club.\n2. Step 2: Open shared/\n\n   Uploads and edits are refused anywhere else. Elsewhere the page says “Uploads are not allowed here” and “Open shared/ first.”\n3. Step 3: Act\n\n   Use New folder or Upload (or drop files on the list), or select a file and press Edit, change it and press Save. Discard throws the edit away. Rename (or F2) and Delete are in the row. Deleting a folder asks you to type its name.\n4. Step 4: Restart if the app needs to re-read it\n\n   A change does not restart the app. Press Restart on the Service card when your app reads the file only at start. The app is unavailable for a moment while it restarts.\n\nCode changes belong in your repository, then a deploy. Files that must last, like data or settings, belong in `shared/`, which deploys never touch. A file you upload over an existing name asks before it replaces it. A link is never written through, and a link to a folder opens as a folder.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\n`current/` is read only even with File changes on because it is the code your app runs: editing it would put code on your container that is in no repository and that the next deploy silently erases. `shared/` is the one place a deploy never touches, so it is the one place a change can safely last.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal idle | 10 minutes | “Terminal closed after a pause”. Anything running in it was stopped. In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.idleMs) |\n| Terminal total | 60 minutes | “Terminal reached its time limit”. In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.maxAgeMs) |\n| Terminals open | 1 per project per person, 5 per account, 50 on the server | “A terminal is already open for this project. Resume or close it first.”, “You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.” or “Too many terminals are open right now. Try again soon.” In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.maxSessionsPerUser maxSessions) |\n| Terminal input | 8 KB a request, 300 requests a minute | “Send at most 8192 bytes at a time.” or “Too many requests for terminal input.” Wait and retry. In the code: server/terminal.ts (inputBytes) server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.terminalInput) |\n| Terminal size | 20 to 400 columns, 5 to 200 rows | “The terminal size must be 20 to 400 columns and 5 to 200 rows.” In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.minCols to maxRows) |\n| Terminal output kept | The last 64 KB | A reload resumes with that much. In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS.bufferBytes) |\n| File preview and edit | The first 1 MB | A larger file shows “Too large to preview”. In the code: server/files-paths.ts (READ_LIMIT) |\n| File upload and download | 50 MB | “That file is over the 50 MB limit.” The row says “Over 50 MB”. In the code: server/files-paths.ts (UPLOAD_LIMIT DOWNLOAD_LIMIT) |\n| Folder listing | 3000 entries | More are not shown. In the code: server/files.ts (LIST_LIMIT) |\n| Path | 1024 characters, and 255 bytes in one name | “Path is longer than 1024 characters.” or “A name in the path is longer than 255 bytes.” In the code: server/files-paths.ts (MAX_PATH_LENGTH MAX_SEGMENT_BYTES) |\n| File reads | 120 a minute | “Too many file requests.” Wait and retry. In the code: server/files.ts (FILE_READ_LIMIT) |\n| File changes | 60 a minute | “Too many requests for file changes.” Wait and retry. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.fileWrite) |\n| File operation time | 30 seconds, 120 for upload, download and delete | The request fails with the container’s answer. Try again. In the code: server/files.ts (timeoutMs) |\n| Leftover removal | 50 items a request, 10 requests a minute | “Remove at most 50 items at a time.” In the code: server/inventory.ts (MAX_ORPHANS_PER_REQUEST) server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.orphanRemoval) |\n| Container reads | 60 seconds a read, 120 seconds for a removal | The read fails with the SSH message. Press Refresh. In the code: server/inventory.ts (timeoutMs) |\n| Conflict checks | 20 a minute | “Too many checks. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.” In the code: app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts (LIMIT) |\n| Build memory | 512 MB to the server’s memory, default 512 MB | “Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts (DEFAULT_CONTAINER_BUILD_MEMORY_MB buildMemoryRange) |\n| Build cap on the container | The Build memory setting, raised to at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when the container’s memory minus 512 MB is at least 1024 MB, and never more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB. No build starts under 256 MB. | “The build did not start: your container reports <n> MB of memory, and 512 MB of that is kept for your app, which leaves too little to build in. Use a larger container.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (containerMemoryCapMb CONTAINER_RESERVE_MB MIN_CONTAINER_CAP_MB) |\n| Build CPU | 0.5 cores to the Nest server’s reported core count, in 0.25-core steps; defaults to 2 or the lower reported count | “Build CPU must be a number from 0.5 to <max> cores in 0.25-core steps.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts (DEFAULT_BUILD_CPUS buildCpuRange checkBuildCpu) |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `The terminal is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Terminal in Account. Nothing else is needed to read files or the Container page.\n- `Changing files is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on File changes. Reading and downloading still work without it.\n- `Removing leftovers is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Removing leftovers. Leftovers stay listed while it is off.\n- `Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning this on needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.`\n\n  403. Flip the switch again. It sends you through Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied.\n- `This server has not been verified yet. Run Test connection on the Connect page first.`\n\n  409. The host key is not pinned yet, and a terminal never opens to a host Egg has not checked. Press Test connection.\n- `A terminal is already open for this project. Resume or close it first.`\n\n  409. Only one terminal per project and person. Press Resume terminal, or Close it, then open a new one.\n- `You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.`\n\n  429. One account may hold 5 terminals across all its projects, so one account cannot use up the server’s share. Close one you are not using.\n- `Too many terminals are open right now. Try again soon.`\n\n  503. The server as a whole holds 50 terminals. Wait a little and open yours again.\n- `The terminal was closed before it opened.`\n\n  403. You signed out, or switched Terminal off, while it was still connecting. Open it again.\n- `This project has no folder on your container yet. Deploy it first.`\n\n  404. The folder is created by the first deploy. Deploy once, then open Files.\n- `This location is read only. Files in shared/ can be changed. The live release (current/) cannot.`\n\n  403. Open shared/ and work there. The page shows the same rule as “current/ is the live release and is read only.”\n- `current/ is the live release and is read only. Changing it would change the code your app runs. Make the change in your repository and deploy, or use shared/ for files that last.`\n\n  403. Change the code in your repository and deploy. Put files that must last in shared/.\n- `This file holds the project's environment variable values, so it is not shown here. Change the values in the project's settings.`\n\n  403. The Files tab never opens a variables file. Change the values in the project’s settings, then deploy.\n- `That file holds the project's environment variable values, which are never returned by the API.`\n\n  403. The API refuses those files too. Use env:read to list names.\n- `Egg did not create this, so it was left alone.`\n\n  The leftover has no marker. If you are sure, remove it yourself in a terminal.\n- `This belongs to one of your projects, so it was left alone.`\n\n  It is not a leftover. Delete the project first if you want it gone.\n- `The container’s answer was cut short, so nothing was classified.`\n\n  Press Refresh.\n- `Remove at most 50 items at a time.`\n\n  400. Remove in batches.\n- `Nothing was changed on your container. <details>`\n\n  The conflict check refused. The details say what is in the way: choose another name or port, or stop that program yourself.\n- `Could not read the container.`\n\n  The page could not reach the container. Check it is running, then press Refresh.\n- `Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.`\n\n  400. Type a whole number inside the range in the hint under Build memory (MB). The top of the range is the memory your container reported when Egg last read it, at Test connection or during a deploy.\n- `The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB. Your app is still running.`\n\n  The build was stopped with exit 137. Your app was not. The second sentence is added only when a release was already serving. Raise Build memory (MB), press Save and deploy again, or make the build use less memory. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- `Could not read the build memory.`\n\n  The Build memory card could not load the setting. Reload the page. It does not need the container to be reachable.\n- `Could not save the build memory.`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try Save again.\n\nThe rest of the terminal and file messages, with their fixes, are on the [troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) page.\n\n## Example: Remove a leftover service\n\n### Given\n\nYou deleted a project named old-api with “Also stop the service” unticked, so `egg-old-api.service` still runs.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Turn on Removing leftovers\n\n   In the dashboard: Accountthen Container permissionsthen Removing leftovers\n2. Step 2: Find the row\n\n   Open Container and look under Orphans for egg-old-api.service.\n3. Step 3: Remove it\n\n   Press Remove, then confirm with Remove service.\n\n### Result\n\nA message says the service was removed and the row leaves Orphans. Its folder, if any, is its own row and stays until you delete it by typing its name.\n\n## Example: Keep an upload across deploys\n\n### Given\n\nAn app that reads uploads from a folder, and File changes switched on.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open the Files tab\n\n   Open shared/ in the project.\n2. Step 2: Make a folder and upload\n\n   Press New folder, name it, then press Upload.\n\n### Result\n\nThe files are still there after the next deploy, because `shared/` is never touched by a release.\n\n## Example: Edit a settings file without a deploy\n\n### Given\n\nAn app that reads `shared/config.json` when it starts, and File changes switched on.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open the file\n\n   In the Files tab open shared/ and select config.json.\n2. Step 2: Edit and save\n\n   Press Edit, change the value, press Save.\n3. Step 3: Restart\n\n   Press Restart on the Service card so the app reads it.\n\n### Result\n\nThe app runs with the new setting. The change is in Activity as “Saved a file on” with the path and no contents.\n\n**Related:** [Account](/docs/account), [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nBots\n\nPage 16 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)\n\nHost a Discord bot written with discord.js: import the repository, add DISCORD_TOKEN, deploy, and let systemd keep it running.\n\nA discord.js bot is a Node.js process that connects to Discord over its gateway. It stays running and does not listen on a port. On Egg it is a **worker** workload: a background process that Egg keeps running instead of publishing a URL. If you do not have a bot yet, follow the [official discord.js guide](https://discordjs.guide/) until one runs locally with `npm start`. A repository that installs with `npm install` and starts with `npm start` deploys here.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL. Egg reads the files and shows what it found under “We found”.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `discord.js` is in the dependencies, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The summary says: “This looks like a Discord bot. It is set up as a background app that needs no port, and DISCORD_TOKEN is offered as an environment variable.” If Egg guessed wrong, the picker below stays editable.\n3. **Add the token.** In the environment section, the row `DISCORD_TOKEN` appears with “suggested for Discord bot (discord.js)” behind it. Paste the bot token from the [Discord developer portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications). Egg saves the value through the normal environment variable path and never shows it again.\n4. **Press Deploy.** There is no port to fill in. When it is live the deployment page says `The worker is running.` The process stays active. Egg checks it every few minutes. If it stops, you get an alert on the channel you set up.\n\n## What Egg does with it\n\nsystemd, the Linux service manager, starts the bot's process. It restarts the process with `Restart=always` if it exits. There is no health path to answer, so Egg checks that the process stays active instead. The Service card on the project page shows the status, the uptime, the memory and a **Restarts** count. A bot that keeps crashing is visible without opening the logs. Read the runtime output on the [Logs page](/docs/logs). Never paste the token into the repository. Use [environment variables](/docs/env).\n\nIf it also serves web pages\n\nSome bots also host a small web dashboard. A worker cannot receive HTTP requests in this version. Keep the bot as a worker and put the dashboard in a separate web project. A Discord webhook or interaction proxy needs its own setup outside the bot for the same reason.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.py)](/docs/bots-discordpy), [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](/docs/bots-slack), [Deploy a Telegram bot](/docs/bots-telegram), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Environment variables](/docs/env).\n\n\n---\n\nBots\n\nPage 17 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Discord bot (discord.py)\n\nHost a Discord bot written with discord.py: a requirements.txt with the framework, DISCORD_TOKEN in the environment, and the Python runtime preset.\n\nA discord.py bot is a Python process that stays connected to Discord. It does not listen on a port, so it deploys as a **worker** workload. If you do not have a bot yet, the framework's own repository carries [runnable examples](https://github.com/Rapptz/discord.py/tree/master/examples), from a first bot to slash commands. Grow one into your own repository. A project with a `requirements.txt` and an entry point such as `main.py` deploys here. The install step reads your `requirements.txt` on the container.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `discord.py` is in your `requirements.txt`, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The summary names DISCORD_TOKEN. The picker stays editable if the guess is wrong. The `python` preset is the right runtime. Check that the start command points at your entry point, for example `python3 main.py`.\n3. **Add the token.** The environment section lists `DISCORD_TOKEN` with “suggested for Discord bot (discord.py)” behind it. Paste the bot token from the [Discord developer portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications). Egg saves the value once and never shows it again.\n4. **Press Deploy.** No port, no domain. When it is live the page says `The worker is running.`\n\nPython specifics\n\nEgg reads your `requirements.txt` for package names only. It uses the file to spot the framework. Version pins stay in the file and apply when the container installs them. discord.py needs Python 3.8 or newer, and prefix commands must be registered in the setup hook. An older pin can fail at startup with an import error. Check the runtime logs first when a Python bot does not start.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](/docs/bots-slack), [Deploy a Telegram bot](/docs/bots-telegram), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Logs](/docs/logs).\n\n\n---\n\nBots\n\nPage 18 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)\n\nHost a Slack app built with Bolt for JavaScript in Socket Mode: the workload is a worker, and the token names are listed for you.\n\nA Slack app built with [Bolt for JavaScript](https://github.com/slackapi/bolt-js) can work two ways. With **Socket Mode** it connects outward to Slack. It never needs a public URL, so it deploys as a **worker** workload like any other bot. With an **HTTP receiver**, Slack sends events to a request URL on your app. That needs a public address. A worker cannot receive HTTP requests in this version. Use Socket Mode here.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL. A Bolt app installs with `npm install` and starts with `npm start`.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `@slack/bolt` is in the dependencies, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The picker stays editable if the guess is wrong.\n3. **Add the tokens.** The environment section lists two suggested names from your [Slack app settings](https://api.slack.com/apps): `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN`, which starts with `xoxb-`, and `SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET`. If your code also reads the app-level Socket Mode token, which starts with `xapp-`, add it under the name your code expects, for example `SLACK_APP_TOKEN`.\n4. **Press Deploy.** No port and no request URL in this mode. When it is live the page says `The worker is running.`\n\n## Why Socket Mode\n\nSocket Mode keeps the advantages of a worker workload. systemd restarts the process if it exits. Egg checks that it stays active. Nothing about the container has to be reachable from the internet. Turn it on in the app settings on api.slack.com. Allow the events and interactivity scopes. Paste the app token into the environment under one of the two names above. An HTTP receiver would change the story. The app would have to answer requests on a URL. A separate web project does that here.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Deploy a Telegram bot](/docs/bots-telegram), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Environment variables](/docs/env).\n\n\n---\n\nBots\n\nPage 19 of 40\n\n# Deploy a Telegram bot\n\nHost a Telegram bot written with Telegraf or python-telegram-bot: import the repository, add TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN, deploy, and let systemd keep the process running.\n\nA Telegram bot is a process that connects to Telegram and waits for updates. It does not listen on a port, so on Egg it is a **worker** workload: a background process that Egg keeps running instead of publishing a URL. Egg reads your files to find the framework. When `telegraf` is in your `package.json` dependencies, or `python-telegram-bot` is in your `requirements.txt`, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The summary names TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN. A project that installs and starts with `npm start`, or with `python3 main.py`, deploys here.\n\n## Deploy it\n\n1. **Import the repository.** On New project, pick it from your GitHub account or paste its URL. Egg reads the files and shows what it found under “We found”.\n2. **Keep the detected workload.** When `telegraf` is in your `package.json` dependencies, or `python-telegram-bot` is in your `requirements.txt`, the workload is already set to `Bot or background worker`. The picker stays editable if the guess is wrong. If you do not have a bot yet, follow the [Telegraf documentation](https://telegraf.js.org/) to write one, or start from the framework's own [runnable python-telegram-bot examples](https://github.com/python-telegram-bot/python-telegram-bot/tree/master/examples).\n3. **Add the token.** The environment section lists `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` with “suggested for Telegram bot (telegraf)” behind it for a Telegraf project, or “suggested for Telegram bot (python-telegram-bot)” for a python-telegram-bot project. Paste the bot token from [BotFather on Telegram](https://t.me/BotFather). Egg saves the value through the normal environment variable path and never shows it again.\n4. **Press Deploy.** There is no port to fill in. When it is live the deployment page says `The worker is running.` The process stays active. Egg checks it every few minutes. If it stops, you get an alert on the channel you set up.\n\n## What Egg does with it\n\nsystemd, the Linux service manager, starts the bot's process. It restarts the process with `Restart=always` if it exits. There is no health path to answer, so Egg checks that the process stays active instead. The Service card on the project page shows the status, the uptime, the memory and a **Restarts** count. A bot that keeps crashing is visible without opening the logs. Read the runtime output on the [Logs page](/docs/logs). Never paste the token into the repository. Use [environment variables](/docs/env).\n\nPolling and webhooks\n\nA worker holds no public URL in this version. Use long polling. It is the default in both Telegraf and python-telegram-bot. A webhook app needs a separate web project. The web project receives the updates and forwards them to the bot until workers can expose a URL.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](/docs/bots-slack), [Keep a bot alive](/docs/bots-alive), [Environment variables](/docs/env).\n\n\n---\n\nBots\n\nPage 20 of 40\n\n# Keep a bot alive\n\nWhat keeps a bot running: systemd restarts, the Restarts count, crash loops and their fixes, and nightly work that belongs in scheduled jobs.\n\nEgg gives a bot the same care a web app gets. It adjusts for a process that does not answer requests. A bot that fails shows no error page to anyone. Read these signals instead.\n\n## Starts, restarts and crash loops\n\n- systemd starts the bot's process with `Restart=always`. If it exits, systemd restarts it within seconds. Most crashes pass unnoticed, which is the point. The first place one shows is the **Restarts** count on the Service card.\n- A stable bot reads `Running` on the Service card with a low, slow-moving Restarts number. A climbing number means the process keeps exiting. Open the [runtime logs](/docs/logs) and look for the error it prints each time it starts.\n- When a bot keeps failing to start, systemd pauses it for a while instead of retrying forever. The status reads `Crashed`. Fix the error. Press **Restart** on the Service card to start the bot again.\n- A missing token is the most common first failure. The process starts, reads an empty DISCORD_TOKEN and exits at once. Set values under [Environment variables](/docs/env) and restart. Values reach the server on the next deploy.\n\n| Signal | Where you see it | What it means |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A climbing Restarts count | Service card | The process exits as soon as it starts. The runtime logs hold the error it prints each time. |\n| Status reads Crashed and stays there | Service card | systemd has paused the retries. Fix the error, then press Restart to start the unit again. |\n| A “has stopped” message | Your [notification channel](/docs/notifications) | The process did not stay up, or keeps restarting. Egg keeps checking. It sends a second message when it is back. |\n| A memory alert | Your notification channel | The bot crossed its memory limit. A leak grows the memory reading on the Service card over hours. A cache without a cap grows it over days. |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA web app that fails shows an error page, and the health check catches it. A bot that fails shows nothing to anyone. Read the process state, the restart count and the alert instead. A bot is healthy when its process stays up, nothing else.\n\n## Nightly work belongs in scheduled jobs\n\nDo not keep a bot awake just to run tomorrow's task. A crash resets a sleep loop, so do not build the schedule on one. Give the schedule to [Scheduled jobs](/docs/jobs) instead. A job is a systemd timer next to your bot. It runs a command inside the current release, with its environment variables, at the times you set, in UTC. The bot stays quiet in between and needs less memory. It cannot miss a schedule it never had.\n\n## When it stops, hear about it\n\nAdd a [notification webhook](/docs/notifications) before the first bad night. A down alert says a worker “has stopped”. Recovery says it is back up. [monitoring](/docs/monitoring) explains the checks behind them, from failed checks in a row to resource limits. The Restarts count on the Service card and these alerts are the whole picture of a bot's health.\n\n**Related resources:** [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](/docs/bots-discordjs), [Scheduled jobs](/docs/jobs), [Logs](/docs/logs), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 21 of 40\n\n# GitHub and previews\n\nEgg reads your code through a GitHub app that you install on chosen repositories, and builds when you push.\n\nThe app lets Egg read a repository and hear about pushes without holding your GitHub password, and you choose which repositories it can see. You need a GitHub account and a connected server, so Egg can use the app for private repositories, pushes to deploy and pull request comments. Egg clones with a short-lived token that stays on the Egg machine and is never sent to your container. You can import a public repository without the app by pasting its address, but then pushes do not deploy.\n\n## Deploying on push\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Install the app\n\n   On the New project page, the “Connect GitHub” card has **Install the GitHub App**, which opens GitHub so you can choose the account and repositories to share. If the list is empty, come back and press **I already installed it**, which signs you in to GitHub once to find your installations, so the sign-in is used once and not stored. Use **Add another account or repository** to share more later, and when you return the page says “GitHub is connected. Pick a repository below.”\n2. Step 2: Import the repository\n\n   Pick your repository under **GitHub account**, find it with **Search repositories…** and press **Import**, so the list shows the newest pushes first. Leave **Deploy automatically on push** on, as it is by default.\n3. Step 3: Push\n\n   A push to the production branch deploys to production, and a push to any other branch builds a preview, so the deployment shows the source “webhook”, the first line of the commit message (cut at 300 characters) and the author.\n4. Step 4: Open a pull request\n\n   A pull request from a branch of the same repository builds a preview too, and gets a comment. See Pull request previews below.\n\n- Branch deletions, tag pushes and other events are ignored.\n- GitHub delivers each push to `/api/webhooks/github` on Egg’s web address, so GitHub must be able to reach it. An Egg on localhost cannot receive pushes. There, press Deploy or use a deploy hook.\n- Only projects with **Deploy automatically on push** on, whose repository name matches and whose GitHub installation is one the project’s owner linked, are built.\n- The same commit on the same branch is not built twice while one for it is already queued, building or deploying.\n- Every distinct push delivery remains queued, even if it arrives after a different commit. Webhook delivery order does not prove commit order, so Egg does not cancel a queued push based on arrival time.\n- GitHub signs every delivery and Egg checks the signature, so a repeat of a signed body is answered “duplicate delivery” and not run again, using a one-way digest Egg keeps in its database. Pressing Redeliver in GitHub for a push that already worked therefore does nothing, so push a new commit or press Deploy.\n- If you rename the repository on GitHub, Egg follows the rename: the stored name and the clone address change to the new name. That needs the app to receive the Repository event.\n- A project made from a pasted public URL is not built by a push. See Projects and runtimes.\n- If you uninstall the app on GitHub, projects keep their records but lose access to private repositories until you install it again.\n\n| Permission or event | What Egg uses it for | If it is missing |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Read access to code and metadata | Listing repositories, reading files for “We found”, cloning to build. | Private repositories cannot be read. |\n| Push event | Deploying on push. | Pushes do not start builds. |\n| Pull requests: read and write, and the Pull request event | Previews for pull requests and the comment. | Previews of pushed branches still build; the comment is skipped quietly. |\n| Repository event | Following a rename. | After a rename, pushes no longer match the project. |\n| Installation event | Noticing an uninstall. | Projects keep a link to an installation that is gone. |\n\n## Pull request previews\n\n- A pull request from the same repository that is not a draft, and not from the production branch, builds a preview, so the app posts one comment when it finishes and edits that same comment when a later build of the pull request finishes. It only ever edits a comment it wrote itself.\n- The comment says “Preview ready” or “Preview failed”, with the project name, branch, short commit and a link, “Open the deployment”, that only you can open. A ready comment also gives the preview’s port and says Nest only gives it a public address once a domain is mapped to that port. A failed one says “The build log is in Egg.” It never includes error output, the server name or variable values.\n- Closing the pull request, merged or not, cancels builds still running and removes its Ready previews from your container. A build that was still running shows as Canceled with “Pull request closed”. A Ready preview is stopped and removed from the container and is marked as cleaned up.\n- Pull requests from forks and from the production branch are not built. The events handled are opened, reopened, synchronize, ready_for_review and closed.\n\nA draft pull request’s branch still builds a preview\n\nA push to the branch of a draft pull request in the same repository still builds a preview, because it comes from the push event, so only the pull request event skips drafts. No comment is written until you mark the pull request ready for review.\n\nPermissions for the comment\n\nCommenting needs Pull requests read and write access and the Pull request event, so without them previews still build and the comment is skipped quietly. Egg logs a 403 from GitHub on the server, and nothing shows on the deployment.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe app model means Egg never holds your password or a personal token, so it gets a short-lived token for one installation, cached only until a minute before it expires, and sees only what you shared. Previews run on a separate port from production, so a branch under test never replaces the live app.\n\n## Building on GitHub Actions\n\nThe setup steps, limits and troubleshooting are on [Build with GitHub Actions](/docs/build-github-actions), so this is the short version.\n\nA project can build on GitHub and send Egg the finished release instead of building on your container, and your project settings make the workflow file while the GitHub build card shows the steps. The workflow reads four credentials, and Egg shows each value once when you make the build credentials.\n\n- **Production.** `EGG_UPLOAD_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` and `EGG_ENV_PRODUCTION_TOKEN` are secrets of a GitHub Environment named `egg-production` (Settings, Environments), so limit that environment to the production branch. You can also turn on Required reviewers, so a person approves each production build.\n- **Previews.** `EGG_UPLOAD_PREVIEW_TOKEN` and `EGG_ENV_PREVIEW_TOKEN` are repository secrets.\n- **Never save the two production values as repository secrets.** A repository secret can be read by a workflow that someone edits on any branch.\n\nTreat workflow editors as trusted operators\n\nTest connection cannot see your GitHub secrets or environment settings, so check by hand that the production values are in the `egg-production` environment and not in the repository, where anyone who can edit workflows could run code with the preview credentials. A project has a single owner, and Egg does not isolate the Nest account from that.\n\n## Limits, errors and an example\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Repositories listed | 5 pages of 100, newest push first | Repositories beyond that are not listed. Paste the address instead. In the code: server/github/app.ts |\n| Installation owner | The first Egg user to link it | “That GitHub installation is not linked to your account.” In the code: server/github/app.ts |\n| App sign-in token | 9 minutes | Renewed automatically. In the code: server/github/app.ts |\n| Comments read when updating | 100 per pull request | A comment beyond that is not found, and a new one is posted. In the code: server/github/pulls.ts |\n| Repeated deliveries | A digest of each signed body is kept in the database, with no expiry | A repeat of the same body is answered “duplicate delivery”. A push with a new commit has a new body. In the code: server/github/webhook.ts |\n| Webhook payload | 5 MB | “payload too large” In the code: app/api/webhooks/github/route.ts |\n| Pull request events handled | opened, reopened, synchronize, ready_for_review, closed | Other events are ignored. In the code: server/github/webhook.ts |\n| Commit message kept | First line, 300 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/github/webhook.ts |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“No repositories shared with the app yet.”`\n\n  Open **Install the GitHub App** and share at least one repository.\n- `“No repositories match “QUERY”.”`\n\n  The search found nothing. Clear it, or add the repository with Add another account or repository.\n- `“The GitHub App is not configured.” or “The GitHub App is not set up on this installation.”`\n\n  This copy of Egg has no GitHub app set up. Whoever runs it sets the GITHUB_APP_* and GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET variables.\n- `“GitHub could not be connected. Try again.” or “That GitHub link expired. Try again.”`\n\n  Press I already installed it again, in one browser, within 10 minutes.\n- `“The GitHub App is no longer installed on that account.”`\n\n  Install it again on GitHub, then press I already installed it.\n- `“GitHub refused to issue an access token (STATUS).”, “GitHub did not accept the authorization (ERROR).” or “Could not list repositories (STATUS).”`\n\n  GitHub rejected the request. Try again. If it repeats, reinstall the app.\n- `“That GitHub installation is not linked to your account.”`\n\n  Another Egg account linked that installation first. Install the app yourself on your own account.\n- A push did not deploy\n\n  Check Deploy automatically on push, that the project came from the app and not a pasted URL, and that the push was to a branch and not a tag or a deletion.\n- A webhook delivery answers 401 or 503 in GitHub’s list\n\n  401 “bad signature” means the webhook secret in GitHub does not match the one Egg has. 503 “webhook not configured” means Egg has none. Whoever runs Egg sets GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET.\n- No comment on a pull request\n\n  Check that the pull request is not a draft or from a fork, and that the app has Pull requests read and write access. The deploy itself is not affected.\n\n### Example: Try a change in a pull request\n\n#### Given\n\nSam wants to try a new command in `recipe-bot` without touching the live bot.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Push a branch\n\n   Sam pushes branch `new-search`. A preview starts building.\n2. Step 2: Open a pull request\n\n   Sam opens a pull request into `main`.\n3. Step 3: Merge it\n\n   Later Sam merges and closes the pull request.\n\n#### Result\n\nA preview deployment builds on port `20000`. When it finishes the pull request gets one comment, “Preview ready”, with an Open the deployment link. When Sam closes the pull request, the preview is stopped and removed from the container and its deployment is marked as cleaned up. The merge to `main` deploys to production on its own.\n\n**Related:** [Deployments](/docs/deployments), [Deploy hooks](/docs/hooks), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 22 of 40\n\n# Deploy hooks\n\nA private URL that starts a deployment when it receives a POST. Use it from CI, a cron job or a script.\n\nUse a deploy hook when the GitHub app cannot help: from CI, a scheduled job, a script on your laptop or a project made from a pasted public address. The URL is the only credential, so the caller needs no access token or cookie, and the call only queues a build. That build runs on the Egg machine, so your app restarts on your container when it goes live. You need a project and a way to send an HTTP POST.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Create the hook\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Deploy Hook**, and press **Create hook**, so the card changes from “No hook yet. Create one to get a URL you can call from CI.”\n2. Step 2: Copy the URL now\n\n   The card shows the URL in a copy field and says “Copy it now. For safety it is not shown again. Anyone with this URL can deploy this project, so treat it like a password.” Egg stores only a hash, and the card also shows a ready-made `curl -X POST` line.\n3. Step 3: Call it\n\n   Send a POST, so an optional JSON body picks a branch, and any branch other than production builds a preview, while no body builds the production branch.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST https://your-egg/api/hooks/deploy/ndh_...\n   curl -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"branch\":\"new-search\"}' https://your-egg/api/hooks/deploy/ndh_...\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Replace or revoke\n\n   **Make a new URL** replaces the old one, which stops working at once, and **Revoke** removes the hook, so after either a call to the old URL answers 404. When a hook exists but its URL is hidden, the card says “A hook is active and its URL is hidden. Make a new one to see a URL again; the old one stops working at once.”\n\n- A good call answers 202 with `{ \"deployment\": { \"id\", \"state\", \"branch\", \"env\" } }`, so the state is “queued” and the deployment’s source is “hook”, its author “deploy hook” and its message “Triggered by deploy hook”. Activity records “Deployed through the deploy hook for” the project.\n- The build is of the tip of the branch at the moment it runs. A hook cannot name a commit. If the branch does not exist, the deployment ends as Error with “Could not clone the repository…”.\n- The body can only hold a `branch`, with the same rules as a project branch: letters, digits and . _ - /, up to 200 characters, no leading hyphen and no “..”. Any other field is refused.\n- The URL looks like `/api/hooks/deploy/ndh_` followed by 32 characters. It does not expire. There is no sign-in, no cookie and no origin check on this route; the URL is the only secret.\n- Replacing or revoking a hook from the API needs the `deployments:write` permission.\n\nTreat it like a password\n\nAnyone with the URL can deploy that project. Do not paste it in a public repository or a chat. If it leaks, press Make a new URL.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA hook refuses a second call while a deployment is running instead of queueing it, so a script stuck in a loop cannot fill the queue, and the body holds only a branch, so a leaked URL can only build code already in your repository, never run a command or change a setting.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Hooks per project | One | Make a new URL replaces it. In the code: server/hooks.ts |\n| Running deployments | One per project at a time | 409 “A deployment is already running for this project.” In the code: server/hooks.ts |\n| Request body | 4 KB | 413 “That request is too large.” In the code: app/api/hooks/deploy/[token]/route.ts |\n| Branch name | Same rules as a project branch | 400 with the reason. In the code: server/hooks.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `404 “Unknown hook”`\n\n  The URL is wrong, was replaced, was revoked, or is cut off. A malformed token gives the same answer. Copy the current URL, or make a new one.\n- `409 “A deployment is already running for this project.”`\n\n  A deployment is Queued, Building or Deploying. Call again when it finishes.\n- `400 “Request body must be JSON”`\n\n  Send valid JSON, or no body at all.\n- `400 “Unrecognized key: \"NAME\"”`\n\n  The body holds a field other than `branch`. Remove it.\n- `400 “Branch names may use letters, digits, . _ - and /” or “Invalid branch name”`\n\n  Use a branch name that git accepts and that does not start with a hyphen.\n- `400 “Invalid input: expected string, received number” or “Too small: expected string to have >=1 characters”`\n\n  The branch must be a non-empty string.\n- `413 “That request is too large.”`\n\n  The body is over 4 KB. Send only `{ \"branch\": \"name\" }`.\n- The call returns 202 but the deployment ends as Error\n\n  The call only queues the build. Open the deployment to read why it failed.\n- `“Could not create the hook” or “Could not revoke the hook”`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try again.\n\n## Example: Redeploy every night\n\n### Given\n\nSam wants `recipe-bot` to redeploy every night so it picks up a data file fetched at build time.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Create the hook\n\n   Sam creates the hook and copies the URL.\n2. Step 2: Store it\n\n   Sam stores the URL as a secret in a cron service.\n3. Step 3: Schedule the call\n\n   Sam runs the `curl -X POST` line at 03:00.\n\n### Result\n\nEach night a deployment appears in the list with source “hook”. If a deployment is already running at that time, the call answers 409 and nothing breaks; the next night’s call works.\n\n**Related:** [GitHub and previews](/docs/github), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 23 of 40\n\n# Scheduled jobs\n\nA command that runs on a schedule inside your current release, such as a nightly clean-up or a weekly report.\n\nA scheduled job is a command and a time pattern, so Egg turns each job into a systemd timer on your container. systemd is the Linux service manager, and a timer starts something at set times. Your command runs in the folder of your current release, the deployed version of your app that is live now, and gets the same environment variables as your app, with the project’s virtual environment first on the path for Python. You need a project that has been deployed once, because a job has nothing to run in before that.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Open the card\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Scheduled Jobs**, so the card says “No jobs yet.” when you have no jobs.\n2. Step 2: Fill in the form\n\n   Under **Add a job**, type a **Name**, a **Schedule** and a **Command**, or use the **Common schedules** list to fill in the schedule for you. A line under it says what the schedule means and when it next runs in UTC, or why it is not valid.\n3. Step 3: Press Add job\n\n   The card says “Added. It is armed on your container now.” because Egg saves the timer on your container before it keeps its record, so a job you can see on the card is installed.\n4. Step 4: Check how it went\n\n   Each row shows the schedule in words, the next run and the last result: “Has not run yet”, “Running now”, “Succeeded” or “Failed (exit N)”, with how long ago. Egg reads your container when the card opens and every 30 seconds while the page is visible, so the row stays current.\n\n- Your name is 1 to 60 characters: letters, numbers, spaces, dots, dashes and underscores, starting with a letter or number. The command is one line of up to 500 characters, so for anything longer, put a script in your repository and run it from the command.\n- Egg runs the command with `bash -lc` from the current release folder, so a relative path such as `node scripts/cleanup.js` works. A deploy replaces the current release, so the next run uses the new code.\n- Job names and commands are shown on the card in plain text. Keep secrets in environment variables, not in the command.\n\n## Schedule format\n\nA schedule is five fields separated by spaces: minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, the form cron uses. Cron is the classic Unix scheduler, and all times are UTC, so a job does not depend on your container’s time zone.\n\n| Field | Values |\n| --- | --- |\n| Minute | 0 to 59 |\n| Hour | 0 to 23 |\n| Day of month | 1 to 31 |\n| Month | 1 to 12 |\n| Day of week | 0 to 7. Both 0 and 7 are Sunday, and 1 is Monday. |\n\nEach field takes `*` for every value, a number, a list such as `1,15`, a range such as `9-17` or a step such as `*/15` or `0-30/10`. Four nicknames are also accepted in any letter case: `@hourly`, `@daily` (and `@midnight`), `@weekly` and `@monthly`, so Egg saves `@daily` as its five fields, `0 0 * * *`.\n\n| Schedule | When it runs |\n| --- | --- |\n| `*/15 * * * *` | Every 15 minutes |\n| `0 * * * *` | Every hour, on the hour. Same as @hourly. |\n| `0 */6 * * *` | Every 6 hours, at minute 0 |\n| `30 3 * * *` | Every day at 03:30 UTC |\n| `0 9 * * 1-5` | Monday to Friday at 09:00 UTC |\n| `0 9 * * 1` | Every Monday at 09:00 UTC |\n| `0 4 1 * *` | On the 1st of each month at 04:00 UTC |\n\n| Schedule | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| `0 9 * * MON` | Names such as MON or JAN are not accepted. Use numbers. |\n| `0 0 L * *` | Cron extras such as ?, L and W are not accepted. |\n| `0 0 * * * *` | Six fields, with seconds, are not accepted. There must be exactly five. |\n| `@yearly` | Only @hourly, @daily, @midnight, @weekly and @monthly are accepted. |\n| `0 9 1 * 1` | Both a day of the month and a day of the week are set. Set only one and leave the other as *. |\n| `*/2 * * * *` | It would run every 2 minutes. A job runs at most once every 5 minutes. |\n\n## How a job behaves\n\n- **It gives way to your app.** A job runs at a lower priority than your app, so if your container runs out of memory, Egg stops the job before the app.\n- **Missed runs are not made up.** If your container was off or restarting at the scheduled time, the job waits for its next time, so it does not run a backlog.\n- **It does not overlap itself.** If a run is still going when the next time arrives, Egg skips that time, so a second copy never starts.\n- **It is stopped after 30 minutes.** Egg ends a run that is still going, so the last result reads Failed.\n- **A run can start a few seconds late.** The timer is accurate to within 10 seconds.\n- **It goes when the project goes.** Deleting a project with “Also stop the service and delete releases” ticked also removes its timers from the container. Egg only removes timers that it made.\n- **A job needs a release.** Egg refuses to add or change a job before the project has been deployed once. Deploy the project, then add the job.\n\n## Actions on a job\n\n| Control | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Run now** | Starts the job once, straight away, without waiting for it to finish. The card says “Started NAME. Open its output to see what it printed.” It works on a paused job too. |\n| **Pause** | Stops the timer, so the job no longer runs on its own. The row reads “Paused”. The button changes to Resume. |\n| **Resume** | Arms the timer again. |\n| **Edit** | The pencil. Opens the name, schedule and command in the row. Save puts the change on your container, and Cancel or the Escape key closes it without saving. |\n| **Output** | The terminal icon. Shows what the job printed on its last runs, up to the latest 120 lines, or “It has not printed anything yet.” |\n| **Remove** | The bin. Asks “Remove NAME?” and, on Remove, stops the job and deletes it from your container. |\n\n## Dashboard routes\n\nThe card uses these routes, which answer only to your signed-in browser, so they are not part of the public `/api/v1` API and an access token cannot call them. `:id` is the project id and `:jobId` the job id.\n\n| Route | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/projects/:id/jobs` | Lists the jobs. With ?status=1 it also reads each job’s last result from your container. |\n| `POST /api/projects/:id/jobs` | Adds a job. The body holds name, schedule and command, and may hold enabled. Answers 201. |\n| `PATCH /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId` | Changes any of name, schedule, command and enabled. |\n| `DELETE /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId` | Removes the job from your container and from Egg. |\n| `POST /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId/run` | Starts the job once. Answers 202. |\n| `GET /api/projects/:id/jobs/:jobId/logs` | Returns the job’s recent output as a list of lines. |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nCron reads a schedule with both a day of the month and a day of the week as “either”, while systemd reads it as “both”, so Egg refuses the mix instead of guessing which one you meant. The 5 minute floor, the low priority and the 30 minute stop exist because your container is small and shared with your app, so a job does not starve it. Egg skips missed runs, so a restart does not trigger a burst of old work.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Jobs per project | 5 | 409 “A project can have 5 jobs. Remove one to add another.” In the code: server/scheduled-jobs.ts MAX_JOBS_PER_PROJECT |\n| Time between runs | At least 5 minutes | The schedule is refused with “Jobs run at most once every 5 minutes so they do not starve your app.” In the code: lib/cron-schedule.ts MIN_GAP_MINUTES |\n| Run time | 30 minutes | systemd stops the run and the last result reads Failed. In the code: server/deploy/jobs-remote.ts JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS |\n| Name | 1 to 60 characters | The form says which characters are allowed. In the code: server/deploy/jobs-remote.ts JOB_NAME |\n| Command | 500 characters, one line | “Keep the command under 500 characters. Put longer scripts in a file in your repository.” In the code: server/scheduled-jobs.ts jobInput |\n| Schedule | 100 characters | “That schedule is too long.” In the code: lib/cron-schedule.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `409 “Deploy the project once first. A job runs inside the current release, and there is none yet.”`\n\n  Deploy the project, then add the job.\n- `“Set either the day of the month or the day of the week, not both.”`\n\n  Leave one of the two fields as `*`.\n- `“That would run every N minutes.”`\n\n  Choose a schedule with at least 5 minutes between runs.\n- `409 “A project can have 5 jobs. Remove one to add another.”`\n\n  Remove a job you no longer need.\n- `409 “A service with that name already exists on your container and was not made by Egg, so it was left alone.”`\n\n  Egg never replaces a unit it did not write. Remove or rename the unit on your container yourself.\n- `“Not on your container. Pause and resume it to put it back.”`\n\n  The timer is gone from the container, for example after someone removed it. Pause the job and press Resume to install it again. If Run now answers “That job is not on your container any more”, save the job again.\n- `504 “Your container did not answer in time.” or 502 “Could not reach your container.”`\n\n  The container may be stopped or starting. Try again in a minute. The list still shows what Egg saved.\n- The job shows Failed (exit N)\n\n  Open the output to read what it printed. Run the same command in the Terminal from the current release folder to see the error.\n\n## Example: Clean up old uploads every night\n\n### Given\n\nSam’s `recipe-bot` is deployed and has a script `scripts/cleanup.js` that deletes old uploads.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Fill in the form\n\n   Sam types the name `Nightly clean-up`, picks “Every day at 03:00” from Common schedules and types `node scripts/cleanup.js`.\n2. Step 2: Add the job\n\n   Sam presses Add job and sees “Added. It is armed on your container now.”\n3. Step 3: Try it\n\n   Sam presses Run now, waits a moment, then opens the output.\n\n### Result\n\nThe row reads “Every day at 03:00 UTC” with the next run time, and the output shows what the script printed. After a deploy, the next night’s run uses the new code.\n\n**Related:** [Deploy hooks](/docs/hooks), [Projects and runtimes](/docs/projects), [Limits](/docs/limits).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 24 of 40\n\n# Notifications\n\nMessages to a Discord or Slack channel from an incoming webhook, when a deployment finishes or an alert fires.\n\nA notification tells you a deployment finished, so nobody has to watch the dashboard. Each project has one webhook address, and every finished deployment, Ready or Error, can post a short message there, sent by Egg from the Egg machine to Discord or Slack. Nothing runs on your container. You need an incoming webhook URL from Discord or Slack, which also carries the alerts described under Monitoring and uptime.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Make the webhook\n\n   In Discord, make a webhook for a channel, or in Slack, make an incoming webhook, then copy its URL.\n2. Step 2: Save it\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Notifications**. Paste the **Webhook URL**, choose **Send**(Every deployment, or Failures only) and press **Save**, so the page says “Saved. Send a test message to check it.” The Save button stays off while the URL box is empty, so to change only **Send** later, paste the URL again.\n3. Step 3: Send a test\n\n   Press **Send test message**, so Egg posts “Test from Egg for PROJECT” and the card says “Sent. Check the channel.” The button appears once you save a URL.\n4. Step 4: Remove it\n\n   Press **Remove** to delete the saved URL, so messages stop at once.\n\n- Egg accepts https only, with no port, query, fragment or credentials, so Discord hosts are `discord.com`, `discordapp.com` and both with a `ptb.` or `canary.` prefix, on a path `/api/webhooks/ID/TOKEN` where ID is digits. Slack is `hooks.slack.com` on a path `/services/X/Y/Z`.\n- The URL is stored encrypted and never shown again. The card says “Sending to Discord for every finished deployment. The URL is hidden. Paste a new one to replace it.” (or “for failed deployments only”, or “Slack”). Saving a new URL replaces the old one.\n- A message reads “PROJECT is live in production” (or “a preview”) with the branch, short commit, the commit message in quotes up to 120 characters and the build time, or “PROJECT failed to deploy to production” (or “a preview”) with the branch, short commit and the error, up to 200 characters. The last line is a link to the deployment. Discord messages are sent so that they cannot ping anyone.\n- Failures only hides the “is live” messages. Canceled deployments never send. A failed post is only logged and never changes the deployment.\n- The Alerts card on the same page can also turn off “Deployment results”, and pause every alert for a while. Both stop these messages too. The deployment still appears in the alert history.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg accepts only Discord and Slack addresses of the exact shape those services use, and refuses redirects, so a saved address cannot send Egg’s request somewhere else, and a failed post is dropped rather than retried, because a broken channel must never hold up or fail a deployment.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Webhook per project | One | Saving a new URL replaces it. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| URL length | 500 characters | The server refuses it. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| Time to post | 5 seconds | The post is dropped and logged; redirects are refused. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| Commit message in a message | 120 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n| Error text in a message | 200 characters | The rest is cut off. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“That is not a valid URL.”`\n\n  Paste the full address, starting with https://.\n- `“Use the plain https webhook URL from Discord or Slack.”`\n\n  Remove any port, query string, or fragment from the URL.\n- `“Only Discord and Slack incoming webhook URLs are supported.”`\n\n  Check the host and path against the list above.\n- `“Add a webhook URL first.”`\n\n  Save a URL before pressing Send test message.\n- `“Discord answered STATUS.” or “Slack answered STATUS.”`\n\n  The service refused the post. The webhook may be deleted or the channel gone. Make a new webhook.\n- `“Could not save”, “Could not send” or “Could not remove”`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try again.\n- Messages stop arriving\n\n  Check the Alerts card: the “Deployment results” channel and an active pause also stop deployment messages. The events still appear in the history. Then check Send is not set to Failures only.\n\n## Example: Hear about failures in Discord\n\n### Given\n\nSam has a private Discord server with a `#bot-deploys` channel.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Make a webhook\n\n   Sam makes a webhook in the `#bot-deploys` channel.\n2. Step 2: Save it\n\n   Sam pastes it into Notifications, chooses Failures only and presses Save.\n3. Step 3: Send a test\n\n   Sam presses Send test message.\n\n### Result\n\nThe channel shows “Test from Egg for recipe-bot”. From now on it only hears about failed deployments, and about alerts if those are on.\n\n## Signed webhooks for your own code\n\nDiscord and Slack are for people, so to tell a program, use **Webhooks** in Account. Egg sends a signed JSON message to an https address when a deployment starts, succeeds or fails, covering every project on your account.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Add the address\n\n   Open Account, find **Webhooks**, paste the address under **Add a webhook address** and press **Add webhook**, so the button reads “Adding…” while it works.\n2. Step 2: Copy the secret\n\n   The card shows the signing secret once, starting with `whsec_`, under “Signing secret for HOST”, so copy it before you press **I have copied it**. Egg cannot show it again.\n3. Step 3: Send a test\n\n   Press **Send test** on the new row. The toast says “Test message delivered”, and the row lists the delivery under **Recent deliveries**. A test is one attempt, with no retries, and carries `\"test\": true`.\n4. Step 4: Check the signature in your code\n\n   Every request has an `X-Egg-Signature` header of the form `t=TIME,v1=HEX`, so the value is an HMAC-SHA256 of `TIME.BODY` keyed with your secret. Refuse a request that does not match, or whose time is more than 300 seconds from yours.\n\n   Node.js\n\n   ```\n   import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from \"node:crypto\";\n\n   export function isFromEgg(secret, header, body) {\n     const m = /^t=(\\d+),v1=([0-9a-f]{64})$/.exec(header ?? \"\");\n     if (!m || Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - Number(m[1])) > 300) return false;\n     const want = createHmac(\"sha256\", secret).update(m[1] + \".\" + body).digest();\n     return timingSafeEqual(want, Buffer.from(m[2], \"hex\"));\n   }\n   ```\n5. Step 5: Pause, replace or delete\n\n   **Pause** (then **Resume**) stops messages without deleting the webhook. **New secret** opens “Replace the secret?”, and **Replace secret** makes the old secret stop working at once and shows the new one once. **Delete** removes the webhook and its history.\n\nThe body is JSON with `event` (`deployment.started`, `deployment.succeeded`, `deployment.failed` or `webhook.test`), `project` with its id and slug, `deployment` with its id, branch, short commit and state, and `time` as an ISO date, so it never carries variables, logs, commit messages, errors or links. The headers `X-Egg-Event` and `X-Egg-Delivery` name the event and this delivery. A canceled deployment sends nothing, and the started message goes out when the build begins.\n\n### Limits\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Webhooks per account | 10 | “You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.” In the code: server/webhooks.ts |\n| Address | https, standard port, a full domain name, no credentials, no #, 500 characters at most | The form says what to change, for example “The URL must start with https://.” or “Use a domain name, not an IP address.” In the code: server/webhook-net.ts |\n| Where it can point | A public address | A private or internal address is refused when the message is sent, as “points at a private address”. In the code: server/webhook-net.ts |\n| Time to answer | 5 seconds | The attempt counts as failed with “timed out after 5 seconds”. Redirects are not followed. In the code: server/webhook-net.ts |\n| Attempts | 3, with pauses of 2 and 10 seconds | A failed delivery is listed with a short reason, such as “receiver answered 500” or “could not connect”. In the code: server/webhooks.ts |\n| Signature age | 300 seconds | Your code should refuse an older timestamp. In the code: server/webhook-sign.ts |\n| Delivery history | 30 days | Older rows are removed. In the code: server/webhooks.ts |\n\n**Related:** [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Deploy hooks](/docs/hooks), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 25 of 40\n\n# Access tokens and the API\n\nMake a token, give it only the permissions you need, and call Egg’s API from a script or an AI agent.\n\nYou make tokens on the Developer page. A token is a bearer secret that starts with `egg_`; send it in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. You call the JSON API under `/api/v1`, you point an agent at `/api/mcp`, and you read the descriptions at `/api/v1/openapi.json` and `/api/v1/agent-spec.json`. The agent spec is a short list of operations built from the same document as the OpenAPI reference, so the two cannot disagree on names. Your token can do nothing its permissions do not name (the API and its code call them scopes), and a few things it can never do at all. Egg runs every call on Egg’s machine. The calls marked “Container session” in the table below also open an SSH connection to your container, so they use a little of its CPU and Egg limits them to 10 a minute for each token.\n\nKeep your token out of public code\n\nAnyone who reads a token you publish can act as you until you revoke it.\n\n## Making a token\n\n### Make a token\n\n1. Step 1: Open Developer\n\n   Open Developer and press Create token. The New token dialog opens.\n\n   In the dashboard: Developerthen Access tokensthen Create token\n2. Step 2: Name it\n\n   Type 1 to 60 characters. Pick a name you will recognise later: Activity shows it for everything the token does.\n3. Step 3: Tick Permissions\n\n   Tick only what you need. Read only ticks every read permission. Deploy ticks projects:read, deployments:read, deployments:write and logs:read.\n4. Step 4: Choose Expires after\n\n   Pick 7, 30, 90 or 365 days; 90 is the default. If you tick a terminal or file permission, you can pick only 7 days.\n5. Step 5: Copy the secret\n\n   Copy it when the dialog says “Copy it now. It is shown once.” Keep it in a secret store, not in code.\n\nCopy the secret before you close the dialog\n\nEgg stores only a hash of it, so no one can show you the secret again.\n\nThe list groups tokens as Active, Expired and Revoked, and flags an active token that ends within 7 days. Each shows when it was last used, at most once an hour, or “Never”. Each active token has Edit and Revoke. Edit changes the name, the permissions and the expiry, counted from now, and takes effect on the next request. Revoke asks you to confirm with “Revoke token”: anything using the token stops working at once and any terminal it opened closes. A revoked token is final. Dropping the deploy permission warns that deploys will stop working for anything that uses the token.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe secret is shown once because only its SHA-256 is stored, so it cannot be shown again, even to Egg. An expired, revoked or unknown token is rejected with the same message so nobody can use the API to find out which tokens once existed. Names matter because Activity shows them: a log that says “a token” cannot tell you which script did it.\n\n## What a token can do\n\n| Permission | What it allows | Calls |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `projects:read` | Read projects, their settings and status page. | list_projects, get_project, get_status_page |\n| `projects:write` | Create, change and delete projects and services, including build and start commands. | create_project, update_project, delete_project, delete_service, deploy_from_github_url |\n| `deployments:read` | Read deployments, build output and deployment metrics. | list_deployments, get_deployment, get_deployment_logs, get_deployment_metrics |\n| `deployments:write` | Deploy, redeploy, cancel and roll back, and replace or revoke the deploy hook. | deploy_branch, deploy_from_github_url, redeploy, cancel_deployment, rollback_deployment, rotate_deploy_hook, revoke_deploy_hook |\n| `logs:read` | Read runtime logs and crash reports. | get_logs, list_crash_reports, get_crash_report |\n| `env:read` | List variable names, and read the values of variables marked non-secret. | list_env_vars, get_env_vars |\n| `env:write` | Set and remove variables, and change whether a variable is secret. | set_env_var, delete_env_var, set_env_var_secret |\n| `domains:read` | Read domains. | list_domains |\n| `domains:write` | Add, check and remove domains. | add_domain, verify_domain, remove_domain |\n| `notifications:read` | Read notification settings and send a test message. | get_notifications, test_notification |\n| `alerts:read` | Read alert settings and history. | get_alerts |\n| `analytics:read` | Read visitor analytics. | get_analytics |\n| `activity:read` | Read your activity log and a project’s change feed. | get_activity, get_changes |\n| `resources:read` | Read CPU, memory and disk, now and over time, and a project’s resource limits and usage. | get_server_usage, get_resource_history, get_project_resources, get_resource_quota |\n| `container:read` | List servers and inspect one project’s containers and health. Read only. | list_servers, get_container_inventory, list_project_containers, get_project_container, get_project_health |\n| `files:read` | List and read files in a project’s folder. | list_files, read_file, download_file |\n| `files:write` | Create, change, rename and delete files in a project’s shared/ folder. | write_file, make_folder, rename_file, delete_file |\n| `terminal:use` | Open and use a shell on the container. | terminal_status, terminal_open, terminal_send, terminal_read, terminal_resize, terminal_close |\n| `webhooks:read` | Read your webhooks and their recent deliveries, never their secrets. | GET /webhooks routes |\n| `webhooks:write` | Create, change, delete and test webhooks, and replace a webhook secret. | POST, PATCH, DELETE /webhooks routes |\n\nYou get write permissions for projects, deployments, variables, domains, files and webhooks, and `terminal:use` lets you run commands. Notifications, alerts, analytics, resources, activity, the container and logs are read only, and no permission changes notification or alert settings: those stay in the browser. You can call `test_notification` with only `notifications:read`, but it posts a real message to your channel. Send it only when you ask for it, and tell an agent to do the same.\n\n## Terminal, files and the 7-day rule\n\nYou switch terminal and file changes on for a server before a token can use them, and Egg asks you to sign in with Hack Club again when you do. A token reaches them only while that switch is on. A token holding `terminal:use` or `files:write` expires within 7 days, whatever length you picked, and the form says so as you tick the permission. Send no expiry and it gets 7 days. Adding either permission to a longer-lived token is refused unless the same edit sets the expiry to 7 days.\n\nSome things stay with you in the browser. You cannot use a token to remove leftovers, change the container permissions, manage your account or sessions, or create other tokens. Files in `current/` stay read only, and a release’s `.env` is never returned.\n\nA leaked terminal token is short lived\n\nWhoever holds it can run commands on your container until its 7 days end or you switch the terminal off.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA terminal can run any command and a file change can alter the code your app runs, so a leaked token with either is the worst case. Capping its life at 7 days, and tying it to a switch you turned on with a recent Hack Club sign-in, keeps that case short. The token does not sign in again: it can use the terminal until its 7 days end or you switch the terminal off.\n\n## The API and agents\n\nYou call the REST API under `/api/v1` with your token as a bearer header. Read the full reference at `/api/v1/openapi.json`, or fetch the shorter `/api/v1/agent-spec.json` when you want only the operation ids, methods, paths and parameters. Start with `/api/v1/me`: it needs no permission, so any valid token can call it. It returns your token’s name, permissions and expiry and how many servers and projects you have, and never an email address.\n\n- **Requests.** Send JSON. If a body field the call does not know is present, Egg refuses it with an “Unknown field” message that lists the allowed ones, so a typo cannot silently do nothing. A value in the path wins over a body field of the same name.\n- **Answers.** You get `200`, or `201` for something new, or `202` for a deployment that is queued and will finish later. An error is always `{ \"error\": { \"code\", \"message\" } }`. Authenticated answers are never cached.\n- **Secrets are write-only.** Variable values marked secret, notification URLs and token secrets are never returned. A variable value comes back only from `get_env_vars`, and only when it is marked non-secret. A deploy hook URL comes back once, from the call that makes it.\n- **Cross-origin use.** The API allows any origin and never reads cookies, so a page on another site can call it only with a token it already holds. Keep your token out of a public page.\n\nYou point an agent at `/api/mcp` instead. It speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 over POST: one message per request, no sessions and no batches. It takes the same bearer token and never reads cookies. Each tool is one API call, with the same scopes, limits and rules, and no way to reach leftover removal, permission switches, your account or token management. It answers `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` and `tools/call`. A tool that fails comes back as a result with `isError` set and text that starts with the error code. A `GET` or `DELETE` to the address gets a 405.\n\n### Connect an agent\n\n1. Step 1: Make a token\n\n   Give your agent only the scopes it needs, and keep the secret in a store it can read. Use the steps above if you have not made a token yet.\n2. Step 2: Point the client at the agent endpoint\n\n   Use an HTTP MCP client with one line of configuration: the address, the token in an Authorization header, and no other settings.\n\n   MCP client config\n\n   ```\n   {\n     \"mcpServers\": {\n       \"egg\": {\n         \"type\": \"http\",\n         \"url\": \"https://your-egg/api/mcp\",\n         \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer egg_...\" }\n       }\n     }\n   }\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Call get_me first\n\n   It needs no permission. The answer tells your agent which scopes the token has and how many projects and servers you have, so it can plan its next calls.\n4. Step 4: Narrow the tool list if you want to\n\n   Add ?toolsets= or ?scope= to the address, as described below, to cut down what the agent sees. Leave both out to see every tool your token allows.\n5. Step 5: Use the compact server when context is tight\n\n   Point the client at /api/mcp/compact instead. It offers three tools instead of one per operation, with the same token, scopes and limits.\n\n### Narrow the tools your agent sees\n\nYou narrow the list with two query parameters, on `/api/mcp` and on `/api/mcp/compact`. Set `?toolsets=` to a comma-separated list such as `toolsets=core,projects,deployments`. The `core` group is always included, and leaving the parameter out shows every group. Set `?scope=read` to keep only tools that change nothing, or `?scope=deploy` to add deployment changes and nothing else. Your token’s own scopes still apply, and the server checks them again on every call: narrowing can only hide tools, never add ones your token could not already run.\n\nA mistyped toolset or scope is refused\n\nEgg answers 400 with the list of valid names instead of quietly showing a different set of tools.\n\n### Use the compact server\n\nYou use `/api/mcp/compact` when the client has a small context window. It answers the same `initialize`, `ping`, `tools/list` and `tools/call` methods but offers three tools: `search_api` finds operations by keyword, `describe_operation` returns one operation’s schema, and `call_api` runs it. A destructive operation does not run until you send its id again as `confirm`. Each result is capped at 24,000 characters.\n\nA destructive call waits for your confirmation\n\nWithout `confirm` set to the operation’s own id, the call changes nothing on Egg.\n\nYou see deploys, redeploys, rollbacks, cancels and file or terminal actions made with a token in [Activity](/docs/activity), named for the token that made them.\n\n| Tool | Method and path | Permission | Allowance |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `get_me` | `GET /me` | None | Reads |\n| `list_projects` | `GET /projects` | `projects:read` | Reads |\n| `get_project` | `GET /projects/{projectId}` | `projects:read` | Reads |\n| `create_project` | `POST /projects` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `update_project` | `PATCH /projects/{projectId}` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `delete_project` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `delete_service` | `DELETE /services/{serviceId}` | `projects:write` | Changes |\n| `get_status_page` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/status-page` | `projects:read` | Reads |\n| `list_deployments` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/deployments` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `deploy_branch` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/deployments` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `get_deployment` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `get_rollout` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}/rollout` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `get_deployment_logs` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}/logs` | `deployments:read` | Container session |\n| `get_deployment_metrics` | `GET /deployments/{deploymentId}/metrics` | `deployments:read` | Reads |\n| `deploy_from_github_url` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/deploy-from-github-url` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `redeploy` | `POST /deployments/{deploymentId}/redeploy` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `cancel_deployment` | `POST /deployments/{deploymentId}/cancel` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `rollback_deployment` | `POST /deployments/{deploymentId}/rollback` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `rotate_deploy_hook` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/hook/rotate` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `revoke_deploy_hook` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/hook` | `deployments:write` | Changes |\n| `get_logs` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/logs` | `logs:read` | Reads |\n| `list_crash_reports` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/crash-reports` | `logs:read` | Reads |\n| `get_crash_report` | `GET /crash-reports/{crashId}` | `logs:read` | Reads |\n| `list_env_vars` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/env` | `env:read` | Reads |\n| `get_env_vars` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/env/values` | `env:read` | Reads |\n| `set_env_var` | `PUT /projects/{projectId}/env/{key}` | `env:write` | Changes |\n| `set_env_var_secret` | `PATCH /projects/{projectId}/env/{key}/{target}/secret` | `env:write` | Changes |\n| `delete_env_var` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/env/{key}` | `env:write` | Changes |\n| `list_domains` | `GET /domains` | `domains:read` | Reads |\n| `add_domain` | `POST /domains` | `domains:write` | Changes |\n| `verify_domain` | `POST /domains/{domainId}/verify` | `domains:write` | Changes |\n| `remove_domain` | `DELETE /domains/{domainId}` | `domains:write` | Changes |\n| `get_notifications` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/notify` | `notifications:read` | Reads |\n| `test_notification` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/notify/test` | `notifications:read` | Changes |\n| `get_alerts` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/alerts` | `alerts:read` | Reads |\n| `get_analytics` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/analytics` | `analytics:read` | Reads |\n| `get_activity` | `GET /activity` | `activity:read` | Reads |\n| `get_changes` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/changes` | `activity:read` | Reads |\n| `get_server_usage` | `GET /servers/{serverId}/usage` | `resources:read` | Reads |\n| `get_resource_history` | `GET /servers/{serverId}/usage/history` | `resources:read` | Container session |\n| `get_project_resources` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/resources` | `resources:read` | Container session |\n| `get_resource_quota` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/resources/quota` | `resources:read` | Container session |\n| `list_servers` | `GET /servers` | `container:read` | Reads |\n| `get_container_inventory` | `GET /servers/{serverId}/inventory` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `list_project_containers` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/containers` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `get_project_container` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/containers/{containerId}` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `get_project_health` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/health` | `container:read` | Container session |\n| `list_files` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/files` | `files:read` | Container session |\n| `read_file` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/files/content` | `files:read` | Container session |\n| `download_file` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/files/download` | `files:read` | Container session |\n| `write_file` | `PUT /projects/{projectId}/files/content` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `make_folder` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/files/folders` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `rename_file` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/files/rename` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `delete_file` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/files` | `files:write` | Container session |\n| `terminal_status` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/terminal` | `terminal:use` | Reads |\n| `terminal_open` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/terminal` | `terminal:use` | Container session |\n| `terminal_send` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}/input` | `terminal:use` | Terminal input |\n| `terminal_read` | `GET /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}/output` | `terminal:use` | Reads |\n| `terminal_resize` | `POST /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}/resize` | `terminal:use` | Changes |\n| `terminal_close` | `DELETE /projects/{projectId}/terminal/{sessionId}` | `terminal:use` | Changes |\n\nYou reach webhooks as plain routes under `/api/v1/webhooks` rather than tools: `GET` and `POST` on `/webhooks`, `GET`, `PATCH` and `DELETE` on `/webhooks/{webhookId}`, and `POST` on `/webhooks/{webhookId}/rotate` and `/webhooks/{webhookId}/test`. To create one you send `url`, an optional `events` list of `deployment.started`, `deployment.succeeded` and `deployment.failed`, and an optional `projectId`. The answer carries the signing secret once. The body is 4 KB at most, and the test call uses the container-session allowance. Webhooks are not in the OpenAPI description or the agent tool list. How your receiver checks the `X-Egg-Signature` header, what a delivery carries and how failed deliveries are tried again are on the [Notifications](/docs/notifications) page.\n\n## Examples and the TypeScript client\n\nYou can start from the ready-made examples in the repository; each says which permissions it needs. Every example uses a placeholder like `egg_...`, so keep your real token in an environment variable or a secret store. The TypeScript client is one file with no dependencies: copy it into your project and call its typed methods.\n\n- [TypeScript client](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/web/src/lib/sdk/egg.ts). One file, no dependencies. Typed methods for projects, deployments, logs, domains, environment names, analytics, usage, alerts and the status page. Runs on Node 20 and in the browser. Depends on the method. Each one names its permission.\n- [Node script](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/node/tail-logs.mjs). Lists your projects and follows a build log until it finishes. Needs projects:read and deployments:read.\n- [Python script](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/python/tail_logs.py). The same, with only the standard library. Needs projects:read and deployments:read.\n- [Static dashboard](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/dashboard/index.html). One HTML file. Paste a token and see your projects and page views. The token stays in the page's memory and is never saved. Needs projects:read and analytics:read.\n- [GitHub Actions workflow](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/examples/github-actions/deploy-on-release.yml). Deploys when you publish a release, through a deploy hook. No token. It uses the project's deploy hook URL.\n- [MCP config](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/tree/main/examples/mcp). Connect Claude Desktop, Cursor or Claude Code to your projects. Whatever you want the agent to do. The first call, get_me, needs none.\n- [Egg command line](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/developers/cli.md) (the script is [egg-cli.mjs](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/web/scripts/egg-cli.mjs)) covers 153 commands for projects, deployments, environments, jobs, services, domains, notifications, observability, GitHub, organizations, context, and local Egg information. For example, run `egg whoami`, `egg projects list`, `egg deploy create <project>`, `egg deploy logs <deployment-id> --follow`, `egg env names <project>`, `egg domains list`, or `egg status`. Remote commands read `EGG_TOKEN` and `EGG_URL` from the environment and never print the token; offline context and information commands do not use a token. Destructive commands require exact confirmation, and a mismatch exits 2. Exit codes are 0 for done, 1 for other failures, 2 for wrong use, 3 for authentication, 4 for not found, 6 for rate limits, 7 for server errors, and 8 for timeouts. The CLI still lacks alert metric rules, status-page management, environment pull, several observability commands, Compose stack creation, and other operations listed in its command reference.\n\n### Common calls\n\n1. Step 1: Check the token\n\n   It needs no permission. The answer shows what your token may do and when it ends.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" https://your-egg/api/v1/me\n   ```\n2. Step 2: List projects and find an id\n\n   You need `projects:read`.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" https://your-egg/api/v1/projects\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Deploy a branch\n\n   You need `deployments:write`. Leave the body out to deploy the production branch; any other branch becomes a preview. The answer is 202 with the queued deployment. The build then runs on your container, and a production deploy restarts the app on your container.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"branch\":\"pricing-page\"}' \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/deployments\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Give each write a key\n\n   Send Idempotency-Key with a fresh value on every write. Use a new UUID for each action you want to happen once. The key belongs to your token, and Egg keeps the answer for 24 hours. A repeat with the same key and the same request returns the kept answer with Idempotent-Replayed: true. A different request with that key gets 422.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     -H \"Idempotency-Key: 7f1c2e6a-4b5d-4c8e-9a1f-3d2b0e5c7a90\" \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/deployments\n   ```\n5. Step 5: Watch it finish\n\n   You need `deployments:read`. A 202 means the deploy is queued, not live. Call the rollout endpoint with `wait=1`: Egg reads the state every 2 seconds for up to 60 seconds and returns early at ready, error or canceled. The answer says `done` and `waitedMs`. If `done` is false, call again.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     \"https://your-egg/api/v1/deployments/DEPLOYMENT_ID/rollout?wait=1\"\n   ```\n6. Step 6: Read why it failed\n\n   You need `deployments:read`. Pass the last line’s id as `after` to continue. It uses the container-session allowance of 10 a minute.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     \"https://your-egg/api/v1/deployments/DEPLOYMENT_ID/logs?after=0&limit=200\"\n   ```\n7. Step 7: Set a variable\n\n   You need `env:write`. `target` is `all`, `production` or `preview`. The value reaches the app at the next deploy. Setting a value never changes its secret flag, and the value comes back only from `get_env_vars`, when the variable is marked non-secret.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X PUT -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"value\":\"sk_test_123\",\"target\":\"production\"}' \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/projects/PROJECT_ID/env/STRIPE_KEY\n   ```\n8. Step 8: Roll back\n\n   You need `deployments:write`. Pass the id of an earlier production deployment whose release is still on your container. Nothing is rebuilt: the service restarts on that older release, so the app is unavailable for a moment.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n     https://your-egg/api/v1/deployments/DEPLOYMENT_ID/rollback\n   ```\n9. Step 9: Wait out a 429\n\n   A rate-limited call answers 429 with a Retry-After header. Wait that many seconds, then repeat the call.\n\n   Node.js\n\n   ```\n   async function call(path, init) {\n     const res = await fetch(\"https://your-egg/api/v1\" + path, {\n       ...init,\n       headers: { Authorization: \"Bearer \" + process.env.NEST_TOKEN, ...init?.headers },\n     });\n     if (res.status === 429) {\n       await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, Number(res.headers.get(\"retry-after\") ?? 5) * 1000));\n       return call(path, init);\n     }\n     const body = await res.json();\n     if (!res.ok) throw new Error(body.error.code + \": \" + body.error.message);\n     return body;\n   }\n   ```\n10. Step 10: Use the typed client\n\n    Copy the one file into your project. Each method names its permission and throws an error that carries the status, the code and Retry-After.\n\n    TypeScript\n\n    ```\n    import { Egg } from \"./egg\";\n\n    const nest = new Egg({ token: process.env.NEST_TOKEN!, baseUrl: \"https://your-egg\" });\n\n    const me = await nest.me();\n    console.log(me.token.scopes);\n\n    const { projects } = await nest.listProjects();\n    for (const p of projects) console.log(p.name, p.state);\n    ```\n11. Step 11: Call a tool over MCP\n\n    You send one JSON-RPC message per POST. Start with get_me, and add ?toolsets= or ?scope= to the address to narrow the tool list.\n\n    Terminal\n\n    ```\n    curl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n      -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n      -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"get_me\",\"arguments\":{}}}' \\\n      https://your-egg/api/mcp\n    ```\n\nEgg holds idempotency keys in memory\n\nA restart of Egg forgets them. Treat the key as protection against a lost connection, not as a long-term record.\n\n## Addresses for tools and agents\n\n| For | Address |\n| --- | --- |\n| API calls | `https://your-egg/api/v1` |\n| The full reference | `https://your-egg/api/v1/openapi.json` |\n| A short index for agents | `https://your-egg/api/v1/agent-spec.json` |\n| AI agents | `https://your-egg/api/mcp` |\n| Agents with a small context window | `https://your-egg/api/mcp/compact` |\n\nReplace `your-egg` with the address of your Egg. Every address takes the same token in an `Authorization: Bearer` header, and none of them reads cookies. The two description documents are public and hold no data. You add `?toolsets=` or `?scope=` to either agent address to narrow the tools; the client configuration is in the steps above.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reads | 120 a minute per token | 429 with a Retry-After header. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.read) |\n| Changes | 30 a minute | 429. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.write) |\n| Calls that open a session to your container (build logs, resource history, project resources, the inventory, opening a terminal, files) | 10 a minute | 429. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.ssh) |\n| Terminal input | 300 a minute | 429. In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (API_LIMITS.input) |\n| Invalid tokens | 60 a minute per address | 429 “Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens.” In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.badToken) |\n| Request body | 16 KB (4 MB for a file write, 64 KB for terminal input and for MCP, 4 KB for webhooks) | 413 “The request body is too large.” In the code: server/api-v1-kit.ts (DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES) server/api-v1-shell.ts app/api/mcp/route.ts server/webhooks-http.ts |\n| File write and download | 1 MB and 2 MB | 413 “The API writes files of up to 1 MB. Upload larger files in the dashboard.” In the code: server/api-v1-shell.ts |\n| Active tokens | 25 | 400 “You can have up to 25 active tokens. Revoke one first.” In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (MAX_TOKENS_PER_USER) |\n| Token life | 7, 30, 90 or 365 days; 7 with terminal or files:write | 400 naming the 7 day rule. In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (createTokenInput SHORT_LIVED_MAX_DAYS) |\n| List sizes | Deployments 1 to 100, runtime log lines 1 to 1000, build log lines 1 to 1000, activity 1 to 100, analytics 1 to 90 days, resource history 1 to 168 hours | 400 for a value outside the range. In the code: server/api-v1.ts server/api-v1-more.ts (query fields) |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Send an access token as an Authorization: Bearer egg_... header.`\n\n  401. Add the header. A cookie is never accepted here.\n- `That access token is not valid.`\n\n  401. Missing, revoked, expired and unknown tokens all look the same on purpose. Make a new one on the Developer page.\n- `This token does not have the <scope> permission.`\n\n  403. The message names it. Press Edit on the token on the Developer page and tick it. The change applies to the next request.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 60 tokens that did not check out from one address in a minute. Fix the token your script sends.\n- `Too many requests for this token. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header. Reads are 120 a minute, changes 30, container sessions 10, terminal input 300.\n- `No such item, or it belongs to someone else.`\n\n  404. Check the id. Another account’s items answer the same way.\n- `The request body is too large.`\n\n  413. 16 KB for most calls, 4 MB for a file write, 64 KB for MCP and 4 KB for webhooks.\n- `Request body must be JSON`\n\n  400. Send valid JSON, or no body.\n- `Request body must be a JSON object`\n\n  400. Send { … }, not a list or a bare value.\n- `Unknown field \"<name>\". Allowed: ...`\n\n  400. Unknown body fields are refused so a typo cannot silently do nothing. The message lists the allowed ones.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Pick 7 days, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Pick 7 days, or untick Use the terminal and Change files.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Set the expiry to 7 days in the same change, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Raised when you edit a token. Set Expires after to 7 days in the same edit.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `400` | The request was invalid. The message says which field. |\n| `401` | Missing or invalid token. The response has a WWW-Authenticate: Bearer header. |\n| `403` | The token lacks the permission, or the switch for terminal, files or leftovers is off. |\n| `404` | No such item, or it belongs to someone else. |\n| `405` | The agent endpoint takes POST only. |\n| `409` | The item is in a state that does not allow this. |\n| `413` | The request body or the file is too large. |\n| `429` | Too many requests. Wait the seconds in Retry-After. |\n| `502` | Your container could not be reached. |\n| `503` | Too many terminals are open on the service right now. |\n\n### Command line messages\n\n- `Set EGG_TOKEN to your personal access token (it starts with egg_).`\n\n  Exit 2. Export EGG_TOKEN with a token from the Developer page.\n- `Set EGG_URL to your Egg address, such as https://deploy.example.com.`\n\n  Exit 2. Export EGG_URL with the address of your Egg.\n- `Unknown command \"<name>\".`\n\n  Exit 2. Run egg --help for the list.\n- `Unknown option <name>.`\n\n  Exit 2. The options are --json, --follow, --branch and --help.\n- `egg <command> needs <argument>.`\n\n  Exit 2. Add the project slug or deployment id the command asks for.\n- `No project with the slug \"<slug>\". Run egg projects list to see yours.`\n\n  Exit 1. Check the slug against the list.\n- `Error: <message>`\n\n  Exit 1. The API refused the call. The message is the one from the tables above. With --json the error is printed as an object with status, code and message.\n\n## Example: List your projects with curl\n\n### Given\n\nA token with projects:read, kept in an environment variable.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Call the projects endpoint\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   export NEST_TOKEN=egg_...\n   curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" https://your-egg/api/v1/projects\n   ```\n\n### Result\n\nA JSON list of your projects. A token without the permission gets 403 naming it.\n\n## Example: Deploy from a script and wait\n\n### Given\n\nA token with projects:read, deployments:read and deployments:write, and a project whose id you found with the call above.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Start the deploy\n\n   POST to the project’s deployments endpoint with no body, and send a fresh Idempotency-Key so a lost connection cannot start a second deploy.\n2. Step 2: Watch the rollout\n\n   Call the rollout endpoint with wait=1. It reads the state every 2 seconds for up to 60 seconds and returns when the deploy is ready, error or canceled. Call again if done is false.\n3. Step 3: On error, read the log\n\n   Fetch the deployment’s logs and print the last lines.\n\n### Result\n\nThe POST answers 202 with the queued deployment. When the rollout says done and the state is ready, the release is live. Activity shows “Started a deployment of” with the token’s name.\n\n**Related resources:** [Security](/docs/security), [Limits](/docs/limits), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Notifications](/docs/notifications).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 26 of 40\n\n# Projects through the API\n\nCreate a project on a server connected to Egg, then deploy it through the API.\n\n## What it does\n\n`POST /api/v1/projects` creates a project record from a public HTTPS Git repository on a server you have connected to Egg. It does not build or deploy anything. The build and the app run on that server when you deploy.\n\nDeploy the production branch with `POST /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/deployments`. Change settings with `PATCH /api/v1/projects/{projectId}`. Creating and changing a project need `projects:write`. Deploying needs `deployments:write`.\n\nPushes do not deploy an API project\n\nA project made through the API has no GitHub App link. A push to its repository does nothing, and private repositories are not supported. Deploy with the API, or with a deploy hook.\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Field | Required | What to send |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `serverId` | Yes | The id of a server connected to Egg. List the ids with `GET /api/v1/servers`. |\n| `name` | Yes | A display name, up to 60 characters. |\n| `repoUrl` | For source projects | A public HTTPS Git repository URL on a supported host, such as https://github.com/owner/name.git. |\n| `runtime` | For source projects | One of node, python, static, command, go, rust, ruby, php or java. |\n| `slug` | No | Lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, up to 40. Defaults to the name. |\n| `productionBranch` | No | The branch that deploys to production. Defaults to main. |\n| `port` | No | The port the app listens on, from 1024 to 65535. Ports 20000 to 29999 are reserved for previews. No other project on the server may use the port. |\n\nThe request returns `409` when the slug or the port is already in use.\n\n**Deploy from a GitHub URL.** `POST /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/deploy-from-github-url` sets the project's repository and production branch, then queues a production deploy in one call. It needs `deployments:write` and `projects:write`. The body takes a github.com URL in the https, `.git` or `git@` form, with an optional `/tree/<branch>`. Egg uses that branch when present, or the repository's default branch otherwise. Credentials in the URL are never shown.\n\n**Delete a service.** `DELETE /api/v1/services/{serviceId}` needs `projects:write`. Its JSON body must include `confirmName`, exactly matching the service name. Without the optional `?removeFromServer=true` query, Egg deletes its project record and the app keeps running on the container, now unmanaged. With it, Egg also stops the service and removes its releases and previews; the shared data folder stays. The TypeScript client call is `deleteService(serviceId, confirmName)`; pass `{ removeFromServer: true }` as its third argument to remove those files too. In the CLI, run `egg services delete <service> --confirm <name> [--remove-from-server]`. The confirmation must exactly match the service name.\n\n## Example\n\nSet `EGG_URL` to your Egg address and `EGG_TOKEN` to a token with `projects:write`. Then run the two requests in order.\n\n1. **Create the project.** Replace `SERVER_ID` and the repository URL. The response contains the project record. Copy its id.\n2. **Deploy the production branch.** Replace `PROJECT_ID` with the id from step 1. The request needs `deployments:write`.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"serverId\": \"SERVER_ID\",\n    \"name\": \"My app\",\n    \"repoUrl\": \"https://github.com/OWNER/REPOSITORY.git\",\n    \"runtime\": \"node\",\n    \"port\": 3000\n  }'\n```\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/deployments\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\"\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Configuration through the API](/docs/api-configuration), [Monitoring and notifications API](/docs/api-monitoring), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 27 of 40\n\n# Configuration through the API\n\nSet project variables and deployment settings with the API.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe API lists variable names, adds or replaces variables and removes selected keys. Values are encrypted at rest. Each variable has a secret flag, on by default. Only a variable marked non-secret returns its value, from `GET /env/values`. Secret values are write-only: no endpoint returns them.\n\nEach variable has a target. `production` covers the production branch, `preview` covers branch previews and `all` covers both. A changed variable reaches the app on the next deploy.\n\nAll four operations use `/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/env`. The method decides what happens.\n\n| Method | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET` | Lists names and targets, one page at a time. Needs env:read. Pass limit, then the nextCursor from a response as cursor. |\n| `POST` | Adds or replaces the variables you send. Other names and targets stay as they are. Needs env:write. |\n| `PUT` | Replaces every variable and target on the project. Names you leave out are deleted. Needs env:write and confirmName. |\n| `DELETE` | Removes the named keys, for every target or for one target. Needs env:write and confirmName. |\n\n| Method and path | Scope | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/env/values` | `env:read` | Returns the value of each variable marked non-secret. Secret variables are left out. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/env/{key}/{target}/secret` | `env:write` | Sets the secret flag for one variable and target. Send secret: false to let get_env_vars return its value. Saving a value never changes the flag. |\n\n## Parameters\n\n- **projectId.** The project id from `GET /api/v1/projects`.\n- **envVars.** An array of objects with `key`, `value` and `target`. A key and target pair may appear once in a request.\n- **target.** `all`, `production` or `preview`.\n- **confirmName.** The exact project name. Required for `PUT` and `DELETE`, because those calls can remove variables. The name must match, or the call is refused.\n- **keys.** For `DELETE`, one to 100 variable names. Each name may appear once. Add `target` to remove only that target.\n\nA write response lists the key and target of each entry. It does not include the value. Send an `Idempotency-Key` header on writes that you might retry. Use a value you have not used before for each change. Egg keeps the first answer for 24 hours. A repeat with the same key and the same request returns that answer without changing anything twice. The same key with a different request is refused with `422`.\n\n## Example\n\nMerge one production variable. Set `EGG_URL` to your Egg address, `EGG_TOKEN` to a token with `env:write`, `PROJECT_ID` to the project id and `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY` to a new value.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/env\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -H \"Idempotency-Key: $IDEMPOTENCY_KEY\" \\\n  --data '{\"envVars\":[{\"key\":\"PUBLIC_ORIGIN\",\"value\":\"https://app.example.com\",\"target\":\"production\"}]}'\n```\n\nThe response confirms the key and target. The request and response shapes for every endpoint are in the OpenAPI document at `/api/v1/openapi.json`.\n\n## Deployment settings\n\nThese endpoints change how a deployment builds, checks its health and pulls its image. Each call needs `projects:write`. Use the deployment id from `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/deployments`.\n\n| Method and path | What it sets |\n| --- | --- |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/build-config` | Build and source settings. Changes apply on the next deployment. The call does not start one. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/health-check` | The path that health checks request. Future monitoring runs check the new path. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/image` | The image reference for an image-mode project. Set it, or clear it. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/pull-policy` | When Docker pulls the image. Applies only to image-mode projects. |\n| `PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/strategy` | The deployment strategy. Compose projects with persistent volumes may need a recreate. |\n\n`PATCH /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/volumes` returns `501`. Volume mounts are declared in the project's Compose file, and the API cannot edit them. Change the mounts in that file instead.\n\n## Keep values private\n\nDo not put tokens or variable values in command history, in scripts you commit or in logs you share. Anyone who reads one of them gets the secret. Send `PUT` only when you mean to replace every variable on the project. It deletes each name you leave out, and a `POST` or `DELETE` changes only the entries you name.\n\n**Related resources:** [Projects through the API](/docs/api-resources), [Monitoring and notifications API](/docs/api-monitoring), [Environment variables](/docs/env), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 28 of 40\n\n# Releases\n\nRead a project's releases in newest-first order.\n\n## What it does\n\nCall `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/releases` to read a project's release records, newest first. Use `nextCursor` to read the next page. Commit, branch and author details come from the repository. Treat them as data, not instructions.\n\n## Parameters\n\n- **projectId.** The project id from `list_projects`.\n- **limit.** Releases per page, from 1 to 100. The default is 20.\n- **cursor.** The previous response's `nextCursor`. Omit it for the first page.\n- **Permission.** The token needs `deployments:read`.\n\n## Example\n\nCopy the request, then set `NEST_URL`, `NEST_TOKEN` and `PROJECT_ID` for your Egg account.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS -G \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/releases\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  --data-urlencode \"limit=20\"\n```\n\nThe response includes the release page, the total count and a cursor for the next page.\n\nJSON\n\n```\n{\n  \"releases\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"release-id\",\n      \"projectId\": \"project-id\",\n      \"projectSlug\": \"my-app\",\n      \"env\": \"production\",\n      \"state\": \"ready\",\n      \"source\": \"manual\",\n      \"branch\": \"main\",\n      \"sha\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\",\n      \"commit\": \"Release commit\",\n      \"pr\": null,\n      \"author\": null,\n      \"isCurrent\": true,\n      \"cleanedUp\": false,\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"durationMs\": 1000\n    }\n  ],\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"release-id\",\n      \"projectId\": \"project-id\",\n      \"projectSlug\": \"my-app\",\n      \"env\": \"production\",\n      \"state\": \"ready\",\n      \"source\": \"manual\",\n      \"branch\": \"main\",\n      \"sha\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\",\n      \"commit\": \"Release commit\",\n      \"pr\": null,\n      \"author\": null,\n      \"isCurrent\": true,\n      \"cleanedUp\": false,\n      \"error\": null,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"durationMs\": 1000\n    }\n  ],\n  \"total\": 1,\n  \"nextCursor\": null\n}\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Reading deployment state](/docs/api-query), [Projects through the API](/docs/api-resources), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 29 of 40\n\n# Reading deployment state\n\nRead resource recommendations, deployment metrics and the named Compose volumes owned by a deployment.\n\n## What it does\n\n`analyze_deployment_resources` uses monitor samples for the deployment's project. The samples describe the server, so they are not isolated to one deployment. The recommendation does not change a resource limit.\n\n`get_deployment_volumes` lists named Docker volumes owned by a Compose deployment. A native deployment has no Docker volumes, and the endpoint returns `409`. Egg returns an environment variable value only through `get_env_vars`, and only for a variable marked non-secret. Use `list_env_vars` to read names and targets.\n\n`get_deployment_metrics` returns bucketed CPU and memory averages and restart counts for the deployment's project. The samples are per project, not per release.\n\n## Parameters\n\nBoth endpoints take one path parameter, `deploymentId`, which is the deployment's id.\n\n| Endpoint | Scope |\n| --- | --- |\n| `analyze_deployment_resources` | `resources:read` |\n| `get_deployment_volumes` | `deployments:read` |\n| `get_deployment_metrics` | `deployments:read` |\n\n## Examples\n\nSet `NEST_URL`, `NEST_TOKEN` and `DEPLOYMENT_ID`, then copy the command that matches your question.\n\nResource recommendation\n\n```\ncurl --fail-with-body \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/deployments/$DEPLOYMENT_ID/resource-analysis\"\n```\n\nCompose volumes\n\n```\ncurl --fail-with-body \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/deployments/$DEPLOYMENT_ID/deployment-volumes\"\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Releases](/docs/api-releases), [Monitoring and notifications API](/docs/api-monitoring), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 30 of 40\n\n# Monitoring and notifications API\n\nRead project containers and health, deployment logs, resource signals and visitor analytics. Manage signed notification channels and their delivery history.\n\n## What it does\n\nMonitoring endpoints return what Egg records for a project: container details, deployment health and output, the service journal, resource samples and counted page views.\n\nPage views are not every HTTP request. Egg does not collect a web server access log, so `/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/request-stats` counts recorded page views only. `/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/attack-signals` lists repeated client-side error groups. Treat them as leads to check, not proof of an attack.\n\nA notification channel sends signed deployment events to a public HTTPS address you choose. Each channel has an event filter. The signing secret comes back once, from the call that creates the channel. No later read returns it, so keep a copy. Each delivery records its event, status (`pending`, `success` or `failed`), attempts and last error code. It does not store the response body.\n\n## Endpoints and scopes\n\n| Endpoint | Scope | What it returns |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/logs/search` | `logs:read` | Service journal lines, filtered by text, level (info, warn or error) and a time range of 1h, 24h or 7d, with a page limit. |\n| `GET /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/build-logs` | `deployments:read` | get_build_logs: build output for one deployment, one page at a time. |\n| `GET /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/logs` | `deployments:read` | get_deployment_logs: build and deploy output for one deployment, oldest first and paged. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/containers` | `container:read` | Production Docker containers for one project, including Compose services. Native systemd projects return an empty list. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/containers/{containerId}` | `container:read` | Safe details for one container in that project's set, including image and creation time. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/health` | `container:read` | The project's existing cached systemd and HTTP health check. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/request-stats` | `analytics:read` | Recorded page views, visitors, top pages and referrers for 1 to 90 days. Defaults to 30. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/anomalies` | `analytics:read` | Client-error and resource anomalies for 1 to 90 days. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/attack-signals` | `analytics:read` | Repeated client-side error groups to investigate. |\n| `GET /api/v1/notification-channels` | `webhooks:read` | Each channel with its receiver host and event filter. Signing secrets are never returned. |\n| `GET /api/v1/notification-channels/{channelId}/deliveries` | `webhooks:read` | Recent sends for one channel, 1 to 100 at a time. Defaults to 20. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/crash-reports` | `logs:read` | Crash reports for the project. Each keeps up to 200 journal lines from the release that was live, with known secrets masked. |\n| `GET /api/v1/crash-reports/{crashId}` | `logs:read` | One crash report, with the same journal lines. |\n| `GET /api/v1/deployments/{deploymentId}/metrics` | `deployments:read` | Bucketed CPU and memory averages and restart counts for the deployment’s project. Samples are per project, not per release. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/resources/quota` | `resources:read` | Limits and current usage. A value Egg has not measured is null, never zero. |\n| `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/changes` | `activity:read` | The project’s change feed, newest first. Environment variable key names appear in deletion and replacement summaries; values never appear. |\n\nThe container endpoints are read only and project scoped. `restartCount` uses systemd's restart counter for the main container and Docker's counter for Compose auxiliaries; `status.restarts` on the health response is systemd's count. Health reuses the existing cached systemd and HTTP check and returns a `cached` flag. These endpoints do not offer Docker exec into containers or per-container log streaming. Use `get_deployment_logs` for build and deploy output. They do not provide a cluster-wide container listing.\n\nIn `get_changes`, an item can include `deploymentId` for API deploys (including a GitHub URL deploy), redeploys, rollbacks, cancellations, project restarts, deployment lifecycle actions and preview deletions when the id belongs to this project. A project-linked domain removal appears in that project's feed. Deletions and replacements show environment variable key names, including for shared environment sets; values never appear.\n\nEach request needs the scope in its row. Use `channelId` from the channel list, and `deliveryId` from the deliveries list. Creating a channel needs `webhooks:write`. The full list of parameters is in the OpenAPI document at `/api/v1/openapi.json`.\n\n## Example\n\nSet `EGG_URL` to your Egg address, `EGG_TOKEN` to a token with `analytics:read` and `PROJECT_ID` to the project id. List the last 30 days of recorded page views:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/request-stats?days=30\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\"\n```\n\nList notification channels with `webhooks:read`. The response shows each receiver and its event filter, and no signing secrets:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -sS \"$EGG_URL/api/v1/notification-channels\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $EGG_TOKEN\"\n```\n\n**Related resources:** [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Logs](/docs/logs), [Reading deployment state](/docs/api-query), [Agent safety rules](/docs/api-agent-safety).\n\n\n---\n\nAutomate\n\nPage 31 of 40\n\n# Agent safety rules\n\nFind an operation, read its inputs, and call scheduled-job or project actions safely.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe compact MCP endpoint at `/api/mcp/compact` offers three tools. `search_api` finds operations the token can use. `describe_operation` returns the route, scope and input schema. `call_api` runs one operation.\n\nThe full MCP endpoint at `/api/mcp` exposes each operation as its own tool. Both endpoints apply the token's scopes and any toolset filter in the URL, such as `?toolsets=core`. Batch requests are refused, so send one message per request.\n\nSet `NEST_URL` to your Egg address and `NEST_TOKEN` to the token. Then run a search:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -X POST \"$NEST_URL/api/mcp/compact\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n    \"params\": {\n      \"name\": \"search_api\",\n      \"arguments\": { \"query\": \"scheduled jobs\", \"limit\": 5 }\n    }\n  }'\n```\n\n## Parameters\n\n- `search_api`: send `query`. Add `limit` from 1 to 50; the default is 10. Results include only operations this token can run in its toolset.\n- `describe_operation`: send an `operationId` returned by search. The result lists the fields a call needs.\n- `call_api`: send `operationId` and an `arguments` object. Some routes also require an exact resource name in their arguments.\n\n## Scheduled jobs\n\nScheduled jobs use `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/jobs` to list jobs and the same path with `POST` to create one. Here is a list request:\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -X GET \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"$NEST_URL/api/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/jobs\"\n```\n\nThe job tools are `list_jobs`, `create_job`, `get_job`, `update_job`, `delete_job`, `list_job_runs`, `get_job_run`, `get_job_run_logs`, `run_job`, `cancel_job_run` and `replace_job_steps`.\n\nEgg stores one command per job and keeps only its latest status, not a run history. A job accepts exactly one step. The run id is the job's own id. Cancel pauses the job's timer. It does not stop a run that has already started.\n\n## Destructive actions\n\nAsk the user before you delete or change a live resource. A permanent delete cannot be taken back. Through `call_api`, Egg refuses an operation flagged as destructive until `confirm` repeats its operation ID exactly. Examples include `delete_project`, `delete_service`, `delete_job`, `run_job` and `cancel_job_run`. Project deletion also needs `confirmName` set to the exact project name:\n\nJSON\n\n```\n{\n  \"operationId\": \"delete_project\",\n  \"confirm\": \"delete_project\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"projectId\": \"PROJECT_ID\",\n    \"confirmName\": \"Recipe bot\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nDeleting a service with `delete_service` also needs `confirmName`, exactly matching the service name. The optional `removeFromServer` flag stops the service and removes its releases and previews from the container; the shared data folder stays.\n\nDeleting a notification channel needs `confirmName` set to the exact channel host, such as `hooks.example.com`. Egg records the channel delete in the audit log with the token that made it.\n\nReplacing or deleting environment variables with `set_env_vars` or `delete_env_vars` needs `confirmName` set to the exact project name. Secret values are write-only, so an agent cannot read one back. Only a variable marked non-secret comes back, from `get_env_vars`.\n\n**Related resources:** [The API and agents](/docs/api#api-mcp), [What a token can do](/docs/api#api-scopes), [Making a token](/docs/api#api-tokens).\n\n\n---\n\nWatch\n\nPage 32 of 40\n\n# Monitoring and uptime\n\nWhat your app is doing now, and what it has been doing.\n\nYou need a deployed project and a running worker, so Egg can check your app. You also need a webhook under Notifications for messages when something goes wrong.\n\n## The Service card\n\nOnce something is live, the Service card appears on your project page, showing Status (Running, Starting, Crashed, Stopped, Not deployed or Unknown), Uptime, Memory, Restarts and Health check (“HTTP CODE in MS ms”, “No answer” or “Not checked”). It reads systemd on your container and sends one real request to the health path with a 3 second limit.\n\n- It looks again every 30 seconds while your tab is visible, so Egg reuses a reading that is under 30 seconds old and opening the page does not always open a new connection to your container. A line under the card says when the reading was taken: “Checked” with the time, “Readings are reused for 30 seconds; Check now asks the container again.”\n- **Check now** always asks your container, whatever the age of the last reading.\n- **Restart** restarts the production service after you confirm “Restart the service?”. It builds nothing.\n\nWhen Egg sees a production service fail, it keeps up to 200 lines of its journal against the current release, once per crash, with known secrets masked. A token with `logs:read` lists them with `list_crash_reports` and reads one with `get_crash_report`.\n\n## Resource use\n\nThe Resource use card on your project page shows this one service’s memory and CPU over the last 24 hours, so it says “Collecting data.” until it has two readings. CPU is a share of one core, so it can pass 100. Egg leaves a point blank when there is no earlier reading, the gap is over 30 minutes or the counter went backwards after a restart. The Resources page in the sidebar shows load, memory and disk for the whole server over 24 hours or 7 days, with a table of each project’s memory, CPU and restarts.\n\nThe worker samples every connected server every 5 minutes by default and keeps 7 days, so it writes project samples only while the service is running.\n\nThrough the API, `get_deployment_metrics` (`deployments:read`) returns bucketed CPU and memory averages and restart counts for the deployment’s project. The samples are per project, not per release. `get_resource_quota` (`resources:read`) returns the limits and current usage of the project. A value Egg has not measured is`null`, never zero.\n\n## Uptime checks and alerts\n\nEvery 5 minutes the worker opens one SSH connection to each connected server, so for each project with a live production release and no deployment in progress it runs a short read-only command on your container. The command asks systemd about the service and makes one request to the app on 127.0.0.1, so a project is up when its service runs and the health path answers with a status from 1 to 499. Without `curl` on your container, running is enough, and a server it cannot reach is skipped rather than reported as down.\n\nEgg declares a project down after a number of failed checks in a row, 2 by default, so you get one message when it goes down and one when it answers again. The project header shows “Answering”, “Not answering since TIME” or “Not checked yet”, and links to the alert history. Each project sets its own alerts.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Open the Alerts card\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Alerts**. For any message to be sent, you must save a webhook in the Notifications card.\n2. Step 2: Set “When to warn”\n\n   Set **Disk full (%)**, **Memory used (%)**, **CPU load per core (%)** and **Failed checks before down**, plus **Slow response limit (ms)** if you want one.\n3. Step 3: Choose the channels\n\n   Under **Send to the webhook**, tick any of The app stops answering, The app answers again, Disk, memory or CPU warnings and Deployment results. All four are on by default.\n4. Step 4: Save\n\n   Press **Save** at the bottom of the card, so a message says “Alert settings saved”.\n5. Step 5: Pause when you need quiet\n\n   Under **Pause all alerts**, choose “For 1 hour”, “For 24 hours” or “Until I resume” and press **Pause**, so it takes effect at once without Save and the card shows “Alerts are paused”. Press **Resume alerts** to end it, because while paused nothing is sent but the history still records the event with the badge “Not sent, paused”.\n\n| Setting | Range | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Disk full (%) | Whole number, 1 to 100 | 90 |\n| Memory used (%) | Whole number, 1 to 100 | 90 |\n| CPU load per core (%) | Whole number, 1 to 100. The 1 minute load divided by the core count. | 90 |\n| Failed checks before down | Whole number, 1 to 10 | 2 |\n| Slow response limit (ms) | Empty (off), or 50 to 60000. A slower answer counts as a failed check. | Off |\n\n- A resource alert fires once when a value reaches its limit and once more when it falls back below it, so nothing is sent in between. Disk, memory and CPU are for the whole server, checked against each project’s own limits.\n- History keeps the newest 50 alerts for 90 days, with the badges “Still happening”, “Resolved” and “Not sent, paused”. Open alerts are kept past 90 days.\n- The “Needs attention” card on the Projects page lists apps not answering, projects whose latest deployment failed, a disk, memory or CPU load at or over the lowest limit you set (90% by default), and projects with alerts paused. It was called the Alerts card in older docs.\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Check interval | 5 minutes by default | A very short outage can be missed. |\n| Failed checks before down | 1 to 10, default 2 | You pick the number from a list, so there is no message. |\n| Resource limits | 1 to 100 each, default 90 | “The disk limit must be a whole number from 1 to 100.” or “The disk limit must be from 1 to 100.” (and memory, CPU) |\n| Slow response limit | 50 to 60000 ms, or empty | “The response time limit must be a whole number from 50 to 60000.” or “The response time limit must be from 50 to 60000.” |\n| Samples kept | 7 days | Older readings are deleted; the charts show 24 hours or 7 days. |\n| Service card refresh | Every 30 seconds, with a 20 second read. A reading under 30 seconds old is reused; Check now is never reused | “Could not read the service from your container.” |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“The service is STATE.” / “The service is running but nothing answered on its health path.”`\n\n  The down message says why. Fix the app, or the health path in Settings, Build and Run.\n- `“The health path took MS ms to answer, over your limit of N ms.”`\n\n  Raise the Slow response limit, or make the page faster.\n- `“The health path answered HTTP CODE.”`\n\n  A 500 or above counts as down. A 404 does not, because the check treats 1 to 499 as healthy.\n- `“Could not load alert settings”`\n\n  Press **Try again** on the Alerts card.\n- No messages at all\n\n  Check that a webhook is saved, the channel is ticked, alerts are not paused, and the app has failed as many checks in a row as your setting.\n\n### Example: Get warned before memory runs out\n\n#### Given\n\n`recipe-bot` sometimes runs out of memory on Sam’s container. Sam has a webhook saved.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Set the limits\n\n   Sam sets Memory used (%) to `80` and Failed checks before down to `3`.\n2. Step 2: Keep the channels\n\n   Sam leaves the four boxes under Send to the webhook ticked and presses Save.\n\n#### Result\n\nWhen the server passes 80% memory, the channel gets one message like “Memory on the server reached 82% (your limit is 80%).” A second arrives when it falls back below. If the bot stops answering for three checks in a row, about 15 minutes, a “recipe-bot is not answering” message follows.\n\n**Related:** [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Status page](/docs/status-page), [Logs](/docs/logs).\n\n\n---\n\nWatch\n\nPage 33 of 40\n\n# Status page\n\nA public page that says whether one app is answering.\n\nYou need a deployed project and a running worker, so the uptime checks can supply the figures. Egg serves the page from its own stored data, so a visitor never reaches your container through it.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Publish it\n\n   Open your project, Settings, **Public Status Page** (the section list at the top of Settings calls it Status Page), and press **Publish status page**, so it stops saying “Not published.”\n2. Step 2: Share the link\n\n   Copy the link from **Status page URL**, which is `/status/KEY` on your Egg address, so the page asks search engines not to index it. The key is 12 random URL-safe characters, and the page reflects the worker’s checks every few minutes.\n3. Step 3: Take it down\n\n   Press **Unpublish**, so Egg deletes the key and the old link stops working. Publishing again makes a new key.\n\n- Anyone with the link sees the project name, a state card, the share of the last 30 days your app was answering, a 30 day strip and the latest 10 outages under “Recent outages”, but nothing else: no repository, port, server or commit.\n- The state card reads “Operational” (“The app is answering.”), “Not responding” (“The app has stopped answering.”) or “No data yet” (“Checks have not run for this app yet.”). The percentage is withheld until a check has run.\n- The strip has one square per UTC day, with a key beneath it. Green is “Answered all day”, amber “Down for part of a day”, red “Down for most of a day” (more than half of it) and grey “No checks yet”. Hover a square to see the minutes, or open “Show each day as text” for a table of every day. With no outages the list says “None in the last 30 days.”, or “Nothing recorded yet.” before any check has run.\n- A slow answer over your Slow response limit counts as down here too, because it fails the check.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Window | 30 days | Older days are not shown. |\n| How long the page is kept before it is read again | 60 seconds, dropped early when the app’s state changes or the page is unpublished | A change can take up to a minute to show on a page that is already open. In the code: server/status-page-cache.ts |\n| Recent outages listed | 10 | Older outages are not listed. |\n| Check interval | 5 minutes | An outage shorter than that can be missed. |\n| Status pages per project | One | Publishing again makes a new key. |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `“No data yet”`\n\n  No check has run yet. Wait for the next 5 minute pass.\n- The link shows a not found page\n\n  The page was unpublished, or you published again and the key changed. Copy the new link.\n- The page says Not responding but the app works\n\n  The check uses your health path. Make sure it answers with a status below 500, within your slow response limit if you set one.\n- An outage is missing\n\n  It was shorter than the 5 minute check interval, or the server could not be reached at all, which is not reported as the app being down.\n\n## Example: Let friends check the bot\n\n### Given\n\nSam’s friends ask whether `recipe-bot` is down.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Publish\n\n   Sam presses Publish status page.\n2. Step 2: Share\n\n   Sam pastes the link into the Discord server’s pinned message.\n\n### Result\n\nThe page shows “Operational”, the 30 day share and a strip of green squares. A night when Sam redeployed twice shows one amber square.\n\n**Related:** [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring), [Notifications](/docs/notifications), [Analytics](/docs/analytics).\n\n\n---\n\nWatch\n\nPage 34 of 40\n\n# Analytics\n\nPage views for your site, counted by a small script. Nest does not share its web server logs, so the numbers have to come from the page itself.\n\nYou need a deployed project that serves web pages and access to the head of those pages, so a bot with no web page has nothing to count. Each visitor’s browser sends the count to Egg’s web address, not your container, so your container does no extra work and Egg must be reachable from your visitors. An Egg on localhost cannot count visitors on a public site.\n\n## Setting it up\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Step 1: Turn it on\n\n   Open your project’s Analytics tab, find **Count visits** and press **Turn on analytics**.\n2. Step 2: Paste the snippet\n\n   The card is now titled **Tracking snippet**, so copy its snippet into the head of every page you want counted.\n\n   HTML\n\n   ```\n   <script defer data-key=\"nda_...\" src=\"https://your-egg/api/analytics/script?key=nda_...\"></script>\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Redeploy and visit\n\n   Redeploy so the page has the snippet, which builds again and restarts your app, then open the page in a normal browser window.\n4. Step 4: Switch off when done\n\n   Press **Turn off** to remove the key, so existing numbers stay until they age out after 90 days, unless you chose 30 or 60 under “Keep the numbers for”.\n\n## What you see\n\nYou see “Page views” and “Visitors” for the last 30 days, “Page views per day” as a bar for each day and your top 10 pages under “Top pages”, so once Referrers is on you also see the top 10 sites under “Where visitors came from”. Turning on Campaigns adds “Campaigns”, and Record visitor countries adds a map under “Where visitors are”. Visitors are counted per day, so someone who comes on two days counts twice, and percentages, shares and the median appear only with at least 50 visits behind them. Below that you see counts and “Not enough data”.\n\nOnce analytics is on, the “What else to record” card adds ten things and a choice of how long to keep the numbers. Each is a checkbox that is off until you tick it and saves at once, with “Saved.” beneath, so with all of them off the script records only page views. Route details default to `/`, so to include a route, set a safe template such as `data-egg-path=\"/users/:id\"` on the page’s `<html>` element. Never put a visitor’s name, token or other personal value in that attribute. Under Settings, “Analytics defaults” has eight of them, all off, and Campaigns and Record visitor countries are not among them, because you set them up for each project. The checkboxes decide what a project starts with the first time you turn analytics on for it, so projects that already have analytics are not changed.\n\n- **Clicks** (“Top clicks”). Only elements you explicitly mark with a `data-egg-track` name are counted, so Egg never reads visible text or accessible labels. Do not put personal or changing values in the tracking name, because Egg never records input values or form content and stores only the project, the UTC day and the label.\n- **Time on page** (a card of the same name, with a Median). How long the page was open and visible, in four groups: under 10 seconds, 10 to 60 seconds, 1 to 5 minutes, over 5 minutes. It is sent when the visitor leaves.\n- **Funnel.** Two to six steps in order. For each, pick Page and type a path starting with `/`, or pick Tracked name. Press **Add step** for more, then **Save steps**. A visitor counts at a step only after reaching the ones before it, on the same UTC day.\n- **Referrers** (“Where visitors came from”). The script sends only the host of the referring site, never its full address or query string, so it does this once per page load and a link between pages of your own site is not a referrer. The host is stored with the page view and kept as sent, so `www.` stays, and turning Referrers off deletes the hosts already stored for your project.\n- **Campaigns** (“Campaigns”) starts off, so you must save at least one allowed value before you can turn it on. Under Allowed campaign values you list the exact `utm_source`, `utm_medium` and `utm_campaign` values you use, so the script reads only those three parameters from the address, and only a value that is on its list, spelled exactly as listed with capital letters included, is sent and stored. Any other value is dropped, and no other parameter is read. Each list holds up to 20 values of up to 40 characters: letters, numbers, dot, dash and underscore. Type a value, press **Add**, then press **Save allowed values**. Turning Campaigns off deletes the campaign values already stored for your project but keeps your lists, so taking a value off a list stops it being recorded while page views already stored with it stay until they age out.\n- **Speed metrics** (“Speed”). Your browser sends five measurements once per page view, when the visitor leaves or switches tab: Largest Contentful Paint, Cumulative Layout Shift, Interaction to Next Paint, First Contentful Paint and Time to First Byte. Times are in milliseconds (ms) and shown in ms or seconds (s), so layout shift is a score with no unit. The table shows the median and 75th percentile for each and labels the 75th percentile Good, Needs work or Poor with Google’s thresholds: LCP 2.5 s and 4 s, CLS 0.1 and 0.25, INP 200 ms and 500 ms, FCP 1.8 s and 3 s, TTFB 800 ms and 1800 ms. Egg does not collect exception messages, because they can contain credentials or personal data.\n- **Custom events** (“Events”). A click on an element with `data-egg-track` is recorded by name. Add `data-egg-value` for a value of up to 40 characters, letters, numbers, spaces and - _ . : only, such as a plan name. A value that breaks the rule is dropped and the event still counts. It stores the name, the value and the day, no visitor id. A funnel step can use the same name.\n- **Live view** (“On the site now”). You see page views in the last 5 minutes and, with Retention on, distinct visitors in that window, so with Retention off it says “Unique visitors need retention on”. While a page is open and visible, the script sends a small signal every 60 seconds, which Egg deletes after an hour, and it sends the signal only when both are on.\n- **Retention** (“Returning visitors”). Egg stores a random id in your visitor’s browser, not a cookie, in local storage under the name `_egg_id`, so a visitor can remove it by clearing site data. Egg keeps only an HMAC (a keyed hash) of it with the project, never the id, and the salt comes from the server secret and changes every 90 days, so someone who returns after a change counts as new. Turning retention off deletes the stored codes and the script removes the id from the browser. To turn it off, clear the Retention checkbox under What else to record on your project’s Analytics tab, and leave it cleared under Analytics defaults in Settings. The card shows visitors in the last 7 days, then of those who first came 7 to 14 days ago how many returned on a later day within 7 days, and of those who first came 30 to 37 days ago how many returned within 30 days. A group under 20 visitors shows “Not enough data yet”.\n- **Record visitor countries** (“Where visitors are”, a map with a table of the same numbers), looks up the visitor’s IP address in a country database that ships with Egg, in the same request that records the page view, so the address is not sent to any other service. Only the two-letter country code, such as `FR`, is stored with the page view. Egg does not store or log the IP address, and keeps no city, region or position. An address that is local or private, or not in the database, has no country, so nothing is stored for it. The map covers the last 30 days, and a country with fewer than 10 page views is shown as “fewer than 10”, with larger counts rounded to two significant figures. Turning it off deletes the countries already stored for your project. This product includes GeoLite2 data created by MaxMind, available from https://www.maxmind.com.\n\n**Keep the numbers for** sets how long Egg keeps page views, events, speed measurements and error records before deleting them: 30, 60 or 90 days for your project. A project keeps 90 days until you change it. Egg always deletes live view signals after an hour.\n\n## Privacy\n\n- By default Egg omits route details and represents them as `/`, so to report routes your site must explicitly provide a safe template in `data-egg-path`. No cookies. Everything else is a switch that starts off.\n- Egg stores no IP address or browser details, so a visitor is a shortened hash of the address, browser, project and date, salted with the server secret. It changes daily, so nobody can be followed across days.\n- Egg counts nothing for browsers that send Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, on localhost or for known bots, so the collector drops requests that carry either signal.\n- The page path is a template your site provides, so the script does not send the browser’s raw address path and legacy snippets without the template marker are stored as `/`. It reduces the referrer to its host.\n- The script stores nothing in your browser unless Retention is on, and then only one random id in local storage. No IP address is stored by any feature, and Egg does not log it, so the address only limits how fast one visitor can send and feeds the daily visitor hash, apart from one country lookup in the same request while Record visitor countries is on.\n- Campaign values are recorded only while Campaigns is on, and only values on the project’s allowed list. Any other value is dropped before it is stored.\n- Turning off Referrers, Campaigns, Retention or Record visitor countries deletes what that switch stored for the project. The other switches stop recording when turned off, and what they already stored stays until it ages out.\n- On a copy of Egg you run yourself behind a reverse proxy, set `TRUST_PROXY=1`, so Egg reads the proxy headers. It is off by default, and without it every visitor counts as one address, so the daily visitor hash tells people apart only by browser and everyone shares the send limits. Turn it on only when every request comes through a proxy you run that sets those headers itself, because with it off Egg has no visitor address and Record visitor countries stores no country either.\n- Egg removes analytics history after 30, 60 or 90 days, as you choose for each project under “Keep the numbers for”, and after 90 days where you have not chosen, so cleanup runs from the app every 15 minutes, independently of the build worker and uptime checks. Backups may keep older database contents under their own retention, and a setting change reaches visitors within about 5 minutes because the script is cached.\n\n### Limits\n\nThese numbers come from the code that enforces them. Each message names the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| History kept | 30, 60 or 90 days for each project, 90 by default | Older rows are deleted by the app cleanup task. |\n| Percentages and median | At least 50 visits | “Not enough data” |\n| Funnel steps | 2 to 6 | “A funnel needs at least 2 steps” or “A funnel has at most 6 steps” |\n| Tracked name | Letters, digits, spaces and - _ . : up to 40 characters | “A tracked name uses letters, numbers, spaces and - _ . : and is at most 40 characters” |\n| Page step | Starts with / | “A page step starts with \"/\", like /pricing” |\n| Page views | 1 per second per project and address. With TRUST_PROXY off, all visitors share one address | Extra views are dropped. In the code: server/analytics.ts |\n| Every other report (clicks, time, goals, events, speed, live signal) | 30 per 10 seconds per project and address. With TRUST_PROXY off, all visitors share one address | Extra events are dropped. In the code: server/analytics-events.ts |\n| Path and referrer | 200 and 100 characters | The rest is cut off. |\n| Allowed campaign values | Up to 20 each for source, medium and campaign, of up to 40 characters: letters, numbers, dot, dash or underscore | “Use short static labels with letters, numbers, dot, dash or underscore.” |\n| Campaign values recorded | Only values on the allowed list, spelled exactly | Any other value is dropped. |\n| Event value | Letters, digits, spaces and - _ . : up to 40 characters | The value is dropped. The event still counts. |\n| Speed figures | At least 50 page views per measurement | “Not enough data” |\n| Live view | Last 5 minutes, signal every 60 seconds, deleted after 1 hour | Older signals are deleted. |\n| Returning visitors | At least 20 visitors in the group | “Not enough data yet” |\n| Retention code | Changes every 90 days | A visitor after a change counts as new. |\n\n### What can go wrong\n\n- `“Nothing counted yet” (or “Analytics is off”)`\n\n  Check the snippet is in the head of a page that was redeployed, that your browser is not sending Do Not Track, and that you are not on localhost.\n- `“Not enough data”`\n\n  The percentages need 50 visits behind them. The counts are still shown.\n- `“Not enough data yet” under Returning visitors`\n\n  The group needs at least 20 visitors. Retention only counts visitors from the day it was turned on.\n- `“Unique visitors need retention on”`\n\n  Turn on Retention under What else to record. Without the random id, visitors cannot be told apart for 5 minutes.\n- `“Could not save” or “Could not change analytics”`\n\n  The change did not go through. Try again.\n- `“Allow at least one safe campaign label before turning campaigns on.” or Campaigns cannot be ticked`\n\n  Add a value to at least one of the three lists and press Save allowed values. Then turn Campaigns on.\n- A campaign you use does not appear under Campaigns\n\n  Only values on the allowed list are recorded, spelled exactly the same, capital letters included. Add the value and save, then visit again.\n- `“Where visitors are” has no countries`\n\n  Turn on Record visitor countries. Views from before it was on have no country. Local and private addresses have none, and on a copy you run yourself, TRUST_PROXY must be on to give Egg the visitor’s address.\n- A funnel does not turn on, or “A funnel needs at least 2 steps”\n\n  Fill in at least 2 steps and press Save steps. A funnel with fewer counts as off.\n\n### Example: Count visits to the bot’s page\n\n#### Given\n\nSam adds a small web page to `recipe-bot` that lists recipes, with a Subscribe button.\n\n#### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Turn it on\n\n   Sam turns on analytics and pastes the snippet in the page head.\n2. Step 2: Record clicks\n\n   Sam ticks Clicks under What else to record and marks the button `data-egg-track=\"subscribe\"`.\n3. Step 3: Redeploy\n\n   Sam redeploys.\n\n#### Result\n\nAfter a week the page shows page views per day, the top pages, and “subscribe” under Top clicks. Sam sees no personal data, because none was stored.\n\n**Related:** [Status page](/docs/status-page), [Domains](/docs/domains), [Security](/docs/security).\n\n\n---\n\nWatch\n\nPage 35 of 40\n\n# Activity\n\nA newest-first log of what you changed and what the monitor saw, with a search, two filters and a CSV export.\n\nOpen Activity in the sidebar. Entries are grouped by day in UTC, with Today and Yesterday named. Each row says what happened, to which project, server or domain, and what did it. A change made with an access token shows the token’s name, so you can tell a script from yourself. A project, token or server that has since been deleted still shows by the name it had, marked as gone. The log is kept in Egg’s database, so reading, searching and exporting it never contacts your container and changes nothing.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nDays are cut at midnight UTC so the log reads the same wherever you are and two people on one account see the same groups. Two entries a second apart on either side of midnight UTC land under different days. The build log on a deployment page shows your browser’s time, which is the only place that does.\n\n## What the log records\n\nThe log covers your own actions and the monitor’s. Examples of the wording: “Created project”, “Changed settings for”, “Saved environment variable on”, “Started a deployment of”, “Redeployed”, “Rolled back”, “Canceled a deployment of”, “Deployed through the deploy hook for”, “Turned on analytics for”, “Published a status page for”, “Detected downtime on”, “Detected recovery of”, “Created an access token”, “Opened a terminal on”, “Saved a file on”, “Created a webhook”, “Replaced the SSH key for”, “Changed the build memory limit for”, “Disconnected”, “Changed container permissions for” and “Removed a leftover from the container”.\n\n| Kind | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Projects** | Creating, changing, restarting and deleting projects. Saving and removing variables. Turning analytics on or off or changing what it records. Publishing or unpublishing a status page. |\n| **Deployments** | Starting, redeploying, rolling back and canceling deployments. Deleting previews. Creating, revoking and using a deploy hook. |\n| **Domains** | Adding, changing and removing a domain. |\n| **Servers** | Replacing the SSH key, changing the build memory limit, changing container permissions, removing leftovers and disconnecting a server. A disconnect entry records how many projects were kept or deleted. |\n| **Access and tokens** | Creating, editing and revoking access tokens. Signing out other browsers. Confirming sign-in with Hack Club. Creating, changing, testing and deleting webhooks. |\n| **Monitoring** | “Detected downtime on” and “Detected recovery of”. |\n| **Terminal and files** | Opening and closing a terminal, and saving, creating, renaming and deleting files. |\n\nAn entry that fits none of these shows only under All. Terminal and file entries record that something happened, with a path or a duration. They never record file contents or what you typed or saw. A detail that looks like a secret, by its name or its value, is dropped before an entry is shown or exported.\n\n## Searching and filtering\n\n### Find an entry\n\n1. Step 1: Search\n\n   Type in “Search by action, project or detail”. Every word you type has to match.\n2. Step 2: Pick a kind\n\n   Choose All, Projects, Deployments, Domains, Servers, Access and tokens, Monitoring, or Terminal and files.\n3. Step 3: Pick a date range\n\n   Choose Last 24 hours, Last 7 days, Last 30 days or All time.\n4. Step 4: Load more\n\n   Press Load more at the bottom to read the next 50.\n5. Step 5: Start again\n\n   Press Clear filters to go back to All kinds, All time and no search.\n\nThe filters are in the address, so a filtered list can be bookmarked or shared with yourself on another device. With Kind on All and nothing in the search box, three or more neighbouring entries of one kind fold into one row such as “4 token and access changes”, and every entry is still inside it. Search and a chosen Kind show every row.\n\n## Exporting to CSV\n\nPress **Export CSV** at the top of the page to download what the list shows. The file follows the search, kind and date range you have set, newest first. Its columns are Time (UTC), Kind, Action, Description, Target, Details, Access token and Entry id. Secrets are left out exactly as they are on the page, and a value that starts with `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` gets a leading quote so a spreadsheet shows it as text. The response carries an `x-export-truncated` header that is `true` when more entries matched than the file holds.\n\n### Reading it from a script\n\nA token with `activity:read` can read the same log with `GET /api/v1/activity`, with the same kinds and ranges. `kind` is one of `all`, `projects`, `deployments`, `domains`, `servers`, `access`, `monitoring` and `terminal`. `range` is `24h`, `7d`, `30d` or `all`. `limit` is 1 to 100 and defaults to 50. The answer has `items`, `nextCursor` and `total`. Pass `nextCursor` back as `cursor` for the next page.\n\nTerminal\n\n```\ncurl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $NEST_TOKEN\" \\\n  \"https://your-egg/api/v1/activity?kind=deployments&range=24h&limit=20\"\n```\n\nA token with `activity:read` can also read the change feed with `GET /api/v1/projects/{projectId}/changes`, or the `get_changes` tool. It is a newest-first list of one project’s deploys, rollbacks, redeploys, domain changes and build-config changes. An item can include `deploymentId` for API deploys, redeploys, rollbacks, cancels, project restarts, deployment lifecycle actions and preview deletions when the id belongs to the project. A project-linked domain removal appears in that project’s feed. Environment deletion and replacement summaries show key names, including for shared environment sets; values never appear.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Entries per page | 50 | Press Load more for the next 50. In the code: server/activity.ts (PAGE_SIZE) |\n| Search depth | The newest 5000 | A search reads in batches of 200 and says “Searched the latest 5,000 entries.” when it stopped at the limit. Narrow the date range instead. In the code: server/activity-query.ts (SCAN_LIMIT SCAN_BATCH) |\n| Search text | 80 characters | Anything longer is cut. In the code: server/activity.ts (MAX_QUERY) |\n| CSV export size | 5000 entries | The file stops there and x-export-truncated is true. Narrow the date range or search to export the rest. In the code: server/export-csv.ts (EXPORT_ROW_LIMIT) |\n| CSV export rate | 10 a minute per account | “Too many exports. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.” In the code: server/export-csv-data.ts (EXPORT_RATE) |\n| API page | 1 to 100, default 50 | A value outside the range is a 400. In the code: server/api-v1-more.ts (activity query) |\n| Detail length | 200 characters | A longer detail is cut with an ellipsis. In the code: server/activity.ts (MAX_VALUE) |\n| Retention | No pruning | Egg does not delete old entries. Deleting your account removes all of them. In the code: server/account.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `No activity yet`\n\n  Nothing has been recorded for your account yet. Make a change and it appears.\n- `Nothing in this range yet`\n\n  Pick a longer date range, or set Kind back to All. Press Clear filters.\n- `Could not load more`\n\n  Press Try again. If it keeps failing, reload the page. Your filters stay as they were.\n- `Searched the latest 5,000 entries.`\n\n  An older match may exist. Narrow the date range and search again.\n- `Too many exports. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 10 exports a minute for your account.\n- `A row shows the action name instead of a sentence`\n\n  The entry is of a kind this version has no wording for. Its time, target and details are still right.\n\n## Example: Find who deployed a project\n\n### Given\n\nA project named recipe-api that went live at an odd hour.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open Activity\n\n   Type recipe-api in the search box.\n2. Step 2: Narrow it\n\n   Set Kind to Deployments and Date range to Last 24 hours.\n\n### Result\n\nRows such as “Deployed through the deploy hook for recipe-api” or “Redeployed recipe-api” show the time and what started it, with the token’s name when a script did it.\n\n## Example: Keep a record of one month\n\n### Given\n\nYou want every token and sign-in change from the last 30 days in a spreadsheet.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Set the filters\n\n   Set Kind to Access and tokens and Date range to Last 30 days.\n2. Step 2: Export\n\n   Press Export CSV.\n\n### Result\n\nA file named for activity downloads with one row per entry, newest first. The Access token column names the token that made a change, and is empty for changes you made in the browser.\n\n**Related:** [Account](/docs/account), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Monitoring and uptime](/docs/monitoring).\n\n\n---\n\nWatch\n\nPage 36 of 40\n\n# Account\n\nYour Hack Club sign-in, the Settings page, the browsers that are signed in, the cards on the Account page, and how to delete your account.\n\nOpen Account from the account menu. It has cards for Profile, GitHub, Container permissions, Webhooks, Signed-in browsers and Delete account. How Egg looks and what new projects start with are on a separate page, Settings, which is in the same menu.\n\n- **Profile** shows “Shown as” (your display name and character, with a “Change in Settings” link), your Hack Club name, and your Email, or “Not shared” when there is no email. The Hack Club name and email are read only: change them at Hack Club. The name and character you are shown as are changed in Settings.\n- **GitHub** lists the accounts where the GitHub app is installed, each with a “Manage on GitHub” link. With none installed it says “Not connected yet. Import a project to install the app.”\n- **Container permissions** are covered on the [container](/docs/container) page.\n- **Webhooks** send a signed message to your own code when a deployment starts, succeeds or fails. They are covered on the [notifications](/docs/notifications) page.\n\n## The Settings page\n\nSettings holds your own choices. They are saved to your account as you make them, with a “Saved at” time under each card, and they follow you to other devices. The look is also kept in a browser cookie called `egg_prefs`, so a page is drawn the right way before it loads. Nothing on this page changes a project that already exists.\n\n| Card and setting | Choices | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Profile, Display name | Up to 40 characters. Shown in the menu and on the Account page. It is not used in any address, and two people can share a name. Control and invisible characters are refused. | Empty: your Hack Club name is shown |\n| Profile, Character | One of eight small pixel characters: Egg, Cat, Ghost, Robot, Frog, Alien, Owl or Mushroom. You cannot upload a picture. | Egg |\n| Appearance, Match my device | On or off. On, Egg uses your light theme while your device is light and your dark theme while it is dark, and switches by itself when the device does. Off, one theme stays whatever the device does. | On |\n| Appearance, Light theme | Shown while Match my device is on. One of the light themes: Egg Light, GitHub Light, VS Code Light+, Inspired by Catppuccin Latte, Inspired by Rosé Pine Dawn, Inspired by Solarized Light, Inspired by Ayu Light or Inspired by One Light, or a light theme of your own. | Egg Light |\n| Appearance, Dark theme | Shown while Match my device is on. One of the dark themes: Egg Dark, GitHub Dark, VS Code Dark+, Nord, Gruvbox Dark, Inspired by Dracula, Inspired by One Dark, Inspired by Tokyo Night, Inspired by Catppuccin Mocha, Inspired by Rosé Pine, Inspired by Monokai, Inspired by Ayu Dark or Inspired by Solarized Dark, or a dark theme of your own. | Egg Dark |\n| Appearance, Theme | Shown while Match my device is off. Any one theme, listed as Light themes and Dark themes, that stays as you picked it. | Egg Dark |\n| Appearance, Your themes | New theme opens an editor with a name, light or dark, and eight colours: page background, card surface, border, text, muted text, primary, link and danger. Each colour is a picker and a hex field. A sample page and the contrast checks update as you type. Edit, Export and Delete are on each saved theme, and Import reads a theme file. Up to 20 themes. | None |\n| Appearance, Density | Comfortable or Compact | Comfortable |\n| Deploys, Default branch | A valid git branch name of up to 100 characters. It is filled in as the production branch when you add a project from a pasted repository URL. A repository you pick from GitHub keeps its own default branch. | `main` |\n| Analytics defaults | Eight checkboxes: Clicks, Time on page, Funnel, Referrers, Speed and errors, Custom events, Live view and Retention. They decide which analytics features a project starts with the first time you turn analytics on for it. | All off |\n| Preferences, Motion | Device follows your system setting. Reduce turns animation off. Allow keeps transitions even if your device asks for less. | Device |\n\n- **Themes** are each light or dark, as in VS Code, and none has a second version. A theme sets every colour together: backgrounds, cards, borders, text, links, buttons, the selected item, the focus ring and the status colours. Themes apply in the signed-in pages; the public site keeps the Egg look. Egg Light and Egg Dark are the two defaults. Themes named “Inspired by” take their colours from well-known palettes and are not made or endorsed by those projects.\n- **Your own themes** are private to your account and follow you to every device. A colour is only ever a hex value such as `#1a2b3c` (3, 4, 6 or 8 digits); nothing else is accepted. Egg builds the rest of the theme from your eight colours. Text on the page and on a card must reach 4.5:1 contrast, or the theme is not saved, and Egg names the pair, shows the ratio and suggests the nearest colour that passes. Weaker pairs (button text, danger text, links, the focus ring at 3:1) only warn. An exported file holds the name, light or dark, and the colours, and nothing about you; an import over 8 KB or with unknown keys is refused. If you delete a theme you are using, Egg Light or Egg Dark takes its place.\n- **Earlier choices** are moved once. An account set to Light or Dark keeps that as one fixed theme, and one set to System now has Match my device on. An old accent becomes the nearest theme (Blue becomes GitHub Light, Nord becomes Nord, Amber and Gruvbox become Gruvbox Dark, and the rest become Egg Light). You can pick another at any time.\n- **Analytics defaults** only apply to a project the first time analytics is turned on for it. A project that already has analytics is not changed, and every project can still change its own switches. A Funnel switched on by default does nothing until the project has two steps. Retention stores a random id in the visitor’s browser, so leave it off unless you want returning visitors. Campaigns and Record visitor countries are not in this list: you turn them on for each project, and Campaigns also needs a list of allowed values. See [Analytics](/docs/analytics).\n- **Default branch** changes only the starting value of the form. You can still type another branch on each project.\n\n## Signing in and out\n\n### Sign in\n\n1. Step 1: Open the login page\n\n   Its heading is “Log in to Egg”.\n2. Step 2: Press Continue with Hack Club\n\n   Approve at Hack Club.\n3. Step 3: Land on Projects\n\n   Egg sets a session cookie that lasts 30 days.\n\nThe account menu has **Sign out**. It ends this browser’s session and closes every terminal your account has open. Some switches ask you to confirm with Hack Club again. That is a second sign-in that counts for 5 minutes, and it must be the same Hack Club account. A copy of Egg running in local mode has no Hack Club account, so it skips the confirmation.\n\nHack Club is the way to sign in until you add another. The **Sign in with a password** card on Account can also turn on sign-in with your email and a password. It is off until you turn it on. You can turn it on only within 5 minutes of a Hack Club sign-in. If your last one is older, Egg answers “Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning on password sign-in needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.” Sign in with Hack Club again, then retry. The account must share an email address with Egg, the password needs at least 12 characters, and Egg shows a recovery key during setup, which you confirm you saved before the password is accepted. You cannot turn it off if it would leave you with no way to sign in.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEgg never stores your Hack Club password. Hack Club proves who you are, and Egg keeps only a hash of its own session token. If you turn on a password, Egg keeps only a salted hash of it and of the recovery key, never the text itself. The 5 minute rule means a borrowed session cookie cannot add a password that would outlive the cookie. The second confirmation also exists because turning on a root shell is the one change that should not ride on a session cookie someone else may have borrowed from a laptop left open.\n\n## Signed-in browsers\n\nThe Signed-in browsers card lists each session with a label such as “Chrome on Windows” and when it was signed in. This one has a “This browser” badge. Press **Sign out** on another to end it, or **Sign out everywhere else** to end them all. Ending another session closes every terminal your account has open, because a terminal belongs to the account and not to one browser.\n\n## Deleting your account\n\n### Delete your account\n\n1. Step 1: Open the Delete account card\n\n   It is at the bottom of Account.\n2. Step 2: Type your name\n\n   Type your Hack Club name exactly as Profile shows it under Hack Club name. Spaces at the start and end are ignored.\n3. Step 3: Press Delete account\n\n   Your terminals close first, then your records are deleted.\n\nThis deletes your projects, servers, domains, keys, access tokens, webhooks, sessions and your activity log from Egg, and it cannot be undone. Nothing on your container is removed. Apps already there keep running, but nothing manages them any more. Delete the key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard as well, so Egg’s old public key does not stay on your container.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session | 30 days | Sign in again. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (SESSION_DAYS) |\n| Fresh sign-in | 5 minutes | It matters only for turning on Terminal, File changes or password sign-in. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (REAUTH_WINDOW_MS) |\n| Sign-in handshake | 10 minutes | Start again from the login page, in one browser. In the code: app/api/auth/login/route.ts (maxAge) |\n| Sign-in attempts | 20 a minute per address | A redirect to the login page. Wait a minute. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.signIn) |\n| Servers | 5 per account | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts (MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER) |\n| Access tokens | 25 active | Revoke one to make another. In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (MAX_TOKENS_PER_USER) |\n| Webhooks | 10 per account | “You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.” In the code: server/webhooks.ts (MAX_WEBHOOKS_PER_USER) |\n| Display name | 40 characters | “The display name can be at most 40 characters” In the code: server/preferences.ts |\n| Default branch | 100 characters, a valid git branch name | “That is not a valid git branch name” In the code: server/preferences.ts lib/preferences.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Hack Club sign-in is not set up on this installation yet.`\n\n  The button is off. On your own copy, set HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID and HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET.\n- `You did not approve the sign-in. Try again when you are ready.`\n\n  Press Continue with Hack Club and approve.\n- `That sign-in link was not valid. Please try again.`\n\n  Start again from the login page.\n- `The sign-in took too long or came from another browser. Please try again.`\n\n  Start and finish in one browser within 10 minutes.\n- `Hack Club could not sign you in. Please try again in a moment.`\n\n  Wait a minute and try again.\n- `Too many sign-in attempts. Wait a minute and try again.`\n\n  More than 20 sign-in attempts in a minute from one address. Wait a minute. On your own copy this only counts attempts when TRUST_PROXY is on.\n- `Something went wrong signing in.`\n\n  Try again. If it keeps happening, open an issue on GitHub.\n- `That is the session you are using. Sign out instead.`\n\n  Use Sign out in the account menu for this browser.\n- `Session not found`\n\n  That session already ended. Reload the page.\n- `Type your name exactly to confirm.`\n\n  Copy your Hack Club name from the Profile card.\n- `Could not delete the account`\n\n  Nothing was deleted. Try again.\n- `Could not sign that browser out`\n\n  Try again.\n- `Could not sign the others out`\n\n  Try again.\n- `A switch stays off after confirming`\n\n  You signed in with a different Hack Club account, so you land back on Account with no message. Sign in with the same account and flip the switch again.\n\n## Example: End a lost laptop’s session\n\n### Given\n\nYou signed in on a laptop you no longer have.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open Account\n\n   Find the other row under Signed-in browsers.\n2. Step 2: Press Sign out\n\n   Use the button on that row.\n\n### Result\n\nThe row disappears and any terminal that session had open closes. This browser stays signed in.\n\n## Example: Start clean after a scare\n\n### Given\n\nYou think a token or a cookie may have leaked.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Sign out everywhere else\n\n   Press it on the Signed-in browsers card. Every other session ends and your terminals close.\n2. Step 2: Revoke the tokens\n\n   On the Developer page, press Revoke on each token you do not recognise.\n3. Step 3: Read Activity\n\n   Set Kind to Access and tokens and look for entries you did not make.\n\n### Result\n\nOnly this browser stays signed in, no token works, and Activity shows what happened and when.\n\n**Related:** [Activity](/docs/activity), [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n\n\n---\n\nReference\n\nPage 37 of 40\n\n# Security\n\nHow Egg keeps your SSH key, your builds and your secrets, what each part can reach, and what the people who run it can and cannot see.\n\nEvery statement on this page is enforced by the server, and every number is the value the code uses. The Limits table at the end says where each one is set. To report a vulnerability, use the [security page](/security), not a public issue.\n\nWords used on this page. Your **container** is the Nest account Egg deploys into. A **server** is one connected container in Egg’s list. **Egg’s machine** is the computer that runs Egg: Egg’s own host, or your computer if you run a copy yourself. The **worker** is the part of Egg that takes queued deployments and runs them. A **build** installs and builds your project on your container, in a new release folder, under a **cap**, a memory and CPU limit that the kernel enforces. **Sealed** means encrypted, so it cannot be read without `APP_SECRET`. A **webhook** is a message one program sends to a web address when something happens. GitHub sends Egg one on every push, and Egg sends one to your own address if you add it under Webhooks. An **access token** is a secret you make on the Developer page for scripts and agents. A **session** is one signed-in browser.\n\n## The SSH key and the host key\n\nPress **Create key** on the Connect page. Egg makes one ed25519 key for that server and for nothing else. Then you paste the public half into the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard. The private half is sealed with AES-256-GCM before it is stored in Egg’s database. The sealing key comes from the `APP_SECRET` setting, and each sealed value is tied to its own record, so a value copied into another row does not open. The private key is never shown, and no API call returns it.\n\nThis key is root on your container\n\nSSH into Nest lands in a root shell inside your own container, so the key can do anything you can do there. The Connect page says so before you add it. Connect only a container you are happy to deploy to.\n\nEgg does not trust Nest’s SSH host on sight. The first time you press **Test connection**, Egg reaches the host, reads its host key, hangs up and shows the SHA-256 fingerprint in a “Confirm host key” box. Nothing is sent to your container yet. Check the fingerprint against the one Nest shows for your server, then press **Confirm host key**. Only then is the fingerprint pinned, which means stored and required from then on. Until it is pinned, no deploy, file change or terminal connects to the server. After that, if the host presents any other key, Egg stops before it sends anything and the connection fails.\n\n**New key** deletes Egg’s old private key at once and keeps the pin. The server goes back to “Waiting for the key”, and deploys, files and terminals fail until you paste the new public key into the Nest dashboard and press **Test connection**. Apps that are already running are not touched. To clear a pin, press **Disconnect** and connect the server again. That asks you to confirm the fingerprint again.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nNest cannot hand Egg a narrower login than yours, so the key is as strong as your own. A key made for one server means you can end Egg’s access by deleting that one key in the Nest dashboard, without touching anything else. The pin exists because SSH trusts whatever answers the first time. After you confirm the fingerprint, a different host is refused before any file or variable is sent. Egg cannot know the right fingerprint for you, so check it against the one Nest shows for your server. If you confirm a fingerprint without checking it and something sat between Egg and Nest at that moment, its key is the one that gets pinned.\n\nDisconnect asks about the server’s projects\n\nDisconnect closes the terminals opened with this server’s key and deletes Egg’s copy of the private key. The public key stays on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard until you delete it there. When the server has projects, a box asks whether to delete them as well: “Also delete the project on this server”, or “Also delete the N projects on this server”. Left unticked, which is the default, the projects are kept, the server shows as disconnected, and connecting the same Nest username again reattaches them. Ticked, the projects and their deployments and logs are deleted from Egg. Nothing on the container is removed either way, and running apps keep running.\n\n### Take access back\n\n1. Step 1: Delete the key in Nest\n\n   Egg’s access ends at once. Your apps keep running, because they are ordinary services in your container (systemd, the Linux service manager) and do not need Egg to run.\n\n   In the dashboard: Nest dashboardthen SSH Keysthen Delete the key\n2. Step 2: Check it\n\n   Press **Test connection** on the Connect page. It should report “Could not connect”.\n3. Step 3: Tidy up\n\n   Press **Disconnect** when you are done with the server, or **New key** to start again with a fresh key.\n\n## Where builds run\n\nA build turns your repository into files your app can run. Egg does it in four steps. Three of them happen on your container, so this page says what they use.\n\n### Where each step runs\n\n1. Step 1: Clone and pack the source, on Egg’s machine\n\n   The worker downloads a shallow copy of your branch from github.com (the latest commit only) into a work folder and packs the source into one archive, a compressed .tar.gz file. Nothing is installed or built there. The GitHub token is used for the clone and is never sent to your container. The clone stops after 5 minutes. The work folder is deleted when the deployment ends.\n2. Step 2: Check your container, then upload and unpack\n\n   Before anything is uploaded, Egg looks at your container: the Node.js version (the project’s engines range, or 22.13 or newer), the package manager the lockfile needs, Python 3 and venv for a Python project, systemd-run, timeout, and at least 2048 MB of free disk. If a check fails, the deployment ends with one line that says what to fix, and nothing is uploaded. If all pass, Egg uploads the source over SSH and unpacks it into a new release, a folder that holds one deployed version of your app. The running release is not touched.\n3. Step 3: Install and build, on your container, under a cap\n\n   Your install and build commands run in the new release folder, then the production install. All of it runs under the cap, a hard memory and CPU limit that the kernel enforces. The table below lists it. Your app keeps serving the whole time. Your project’s variables are written to a file with mode 600 for the build, and Egg deletes that file when the build ends. If the build fails, runs out of memory, times out or is canceled, Egg deletes the new folder and the running release stays.\n4. Step 4: Switch, on your container\n\n   Egg writes the release’s variables file, points the service at the new release and restarts it, so the app is unavailable for a moment. Then it waits up to 45 seconds for the app to answer.\n\nIf the clone, the checks, the build or the upload fails, the deployment shows Error and your container keeps running its current release. If the new release does not answer after the restart, a production deploy puts the previous release back and shows Error.\n\nThe cap is set like this:\n\n| Setting | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Memory | The server’s Build memory (MB), 512 MB by default, set on the Container page. A project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB while it builds, if your container has room for it (its memory minus 512 MB is at least 1024 MB). Installs use the setting. It is never more than your container’s memory minus 512 MB, which stays free for your app. If that leaves less than 256 MB, the build does not start. There is no swap, so the limit is real. |\n| CPU | The server’s Build CPU limit (cores), 2 by default, as a quota of 100% for each core. The build also runs at a low priority, so your app gets the CPU first when it is busy. |\n| Processes | At most 4096. |\n| Time | 15 minutes, then the build is stopped. This limit cannot be changed. |\n| Builds at once | One at a time on a container. A second build waits for up to 30 minutes. |\n| If memory runs out | The kernel is told to stop the build before your app, so a build that goes over its cap is the one that dies. |\n| What it can see | Everything your Nest user can see on your container. The cap limits memory and CPU. It does not hide your files from the build, which runs as the same user as your app. |\n| Network | Normal access from your container, because installing packages needs it. |\n| What it is given | Your project’s variables for a production or a preview deployment, and `CI=true`. |\n\n- **What is checked first.** The repository must be a plain `https` URL on `github.com`. A branch name may use letters, digits, dots, underscores, hyphens and slashes, and may not start with a hyphen or contain `..`, so it cannot be read as a Git option. The root and output folders are resolved to their real paths, so a link inside a repository cannot pull in a file from Egg’s machine.\n- **What is limited.** The packed source may be 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes). A larger one fails the deployment before anything is uploaded. The work folder is deleted when the deployment ends. A deployment stores at most 20,000 log lines, and then one line says “Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.” The build carries on. Colour codes and control characters are removed from each line, and a line is cut at 4000 characters.\n- **Pull requests from forks are never built**, because that would run a stranger’s code next to your variables.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nA build runs code from a repository, so it has to run somewhere. Egg runs it on your container, a machine you already control and already run your own code on, and keeps strangers’ build code off the machine that holds everyone’s data. The cap protects your running app from a build that uses too much memory or CPU. It does not protect your files from the build. That is a deliberate trade, and it is why you should build only code you wrote or trust, and why variables should hold only what the build needs. The build has network access, because a build that cannot install packages is no build.\n\n## Variables and secrets\n\n- **Environment variables** are sealed with AES-256-GCM, tied to the project, the name and where the value applies. The list shows names only. The browser routes never send a value back, and the API returns one only through `get_env_vars` for a variable marked non-secret. Each variable has a secret flag, on by default. At deploy time they are written to a `.env` file on your container with mode 600.\n- **Log masking.** A value is replaced by `***` in build and deploy logs and in a deployment’s error text. The base64 form of a value (padded, unpadded or URL-safe) and its percent-encoded form are replaced too. A value of 2 or 3 characters is masked only where it stands alone, not inside a longer word or number. A value of one character is never masked, because it would garble every line. A value that spans several lines, such as a PEM key, is also masked line by line for each line of 8 or more characters. Any other change to a value, such as hex or a hash, is not recognised. Do not print secrets.\n- **Deploy hooks, access tokens and sessions.** Only a SHA-256 of each is stored. A hook URL and a token secret are shown once, when you make them.\n- **Notification webhook URLs and webhook signing secrets** are sealed. A Discord or Slack URL is never shown again. A signing secret starts with `whsec_` and is shown once.\n- **Analytics keys** are the exception: a key starting with `nda_` sits in your page source on purpose, and can only add a page view to its own project.\n\nWhere a value can still be read\n\nThe Files tab and the API both refuse `current/.env` and each release’s `.env`, by name and after links are followed, so a link in `shared/` cannot reach them. A terminal can still read them, because a shell reads anything its user can. That is why the terminal is off by default and why an access token with the terminal permission lives 7 days at most. Treat the terminal switch, and your Egg sign-in, as the key to your variables.\n\n## Sign-in and requests\n\n- **Sign-in.** Hack Club Auth proves who you are. Egg asks only for `openid`, `profile` and `email`, and none of that reaches your container. The sign-in handshake must finish within 10 minutes, in the browser that began it.\n- **Session.** A random token in an `httpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax` cookie called `egg_session`, marked `Secure` in production. It lasts 30 days. Only a hash is stored. Signing in again replaces the session you were using, so an old cookie does not live on.\n- **Requests.** Every change from the browser must come from this site, because the `Origin` header is checked. A write with no `Origin` that the browser marks as cross-site is refused too. The API and the MCP endpoint never read cookies. They take a bearer token only. A JSON request from the dashboard is read up to 256 KB and a larger one is refused with 413.\n- **Sensitive switches.** The Terminal and File changes switches need a Hack Club sign-in from the last 5 minutes before they turn on. Turning one off never asks.\n- **Guessing.** One IP address may make 20 sign-in attempts a minute, and 60 requests a minute with access tokens that are not valid. Past that the answer is 429. Egg reads the address from the proxy headers only when the `TRUST_PROXY` setting is on. It is off by default, so with it off there is no address to count and these two limits do not apply. Turn it on only when every request reaches Egg through a reverse proxy you run.\n- **GitHub events.** A message from GitHub is accepted only with a valid `X-Hub-Signature-256` signature over the raw body. The body is capped at 5 MB, and a request with no length or a missing or malformed signature is refused before its body is read. Each message has an id, called a delivery id, and Egg remembers it for 1 hour, so a replayed delivery does nothing. A deploy hook takes a body of 4 KB at most.\n- **Browser headers.** Every page is sent with `X-Frame-Options: DENY` and a policy that forbids framing, `nosniff`, a strict referrer policy and a permissions policy that turns off the camera, microphone, location and payment. In production it also sends `Strict-Transport-Security` for two years. The content policy still allows inline scripts, because Next.js writes the ones that start a page.\n\n## Who can see what\n\n- **You** can see everything that belongs to your account and nothing that belongs to anyone else. Another account’s project, server or deployment answers as if it did not exist.\n- **The people who run Egg** have no admin screen in the product. What shows through the site is the public stats page, which gives totals only: a count under 10 shows as “fewer than 10”, a larger one keeps two significant figures, and a percentage or median appears only with at least 50 items behind it.\n- **They also host the database and hold the secret.** `APP_SECRET` seals your SSH key and your variables. The sealing protects a copied database or a backup. It does not stop whoever holds both the database and the secret. If that is not acceptable, run your own copy.\n- **The public** can see three things you turn on: a status page, the analytics script and its collector, and the comment on a pull request. A status page shows the project name, whether the app answers, a 30-day strip and the latest 10 outages, and nothing else. The collector answers 204 whether or not a key exists, so keys cannot be probed.\n- **Your receivers**, the addresses you add under Webhooks, get a signed message when a deployment starts, succeeds or fails. It never carries variables, logs, commit messages or errors. Egg refuses to send to a private or internal address and does not follow redirects.\n- **The log of your actions** records what you did, with names and paths. It never records the contents of a file, a variable’s value, or what you typed or saw in the terminal, and anything that looks like a secret is dropped before an entry is shown.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session | 30 days | The cookie and the stored session both expire. Sign in again. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (SESSION_DAYS) |\n| Fresh sign-in | 5 minutes | Older than that, turning on Terminal or File changes sends you to Hack Club again, and turning on password sign-in is refused until you sign in again. In the code: server/auth/session.ts (REAUTH_WINDOW_MS) |\n| Sign-in handshake | 10 minutes | A sign-in that takes longer fails. Start again from the login page, in one browser. In the code: app/api/auth/login/route.ts (maxAge) |\n| Sign-in attempts | 20 a minute per address | A redirect to the login page. Wait a minute. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.signIn) |\n| Invalid access tokens | 60 a minute per address | 429 with Retry-After. In the code: server/rate-limit.ts (RATE_LIMITS.badToken) |\n| Token with terminal or file changes | 7 days at most | The token form refuses a longer expiry. In the code: server/api-tokens.ts (SHORT_LIVED_MAX_DAYS) |\n| Terminals | 5 per account, 50 on the server | “You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.” In the code: server/terminal.ts (TERMINAL_LIMITS) |\n| Servers per account | 5 | “You can connect up to 5 servers.” In the code: server/servers.ts (MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER) |\n| GitHub delivery | 5 MB, id remembered 1 hour | A larger body or a repeated id is dropped. In the code: app/api/webhooks/github/route.ts server/github/webhook.ts (DELIVERY_TTL_MS) |\n| Deploy hook body | 4 KB | 413. In the code: app/api/hooks/deploy/[token]/route.ts (MAX_BODY_BYTES) |\n| Dashboard JSON request | 256 KB | 413 “That request is too large.” In the code: server/body-limit.ts (JSON_BODY_LIMIT) |\n| Host key | Confirmed by you on the first Test connection | Nothing connects until it is confirmed. A different key later stops the connection. In the code: server/servers.ts |\n| Clone | 5 minutes | “The build timed out.” Nothing changes on your container. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts (clone timeoutMs) |\n| Build time | 15 minutes. It cannot be changed. | The build is stopped and the deployment fails with “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” The new release folder is deleted and the live release keeps running. In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) |\n| Free disk before a build | 2048 MB | The deployment ends before the upload with “Free up disk space on your container: <n> MB is free and a build needs at least 2048 MB.” In the code: server/deploy/container-probe.ts (MIN_FREE_DISK_MB) |\n| Build output | 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes), measured on the packed archive | The deployment fails with the size in the message, before the upload. In the code: server/deploy/build.ts (MAX_ARCHIVE_BYTES) |\n| Unpack the source on your container | 15 minutes | The deployment fails and the live release keeps running. In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (unpackSourceScript) |\n| Build log | 20,000 lines | One line says the rest was dropped. The build carries on. In the code: server/deploy/logger.ts (MAX_LOG_ROWS) |\n| Build memory | 512 MB by default, 512 MB up to the server’s memory. A project with a build step gets at least 1024 MB while it builds, if the container has room. A build never gets more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB, and does not start under 256 MB. | “Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts server/deploy/container-build.ts (containerMemoryCapMb) |\n| Build CPU | 2 cores by default, 0.5 to the Nest server’s reported cores in 0.25-core steps | “Build CPU must be a number from 0.5 to <max> cores in 0.25-core steps.” In the code: lib/build-memory.ts server/deploy/container-build.ts (cpuQuotaPercent) |\n| Notification post | 5 seconds | The post is dropped and logged. Redirects are refused. In the code: server/notify.ts |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `The host key of <host> changed (was <old>, now <new>). Nothing was sent. If you expected this, reconnect the server.`\n\n  Stop. Something else answered where Nest should be, or Nest changed its key. If you expected it, Disconnect and add the server again, and choose whether to keep its projects.\n- `Nest rejected the key. Check that you pasted the public key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and that the username is exactly your Nest username.`\n\n  Paste the public key again. After New key, the old one stops working.\n- `Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning this on needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.`\n\n  403. Flip the switch again. It sends you through Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied.\n- `<name> is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.`\n\n  409. You kept the projects when you disconnected. Connect the same Nest username again from Connect and run Test connection.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Many invalid tokens came from your address. Fix the token your script sends, then wait.\n\n## Example: End Egg’s access\n\n### Given\n\nA server connected as sam-r, with two projects running.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Delete the key in Nest\n\n   In the dashboard: Nest dashboardthen SSH Keysthen Delete the key\n2. Step 2: Test the connection\n\n   In Egg, open Connect and press **Test connection**.\n\n### Result\n\nThe server shows “Could not connect” with “Nest rejected the key.” Both apps still answer, because their services are still running on your container.\n\n## Example: See a secret masked\n\n### Given\n\nA project with a variable `STRIPE_KEY` set to `sk_live_abc123`, and a build command of `echo \"key is $STRIPE_KEY\"`.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Deploy\n\n   Press Deploy and open the deployment’s log.\n2. Step 2: Find the line\n\n   Look for the line the echo printed.\n\n### Result\n\nThe log shows `key is ***`. The same happens if the build prints the base64 form of the value. Had the value been printed as a hash or in hex, the log would have shown it.\n\n**Related:** [Servers](/docs/servers), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container), [Environment variables](/docs/env), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api).\n\n\n---\n\nReference\n\nPage 38 of 40\n\n# Running your own\n\nEgg is open source. You can run your own copy, with your own database, secret and worker.\n\nYou need Node.js and pnpm. This page lists what the code reads. The [self-hosting guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md) walks through a private install on your own computer and a server run with Docker Compose, the [customising guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/customising.md) lists the files to change for the name and the wording, and the [backend setup guide](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/backend-setup.md) has the Hack Club and GitHub registration steps in full.\n\n## Starting your own copy\n\n### Start it locally\n\n1. Step 1: Install and set up\n\n   From the `web` folder, `pnpm run setup` writes `.env.local` with a fresh `APP_SECRET`. It never overwrites an existing file. Use `pnpm run setup`, not `pnpm setup`: pnpm has a built-in command of that name that does something else.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   cd web\n   pnpm install\n   pnpm run setup\n   ```\n2. Step 2: Pick a way to sign in\n\n   Setup writes `LOCAL_MODE=1`, so the login page gets a **Sign in on this computer** button and you need no external account. On first use, enter the generated `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET` from `.env.local`and choose a password; use that password for later sign-ins. Or set the Hack Club values to use Hack Club sign-in. Development sign-in is off by default.\n3. Step 3: Start it\n\n   `pnpm egg start` creates `.env.local` if it is missing, checks the computer, builds Egg itself the first time, which makes that first start slower and uses your computer’s CPU and disk, starts it on the port in `APP_URL` and opens the browser. Setup turns on `RUN_WORKER=1`, so the queue runs in the same process. Ctrl+C stops it. Add `--dev` to run the development server instead.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   pnpm egg start\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Try deploys without a Nest account\n\n   Run `pnpm fake-nest` in a second terminal. Set `NEST_HOST=127.0.0.1` and `NEST_PORT=2222`, plus the `UNIT_DIR` value it prints, before you add the server. It is for trying things out only, never a substitute for a real Nest container. On Windows it runs its commands in WSL. The first Test connection still asks you to confirm the host key.\n\n`pnpm run doctor` only checks the computer and starts nothing. A cross stops Egg from starting. A warning does not: for example, a missing optional tool is shown as a warning, and Egg itself starts without it. You can also run `pnpm build` and then `pnpm start` yourself. The database changes apply the first time a new version opens the file.\n\nYour data is one file, `web/data/egg.db`. It stays on disk when Egg stops. Back it up together with `APP_SECRET`, because the file cannot open your sealed keys without it.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nLocal mode is off unless you set `LOCAL_MODE=1`. It requires a private `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET` of at least 32 characters to set or recover the owner password. Keep the recovery key private. It also refuses to start unless `APP_URL`is localhost, `127.0.0.1` or `::1`. `LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE=1` exists for a copy behind a VPN or an access proxy you control, where something else decides who can reach the site.\n\n## Start on login\n\n`pnpm run service:install` writes one file that starts Egg when you sign in to your computer. It is optional. Nothing starts until you run the command it prints. Run `pnpm egg start` once first, so a build exists, then press Ctrl+C. `pnpm run service:remove` stops the service and deletes the file.\n\n- **macOS.** A LaunchAgent at `~/Library/LaunchAgents/app.egg.web.plist`. Turn it on with `launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u)` and that file. The log is `~/Library/Logs/egg.log`.\n- **Linux.** A systemd user unit at `~/.config/systemd/user/egg.service`. Turn it on with `systemctl --user enable --now egg`. To start at boot without signing in, run `loginctl enable-linger`.\n- **Windows.** A script at `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Egg\\egg-task.ps1`. Run it to register a Scheduled Task named Egg that runs `pnpm start` at sign-in.\n\n## Settings Egg reads\n\n| Setting | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `APP_SECRET` | Required, 32 or more characters. Seals SSH keys and variables. Back it up: losing or changing it makes stored keys unreadable. |\n| `APP_URL` | The public address. Used for sign-in redirects, hook URLs and links. Default http://localhost:3000. |\n| `DATABASE_URL` | The SQLite file. Default file:./data/egg.db. Migrations apply on startup. |\n| `HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID, HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET` | From a Hack Club Auth app. Without them the Continue with Hack Club button is off. |\n| `LOCAL_MODE` | 1 or true enables password-based owner sign-in without an external account. Off by default. Refused unless APP_URL is localhost, 127.0.0.1 or ::1. |\n| `LOCAL_MODE_SECRET` | Required in local mode; a private random recovery key of at least 32 characters for first-time setup and password reset. Setup generates it for new installations. |\n| `LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE` | 1 lets LOCAL_MODE run on another address. Use it only behind a VPN or access proxy you control. |\n| `TRUST_PROXY` | Off by default. 1 or true makes Egg believe X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP for the visitor’s address. Set it to 1 only when every request reaches Egg through a reverse proxy you run that sets those headers itself. Without it, every visitor counts as one address for analytics, and the sign-in and invalid-token limits do not apply. |\n| `DEV_LOGIN` | 1 shows a fake sign-in in pnpm dev. It is ignored in a production build. |\n| `GITHUB_APP_ID, GITHUB_APP_SLUG, GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID, GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET, GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY, GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | All six are needed for private repositories, the repository picker and deploys on push. The private key may use \\n escapes. |\n| `RUN_WORKER` | 1 runs the queue inside the web process. Anything else means no builds run there. Use pnpm worker. |\n| `WORKER_CONCURRENCY` | Deployments at once. Default 2. One project never builds twice at a time. Read by the worker, not by config.ts. |\n| `DEPLOY_WORK_DIR` | Where builds are cloned. Default ./data/work. |\n| `MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS` | How often uptime and usage are checked. Default 300000. 0 turns the checks off. |\n| `NEST_HOST, NEST_PORT, UNIT_DIR` | Nest’s SSH host (hackclub.app, 22) and where systemd units are written (/etc/systemd/system). Change only for testing. |\n\nBuild memory is not a setting in the file. It is per server, on that server’s Container page. See [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container).\n\n## The worker that runs builds\n\nThe worker takes queued deployments and runs the uptime monitor. It polls every second and never runs two jobs of one project at once. With `RUN_WORKER=1` it lives inside the web process. For production you can set `RUN_WORKER=0` and run `pnpm worker` on another machine. It finishes running jobs when it gets SIGINT or SIGTERM. Every project needs a worker, because the worker clones the repository and runs the container build over SSH. The build itself runs on the project’s own container, so the machine that runs the worker needs no Docker.\n\nWhat a deployment costs the worker’s machine: a clone and a packed copy of the source in `DEPLOY_WORK_DIR`, kept until the deployment ends, and an upload over SSH. Two deployments can run at once by default. Set `WORKER_CONCURRENCY=1` to allow one at a time. The install and build use the container’s memory and CPU, under the cap set for that server.\n\nA worker renews a lease on its running job every 15 seconds. If the lease is 60 seconds old, Egg logs a warning and leaves the job running, because commands it started on your container may still be changing files. That deployment keeps its project blocked until you recover it by hand. See [Recovering a stale deployment](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg/blob/main/docs/selfhost/README.md) in the self-hosting guide.\n\nWithout a worker, nothing builds\n\nIf `RUN_WORKER` is not `1` and no `pnpm worker` is running, deployments stay queued, and uptime checks and resource history do not run.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nThe queue is a table in the database, and a job is claimed with one conditional update, so two workers can share it and neither takes the same job. Builds are kept off the web process on purpose when you can: a long build then cannot slow the pages. One process is still enough for one person, which is why setup turns the embedded worker on.\n\n## The database and backups\n\nThe database is one SQLite file at `DATABASE_URL`. Users, servers, projects, deployments, logs, jobs and history are all in it. Back up the file together with `APP_SECRET`: the file alone cannot open your sealed keys, and the secret alone has nothing to open. Keep them in different places if the backup leaves your machine. Stop Egg before you copy the file, and copy any `-wal` or `-shm` files beside it. Foreign keys are on, so deleting a server record deletes its projects.\n\n## Hack Club and GitHub apps\n\n- **Hack Club Auth.** Make an app at `auth.hackclub.com/developer/apps` with the redirect URI `<APP_URL>/api/auth/callback`.\n- **GitHub App.** Callback and setup URL `<APP_URL>/api/github/callback` with “Request user authorization (OAuth) during installation” on, webhook URL `<APP_URL>/api/webhooks/github`, repository permissions Contents and Metadata read only, Pull requests read and write, and the Push and Pull request events.\n\n## Run on a Linux server with Docker Compose\n\nTo run Egg on a Linux server, use Docker Compose. You need Docker and Docker Compose, a domain that points at the server, and ports 80 and 443 open. Caddy sits in front of the app, and gets and renews HTTPS by itself on ports 80 and 443, and 443/udp for HTTP/3. The app is not reachable on any other port.\n\n### Start it on a server\n\n1. Step 1: Get the code and the settings file\n\n   Clone the repository and copy the example file to .env in the repository root.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   git clone https://github.com/jeremy341/egg.git\n   cd egg\n   cp deploy/.env.example .env\n   ```\n2. Step 2: Set the address and the secret\n\n   In `.env`, set `APP_URL` (starting with https://), `APP_HOST` (the same name without https://) and `APP_SECRET`. Make the secret with the command below and back it up.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   openssl rand -base64 32\n   ```\n3. Step 3: Make the data folder\n\n   The Docker containers that run Egg use user 1000, so that user must own the data folder. The path must match `EGG_DATA_DIR`.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   sudo mkdir -p /srv/egg/data && sudo chown -R 1000:1000 /srv/egg/data\n   ```\n4. Step 4: Start it\n\n   From `web/`, `pnpm run compose:up` does the same.\n\n   Terminal\n\n   ```\n   docker compose up -d --build\n   ```\n\nNothing is built on this server\n\nThe `worker` service in the Compose file runs every deployment. It clones the repository, packs the source and sends it to the project’s Nest container, where the install and build run under a cap. So no service here needs Docker access or extra privileges, and the worker starts with the rest. Without a running worker, deployments stay queued.\n\nThe example file sets `TRUST_PROXY=1`, because every request comes through Caddy. Do not publish the app port yourself. `sh deploy/backup.sh` (or `pnpm run backup`) makes a consistent copy while Egg runs, gzips it into `backups/` and keeps the newest 14. `sh deploy/upgrade.sh` backs up first, pulls, rebuilds and restarts. Database changes apply when the app starts.\n\nSecurity on a server: a signed-in JSON request is limited to 256 KB. One address may make 20 sign-in attempts a minute and 60 requests a minute with unrecognised tokens, counted only when `TRUST_PROXY` is on. The content policy still allows inline scripts, and the nonce fix is open. See [Security](/docs/security).\n\n## What works where\n\n| System | Support |\n| --- | --- |\n| Windows | Full. pnpm fake-nest needs WSL. |\n| Linux | Full. |\n| macOS | The same as Linux. |\n\nThe CI workflow runs the typecheck, lint, tests and the dependency audit on Linux and macOS. The audit fails the Linux run on a high finding and only reports it on macOS.\n\n## Limits\n\nThese numbers are read from the code that enforces them. Under each message is the file that holds the number, relative to the web/src folder.\n\n| Limit | Value | What happens at it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Concurrent deployments | 2 by default | Set WORKER_CONCURRENCY to change it. In the code: instrumentation.ts (WORKER_CONCURRENCY) |\n| Build time | 15 minutes, on the container. It cannot be changed. | “The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) |\n| Build memory and CPU | 512 MB (at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step, when the container has room) and 2 cores by default for each server; both are configurable | The kernel stops the build at its cap. With memory, exit 137 and “The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB.” In the code: server/deploy/container-build.ts (systemdRunArgs) lib/build-memory.ts |\n| Uptime check | Every 300000 ms by default | Set MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS to 0 to stop checks. In the code: server/jobs.ts (MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS) |\n| Secret length | 32 characters at least | “APP_SECRET must be at least 32 characters” In the code: server/config.ts (APP_SECRET) |\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Invalid environment configuration`\n\n  The first request or the worker start checks every setting, and the lines under it name the one that is wrong. Fix it, or run pnpm run setup.\n- `APP_SECRET must be at least 32 characters`\n\n  Use a longer secret. pnpm run setup makes one.\n- `LOCAL_MODE signs anyone in as the owner, so APP_URL must be localhost or 127.0.0.1. Set APP_URL to a local address, or set LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE=1 if you control who can reach the site.`\n\n  Set APP_URL=http://localhost:3000, or read the override above before you set LOCAL_MODE_ALLOW_REMOTE.\n- `The recovery key is incorrect.`\n\n  Enter the LOCAL_MODE_SECRET from your private .env.local file.\n- `The login page has no Sign in on this computer button`\n\n  LOCAL_MODE is not 1 or true, or Egg was not restarted after the change. Edit .env.local and restart.\n- `The GitHub App is not configured.`\n\n  Set all six GITHUB_APP settings and GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET.\n- `webhook not configured`\n\n  The webhook route answers 503 until GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET is set.\n- `Hack Club sign-in is not set up on this installation yet.`\n\n  Set HACKCLUB_CLIENT_ID and HACKCLUB_CLIENT_SECRET, or use local mode.\n- `Egg did not start. Fix the lines marked with a cross, then run this again.`\n\n  The doctor found a problem that stops Egg. Read the Fix line under each cross. pnpm run doctor shows the same list.\n- `Port 3000 is already in use`\n\n  Another program uses the port in APP_URL. Stop it, or change the port in APP_URL in .env.local.\n- `Deployments stay Queued on a server`\n\n  The worker service is not running. Run docker compose ps, then docker compose up -d. Read its output with docker compose logs worker.\n- `Deployments stay Queued`\n\n  No worker is running. Set RUN_WORKER=1, or start pnpm worker.\n\n## Example: Move to a new machine\n\n### Given\n\nA copy running on one machine with a database file and an .env.local.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Stop it\n\n   Stop the web process and any worker.\n2. Step 2: Copy two things\n\n   Copy the database file and the APP_SECRET value to the new machine.\n3. Step 3: Set and start\n\n   Set DATABASE_URL and APP_SECRET, then start it.\n\n### Result\n\nYour servers and variables open as before. With a different APP_SECRET, stored keys cannot be read and you would have to make new keys.\n\n## Example: Run a private copy on your laptop\n\n### Given\n\nA laptop with Node.js and pnpm, and a Nest container to deploy to.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Set up\n\n   Run pnpm install and pnpm run setup in the web folder.\n2. Step 2: Check local mode\n\n   Setup wrote LOCAL_MODE=1 and LOCAL_MODE_SECRET to .env.local. Add a random secret of at least 32 characters to older files.\n3. Step 3: Start\n\n   Run pnpm egg start. It checks the computer, builds the first time and opens the browser.\n4. Step 4: Sign in\n\n   Use LOCAL_MODE_SECRET from .env.local to set your password on first use, then sign in with that password.\n\n### Result\n\nYou land on Projects as the owner. Connect your Nest username, paste the key into the Nest dashboard, press Test connection, and deploy as usual.\n\n**Related:** [Security](/docs/security), [Limits](/docs/limits), [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting).\n\n\n---\n\nReference\n\nPage 39 of 40\n\n# Limits\n\nEvery number Egg enforces, grouped by what it limits, with the place in the code that sets it.\n\nEach table has a third column with the setting or constant that holds the number. Paths are inside `web/src`. If a number here ever disagrees with what you see, the code is right and the page is the bug.\n\n## Projects and deploys\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Servers per account | 5. | `MAX_SERVERS_PER_USER, server/servers.ts` |\n| Project name | 1 to 60 characters. | `createProjectInput, server/projects.ts` |\n| Project slug | Lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, 1 to 40 characters, not starting or ending with a hyphen. | `SLUG, server/deploy/layout.ts` |\n| Port | 1024 to 65535, and not 20000 to 29999, which previews use. | `port, server/projects.ts` |\n| Commands | 500 characters each, on one line. | `command, server/projects.ts` |\n| Root directory | 200 characters, relative, no .. | `rootDir, server/projects.ts` |\n| Branch name | 200 characters. Letters, digits, dots, underscores, hyphens and slashes, not starting with a hyphen. | `branch, server/projects.ts` |\n| Deployments at once | One per project. Two at a time overall by default. One build at a time on a container. | `WORKER_CONCURRENCY, instrumentation.ts, server/deploy/build-lock.ts` |\n| Clone | 5 minutes, on Egg’s machine. | `server/deploy/build.ts` |\n| Build | 15 minutes, on your container. It cannot be changed. | `BUILD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, server/deploy/container-build.ts` |\n| Build output | 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes), measured on the packed archive. | `MAX_ARCHIVE_BYTES, server/deploy/build.ts` |\n| Build cap | On your container, for each build: a CPU cap (2 cores by default), 4096 processes, no swap, and memory (512 MB by default, at least 1024 MB for a project with a build step when the container has room, never more than the container’s memory minus 512 MB). The CPU cap and memory are set for each server. | `systemdRunArgs, server/deploy/container-build.ts` |\n| Build memory | 512 MB up to the server’s reported memory, or 8192 MB when it reports none. Default 512 MB. | `lib/build-memory.ts` |\n| Free disk before a build | 2048 MB. | `MIN_FREE_DISK_MB, server/deploy/container-probe.ts` |\n| Build log | 20,000 lines, 4000 characters each. | `MAX_LOG_ROWS, server/deploy/logger.ts` |\n| Health wait | 45 seconds after the switch. The service restarts at the switch, so the app is unavailable for a moment. A failed production deploy puts the previous release back. | `healthTimeoutSeconds, server/deploy/pipeline.ts` |\n| Production releases kept | The 5 newest ready ones, plus the live one. | `KEEP_RELEASES, server/deploy/layout.ts` |\n| Previews kept | The 2 newest. | `KEEP_PREVIEWS, server/deploy/layout.ts` |\n| Preview ports | 20000 to 29999, one per preview. | `PREVIEW_PORT_MIN, PREVIEW_PORT_MAX, server/projects.ts` |\n| Environment variables | 100 per project, names up to 100 characters, values up to 10,000. | `envInput, server/projects.ts` |\n| Bulk cancel or delete of previews | 50 per request. | `MAX_BULK, server/deployments-bulk.ts` |\n\n## Container features\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Terminal | Closes after 10 minutes without input or 60 minutes in total. | `TERMINAL_LIMITS, server/terminal.ts` |\n| Terminals open | 1 per project per person, 5 per account, 50 on the server. | `TERMINAL_LIMITS, server/terminal.ts` |\n| Terminal input | 8 KB a request, 300 requests a minute. | `inputBytes, RATE_LIMITS.terminalInput` |\n| Terminal output kept | The last 64 KB. | `bufferBytes, server/terminal.ts` |\n| File preview and edit | The first 1 MB. | `READ_LIMIT, server/files-paths.ts` |\n| File upload and download | 50 MB. | `UPLOAD_LIMIT, DOWNLOAD_LIMIT, server/files-paths.ts` |\n| Folder listing | 3000 entries. | `LIST_LIMIT, server/files.ts` |\n| File reads | 120 a minute. | `FILE_READ_LIMIT, server/files.ts` |\n| File changes | 60 a minute. | `RATE_LIMITS.fileWrite, server/rate-limit.ts` |\n| Leftover removal | 50 items a request, 10 requests a minute. | `MAX_ORPHANS_PER_REQUEST, server/inventory.ts` |\n| Conflict checks | 20 a minute. | `app/api/projects/conflicts/route.ts` |\n| Scheduled jobs | 5 per project. At most one run every 5 minutes. A run is stopped after 30 minutes. | `MAX_JOBS_PER_PROJECT, server/scheduled-jobs.ts; MIN_GAP_MINUTES, lib/cron-schedule.ts; JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, server/deploy/jobs-remote.ts` |\n\n## Access tokens and the API\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Active tokens | 25 per account. Revoked and expired ones do not count. | `MAX_TOKENS_PER_USER, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Token name | 1 to 60 characters. | `createTokenInput, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Token life | 7, 30, 90 or 365 days. 90 is the default. | `createTokenInput, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Terminal or file changes | A token holding either lives 7 days at most. | `SHORT_LIVED_MAX_DAYS, server/api-tokens.ts` |\n| Reads | 120 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.read, server/api-v1-kit.ts` |\n| Changes | 30 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.write` |\n| Calls that open a session to your container | 10 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.ssh` |\n| Terminal input through the API | 300 a minute per token. | `API_LIMITS.input` |\n| Invalid tokens | 60 a minute per address. Counted only when TRUST_PROXY is on. | `RATE_LIMITS.badToken, server/rate-limit.ts` |\n| Request body | 16 KB. A file write takes 4 MB, terminal input 64 KB, webhooks 4 KB and the MCP endpoint 64 KB. | `DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES, server/api-v1-kit.ts` |\n| File write through the API | 1 MB. A download is 2 MB. | `server/api-v1-shell.ts` |\n| List sizes | Deployments 1 to 100, runtime log lines 1 to 1000, build log lines 1 to 1000, activity 1 to 100, analytics 1 to 90 days, resource history 1 to 168 hours. | `query fields in server/api-v1*.ts` |\n| Webhooks | 10 per account. | `MAX_WEBHOOKS_PER_USER, server/webhooks.ts` |\n\n## Watching and history\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Uptime checks | Every 5 minutes by default. Down after 2 failed checks in a row, which you can set from 1 to 10 per project. | `MONITOR_INTERVAL_MS, server/jobs.ts` |\n| Alert limits | Disk, memory and CPU 1 to 100, default 90. Slow response 50 to 60000 ms. | `server/notify.ts` |\n| Resource history | Sampled every 5 minutes, kept 7 days, shown for 24 hours or 7 days. | `SAMPLE_KEEP_MS, server/monitor.ts` |\n| Runtime logs | Up to 300 lines shown, and 300 more each time you press Load older lines. A search or the Warnings and errors level reads up to 1000 lines for each read. | `components/logs-view.tsx, lib/log-page.ts` |\n| Alert history | The newest 50 shown, kept 90 days. | `ALERT_KEEP_MS, server/notify.ts` |\n| Analytics history | 30, 60 or 90 days for each project, 90 by default. | `retentionDays, server/analytics-config.ts; prunePageviews, server/analytics.ts` |\n| Funnel | 2 to 6 steps. | `MIN_STEPS, MAX_STEPS, server/analytics-config.ts` |\n| Analytics events | 30 per 10 seconds per project and address. Page views are 1 per second. With TRUST_PROXY off every visitor shares one address. | `server/analytics-events.ts, server/analytics.ts` |\n| Analytics fields | Allowed campaign values up to 20 each for source, medium and campaign, 40 characters each, event value 40, error message 200, 5 errors per page view. Speed and returning-visitor figures need 50 page views and 20 visitors. | `server/analytics-fields.ts, server/analytics-retention.ts` |\n| Live view and retention | A 5 minute window. Signals are kept 1 hour. The retention code changes every 90 days. | `server/analytics.ts (LIVE_WINDOW_MS, LIVE_KEEP_MS, RETENTION_PERIOD_MS)` |\n| Status page | 30 days and the latest 10 outages. | `server/status-page.ts` |\n| Webhook delivery | 3 attempts, 5 seconds each, history kept 30 days. | `MAX_ATTEMPTS, DELIVERY_RETENTION_MS, server/webhooks.ts` |\n| Deployments page | All projects: the newest 100, shown 25 at a time. One project’s tab: 25 at a time with Load older, and search and filters run on the server. | `server/queries.ts, server/deployment-page.ts, components/deployments-view.tsx` |\n| Activity | 50 a page. A search reads the newest 5000. An export is 5000 entries and 10 a minute. | `PAGE_SIZE, SCAN_LIMIT, EXPORT_ROW_LIMIT` |\n\n## Sign-in\n\n| What | Limit | Set in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session | 30 days. | `SESSION_DAYS, server/auth/session.ts` |\n| Fresh sign-in | 5 minutes. | `REAUTH_WINDOW_MS, server/auth/session.ts` |\n| Sign-in handshake | 10 minutes. | `app/api/auth/login/route.ts` |\n| Sign-in attempts | 20 a minute per address. Counted only when TRUST_PROXY is on. | `RATE_LIMITS.signIn, server/rate-limit.ts` |\n| Dashboard JSON request | 256 KB. | `JSON_BODY_LIMIT, server/body-limit.ts` |\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEach allowance is counted against the thing that can misbehave. An access token has its own counts, so one noisy script cannot use up the allowance of another script or of you in the browser. Terminal and file requests are counted per account because the container behind them is shared by every project you have. Bad tokens are counted per address, because a token that does not check out has no account to count against. The counters live in the memory of the running process, so a restart of Egg clears them, and a copy of Egg that runs several processes counts in each one.\n\n## What can go wrong\n\n- `Too many requests for this token. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. A token went over its reads, changes, container sessions or terminal input. Wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 60 tokens that did not check out came from one address in a minute. Fix the token your script sends.\n- `Too many requests for terminal input. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 300 input requests a minute for your account.\n- `Too many file requests. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Listing, reading and downloading are 120 a minute.\n- `Too many requests for file changes. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Creating, writing, renaming and deleting are 60 a minute.\n- `Too many exports. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 10 CSV exports a minute.\n- `Too many checks. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 20 conflict checks a minute.\n- `You can connect up to 5 servers.`\n\n  Disconnect one you no longer use.\n- `You can have up to 25 active tokens. Revoke one first.`\n\n  Revoke a token you no longer use.\n- `You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.`\n\n  Delete a webhook you no longer use.\n- `A project can have up to 100 variables.`\n\n  Delete a variable you do not need.\n\nYour container’s size\n\nHow much CPU, memory and disk your Nest container has is set by Nest, not by Egg. Test connection and the Container page show what it has.\n\n## Example: Poll a deploy without hitting a limit\n\n### Given\n\nA script that starts a deploy and waits for it to finish.\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Start it\n\n   One POST to the deployments endpoint is one of your 30 changes a minute.\n2. Step 2: Poll\n\n   List the project’s deployments every 2 seconds. That is 30 reads a minute, a quarter of the 120 allowed.\n3. Step 3: Back off\n\n   On a 429, wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header, then carry on.\n\n### Result\n\nThe script finishes a normal build without a 429. Fetching the build log counts against the smaller 10 a minute allowance, so ask for it once the deploy is done.\n\n**Related:** [Security](/docs/security), [Access tokens and the API](/docs/api), [Container, terminal and files](/docs/container).\n\n\n---\n\nReference\n\nPage 40 of 40\n\n# Troubleshooting\n\nOne entry per message Egg shows, with the fix. Find the heading for what you were doing.\n\nMessages are quoted as the product prints them. A part in angle brackets, such as `<n>`, is filled in with a name or a number. Where a message comes with a status code, the code is the first thing in the fix.\n\n## Test connection fails\n\n- `Nest rejected the key. Check that you pasted the public key on the SSH Keys page of the Nest dashboard, and that the username is exactly your Nest username.`\n\n  Paste the public key again, exactly as shown. After New key, the old one is dead. Check the username.\n- `Timed out connecting to Nest. Your container may be stopped or starting; try again in a minute.`\n\n  Wait a minute and press Test connection again.\n- `Could not reach the Nest SSH host (<reason>).`\n\n  The reason is the network error. Try again later.\n- `Confirm host key`\n\n  The first Test connection stops here on purpose. Check the fingerprint against the one Nest shows for your server, then press Confirm host key.\n- `Confirm host key: open the Connect page, check the fingerprint of this server and confirm it first.`\n\n  409. A deploy, file change or terminal was tried before the host key was confirmed. Open Connect, press Test connection, then Confirm host key.\n- `The host key of <host> changed (was <old>, now <new>). Nothing was sent. If you expected this, reconnect the server.`\n\n  This stops on purpose. Disconnect and add the server again. The box asks whether to keep or delete the server’s projects. You confirm the new fingerprint on the next Test connection.\n- `SSH error: <message>`\n\n  Anything else. Read the message, then try again.\n- `That does not look like a Nest username. Use the name you log in with, for example sam-r.`\n\n  Use letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen, starting with a letter or digit, up to 32 characters.\n- `<name> is already connected.`\n\n  One server per Nest username. Use the one you have.\n- `<name> is disconnected. Connect it again to manage its projects.`\n\n  409. You disconnected it and kept its projects. Connect the same username again.\n- `You can connect up to 5 servers.`\n\n  Disconnect one you no longer use.\n\nAfter a successful test, the list of issues may show warnings such as “curl is missing. Health checks fall back to a plain TCP connection check.” They are shown for your information and do not block a deploy.\n\n## The build fails\n\n- `Could not clone the repository. Check the branch name and that Egg has access to it.`\n\n  Check the production branch in Settings and, for a private repository, that the GitHub app is installed on it.\n- `The root directory \"<dir>\" does not exist in this repository.`\n\n  Fix Root directory in Settings, Build and Run.\n- `The build failed (exit <code>).`\n\n  Read the log above it. The usual causes are a missing lockfile or build script, the wrong root directory, or a command that needs a variable you have not set.\n- `The build timed out.`\n\n  The clone took longer than 5 minutes. The limit is fixed. Nothing changes on your container.\n- `The build took longer than 15 minutes and was stopped. Your app is still running.`\n\n  The build on your container ran past 15 minutes. Make the build faster. This limit is fixed. “Your app is still running.” is added only when a release was already serving. Egg deletes the new release folder.\n- `The build needed more memory than the cap of <n> MB. Your app is still running.`\n\n  Exit 137. The build went over its cap and was stopped, and your app was not. Raise Build memory (MB) on the Container page and deploy again, or use less memory in the build. The cap never goes above your container’s memory minus 512 MB.\n- `The build did not start: your container reports <n> MB of memory, and 512 MB of that is kept for your app, which leaves too little to build in. Use a larger container.`\n\n  The cap would be under 256 MB. Egg keeps 512 MB of your container’s memory for your app, so a container with under 768 MB cannot build.\n- `Another build has been running on this container for more than 30 minutes, so this one did not start. Your app is still running.`\n\n  One build runs at a time on a container. Wait for the other build to finish or cancel it, then deploy again.\n- `Egg could not check your container before building, so nothing was uploaded. Check that the container is running and try again.`\n\n  The check before the upload got no answer. Check the Container page and Test connection, then deploy again.\n- `Free up disk space on your container: <n> MB is free and a build needs at least 2048 MB.`\n\n  Delete files you do not need on your container, then deploy again. Nothing was uploaded.\n- `Builds on your container run under a memory cap with systemd-run, which is not installed here, so the build did not start. Install systemd.`\n\n  Your container has no systemd-run, which the cap needs. Install systemd.\n- `Your container cannot apply a memory cap to a build (it needs systemd with cgroup v2 and the memory controller), so the build did not start rather than run without a limit.`\n\n  Egg does not run a build without a limit. Use a container with systemd, cgroup v2 and the memory controller.\n- `Install coreutils on your container: sudo apt install coreutils (the build needs the timeout command).`\n\n  Run the command on your container, then deploy again.\n- `Install Node.js <version> or newer on your container (nodejs.org), then deploy again.`\n\n  No Node.js was found. <version> is 22.13 when the project has no engines range. With a range, the line says: Install Node.js on your container (this project needs <range>), then deploy again.\n- `Node.js <installed> on your container does not match what this project needs (<wanted>). Install a matching Node.js on your container (nodejs.org), then deploy again.`\n\n  <wanted> is the engines range in package.json, or >=22.13 when there is none.\n- `Install npm on your container: sudo apt install npm (installing Node.js from nodejs.org includes it).`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again.\n- `This project uses <pnpm or yarn>, which needs corepack. On your container run: npm install -g corepack`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again.\n- `Install Python 3 on your container: sudo apt install python3`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again.\n- `Install python3-venv on your container: sudo apt install python3-venv`\n\n  Run the command, then deploy again. On Debian and Ubuntu venv is a separate package.\n- `Build memory must be a whole number of MB from 512 to <max>. Change it on the Container page.`\n\n  The saved build memory no longer fits the range, for example after the server reported less memory. Open Container, set Build memory (MB) inside the range and press Save.\n- `The output folder \"<dir>\" was not created by the build.`\n\n  Set Output directory to the folder your build really writes.\n- `The build output is <n> MB, over the 524.288 MB limit.`\n\n  Leave out what the app does not need to run. The limit is 500 MiB (524,288,000 bytes), measured on the packed archive. Nothing was uploaded to your container.\n- `Set a start command for this project (for example ./my-binary).`\n\n  A Custom command project needs a Start command.\n- `Static sites need Python 3 or Node.js on your Nest container to be served.`\n\n  Install one of them on the container.\n- `A deployment stays on Building or Deploying and nothing moves`\n\n  The worker that owned it stopped. Egg does not free the job on its own, because commands it started on your container may still be running, and that project cannot start another deployment. Only the person who runs Egg can fix it: see Recovering a stale deployment in the self-hosting guide.\n- `No free preview ports on this server.`\n\n  All of 20000 to 29999 are in use. Delete old previews.\n- `Further log output was dropped: this deployment reached its log limit.`\n\n  The log holds 20,000 lines. Make the build print less. The build itself carried on.\n\n## It built, but the app never answered\n\n- `The new release did not become healthy on port <port>. Make sure your app listens on the PORT environment variable and on 0.0.0.0.`\n\n  Listen on PORT and on 0.0.0.0, not only localhost. The log shows the last 30 lines of the app’s output. For a production deploy, the previous release is put back, so the app is unavailable for a short time while the new release starts and again while the old one returns.\n- `Service crashed on start`\n\n  The service failed before the wait ended. Read the output printed under it.\n- `The previous release did not come back either. Check the logs.`\n\n  Open Logs and read the runtime lines, then redeploy or use Instant rollback to an earlier release.\n\nA health path that answers with anything from 200 to 499 counts as healthy, so a 404 passes. Only 500 and above fails.\n\n## A deploy is blocked by something on the container\n\n### The conflict check\n\n- `Nothing was changed on your container. <details>`\n\n  The conflict check refused. The details say what is in the way. Choose another name or port, or stop that program yourself first.\n- `A service called egg-<slug>.service already exists and Egg did not create it. It will not be replaced. Choose another project name.`\n\n  Rename the project.\n- `Port <n> is already in use by <process>. Egg will not stop it. Choose another port, or stop that program yourself first.`\n\n  Pick another port, or stop that program.\n- `<path> already exists and was not created by Egg, so it was left alone.`\n\n  Pick another name, or move that folder yourself.\n- `Could not check this container just now: <reason>. The deploy checks again before it changes anything.`\n\n  Try again in a moment.\n- `Too many checks. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. The check is 20 a minute for your account.\n\n## A project will not create\n\n### Project messages\n\n- `You already have a project called \"<slug>\".`\n\n  Pick another name.\n- `Port <n> is already used by another project on this server.`\n\n  Pick another port. In Settings the same refusal reads “Port <n> is already used on this server.”\n- `Ports 20000 to 29999 are reserved for previews`\n\n  Choose a port from 1024 to 19999 or 30000 to 65535.\n- `The project name must use lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (up to 40).`\n\n  Fix the name. The slug is at most 40 characters.\n- `Only plain https repository URLs are supported.`\n\n  Use an https URL. Only github.com is supported.\n- `The project was created, but <NAME> was not saved: <reason>. Press Deploy to try again.`\n\n  The project exists. Fix the variable in Settings and deploy.\n- `Could not remove the project from your container.`\n\n  Your records are kept so you can try again.\n\nA project made from a pasted public URL does not deploy on push. It builds from Deploy, a deploy hook or the API. Import it through the GitHub app for push deploys.\n\n## A rollback or cancel is refused\n\n### Rollback and cancel messages\n\n- `That release was removed from your server to save space.`\n\n  Only 5 production releases are kept. Deploy the commit again.\n- `That deployment is already live.`\n\n  Pick an earlier one.\n- `Only production deployments can be rolled back to.`\n\n  Choose a production deployment.\n- `That deployment never finished, so there is nothing to roll back to.`\n\n  Choose one that was Ready.\n- `The older release did not become healthy, so nothing was changed.`\n\n  The release that was live is still live. Check the older release’s logs.\n- `That deployment is already finished.`\n\n  Cancel works only while a deployment is Queued, Building or Deploying.\n- `Only old previews that are not live or removed can be deleted in bulk.`\n\n  409. Pick previews that are finished and not the current one.\n- `A cancel by API returns 409 once the release has been switched.`\n\n  The live release keeps running. Check the deployment’s state.\n\n## A variable will not save\n\n### Variable messages\n\n- `Names use letters, digits and underscores, and do not start with a digit`\n\n  Rename the variable.\n- `That name is set by Egg`\n\n  PORT, HOST, PATH, HOME and EGG are reserved.\n- `A project can have up to 100 variables.`\n\n  Delete one you do not need.\n- `No example env file (such as .env.example) was found in the repository.`\n\n  Add .env.example, or type the names by hand.\n\nA saved change reaches your app at the next deployment. Redeploy to apply it. A variable with an empty value is left out of the app’s environment rather than set to an empty string.\n\n## A domain stays on “Needs attention”\n\n### Domain messages\n\n- `No CNAME found yet. Create one pointing to <target>, or add a TXT record with the value domain-verification=<name>. DNS changes can take a few minutes.`\n\n  Add the CNAME or the TXT record at your DNS provider, wait, and press Check.\n- `The CNAME points to <x>. It should point to <target>.`\n\n  Change the CNAME value.\n- `The name does not resolve yet. Add it in the Nest dashboard first.`\n\n  Add the domain and port on the Domains page of the Nest dashboard.\n- `DNS could not be checked right now. Try again in a minute.`\n\n  Press Check again.\n- `That name is reserved. Choose another domain.`\n\n  The name is on the reserved list, for example admin, api, dashboard, status or www. Pick another name or a domain of your own.\n- `Only your Nest name, <name>.hackclub.app, can be used under hackclub.app.`\n\n  Under hackclub.app only your own Nest name and names beneath it are allowed.\n- `Use a public domain name such as app.example.com. IP addresses, localhost and internal names cannot be used.`\n\n  Use a real public domain name.\n- `DNS no longer points here. Check your record.`\n\n  A domain that was active no longer passes the check. Restore the CNAME or TXT record and press Check. Egg keeps the domain.\n- `<domain> is already added.`\n\n  A domain can be added once across all accounts.\n- `Certificate not verified yet`\n\n  The app check found no trusted certificate. Wait for Nest to issue one, then press Check again.\n\n## A deploy hook answers 409\n\n### Deploy hook messages\n\n- `A deployment is already running for this project.`\n\n  The hook answers 409 while any deployment of the project is queued, building or deploying. Try again when it finishes.\n- `Unknown hook`\n\n  The URL is wrong or was replaced. Make a new URL.\n- `Request body must be JSON`\n\n  Send valid JSON, or no body. The only field is branch.\n\n## No comment appears on a pull request\n\nThe pull request must come from the same repository, must not be a draft, and its branch must not be your production branch. The GitHub app needs Pull requests read and write and the Pull request event. When a comment fails the deploy is unaffected and nothing is shown to you. A pull request from a fork is never built.\n\n## Alerts or notifications do not arrive\n\n### Notification and alert messages\n\n- `Add a webhook URL first.`\n\n  Save a Discord or Slack webhook URL in Notifications.\n- `Use the plain https webhook URL from Discord or Slack.`\n\n  Remove ports, queries and credentials.\n- `Only Discord and Slack incoming webhook URLs are supported.`\n\n  Use a discord.com, discordapp.com or hooks.slack.com URL.\n- `Discord answered <status>.`\n\n  Slack gives the same message with its own name. The webhook was deleted or the URL is wrong. Make a new one.\n- `Failed checks must be a whole number from 1 to 10.`\n\n  Fix the number in Settings, Alerts.\n- `The response time limit must be at least 50 ms.`\n\n  Use 50 to 60000, or leave it empty to turn the limit off.\n- `Could not load alert settings`\n\n  Press Try again.\n\nIf the test arrives but alerts do not, check three things. An app is reported down only after 2 failed checks in a row by default. Alerts may be paused for the project. And the channel you want may be switched off.\n\n### Webhook messages\n\n- `The URL must start with https://.`\n\n  A signed webhook goes to an https address only.\n- `Use the standard https port (443).`\n\n  Remove the port from the address.\n- `Use a domain name, not an IP address.`\n\n  Use a full public domain name.\n- `Use a full public domain name.`\n\n  A name with no dot, such as localhost, is refused.\n- `Remove the user name and password from the URL.`\n\n  Credentials in an address are never accepted.\n- `Remove the # part from the URL.`\n\n  Take off the fragment.\n- `You can have up to 10 webhooks. Delete one first.`\n\n  409. Delete a webhook you no longer use.\n- `Test message delivered`\n\n  The receiver answered with a 2xx status. The delivery is listed under Recent deliveries.\n\nA delivery that fails shows a short code instead of a message from your receiver, because the response is never stored. `http_500` (or another `http_` status) means your receiver answered that status. `timeout` means it did not answer within 5 seconds. `redirect` means it answered with a redirect, which is not followed. `blocked_address` means the name points at a private or internal address. `dns_failed` means the name did not resolve. `network` means the connection failed. `interrupted` means a restart cut the delivery off. A status of 500 and above, 429, 408, a timeout, a network failure and a failed lookup are tried again up to 3 times in all, with pauses of 2 and 10 seconds. Any other 4xx status is not.\n\n## The terminal, files or leftovers are refused\n\nThese refusals are about the terminal, files and leftovers. The [container](/docs/container) page explains the switches.\n\n### Terminal messages\n\n- `The terminal is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Terminal in Account. Nothing else is needed to read files or the Container page.\n- `Changing files is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on File changes. Reading and downloading still work without it.\n- `Removing leftovers is turned off for this server. Turn it on in Account, under Container permissions.`\n\n  403. Turn on Removing leftovers. Leftovers stay listed while it is off.\n- `Confirm with Hack Club first. Turning this on needs a sign-in in the last 5 minutes.`\n\n  403. Flip the switch again. It sends you through Hack Club and back to Account, where the switch is applied.\n- `This server has not been verified yet. Run Test connection on the Connect page first.`\n\n  409. The host key is not pinned yet, and a terminal never opens to a host Egg has not checked. Press Test connection.\n- `A terminal is already open for this project. Resume or close it first.`\n\n  409. Only one terminal per project and person. Press Resume terminal, or Close it, then open a new one.\n- `You can have 5 terminals open at once. Close one first.`\n\n  429. One account may hold 5 terminals across all its projects, so one account cannot use up the server’s share. Close one you are not using.\n- `Too many terminals are open right now. Try again soon.`\n\n  503. The server as a whole holds 50 terminals. Wait a little and open yours again.\n- `The terminal was closed before it opened.`\n\n  403. You signed out, or switched Terminal off, while it was still connecting. Open it again.\n- `Could not open a terminal: <reason>`\n\n  502. Egg could not reach your container. The reason is the SSH error. Check the container is running, then try again.\n- `Could not open a terminal on your container.`\n\n  502. The same, with no detail from the connection. Try again in a minute.\n- `The terminal size must be 20 to 400 columns and 5 to 200 rows.`\n\n  400. Only scripts and the API see this: the page sends a size that fits.\n- `Send at most 8192 bytes at a time.`\n\n  413. One input request carries at most 8 KB. Split the text.\n- `That is too much input at once.`\n\n  413. The whole request was over 64 KB, which is room for 8 KB of text after JSON escaping.\n- `Too many requests for terminal input. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 300 input requests a minute for your account. Wait for the seconds in Retry-After.\n- `Terminal session not found`\n\n  404. The session ended, or the id is wrong, or it belongs to someone else. Open a new one.\n\n### File messages\n\n- `This project has no folder on your container yet. Deploy it first.`\n\n  404. The folder is created by the first deploy. Deploy once, then open Files.\n- `This location is read only. Files in shared/ can be changed. The live release (current/) cannot.`\n\n  403. Open shared/ and work there. The page shows the same rule as “current/ is the live release and is read only.”\n- `current/ is the live release and is read only. Changing it would change the code your app runs. Make the change in your repository and deploy, or use shared/ for files that last.`\n\n  403. Change the code in your repository and deploy. Put files that must last in shared/.\n- `This file holds the project's environment variable values, so it is not shown here. Change the values in the project's settings.`\n\n  403. The Files tab never opens a variables file. Change the values in the project’s settings, then deploy.\n- `That file holds the project's environment variable values, which are never returned by the API.`\n\n  403. The API refuses those files too. Use env:read to list names.\n- `Nothing exists at that path.`\n\n  404. Check the spelling, or refresh the folder.\n- `That path is not a folder.`\n\n  409. You asked to list a file. Open its folder instead.\n- `That path is not a file.`\n\n  409. You asked to read or download a folder.\n- `That path is a folder.`\n\n  409. You tried to write over a folder. Pick a file name.\n- `That path is a symbolic link. Files are not written through links.`\n\n  409. A link could lead outside shared/, so it is never written through. Write to the real path.\n- `Something with that name already exists.`\n\n  409. Pick another name. An upload to an existing file asks whether to replace it.\n- `That path leaves the project folder.`\n\n  403. A path must stay inside the project’s folder, links included.\n- `That file is over the 50 MB limit.`\n\n  413. Uploads and downloads stop at 50 MB. Put larger files on the container by SSH.\n- `The upload arrived incomplete. Nothing was saved.`\n\n  The connection broke part way. Upload again.\n- `Too many file requests. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Listing, reading and downloading are 120 a minute for your account.\n- `Too many requests for file changes. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Creating, writing, renaming and deleting are 60 a minute for your account.\n- `Enter a name.`\n\n  A new name cannot be empty.\n- `A name cannot contain a slash.`\n\n  A rename or new folder takes one name. Open the folder first.\n- `That name is not allowed.`\n\n  “.” and “..” are not names.\n- `That is the same path.`\n\n  A rename must change the name.\n- `A folder cannot move into itself.`\n\n  Pick a destination outside the folder you are moving.\n- `This container's tools are too limited to list files.`\n\n  502. The container lacks tools Egg uses to list a folder. Test connection lists what is missing.\n- `Your container could not finish that. Try again.`\n\n  502. The container failed part way. Try again, and check Container for disk space.\n\n## The API answers 401, 403 or 429\n\n### API and token messages\n\n- `Send an access token as an Authorization: Bearer egg_... header.`\n\n  401. Add the header. A cookie is never accepted here.\n- `That access token is not valid.`\n\n  401. Missing, revoked, expired and unknown tokens all look the same on purpose. Make a new one on the Developer page.\n- `This token does not have the <scope> permission.`\n\n  403. The message names it. Press Edit on the token on the Developer page and tick it. The change applies to the next request.\n- `Too many requests for unrecognised access tokens. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. 60 tokens that did not check out from one address in a minute. Fix the token your script sends.\n- `Too many requests for this token. Wait <n> second(s) and try again.`\n\n  429. Wait for the seconds in the Retry-After header. Reads are 120 a minute, changes 30, container sessions 10, terminal input 300.\n- `No such item, or it belongs to someone else.`\n\n  404. Check the id. Another account’s items answer the same way.\n- `The request body is too large.`\n\n  413. 16 KB for most calls, 4 MB for a file write, 64 KB for MCP and 4 KB for webhooks.\n- `Request body must be JSON`\n\n  400. Send valid JSON, or no body.\n- `Request body must be a JSON object`\n\n  400. Send { … }, not a list or a bare value.\n- `Unknown field \"<name>\". Allowed: ...`\n\n  400. Unknown body fields are refused so a typo cannot silently do nothing. The message lists the allowed ones.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Pick 7 days, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Pick 7 days, or untick Use the terminal and Change files.\n- `A token with the terminal or file change permission expires in 7 days at most. Set the expiry to 7 days in the same change, or remove that permission.`\n\n  400. Raised when you edit a token. Set Expires after to 7 days in the same edit.\n- `You can have up to 25 active tokens. Revoke one first.`\n\n  400. Revoke a token you no longer use. Expired and revoked ones do not count.\n- `Give the token a name.`\n\n  400. A name is 1 to 60 characters.\n- `Pick at least one permission.`\n\n  400. A token with no permissions can do nothing.\n- `Send at least one field to change.`\n\n  400. An edit must change the name, the permissions or the expiry.\n- `This token is revoked and cannot be edited.`\n\n  409. A revoked token is final. Make a new one.\n- `Token not found`\n\n  404. It was deleted, or it is not yours.\n- `content is not valid base64.`\n\n  400. With encoding set to base64, send standard base64 text.\n- `The API writes files of up to 1 MB. Upload larger files in the dashboard.`\n\n  413. Use the Files tab for bigger files.\n- `That file is over the 2 MB limit for this endpoint. Download larger files in the dashboard.`\n\n  413. The API download returns at most 2 MB, as base64.\n- `This endpoint only accepts POST.`\n\n  405. The MCP address takes POST only.\n\n### Agent endpoint messages\n\n- `Parse error`\n\n  400. The body was not JSON.\n- `Not a JSON-RPC 2.0 message.`\n\n  400. Send { \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 1, \"method\": ..., \"params\": ... }.\n- `Batches are not supported. Send one message per request.`\n\n  400. Send one message at a time.\n- `Method not found: <method>`\n\n  Use initialize, ping, tools/list or tools/call.\n- `Unknown tool: <name>`\n\n  That tool is not in this connection’s list. Call tools/list to see what your token can run; a tool can also be hidden by the toolset or scope you picked on the address.\n- `Unknown toolset \"<name>\". Choose from: ...`\n\n  400. Pick a name from the list in the message. core is added for you.\n- `Unknown scope \"<name>\". Use read or deploy.`\n\n  400. Use read or deploy.\n- `This operation changes live systems and has not run. To run it, call call_api again with confirm set to \"<operationId>\".`\n\n  The compact server held the call. Send the same call again with confirm set to that id.\n- `tools/call needs a tool name.`\n\n  Send params with a name.\n- `Tool arguments must be an object.`\n\n  Send arguments as { … }.\n- `<code>: <message>`\n\n  A tool that fails comes back as a result with isError set, and the text starts with the error code, for example forbidden: This token does not have the files:write permission.\n\n## Sign-in fails\n\nThe sign-in messages and their fixes are on the [account](/docs/account) page.\n\n## Monitoring, the status page and analytics\n\n### What you see\n\n- `Could not read the service from your container.`\n\n  The Service card could not reach the container within 20 seconds. Press Check now, and check the container is running.\n- `The service is running but nothing answered on its health path.`\n\n  The app starts but the health path gives no answer. Fix the path in Settings, Build and Run, or the app.\n- `The health path answered HTTP <code>.`\n\n  A status of 500 or above counts as down. A 404 does not.\n- `The health path took <ms> ms to answer, over your limit of <n> ms.`\n\n  Raise the Slow response limit, or make the page faster.\n- `No data yet`\n\n  A status page says this until the first check has run. Wait for the next 5 minute pass.\n- `Not responding`\n\n  The app has stopped answering, by the same check as the alerts.\n- `Not enough data`\n\n  Percentages and medians need 50 visits behind them, and speed figures need 50 page views. The counts are shown.\n- `Not enough data yet`\n\n  Returning visitors need at least 20 visitors in the group, counted from the day Retention was turned on.\n- `Unique visitors need retention on`\n\n  Turn on Retention under What else to record. Without the random id in the browser, visitors cannot be told apart.\n- `A funnel needs at least 2 steps`\n\n  Add a second step, or leave the funnel empty.\n- `A funnel has at most 6 steps`\n\n  Remove a step.\n- `Nothing is counted on a page`\n\n  The snippet is missing or the page was not redeployed. Nothing is counted for Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, on localhost, or for known bots.\n\nWhy it works this way\n\nEach entry quotes the message word for word so you can search this page for the text you saw. The status code leads the fix because it tells you what kind of problem it is: a 4xx means the request needs to change, a 429 means wait for the Retry-After seconds, and a 502 or 503 means Egg could not reach your container or has no room right now, so check the container and try again.\n\n## Example: Fix a build that stops with exit 137\n\n### Given\n\nA project named recipe-api whose deployment fails with “The build needed more memory than the cap of 1024 MB. Your app is still running.”\n\n### Do\n\n1. Step 1: Open Container\n\n   Pick the server that holds recipe-api, then find the Build memory card.\n\n   In the dashboard: Containerthen Build memory\n2. Step 2: Raise the number\n\n   Read the range in the hint under Build memory (MB). Type 1536 if the hint allows it, then press Save.\n3. Step 3: Deploy again\n\n   Open the project and press Redeploy, or push a commit.\n\n### Result\n\nThe message “Build memory set to 1536 MB” appears, the next build runs with that limit and finishes, and Activity lists “Changed the build memory limit for” the server. The cap is at most your container’s memory minus 512 MB, so on a container that reports less than 2048 MB, make the build use less instead.\n\nStill stuck?\n\nLook at [your dashboard](/projects) or open an issue on [GitHub](https://github.com/jeremy341/egg). Leave real keys, tokens and variable values out of it.\n\n";
export const INDEX = "# Egg docs\n\n> Egg pushes a GitHub repository to a free Hack Club Nest container. These are its docs.\n\n## Start\n\n- [How it works](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/overview.md): What Egg does, what it needs from you and the steps it takes on every push.\n- [Getting started](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/getting-started.md): The four chapters of the guided first-project tour in text: connect your container, import a repository, watch the first deployment and give it an address.\n- [Quick start](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/quickstart.md): From a new account to a deployed app in four steps, with a sample repository to follow.\n- [Run Egg yourself](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/run-yourself.md): Run your own copy of Egg with or without a Hack Club account: the setups, how sign-in works, where your apps run, and a link to the full guide.\n- [Run Egg locally](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/run-locally.md): Commands for working on the Egg code: prerequisites, install, the env file, the dev server, development sign-in, demo data, tests, lint and the production build.\n\n## Use it\n\n- [Servers](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/servers.md): Connect your Nest account with its own SSH key, test the link, replace the key or disconnect.\n- [Keys](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/keys.md): The three kinds of SSH key on the Keys page: a read-only GitHub deploy key, a git key for your own host and a personal key, with the one-time private key, replacing and revoking.\n- [Projects and runtimes](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/projects.md): Link a repository, choose a runtime, set the build and run commands, and see what every app can rely on.\n- [Build with GitHub Actions](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/build-github-actions.md): Build on GitHub and send the finished release to Egg: the setup steps, the four secrets, the pull request, safety, limits and what each failing check means.\n- [Build on Nest](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/build-on-nest.md): Build on your own container under a memory and CPU cap, and when to choose it over GitHub Actions.\n- [Deployments](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/deployments.md): What each deployment state means, how to redeploy, roll back, cancel or restart, how many releases are kept and how branch previews work.\n- [Environment variables](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/env.md): Add environment variables for production or previews, import a .env file, and see how values are stored and hidden in logs.\n- [Logs](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/logs.md): Build logs for each deployment and runtime logs for the running app, with a time range, level filter, search, older lines and download.\n- [Domains](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/domains.md): Add your own domain, point its DNS at your container and read the DNS and app checks.\n- [Container, terminal and files](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/container.md): Three switches that decide whether Egg may open a terminal, change files or remove leftovers on your container, and the pages that use them.\n\n## Bots\n\n- [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.js)](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/bots-discordjs.md): Host a discord.js bot as a background app that needs no port: import the repository, add DISCORD_TOKEN and deploy, and systemd keeps the process running.\n- [Deploy a Discord bot (discord.py)](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/bots-discordpy.md): Host a discord.py bot the same way, from a requirements.txt that lists the framework to DISCORD_TOKEN in the environment, with the matching Python runtime.\n- [Deploy a Slack app (Bolt)](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/bots-slack.md): Run a Bolt for JavaScript app in Socket Mode as a worker with SLACK_BOT_TOKEN and SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET, and why an HTTP receiver needs a separate web project.\n- [Deploy a Telegram bot](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/bots-telegram.md): Run a Telegraf or python-telegram-bot bot as a worker with TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN in the environment, and read what Egg checks before it counts as running.\n- [Keep a bot alive](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/bots-alive.md): What keeps a bot running: systemd restarts, the Restarts count on the Service card, crash loops to fix, and moving nightly work to scheduled jobs.\n\n## Automate\n\n- [GitHub and previews](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/github.md): Install the GitHub app, deploy on every push to the production branch and get a preview for each pull request.\n- [Deploy hooks](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/hooks.md): A private URL that starts a deployment from CI, a cron job or a script.\n- [Scheduled jobs](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/jobs.md): Run a command on a schedule inside your current release, as a timer on your container, with the schedule format, limits and actions.\n- [Notifications](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/notifications.md): Discord and Slack messages when a deploy finishes or an app goes down.\n- [Access tokens and the API](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api.md): Make an access token, pick its permissions and call the API or the agent endpoint from scripts and AI agents.\n- [Projects through the API](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api-resources.md): Create a project record from a public HTTPS repository, deploy it and change its settings, with an access token.\n- [Configuration through the API](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api-configuration.md): Read and change a project's variables and a deployment's build, health check, image, pull policy and strategy settings through the API.\n- [Releases](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api-releases.md): List a project's release records, newest first, and page through them with the cursor from each response.\n- [Reading deployment state](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api-query.md): Read a deployment's resource analysis and its volumes through the API, and the scope each read needs.\n- [Monitoring and notifications API](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api-monitoring.md): Read deployment health, logs and resource signals, and manage signed notification channels and their delivery history through the API.\n- [Agent safety rules](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/api-agent-safety.md): The rules agents follow when they call Egg's API: confirm before changing a live resource, and the confirmation field each destructive call needs.\n\n## Watch\n\n- [Monitoring and uptime](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/monitoring.md): Whether the service is running, memory and CPU over time, and uptime checks with alerts.\n- [Status page](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/status-page.md): A public page that says whether an app is answering, with a 30-day outage strip.\n- [Analytics](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/analytics.md): Cookie-free page views for your site, counted by a small script you paste in, with opt-in referrers, campaigns, visitor countries, speed, events and returning visitors.\n- [Activity](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/activity.md): A newest-first log of what you changed and what the uptime monitor saw, with search, filters and a CSV export.\n- [Account](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/account.md): Your Hack Club sign-in, the Settings page, signed-in browsers, container permissions and how to delete your account.\n\n## Reference\n\n- [Security](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/security.md): Where Egg keeps your SSH key and secrets, where builds run and what they can reach, and who can see what.\n- [Running your own](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/selfhost.md): Run your own copy of Egg on your computer or a server, with what it needs and what builds cost that machine.\n- [Limits](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/limits.md): Every number Egg enforces, grouped by what it limits.\n- [Troubleshooting](__EGG_ORIGIN__/docs/troubleshooting.md): What to check when a connection, build, domain, hook, alert or token call goes wrong.\n";
